INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_3_45',
  'An operations team needs to quickly identify all installed applications and their versions across hundreds of Amazon EC2 instances to assess potential vulnerabilities. Which AWS Systems Manager capability can provide this information?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Systems Manager Inventory","AWS Config","AWS Systems Manager Patch Manager","AWS Systems Manager State Manager"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is AWS Systems Manager Inventory. It automatically collects software inventory from your managed instances, including installed applications, their versions, and other system configurations, which is crucial for vulnerability assessments. Option B (AWS Config) monitors resource configurations. Option C (Patch Manager) applies updates. Option D (State Manager) enforces desired state.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  68,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_3',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_3_46',
  'A compliance officer needs to assess whether a group of AWS accounts adheres to a specific set of security and operational best practices, defined by multiple AWS Config rules. Which AWS Config feature simplifies this assessment?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Systems Manager State Manager","AWS Config conformance packs","AWS CloudFormation Guard","AWS Service Catalog"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is AWS Config conformance packs. They allow you to deploy and manage a collection of AWS Config rules and remediation actions as a single entity across multiple accounts and Regions, providing a simplified way to assess compliance against a set of best practices. Option A (Systems Manager State Manager) enforces configuration on running instances. Option C (CloudFormation Guard) validates templates. Option D (Service Catalog) manages approved IT services.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  69,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_3',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_3_47',
  'What is the primary risk associated with managing Amazon EC2 instance configurations manually without automated tooling?',
  'mcq',
  '["Increased initial setup time for new instances.","Reduced security patching frequency across the fleet.","Configuration drift, leading to inconsistencies across instances.","Higher AWS service costs due to inefficient resource utilization."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is configuration drift, which occurs when manual changes are made to instances, causing them to deviate from a desired baseline. This leads to inconsistencies, makes troubleshooting difficult, and can introduce security vulnerabilities. Option A (increased setup time) is a consequence of manual processes, but drift is the core risk. Option B (reduced patching frequency) is a potential outcome but not the primary risk of manual configuration itself. Option D (higher costs) is generally not a direct result of manual configuration, but rather inefficient resource management.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  70,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_3',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_3_48',
  'A company operates a fleet of Amazon EC2 instances and needs to ensure that all instances consistently have specific software installed and configurations applied. They want to automatically remediate any deviations from the desired state. Which AWS Systems Manager capability is best suited for this task?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Systems Manager Inventory","AWS Systems Manager Session Manager","AWS Systems Manager State Manager","AWS Systems Manager Patch Manager"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (AWS Systems Manager State Manager) is used to define and maintain a desired configuration for your instances, automatically enforcing compliance and remediating any drift. This directly addresses anti-pattern ap-2.2-001 by providing automated configuration management. Option A (AWS Systems Manager Inventory) collects information about your instances, but does not enforce configuration. Option B (AWS Systems Manager Session Manager) provides secure shell access to instances. Option D (AWS Systems Manager Patch Manager) automates the patching process for instances, which is a specific type of configuration management but not for general software installation or configuration enforcement.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  71,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_3_49',
  'A company wants to ensure that all newly provisioned Amazon EC2 instances automatically have a specific set of security agents installed and configured. They need a solution that enforces this configuration consistently across their fleet without manual intervention. Which AWS Systems Manager capability should be used?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Systems Manager Inventory","AWS Systems Manager Session Manager","AWS Systems Manager State Manager","AWS Systems Manager Run Command"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (AWS Systems Manager State Manager) is designed to define and maintain a desired state for your instances, ensuring that specific software is installed and configurations are applied consistently and automatically. This prevents configuration drift and automates remediation. Option A (AWS Systems Manager Inventory) collects data about instances but doesn''t enforce configuration. Option B (AWS Systems Manager Session Manager) provides interactive shell access. Option D (AWS Systems Manager Run Command) executes commands on instances on-demand or on a schedule, but State Manager is better for continuous enforcement of a desired state.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  72,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_3_50',
  'A company has a large fleet of Amazon EC2 instances and needs to ensure that all instances are regularly updated with the latest security patches. They want to automate this process to minimize manual effort and maintain a consistent patching schedule. Which AWS Systems Manager capability is designed for this purpose?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Systems Manager Inventory","AWS Systems Manager State Manager","AWS Systems Manager Patch Manager","AWS Systems Manager Run Command"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (AWS Systems Manager Patch Manager) automates the process of patching managed instances with security updates and other types of updates. It allows defining patch baselines and scheduling patching operations across entire fleets. Option A (AWS Systems Manager Inventory) collects data about instances. Option B (AWS Systems Manager State Manager) enforces a desired configuration state, which can include patching, but Patch Manager is specifically optimized for patching. Option D (AWS Systems Manager Run Command) executes commands on instances on demand, but Patch Manager provides a more comprehensive and automated patching solution.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  73,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_3_51',
  'An organization needs to assess the compliance of its AWS resources against a predefined set of security and operational best practices, such as HIPAA or PCI DSS. They want to evaluate multiple AWS Config rules simultaneously as a single unit. Which AWS Config feature facilitates this?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Config Rules","AWS Config Recorders","AWS Config Conformance Packs","AWS Config Aggregators"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (AWS Config Conformance Packs) provide a collection of AWS Config rules and remediation actions that can be deployed as a single entity. This allows for easy assessment of compliance against common frameworks or organizational standards. Option A (AWS Config Rules) are individual rules that assess resource compliance. Option B (AWS Config Recorders) record configuration changes for resources. Option D (AWS Config Aggregators) collect AWS Config data from multiple accounts and Regions into a single account.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  74,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_3_52',
  'A company manages a fleet of hundreds of Amazon EC2 instances. Historically, instance configurations have been managed manually, leading to inconsistencies and ''configuration drift'' over time. The DevOps team needs to implement a solution to automatically enforce a desired state for these instances. Which AWS service should they use?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CloudFormation","AWS OpsWorks","AWS Systems Manager State Manager","AWS Config"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (AWS Systems Manager State Manager) is the ideal service for enforcing and remediating desired state continuously across entire fleets of instances, directly addressing the anti-pattern ap-2.2-001 of manual configuration management. It ensures consistency and prevents configuration drift. Option A (AWS CloudFormation) is for provisioning infrastructure, not for ongoing configuration management of running instances. Option B (AWS OpsWorks) is a legacy service for Chef/Puppet-based configuration management. Option D (AWS Config) assesses and audits resource configurations but does not actively enforce or remediate desired state like State Manager does.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  75,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_3_53',
  'An organization needs to collect detailed software and hardware configuration information, including installed applications, operating system patches, and network configurations, from all its Amazon EC2 instances. Which AWS Systems Manager capability is designed for this data collection?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Systems Manager State Manager","AWS Systems Manager Patch Manager","AWS Systems Manager Inventory","AWS Systems Manager Run Command"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (AWS Systems Manager Inventory) collects and displays detailed information about your managed instances, including operating system, applications, network configuration, and more. This helps in auditing and managing your fleet. Option A (AWS Systems Manager State Manager) enforces desired configurations. Option B (AWS Systems Manager Patch Manager) automates patching. Option D (AWS Systems Manager Run Command) executes commands on instances.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  76,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_3_54',
  'A company needs to define a set of approved patches for their Amazon EC2 instances and automate the application of these patches on a regular schedule. They also need to ensure that only approved patches are installed. Which AWS Systems Manager feature helps manage this process?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Systems Manager Inventory","AWS Systems Manager State Manager","AWS Systems Manager Patch Baselines","AWS Systems Manager Run Command"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (AWS Systems Manager Patch Baselines) allow you to define rules for auto-approving patches within days of their release, as well as a list of approved and rejected patches. This is a core component of AWS Systems Manager Patch Manager for controlling which patches are applied. Option A (AWS Systems Manager Inventory) collects data about instances. Option B (AWS Systems Manager State Manager) enforces a desired configuration state, which can include applying patches, but Patch Baselines specifically define the approved patches. Option D (AWS Systems Manager Run Command) executes commands on instances.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  77,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_3_55',
  'A DevOps team wants to centralize the management of configuration compliance for a large fleet of Amazon EC2 instances. They need to define a desired state for these instances and automatically apply updates or remediate deviations. Which AWS Systems Manager feature is used to define these desired configurations?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Systems Manager Inventory documents","AWS Systems Manager Patch Baselines","AWS Systems Manager State Manager associations","AWS Systems Manager Run Command documents"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (AWS Systems Manager State Manager associations) are used to define the desired state for your instances. An association specifies the configuration that you want to apply to your instances, such as installing software, joining a domain, or applying patches. Option A (AWS Systems Manager Inventory documents) are used to collect data, not define desired state. Option B (AWS Systems Manager Patch Baselines) define approved patches, which is a specific type of configuration. Option D (AWS Systems Manager Run Command documents) are used to execute commands on demand, not for continuous state enforcement.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  78,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_3_56',
  'A company needs to ensure that all Amazon EC2 instances within their environment maintain a specific baseline configuration, including required software and security settings. Any deviation from this baseline should be automatically detected and remediated. Which AWS service provides this continuous configuration enforcement?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Config","AWS CloudFormation","AWS Systems Manager State Manager","AWS OpsWorks"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (AWS Systems Manager State Manager) continuously enforces the desired configuration state on Amazon EC2 instances, automatically detecting and remediating any configuration drift. This is crucial for maintaining consistent and compliant environments. Option A (AWS Config) monitors and audits resource configurations but does not actively enforce them. Option B (AWS CloudFormation) provisions infrastructure but doesn''t manage the ongoing configuration of running instances. Option D (AWS OpsWorks) is a legacy configuration management service that uses Chef/Puppet, and while it can enforce configuration, State Manager is the more modern and integrated AWS service for this purpose.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  79,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_3_57',
  'A company has a critical production application managed by AWS CloudFormation. Before any update to the application''s infrastructure, the DevOps team must thoroughly review the exact changes that will be applied to avoid unintended resource replacements or deletions. Which CloudFormation feature provides a summary of proposed changes to a stack before they are executed?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CloudFormation change sets","AWS CloudFormation drift detection","AWS CloudFormation custom resources","AWS CloudFormation nested stacks"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) AWS CloudFormation change sets allow you to preview the changes that CloudFormation will make to your stack before you execute them. This is crucial for production environments to understand the impact of an update, including resource additions, modifications, or deletions, and to prevent unexpected outcomes. Option B (incorrect) AWS CloudFormation drift detection identifies when a stack''s actual configuration deviates from its template, which is for post-deployment auditing, not for previewing future changes. Option C (incorrect) AWS CloudFormation custom resources extend CloudFormation''s capabilities to manage unsupported resource types, which is unrelated to previewing changes. Option D (incorrect) AWS CloudFormation nested stacks are used for modularizing templates, not for previewing changes to a stack.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  80,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_3_58',
  'A large enterprise uses AWS Service Catalog to provide approved IT products to its development teams. They have multiple AWS accounts organized into an AWS Organization. The enterprise needs to share a portfolio of standardized products, such as secure VPCs and compliant Amazon RDS instances, with all development accounts in a specific Organizational Unit (OU) without manually configuring each account. Which AWS Service Catalog feature enables this centralized sharing of portfolios across an AWS Organization?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Service Catalog portfolio sharing with AWS Organizations","AWS Service Catalog product versioning","AWS Service Catalog constraints","AWS Service Catalog launch roles"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) AWS Service Catalog portfolio sharing, when integrated with AWS Organizations, allows administrators to share portfolios of approved products with specific Organizational Units (OUs) or the entire organization. This automates the distribution of standardized products to multiple accounts, ensuring consistency and reducing administrative overhead. Option B (incorrect) AWS Service Catalog product versioning allows managing different versions of a product, which is for lifecycle management, not for sharing across accounts. Option C (incorrect) AWS Service Catalog constraints define rules for how users can provision products, such as specifying allowed instance types, but they don''t facilitate portfolio sharing. Option D (incorrect) AWS Service Catalog launch roles define the IAM role that Service Catalog assumes to provision a product, which is for permissions, not for sharing portfolios across an organization.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  81,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_3_59',
  'A security team needs to ensure that all Amazon EC2 instances in their fleet are configured with the latest security patches. They want to define a specific set of approved patches, including operating system and application updates, and ensure that only these approved patches are applied during automated patching windows. Which AWS Systems Manager feature allows the definition of a curated list of approved patches and rules for automatic approval?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Systems Manager Patch Baselines","AWS Systems Manager State Manager","AWS Systems Manager Inventory","AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) AWS Systems Manager Patch Baselines allow you to define rules for auto-approving patches within days of their release, as well as specify a list of approved and rejected patches. This ensures that only tested and approved patches are applied to your instances during patching operations. Option B (incorrect) AWS Systems Manager State Manager enforces and maintains the desired state of instances, which can include patching as part of a broader configuration, but Patch Baselines are the specific feature for defining patch approval rules. Option C (incorrect) AWS Systems Manager Inventory collects data about instances, but it does not define patch approval rules. Option D (incorrect) AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store is for storing configuration data and secrets, not for managing patch baselines.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  82,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_3_60',
  'A DevOps team manages infrastructure using AWS CloudFormation. They recently detected that an Amazon S3 bucket, part of a CloudFormation stack, had its encryption settings manually disabled outside of the template. The team needs to automatically revert this change and bring the S3 bucket back into compliance with the CloudFormation template''s defined state. Which CloudFormation capability, often combined with AWS Config or custom automation, can be used to remediate such detected drift?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CloudFormation drift detection with remediation via stack update","AWS CloudFormation change sets for manual rollback","AWS CloudFormation custom resources for manual intervention","AWS CloudFormation StackSets for re-deployment"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) AWS CloudFormation drift detection identifies when a resource''s actual configuration deviates from its template. To remediate this, the most common approach is to perform a stack update without changing the template. CloudFormation will then re-apply the desired state defined in the template, effectively reverting the manual change. This can be automated by triggering a stack update upon drift detection. Option B (incorrect) AWS CloudFormation change sets are for previewing future changes, not for remediating existing drift, and a manual rollback is not an automated remediation. Option C (incorrect) AWS CloudFormation custom resources extend capabilities but are not the primary mechanism for automated drift remediation of native resources; they would require significant custom development for this specific task. Option D (incorrect) AWS CloudFormation StackSets are for deploying stacks across multiple accounts/Regions and are not the direct mechanism for remediating drift in a single stack.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  83,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_3_61',
  'A company has a strict policy that no Amazon EC2 instance should be launched without an associated IAM role, and all Amazon S3 buckets must have default encryption enabled. They want to enforce these policies during the CloudFormation template deployment process, preventing the creation of non-compliant resources. Which AWS service is best suited to evaluate CloudFormation templates against these predefined policies before stack creation?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CloudFormation Guard","AWS Config conformance packs","AWS Service Catalog","AWS Systems Manager State Manager"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) AWS CloudFormation Guard is a policy-as-code language that allows you to define rules to check CloudFormation templates for compliance with organizational policies before deployment. It can prevent non-compliant infrastructure from being provisioned by validating the template against defined rules. Option B (incorrect) AWS Config conformance packs are used to assess the compliance of *already deployed* resources, not to validate templates pre-deployment. Option C (incorrect) AWS Service Catalog helps standardize product provisioning but does not inherently enforce policy checks on arbitrary CloudFormation templates at the validation stage. Option D (incorrect) AWS Systems Manager State Manager is for configuring and maintaining the desired state of EC2 instances, not for validating CloudFormation templates.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  84,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_3_62',
  'A DevOps team is managing a fleet of Amazon EC2 instances that require consistent software installations and configuration. They are currently using AWS OpsWorks for Chef Automate. The team is considering migrating to a more modern, integrated AWS service for configuration management that doesn''t require managing Chef recipes or infrastructure. Which AWS service offers similar desired state configuration capabilities with less operational overhead for managing the configuration tool itself?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Systems Manager State Manager","AWS Config conformance packs","AWS CloudFormation StackSets","AWS Service Catalog"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) AWS Systems Manager State Manager provides desired state configuration management without the need to manage Chef or Puppet infrastructure. It uses Systems Manager documents to define and enforce configurations, offering a more integrated and often lower-overhead solution compared to AWS OpsWorks for Chef Automate, which requires managing Chef recipes and the Chef server itself (even if managed by AWS). Option B (incorrect) AWS Config conformance packs are for auditing compliance, not for active configuration management. Option C (incorrect) AWS CloudFormation StackSets are for deploying infrastructure across accounts/Regions, not for instance configuration management. Option D (incorrect) AWS Service Catalog is for provisioning approved IT services, not for ongoing instance configuration management.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  85,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_3_63',
  'A DevOps team is managing a fleet of Amazon EC2 instances that host various microservices. They need to ensure that all instances have a specific version of a monitoring agent installed and that the agent''s configuration file is always set to a predefined standard. This must be a continuous process, automatically correcting any instances that drift from the desired state. Which AWS Systems Manager capability is specifically designed for enforcing and maintaining this desired configuration state across the fleet?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Systems Manager State Manager","AWS Systems Manager Patch Manager","AWS Systems Manager Inventory","AWS Systems Manager Run Command"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) AWS Systems Manager State Manager is designed to define and enforce a desired configuration state across your Amazon EC2 instances and on-premises servers. It can automatically apply configurations, install software, and ensure continuous compliance, making it ideal for maintaining specific agent versions and configuration files. Option B (incorrect) AWS Systems Manager Patch Manager is specifically for applying operating system and software patches, not for general configuration management or agent installation. Option C (incorrect) AWS Systems Manager Inventory collects data about instances, but it does not enforce configurations. Option D (incorrect) AWS Systems Manager Run Command allows you to execute commands on instances, but it''s for ad-hoc execution, not for continuous enforcement of a desired state.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  86,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_3_64',
  'An organization needs to audit the compliance of its AWS resources against a set of security best practices. They have defined several individual AWS Config rules to check for specific conditions, such as S3 bucket public access and EBS volume encryption. To simplify management and reporting, they want to group these individual rules into a single, deployable unit that provides an aggregated compliance score. Which AWS Config feature allows for this grouping and aggregated reporting of multiple rules?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Config conformance packs","AWS Config recorder","AWS Config rules (individual)","AWS Config remediation actions"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) AWS Config conformance packs allow you to group multiple AWS Config rules and remediation actions into a single package. This simplifies deployment, management, and reporting by providing a consolidated compliance score for the entire set of rules, which is ideal for assessing compliance against a framework of best practices. Option B (incorrect) AWS Config recorder records configuration changes for resources, but it doesn''t group or aggregate compliance reports. Option C (incorrect) AWS Config rules (individual) are the building blocks, but they don''t provide the aggregated reporting capability on their own. Option D (incorrect) AWS Config remediation actions are automated actions taken when a resource is found non-compliant, which is a component of compliance, but not the grouping mechanism.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  87,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_3_65',
  'A company is using AWS CloudFormation to manage its infrastructure. They have a large, complex application that requires a shared networking stack (VPC, subnets, route tables) and separate application stacks (EC2 instances, databases, load balancers) for different environments (dev, test, prod). They want to define the networking stack once and reuse it across all application stacks, while maintaining clear separation of concerns. Which CloudFormation feature allows for this modular, reusable architecture?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CloudFormation nested stacks","AWS CloudFormation StackSets","AWS CloudFormation custom resources","AWS CloudFormation change sets"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) AWS CloudFormation nested stacks allow you to create modular and reusable CloudFormation templates. A parent stack can deploy child (nested) stacks, enabling the separation of concerns, such as a reusable networking stack, from application-specific stacks. This promotes modularity and reusability within a single account and Region. Option B (incorrect) AWS CloudFormation StackSets are for deploying stacks across multiple accounts and Regions, which is not the primary use case for modularizing within a single application architecture. Option C (incorrect) AWS CloudFormation custom resources extend CloudFormation''s capabilities to manage unsupported resource types, which is unrelated to modular template design. Option D (incorrect) AWS CloudFormation change sets are used to preview changes before applying a stack update, which is a safety mechanism, not an architectural pattern for modularity.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  88,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_3_66',
  'A DevOps team is adopting AWS CDK to define their infrastructure. They need to ensure that all resources created by their CDK applications adhere to specific security policies, such as requiring encryption for all S3 buckets and disallowing public access. They want to integrate this policy enforcement directly into their CDK code. Which AWS CDK feature allows for defining and applying policy checks across constructs in a CDK application?',
