INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_1_35',
  'Which AWS service enables you to route user traffic to the nearest healthy endpoint across multiple AWS Regions for improved performance and availability?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon Route 53 with latency routing policy","Amazon CloudFront","AWS Lambda","AWS Global Accelerator"]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'Option D (correct) is AWS Global Accelerator, which uses the AWS global network to route user traffic to the closest healthy endpoint, improving performance and availability for global applications. Option A (incorrect) is a DNS service, and while latency routing can direct to the closest endpoint, it relies on DNS TTL and doesn''t inherently check endpoint health for failover in the same way Global Accelerator does. Option B (incorrect) is a Content Delivery Network (CDN) for caching content, not routing live application traffic for performance and availability across regions. Option C (incorrect) is a serverless compute service.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  33,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_1',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_1_36',
  'What is the primary benefit of using Amazon Aurora Global Database?',
  'mcq',
  '["Provides fast local reads with low latency and disaster recovery across regions.","Enables serverless execution of database queries without managing servers.","Automatically scales compute capacity based on database workload.","Offers in-memory caching for frequently accessed database queries."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) accurately describes the primary benefit of Amazon Aurora Global Database, which is to provide a single Aurora database that spans multiple AWS Regions, allowing for fast local reads and disaster recovery with minimal RPO. Option B (incorrect) refers to serverless database capabilities, which Aurora also offers but is not the unique benefit of Global Database. Option C (incorrect) describes Aurora''s general auto-scaling capabilities. Option D (incorrect) describes caching mechanisms, which are separate from Global Database''s core function.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  34,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_1',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_1_37',
  'A company needs to ensure their relational database has a low Recovery Time Objective (RTO) and Recovery Point Objective (RPO) for disaster recovery across AWS Regions. Which Amazon RDS feature supports this?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon RDS Multi-AZ deployment","Amazon RDS cross-region read replicas","Amazon RDS automated backups","Amazon RDS storage auto-scaling"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is Amazon RDS cross-region read replicas. These replicas asynchronously replicate data to a different AWS Region, allowing for rapid promotion to a standalone database in a disaster scenario, thus achieving low RTO and RPO for cross-region DR. Option A (incorrect) provides high availability within a single Region, not cross-region DR. Option C (incorrect) is for backup and restore, which typically has a higher RTO. Option D (incorrect) is for scaling storage capacity, not disaster recovery.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  35,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_1',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_1_38',
  'Which Amazon Route 53 routing policy directs traffic to a healthy resource in a primary Region and automatically shifts to a secondary Region if the primary becomes unhealthy?',
  'mcq',
  '["Latency routing policy","Weighted routing policy","Failover routing policy","Geolocation routing policy"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) describes the Amazon Route 53 failover routing policy. This policy allows you to configure active-passive or active-active failover between primary and secondary resources, based on health checks. Option A (incorrect) routes based on lowest latency, not health for primary failover (anti-pattern ap-3.1-001). Option B (incorrect) distributes traffic based on weights. Option D (incorrect) routes traffic based on the geographic location of the user.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  36,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_1',
  'easy'
);

