);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_1_51',
  'What is the primary purpose of an Amazon RDS cross-Region read replica?',
  'mcq',
  '["To enable multi-master writes across regions for a single database instance","To reduce database storage costs by offloading data to a secondary region","To improve read performance for geographically dispersed users and provide a disaster recovery option","To provide automatic failover within a single region for high availability"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) accurately describes the primary purpose of an Amazon RDS cross-Region read replica. It allows you to create a read-only copy of your database in a different AWS Region, which can serve read traffic from users closer to that region, improving performance. It also acts as a disaster recovery option, as it can be promoted to a standalone database in case of a regional outage. Option A is incorrect as RDS read replicas are read-only. Option B is not its primary purpose. Option D describes Amazon RDS Multi-AZ deployments.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  49,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_1_52',
  'Which AWS service offers DNS resolution and can perform health checks on endpoints to determine their availability for routing traffic?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Global Accelerator","Amazon CloudFront","Amazon Route 53","AWS Shield"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is Amazon Route 53. It is a highly available and scalable cloud Domain Name System (DNS) web service. It can perform health checks on your resources (e.g., EC2 instances, load balancers) and automatically route traffic away from unhealthy endpoints using various routing policies. Option A (AWS Global Accelerator) routes traffic at the network layer. Option B (Amazon CloudFront) is a CDN. Option D (AWS Shield) is a DDoS protection service.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  50,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_1_53',
  'A DevOps team needs to ensure their application''s database can withstand a regional outage with minimal data loss and provide read scalability in a secondary region. The database is currently Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL.',
  'mcq',
  '["Migrate to Amazon DynamoDB Global Tables for multi-Region active-active capabilities.","Configure an Amazon RDS cross-Region read replica to the secondary region.","Implement Amazon RDS Multi-AZ within the primary region for high availability.","Use AWS Backup to regularly back up the database to another Region for recovery."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the most appropriate solution. An Amazon RDS cross-Region read replica provides read scalability in the secondary region and can be promoted to a standalone database in case of a primary region outage, offering minimal data loss (low RPO) and good recovery time (RTO). Option A (DynamoDB Global Tables) is a NoSQL database and would require a significant migration effort. Option C (Amazon RDS Multi-AZ) provides high availability within a single region but does not protect against a regional outage. Option D (AWS Backup) provides backups but typically has a higher RTO than a read replica for a full database recovery.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  51,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_1_54',
  'What is the anti-pattern associated with using Amazon Route 53 latency routing as a primary disaster recovery mechanism?',
  'mcq',
  '["Latency routing is too complex to configure for disaster recovery scenarios.","Latency routing does not consider endpoint health and can route traffic to an unhealthy, low-latency endpoint.","Latency routing is more expensive than other Route 53 routing policies for DR.","Latency routing only works for static websites, not dynamic applications requiring failover."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) describes the anti-pattern (ap-3.1-001). Route 53 latency routing policy routes traffic to the region that provides the lowest latency for the user, but it does not inherently check the health of the endpoints. This means it could direct users to an unhealthy endpoint if that endpoint still offers the lowest latency. For disaster recovery, it must be combined with health checks and a failover routing policy. Options A, C, and D are incorrect statements about latency routing.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  52,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_1_55',
  'A large e-commerce platform relies on an Amazon DynamoDB database for its product catalog, which experiences high read and write traffic globally. The company needs to ensure that the product catalog remains available and performant even during a regional outage, with near-zero RPO and RTO. The DevOps team must implement a solution that allows active-active read and write operations across multiple AWS Regions, automatically handles data replication and conflict resolution, and provides the lowest possible latency for global users. Which AWS service should the team choose?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon DynamoDB Global Tables configured with multiple replica Regions.","Amazon Aurora Global Database with DynamoDB as a secondary data store.","AWS Global Accelerator to route traffic to regional DynamoDB instances.","Amazon Route 53 with latency-based routing to regional DynamoDB instances."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the ideal solution. Amazon DynamoDB Global Tables provide a fully managed, multi-Region, multi-master database that enables you to run globally distributed applications with low-latency data access for users worldwide. It automatically replicates data across your chosen AWS Regions and handles conflict resolution, ensuring active-active read and write capabilities with near-zero RPO and RTO for the database, perfectly matching the requirements.
Option B (incorrect) Amazon Aurora Global Database is a relational database service and is not suitable for a NoSQL product catalog that requires active-active multi-master replication with DynamoDB''s specific characteristics. Using DynamoDB as a secondary data store would introduce unnecessary complexity and not provide the native global table functionality.
Option C (incorrect) AWS Global Accelerator can improve performance by routing traffic to the nearest healthy endpoint, but it does not provide data replication or conflict resolution for DynamoDB itself. It would route users to a regional DynamoDB instance, but without Global Tables, that instance would not be active-active with other regions for data synchronization.
Option D (incorrect) Amazon Route 53 with latency-based routing would direct users to the DynamoDB instance in the lowest latency region. However, like Global Accelerator, Route 53 does not provide data replication or conflict resolution. Without DynamoDB Global Tables, this approach would lead to regional data silos and potential data consistency issues for a global active-active requirement.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  53,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_1_56',
  'A global media company operates a content delivery platform that uses Amazon Aurora MySQL in its primary AWS Region. The platform experiences high read traffic from users worldwide, and the company needs to improve read performance for users in geographically distant regions while also establishing a robust disaster recovery solution with an RPO of less than 5 seconds and an RTO of under 1 minute for the database. The DevOps team seeks a solution that minimizes replication lag and allows for quick failover to a secondary region. Which Aurora feature should be implemented?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon Aurora Global Database, configured with a primary Region and multiple secondary Regions for read replicas.","Amazon RDS cross-Region read replicas for the Aurora MySQL database.","AWS Backup for continuous point-in-time recovery and cross-Region replication of snapshots.","Amazon Route 53 with weighted routing to distribute read traffic across multiple Aurora clusters."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the most suitable solution. Amazon Aurora Global Database is designed for globally distributed applications, allowing a single Aurora database to span multiple AWS Regions. It provides fast, low-latency global reads from secondary regions and enables rapid disaster recovery with an RPO typically under 1 second and RTO under 1 minute. Data is replicated with minimal lag (typically less than 1 second), making it ideal for both read scaling and disaster recovery for mission-critical databases.
Option B (incorrect) Amazon RDS cross-Region read replicas can provide read scaling and a disaster recovery option, but they typically have higher replication lag and a longer RTO compared to Aurora Global Database. While a viable option for some use cases, it does not meet the stringent RPO and RTO requirements as effectively as Aurora Global Database.
Option C (incorrect) AWS Backup can provide continuous point-in-time recovery and cross-Region replication of snapshots. However, this is a backup and restore strategy, which would result in a significantly higher RTO (minutes to hours) than the required under 1 minute, as it involves restoring the database from a snapshot rather than a direct failover of a replicated instance.
Option D (incorrect) Amazon Route 53 with weighted routing can distribute traffic, but it operates at the DNS level and does not provide database replication or disaster recovery capabilities. It would route users to existing database endpoints but does not inherently improve replication lag or provide the rapid failover mechanism of Aurora Global Database.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  54,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_1_57',
  'A global SaaS company provides an API-driven service that is deployed across multiple AWS Regions. Users expect consistently low latency, and the company needs to ensure that traffic is routed to the optimal healthy endpoint, even if a regional endpoint experiences performance degradation or becomes unhealthy. The DevOps team wants to implement a solution that improves application performance by routing user requests to the nearest healthy AWS edge location and then over the AWS global network, rather than relying solely on public internet routing. Which AWS service should be used?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Global Accelerator with endpoint groups configured for each regional deployment.","Amazon Route 53 with latency-based routing and health checks for regional endpoints.","Amazon CloudFront with origin failover configured for regional API endpoints.","An AWS Transit Gateway connecting VPCs in different Regions to centralize traffic."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the most effective solution. AWS Global Accelerator improves the availability and performance of applications by directing user traffic to the nearest healthy endpoint over the AWS global network. It uses static Anycast IP addresses, which are advertised from AWS edge locations worldwide, ensuring that user traffic enters the AWS network as close as possible to the user and traverses the optimized AWS backbone to the application endpoint. This approach inherently provides faster routing and automatic failover to healthy endpoints, fulfilling the requirements for low latency and resilience.
Option B (incorrect) Amazon Route 53 with latency-based routing and health checks can route users to the lowest latency healthy endpoint. However, it operates at the DNS level, meaning traffic still traverses the public internet from the user to the chosen regional endpoint. This does not offer the same performance benefits as Global Accelerator, which uses the AWS global network for routing, and it is also susceptible to DNS caching issues.
Option C (incorrect) Amazon CloudFront is a content delivery network (CDN) primarily used for caching static and dynamic content closer to users. While it can improve performance for web assets and supports origin failover, it is not primarily designed for optimizing API traffic routing over the AWS global network in the same way Global Accelerator does for direct application access.
Option D (incorrect) An AWS Transit Gateway connects VPCs across regions, facilitating network connectivity between them. It is a networking service for inter-VPC communication and hybrid cloud setups, but it does not directly address global user traffic routing, performance optimization from the edge, or automatic failover for external users accessing an application.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  55,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_1_58',
  'A DevOps team is designing a highly available and scalable solution for a new global application. The application''s backend consists of stateless Amazon EC2 instances and an Amazon Aurora MySQL database. The team needs to distribute incoming user traffic across multiple AWS Regions to minimize latency and ensure continuous availability in case of a regional outage. The solution must also provide automatic failover to healthy endpoints and leverage the AWS global network for optimal performance. Which combination of services should the team use to achieve these goals?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Global Accelerator for traffic routing and Amazon Route 53 with failover routing policies for health checks.","Amazon Route 53 with latency-based routing and health checks, combined with Amazon RDS cross-Region read replicas.","AWS Global Accelerator for traffic routing and Amazon Aurora Global Database for the database tier.","Amazon CloudFront for content delivery and AWS Transit Gateway for inter-region connectivity."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) provides the most comprehensive solution for high availability and scalability for a global application. AWS Global Accelerator uses static Anycast IP addresses and the AWS global network to route user traffic to the nearest healthy endpoint, significantly reducing latency and providing automatic failover across regions. For the database, Amazon Aurora Global Database offers a globally distributed, highly available database with fast cross-region replication (low RPO) and rapid failover (low RTO), making it ideal for the database tier of a global application. This combination ensures both application and database resilience and performance.
Option A (incorrect) while AWS Global Accelerator is excellent for traffic routing, relying solely on Amazon Route 53 with failover routing for health checks of the database would not provide the same low RPO/RTO as Aurora Global Database for the database tier. Route 53 DNS failover can be slower due to DNS propagation and TTLs, and it doesn''t offer the same level of database-specific replication and failover as Aurora Global Database.
Option B (incorrect) Amazon Route 53 with latency-based routing routes traffic over the public internet, which is less performant than Global Accelerator''s use of the AWS global network. Amazon RDS cross-Region read replicas, while providing read scaling and DR, typically have higher RPO/RTO than Aurora Global Database and do not offer the same level of integrated global database management.
Option D (incorrect) Amazon CloudFront is a CDN for content delivery and caching, not primarily for routing application traffic to backend EC2 instances or databases. AWS Transit Gateway is for inter-VPC and hybrid cloud network connectivity, not for external user traffic routing and global application performance optimization.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  56,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_1_59',
  'A DevOps team is managing a critical application that relies on an Amazon Aurora MySQL database. The application is deployed in a single AWS Region across multiple Availability Zones. To enhance the application''s resilience, the team needs to implement a disaster recovery strategy that protects against a regional outage. The strategy must ensure an RPO of less than 1 hour and an RTO of less than 4 hours, while minimizing the cost of the DR environment. Which database DR strategy should the team implement?',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure Amazon Aurora cross-Region read replicas and promote one during a disaster.","Implement Amazon Aurora Global Database with a secondary Region.","Utilize AWS Backup to create daily snapshots and replicate them to a secondary Region.","Set up a warm standby Aurora cluster in a secondary Region, running at full capacity."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is a cost-effective and robust solution for the given RPO/RTO. Configuring Amazon Aurora cross-Region read replicas allows for asynchronous replication of data to a secondary Region. In a disaster, a read replica can be promoted to a standalone Aurora DB cluster, providing a relatively low RPO (often in minutes) and RTO (tens of minutes to a few hours depending on application setup) while keeping the cost of the DR environment lower than a full warm standby. This aligns well with the RPO < 1 hour and RTO < 4 hours.
Option B (incorrect) implementing Amazon Aurora Global Database would provide an even lower RPO (seconds) and RTO (minutes), but it is a more expensive solution than a simple cross-Region read replica. While it meets the RPO/RTO, it does not prioritize minimizing the cost of the DR environment as much as Option A.
Option C (incorrect) utilizing AWS Backup for daily snapshots and replicating them to a secondary Region represents a backup and restore strategy. This approach would likely exceed the RTO of 4 hours, as restoring an entire Aurora database from a snapshot, even if cross-region replicated, takes significant time for provisioning and data loading. It also has an RPO tied to the last snapshot, which could be up to 24 hours.
Option D (incorrect) setting up a warm standby Aurora cluster in a secondary Region running at full capacity would be the most expensive option, as it duplicates the primary environment''s resources. While it could achieve very low RTO, it contradicts the goal of minimizing the cost of the DR environment.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  57,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_1_60',
  'A DevOps team is tasked with ensuring the highest level of availability for a critical web application that uses Amazon Route 53 for DNS resolution. The application is deployed across two AWS Regions, each with an Application Load Balancer (ALB) as the entry point. The team needs to configure Route 53 to automatically direct users to the healthy ALB in the closest Region, but also to fail over to the other Region if the closest one becomes unhealthy. The solution must prioritize user experience (lowest latency) while guaranteeing high availability. Which Route 53 routing policy combination should be implemented?',
  'mcq',
  '["A combination of latency-based routing and failover routing policies, with health checks associated with each ALB.","Weighted routing policies with health checks, distributing traffic evenly across both Regions.","Simple routing policy with health checks, directing all traffic to the primary Region and failing over to the secondary.","Geolocation routing policy to direct users to specific regions, with a backup region configured for failover."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the most effective approach for this scenario. A combination of latency-based routing and failover routing policies, along with Route 53 health checks, ensures that users are directed to the closest healthy endpoint. Latency-based routing directs users to the region with the lowest latency, while failover routing ensures that if the primary (closest) region becomes unhealthy, traffic is automatically rerouted to a healthy secondary region. Health checks are crucial for Route 53 to determine endpoint health and trigger failover accurately. This combination prioritizes both low latency and high availability.
Option B (incorrect) weighted routing policies distribute traffic based on assigned weights. While health checks can be used, weighted routing doesn''t inherently prioritize latency or provide a clear primary/secondary failover mechanism based on health in the same way as a dedicated failover policy. Distributing traffic evenly might not always send users to the closest region.
Option C (incorrect) a simple routing policy directs all traffic to a single resource. While it can be combined with health checks for basic failover, it does not provide latency-based routing to direct users to the closest region. All users would initially go to the designated primary, regardless of their geographic proximity to another healthy region.
Option D (incorrect) geolocation routing policy directs users to a specific resource based on their geographic location. While it can be combined with failover, it requires defining specific geographic regions and their corresponding endpoints. It might not always select the ''closest'' healthy endpoint dynamically based on real-time latency measurements like latency-based routing, and managing it for optimal latency across all users can be more complex.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  58,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_1_61',
  'A global e-commerce company requires a highly available database that supports active-active replication across multiple AWS Regions to ensure continuous operation and low-latency writes for customers worldwide. The solution must minimize operational overhead for database management. Which AWS service best meets these requirements?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon DynamoDB Global Tables, configured for multi-Region replication.","Amazon Aurora Global Database, with cross-Region read replicas and manual failover.","Amazon RDS for MySQL with cross-Region read replicas and custom replication logic.","Self-managed Cassandra cluster on Amazon EC2 instances across multiple Regions with custom replication."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) provides Amazon DynamoDB Global Tables, which are designed for multi-Region, active-active replication, offering automatic, asynchronous replication across chosen AWS Regions. This setup ensures high availability and low-latency access for global applications with minimal operational overhead, as DynamoDB is a fully managed service. Option B (incorrect) Amazon Aurora Global Database provides fast cross-Region read replicas and disaster recovery, but it is primarily active-passive for writes, requiring a failover to promote a secondary region for write operations, which doesn''t fully meet the active-active write requirement. Option C (incorrect) Amazon RDS for MySQL with cross-Region read replicas also supports active-passive replication for writes and would require significant custom logic and operational overhead to achieve an active-active setup, which is not its primary design. Option D (incorrect) A self-managed Cassandra cluster on Amazon EC2 instances would provide active-active capabilities but would incur significant operational overhead for setup, maintenance, patching, and replication management across multiple Regions, failing the minimal operational overhead constraint.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  59,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_1_62',
  'A company operates a critical relational database application that requires low-latency global reads and a robust disaster recovery strategy with a fast Recovery Time Objective (RTO) in case of a regional outage. The database is currently running on Amazon Aurora in a single AWS Region. Which solution offers the best combination of global read performance and regional disaster recovery capabilities?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon Aurora Global Database, with a primary Region and multiple secondary Regions for read replicas and fast failover.","Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL with cross-Region read replicas and manual failover procedures.","AWS Backup for Aurora clusters, with daily backups replicated to a secondary Region.","A self-managed PostgreSQL cluster on Amazon EC2 instances with logical replication across Regions."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) Amazon Aurora Global Database is specifically designed for global applications, providing fast local reads in secondary Regions and rapid disaster recovery with an RTO typically under 1 minute for regional outages. It replicates data across multiple AWS Regions with minimal latency, making it ideal for both global read performance and robust DR. Option B (incorrect) Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL with cross-Region read replicas can provide read scalability and a DR option, but its failover process is typically slower than Aurora Global Database, and it does not offer the same level of global read performance optimization. Option C (incorrect) AWS Backup for Aurora clusters provides backup and restore capabilities, but it is primarily a backup solution and would result in a significantly higher RTO compared to an active/passive or active/active replication setup like Aurora Global Database. Option D (incorrect) A self-managed PostgreSQL cluster on Amazon EC2 instances with logical replication would incur substantial operational overhead for setup, maintenance, and failover management, which is not ideal for minimizing management burden.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  60,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_1_63',
  'A company wants to improve the performance and availability of its global web application by routing user traffic to the optimal healthy endpoint across multiple AWS Regions. They need a service that uses the AWS global network infrastructure to reduce internet latency and automatically directs users away from unhealthy endpoints. Which AWS service provides this capability?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon CloudFront, configured with origin failover.","Amazon Route 53, using a latency routing policy.","AWS Global Accelerator, with endpoint groups in multiple Regions.","An Application Load Balancer (ALB) with cross-Region load balancing."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) AWS Global Accelerator uses the AWS global network to route user traffic to the nearest healthy endpoint, improving performance by reducing internet latency and providing automatic failover across Regions. It provides static Anycast IP addresses that act as a fixed entry point to your application, directing traffic to the optimal healthy endpoint. Option A (incorrect) Amazon CloudFront is a Content Delivery Network (CDN) that caches content closer to users, improving performance for static and dynamic content, but its primary function is not to optimize routing for application endpoints across Regions using the global network for all traffic. While it supports origin failover, Global Accelerator is designed for application-level traffic optimization. Option B (incorrect) Amazon Route 53 with a latency routing policy directs users to the endpoint with the lowest latency based on DNS resolution, but it operates at the DNS level and does not leverage the AWS global network for traffic acceleration or provide continuous health checks for automatic re-routing in the same way Global Accelerator does. This option represents anti-pattern ap-3.1-001, as latency routing alone is not a robust DR mechanism. Option D (incorrect) An Application Load Balancer (ALB) operates within a single Region or across Availability Zones within a Region. It does not provide cross-Region load balancing or global traffic acceleration using the AWS global network.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  61,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_1_64',
  'A company is deploying a new mission-critical application that requires maximum availability and resilience against regional outages. The application components, including web servers, application servers, and databases, must be distributed to minimize single points of failure. Which architectural pattern should be implemented to achieve this goal?',
  'mcq',
  '["Deploying all application components within a single Availability Zone in one AWS Region.","Distributing application components across multiple Availability Zones within a single AWS Region.","Deploying application components across multiple AWS Regions with active-passive failover.","Using a single Amazon EC2 instance with an Elastic IP address for all application services."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) Deploying application components across multiple AWS Regions with active-passive failover provides the highest level of availability and resilience against regional outages. This pattern ensures that if an entire Region becomes unavailable, the application can be failed over to a secondary Region, minimizing downtime. Option A (incorrect) Deploying all components within a single Availability Zone is highly susceptible to an AZ outage and does not provide high availability. Option B (incorrect) Distributing components across multiple Availability Zones within a single AWS Region enhances resilience against AZ failures but does not protect against a complete regional outage. Option D (incorrect) Using a single Amazon EC2 instance creates a single point of failure at the instance level and does not provide any resilience against hardware failures, software issues, or AZ/Region outages.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  62,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_1_65',
  'A company uses Amazon Route 53 with a latency routing policy to direct users to their web application hosted in either us-east-1 or eu-west-1, aiming to provide the fastest response. They consider this sufficient for disaster recovery. What is the primary architectural anti-pattern in relying solely on latency routing for disaster recovery?',
  'mcq',
  '["Latency routing does not distribute traffic evenly across all endpoints.","Latency routing prioritizes cost efficiency over performance optimization.","Latency routing does not inherently perform health checks and can route traffic to an unhealthy endpoint if it has lower latency.","Latency routing requires manual intervention to update DNS records during an outage."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) The primary anti-pattern (ap-3.1-001) in relying solely on Route 53 latency routing for disaster recovery is that it does not inherently perform health checks. It routes traffic based on the lowest network latency from the user''s DNS resolver to the AWS Region, regardless of the application''s health within that Region. This means traffic could be directed to an unhealthy endpoint if it still appears to have the lowest latency. For robust disaster recovery, latency routing must be combined with health checks and failover routing policies. Option A (incorrect) Latency routing is designed to direct traffic to the lowest latency endpoint, not necessarily to distribute it evenly. Uneven distribution is a characteristic, not the primary DR anti-pattern. Option B (incorrect) Latency routing prioritizes performance (lowest latency) over cost efficiency, so this statement is incorrect. Option D (incorrect) Route 53 routing policies are typically automated based on their configuration; they do not usually require manual intervention for DNS record updates unless the underlying infrastructure changes.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  63,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_1_66',
  'What is the primary purpose of Amazon DynamoDB Global Tables?',
  'mcq',
  '["To provide a relational database with cross-Region read replicas for analytics.","To enable multi-Region, active-active replication for DynamoDB tables with low-latency access.","To store large objects in a highly durable and available manner across multiple Regions.","To create a serverless data warehouse for global business intelligence applications."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) Amazon DynamoDB Global Tables are designed to provide multi-Region, active-active replication for DynamoDB tables. This allows applications to access and update data in any configured Region with low latency, ensuring high availability and global scalability. Option A (incorrect) This describes a feature of relational databases like Amazon RDS or Aurora, not DynamoDB Global Tables. Option C (incorrect) This describes Amazon S3, which is an object storage service, not DynamoDB. Option D (incorrect) This describes services like Amazon Redshift or Snowflake, which are data warehousing solutions, not DynamoDB Global Tables.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  64,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_1_67',
  'What is the main role of Amazon Route 53 health checks in a multi-Region architecture?',
  'mcq',
  '["To distribute incoming traffic evenly across all available endpoints.","To provide DNS resolution for private hosted zones within a VPC.","To monitor the health and availability of application endpoints and trigger DNS failover if an endpoint becomes unhealthy.","To reduce the latency of DNS queries by caching responses at edge locations."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) In a multi-Region architecture, Amazon Route 53 health checks are crucial for monitoring the health and availability of application endpoints. When combined with routing policies like failover or weighted routing, they enable Route 53 to automatically direct traffic away from unhealthy endpoints to healthy ones, ensuring high availability and resilience. Option A (incorrect) Distributing traffic evenly is typically done by weighted routing policies, not solely by health checks. Option B (incorrect) While Route 53 provides DNS resolution for private hosted zones, this is a separate function from health checks in a multi-Region failover context. Option D (incorrect) Reducing DNS query latency is a general benefit of Route 53''s global network and caching, but it''s not the main role of health checks themselves.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  65,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_1_68',
  'What is a key benefit of using AWS Global Accelerator for a multi-Region application?',
  'mcq',
  '["It caches static content at edge locations to reduce load on origin servers.","It provides static Anycast IP addresses that route user traffic over the AWS global network to the nearest healthy endpoint, improving performance and availability.","It automatically scales EC2 instances up or down based on traffic demand.","It encrypts data in transit between clients and application servers using SSL/TLS."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) AWS Global Accelerator provides static Anycast IP addresses that serve as a fixed entry point to your application. It routes user traffic over the AWS global network infrastructure to the nearest healthy endpoint in the optimal AWS Region, significantly improving performance by reducing internet latency and enhancing availability through automatic failover. Option A (incorrect) This describes the primary function of a Content Delivery Network (CDN) like Amazon CloudFront, not AWS Global Accelerator. Option C (incorrect) This describes the function of Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling, which scales compute capacity, not a global traffic routing service. Option D (incorrect) While AWS services support encryption in transit, this is a general security feature and not the unique key benefit of AWS Global Accelerator, which focuses on performance and availability through optimized routing.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  66,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_1',
  'medium'
);

