INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_1_85',
  'A DevOps team wants to organize its AWS CloudFormation templates into reusable components to avoid monolithic stacks. Which two CloudFormation features support this goal? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Nested Stacks","StackSets","Change Sets","Custom Resources","CloudFormation Guard"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Nested Stacks) allow you to create modular, reusable CloudFormation templates, breaking down a large stack into smaller, manageable components. Option B (StackSets) enable you to provision a common set of AWS resources across multiple AWS accounts and Regions from a single CloudFormation template, promoting reusability and standardization at scale. Option C (Change Sets) are for previewing changes. Option D (Custom Resources) extend CloudFormation''s capabilities to include unsupported resource types. Option E (CloudFormation Guard) is for policy-as-code compliance checking, not for structuring templates.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  1,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_1',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_1_86',
  'A company needs to allow its development teams to provision pre-approved AWS resources in a self-service manner, ensuring compliance with organizational standards. Which two AWS services are primarily designed for this purpose? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Service Catalog","AWS CloudFormation","AWS Systems Manager State Manager","AWS Config","AWS OpsWorks"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (AWS Service Catalog) allows organizations to create and manage catalogs of IT services that are approved for use on AWS. This enables end-users to provision approved products in a self-service manner, adhering to governance. Option B (AWS CloudFormation) is the underlying Infrastructure as Code service used to define these ''products'' in Service Catalog, making it essential for creating the standardized templates. Option C (AWS Systems Manager State Manager) is for enforcing configuration compliance on EC2 instances. Option D (AWS Config) assesses, audits, and evaluates the configurations of your AWS resources. Option E (AWS OpsWorks) is a configuration management service for Chef and Puppet, which is considered legacy.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  2,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_1',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_1_87',
  'Which two statements accurately describe the purpose of AWS CloudFormation Guard? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["It provides policy-as-code compliance checking for CloudFormation templates.","It enforces runtime configuration compliance on EC2 instances.","It helps detect drift between CloudFormation templates and deployed resources.","It allows defining custom resource types for CloudFormation.","It is used for deploying CloudFormation stacks across multiple accounts."]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'Option A (It provides policy-as-code compliance checking for CloudFormation templates) is correct. CloudFormation Guard allows you to define policies in a declarative language to check if your CloudFormation templates comply with security and operational guidelines before deployment. Option C (It helps detect drift between CloudFormation templates and deployed resources) is also correct, as CloudFormation Guard can be used to validate the state of deployed resources against policies, indirectly helping to identify drift. Option B (It enforces runtime configuration compliance on EC2 instances) is a function of AWS Systems Manager State Manager. Option D (It allows defining custom resource types for CloudFormation) describes CloudFormation Custom Resources. Option E (It is used for deploying CloudFormation stacks across multiple accounts) describes CloudFormation StackSets.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  3,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_1',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_1_88',
  'Which two components are used within AWS CloudFormation to extend its capabilities beyond native resource types or to simplify template definitions? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Custom Resources","Macros and Transforms","Change Sets","StackSets","CloudFormation Guard"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Custom Resources) allows you to write custom provisioning logic for resources not natively supported by CloudFormation, effectively extending its capabilities. Option B (Macros and Transforms), such as the AWS Serverless Application Model (SAM) transform, simplify template definitions by allowing you to use shorthand syntax that CloudFormation expands into standard resources. Option C (Change Sets) are for previewing changes to a stack. Option D (StackSets) are for deploying stacks across multiple accounts and Regions. Option E (CloudFormation Guard) is for policy-as-code compliance checking.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  4,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_1',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_1_89',
  'A company needs to deploy a common set of infrastructure resources, such as VPCs and IAM roles, consistently across several AWS accounts and Regions. Which two AWS CloudFormation features are best suited for this requirement? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["StackSets","Nested Stacks","Change Sets","Custom Resources","CloudFormation Guard"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (StackSets) is specifically designed to deploy CloudFormation stacks across multiple AWS accounts and Regions from a single template, making it ideal for consistent multi-account, multi-Region infrastructure. Option B (Nested Stacks) allow you to reuse common template patterns and organize your infrastructure into modular components, which can then be deployed via StackSets for consistency. Option C (Change Sets) are for previewing changes. Option D (Custom Resources) extend CloudFormation''s capabilities to include unsupported resource types. Option E (CloudFormation Guard) is for policy-as-code compliance checking.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  5,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_1',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_1_90',
  'When defining infrastructure as code using AWS CloudFormation, which two components enable you to integrate third-party services or custom scripts into your provisioning process? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Custom Resources","CloudFormation Macros","Change Sets","StackSets","CloudFormation Guard"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Custom Resources) allows you to write custom provisioning logic for resources not natively supported by CloudFormation, enabling integration with third-party services or custom scripts. Option B (CloudFormation Macros) can be used to process template content before CloudFormation creates or updates a stack, allowing for custom transformations or integrations. Option C (Change Sets) are for previewing changes. Option D (StackSets) are for deploying stacks across multiple accounts and Regions. Option E (CloudFormation Guard) is for policy-as-code compliance checking.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  6,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_1',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_1_91',
  'Which two benefits are primarily associated with implementing Infrastructure as Code (IaC) using services like AWS CloudFormation or AWS CDK? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Increased consistency and repeatability of deployments","Reduced manual configuration and human error","Automated application code deployment to EC2 instances","Real-time monitoring of application performance metrics","Centralized management of containerized application images"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Increased consistency and repeatability of deployments) is a core benefit of IaC, as infrastructure is defined in templates that can be deployed identically every time. Option B (Reduced manual configuration and human error) is also a primary advantage, as IaC automates the provisioning process, minimizing manual steps prone to errors. Option C (Automated application code deployment to EC2 instances) is typically handled by CI/CD services like AWS CodeDeploy, not directly by IaC for infrastructure provisioning. Option D (Real-time monitoring of application performance metrics) is a function of services like Amazon CloudWatch. Option E (Centralized management of containerized application images) is handled by services like Amazon ECR.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  7,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_1',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_1_92',
  'A DevOps team is adopting Infrastructure as Code (IaC) to manage their AWS resources. Which two core principles are directly supported by using AWS CloudFormation? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Declarative resource definition","Idempotent deployments","Imperative scripting for resource creation","Manual resource provisioning","Application code deployment"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Declarative resource definition) is a core principle of CloudFormation, where you describe the desired state of your infrastructure, and CloudFormation figures out how to achieve it. Option B (Idempotent deployments) means that applying the same CloudFormation template multiple times will result in the same infrastructure state, without unintended side effects. Option C (Imperative scripting for resource creation) is more characteristic of direct API calls or AWS CLI scripts, not CloudFormation. Option D (Manual resource provisioning) is what IaC aims to replace. Option E (Application code deployment) is typically handled by CI/CD tools, not directly by CloudFormation.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  8,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_1',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_1_93',
  'A DevOps team wants to define their AWS infrastructure using a programming language like Python or TypeScript, leveraging higher-level abstractions to simplify complex deployments. They also need to ensure reusability and maintainability of their infrastructure code. Which of the following approaches would best meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Utilize AWS CloudFormation with YAML templates and custom resources for advanced logic.","Adopt AWS CDK to define infrastructure using familiar programming languages.","Implement AWS Service Catalog to provision pre-approved AWS resources for end-users.","Use AWS Systems Manager State Manager to enforce desired state configuration on EC2 instances.","Develop CloudFormation templates with nested stacks to create modular and reusable components."]'::json,
  '{1,4}',
  'Option B (correct) allows defining cloud infrastructure using familiar programming languages like Python or TypeScript, offering higher-level abstractions and simplifying complex deployments compared to raw CloudFormation. Option E (correct) enables the creation of modular and reusable CloudFormation components, which helps in maintaining and organizing infrastructure code, especially when combined with a language-based approach like CDK. Option A (incorrect) uses YAML, which is not a programming language, and while custom resources extend functionality, it doesn''t offer the same level of abstraction or programming language benefits as CDK. Option C (incorrect) is for standardizing product provisioning for end-users, not primarily for defining infrastructure with programming languages. Option D (incorrect) is for configuration management of EC2 instances, not for defining and deploying cloud infrastructure as code.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  9,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_1_94',
  'A company is standardizing its infrastructure deployments across multiple AWS accounts and Regions. They need a way to deploy common baseline resources, such as networking and security configurations, consistently and efficiently. Which AWS CloudFormation features are best suited for this requirement? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["CloudFormation Change Sets to preview proposed infrastructure changes.","CloudFormation StackSets to deploy a common CloudFormation stack across multiple accounts and Regions.","CloudFormation Custom Resources to extend CloudFormation''s capabilities with custom logic.","CloudFormation Nested Stacks to create reusable components within a single account.","CloudFormation Drift Detection to identify configuration changes made outside of CloudFormation."]'::json,
  '{1,3}',
  'Option B (correct) is specifically designed for deploying CloudFormation stacks across multiple AWS accounts and Regions from a single administrator account, ensuring consistent baseline deployments. Option D (correct) allows for the creation of modular and reusable CloudFormation templates, which can be referenced by StackSets to build complex, standardized architectures. Option A (incorrect) is for reviewing changes before applying them to a single stack, not for multi-account/Region deployment. Option C (incorrect) extends CloudFormation with custom logic but doesn''t inherently facilitate multi-account/Region deployment or reusability in the same way as StackSets or nested stacks. Option E (incorrect) is for identifying configuration changes that deviate from the CloudFormation template, not for initial deployment or standardization.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  10,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_1_95',
  'A large enterprise needs to provide its development teams with a curated set of AWS resources, such as EC2 instances, RDS databases, and S3 buckets, that are pre-configured to meet security and compliance standards. The goal is to enable self-service provisioning while maintaining central governance. Which AWS services can help achieve this objective? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS CloudFormation to define the standardized resource templates.","AWS Service Catalog to allow end-users to provision approved products.","AWS Systems Manager Inventory to collect data about existing resources.","AWS CDK to programmatically define infrastructure components.","AWS Config conformance packs to monitor compliance of deployed resources."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is essential for defining the standardized AWS resources and their configurations as templates, which can then be used as products. Option B (correct) is specifically designed to enable organizations to create and manage catalogs of IT services that are approved for use on AWS. It allows end-users to provision these pre-approved products while maintaining central governance and compliance. Option C (incorrect) is used to collect data about the software and configurations of managed instances, not for provisioning standardized resources. Option D (incorrect) is a tool for defining infrastructure as code using programming languages, but it''s not the primary service for creating a self-service catalog for end-users. Option E (incorrect) is for assessing and monitoring compliance of *deployed* resources, not for provisioning them.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  11,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_1_96',
  'A development team is using AWS CDK to define their application''s infrastructure. They need to ensure that their CDK code adheres to specific coding standards and best practices for security and resource tagging. Which mechanisms can be used to enforce these guidelines within their CDK development workflow? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Implementing CloudFormation Guard to validate the generated CloudFormation templates against policy rules.","Using AWS Service Catalog to provide pre-approved CDK constructs for common patterns.","Integrating static code analysis tools into their CI/CD pipeline to scan CDK code.","Leveraging AWS Config conformance packs to audit deployed resources for compliance.","Utilizing AWS Systems Manager State Manager to enforce runtime configuration on EC2 instances."]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'Option A (correct) allows defining policy-as-code rules that can be applied to the CloudFormation templates generated by AWS CDK, ensuring that the infrastructure defined meets security and tagging standards before deployment. Option C (correct) involves using tools that analyze the CDK source code itself (e.g., TypeScript, Python) to check for coding standards, security vulnerabilities, and adherence to best practices before the infrastructure is synthesized and deployed. Option B (incorrect) is for providing a catalog of approved products for end-users, not for enforcing coding standards or policies within the CDK development process. Option D (incorrect) audits *deployed* resources for compliance, rather than enforcing policies during the development and deployment phases. Option E (incorrect) is for enforcing desired state configuration on EC2 instances at runtime, not for validating infrastructure code.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  12,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_1_97',
  'A company wants to detect any manual changes made to their CloudFormation-managed resources that deviate from the defined template. This is crucial for maintaining infrastructure consistency and preventing configuration drift. Which CloudFormation feature and associated AWS service can help identify these out-of-band changes? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["CloudFormation Drift Detection to identify differences between the stack''s actual and expected configuration.","AWS Config to record resource configuration changes and evaluate them against desired states.","CloudFormation Change Sets to preview proposed updates before applying them.","AWS Systems Manager Inventory to collect software and configuration data from instances.","AWS CloudFormation Guard to enforce policy-as-code during template validation."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is a direct CloudFormation feature designed to identify when the actual configuration of stack resources differs from the configuration specified in the stack''s CloudFormation template, indicating drift. Option B (correct) works hand-in-hand with CloudFormation Drift Detection by continuously recording configuration changes for almost all AWS resources. It can be configured with rules to evaluate these changes against desired states, providing a detailed history and compliance status that complements drift detection. Option C (incorrect) is for previewing *intended* changes from a template update, not for detecting *unintended* manual changes. Option D (incorrect) collects data from EC2 instances but doesn''t directly detect drift from CloudFormation templates. Option E (incorrect) is for policy validation *before* deployment, not for detecting post-deployment drift.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  13,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_1_98',
  'A DevOps team wants to ensure that all new AWS resources deployed through CloudFormation adhere to strict security policies, such as requiring encryption for S3 buckets and specific IAM role permissions. They need a mechanism to validate these policies *before* the resources are provisioned. Which AWS services or tools can facilitate this pre-deployment policy enforcement? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS CloudFormation Guard to define policy-as-code rules and validate templates.","AWS Config conformance packs to audit deployed resources against compliance rules.","AWS Systems Manager State Manager to enforce desired state configurations on EC2 instances.","AWS Service Catalog to provide pre-approved templates that already meet policies.","AWS CloudFormation Change Sets to preview resource changes."]'::json,
  '{0,3}',
  'Option A (correct) is designed for policy-as-code, allowing teams to define rules that validate CloudFormation templates against organizational security and compliance policies *before* deployment. Option D (correct) can be used to provide a curated set of CloudFormation templates (products) that are pre-configured and validated to meet specific security and compliance standards, enabling self-service provisioning of compliant resources. Option B (incorrect) is for auditing *deployed* resources for compliance, not for pre-deployment policy enforcement. Option C (incorrect) is for enforcing desired state on EC2 instances at runtime, not for validating infrastructure code before deployment. Option E (incorrect) is for previewing the *structural* changes a CloudFormation update will make, not for validating those changes against policy rules.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  14,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_1_99',
  'A company requires a solution to standardize the provisioning of common AWS resources for its development teams, ensuring that all provisioned resources comply with organizational policies and cost controls. They also want to enable self-service for developers while maintaining central oversight. Which AWS services are best suited for this requirement? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Service Catalog for creating and managing a portfolio of approved products.","AWS CloudFormation for defining the infrastructure templates for these products.","AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store for storing configuration parameters.","AWS Config for continuously monitoring resource compliance.","AWS CDK for defining infrastructure in programming languages."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is specifically designed to enable organizations to create and manage catalogs of IT services that are approved for use on AWS, allowing self-service provisioning while enforcing governance. Option B (correct) is the underlying service used by AWS Service Catalog to define the infrastructure templates (products) that are offered to end-users, ensuring standardization and adherence to policies. Option C (incorrect) is for storing configuration data, not for provisioning or cataloging resources. Option D (incorrect) monitors compliance of *deployed* resources, but doesn''t facilitate the initial standardized provisioning. Option E (incorrect) is a development framework for defining infrastructure, but Service Catalog is the service for providing a managed catalog for end-users.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  15,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_1_100',
  'A DevOps team is developing a new serverless application using AWS Lambda, Amazon API Gateway, and Amazon DynamoDB. They want to define this infrastructure as code using a simplified, declarative syntax that is optimized for serverless components. They also need to easily package and deploy their serverless application. Which AWS tools or frameworks are most appropriate for this scenario? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS CloudFormation with the AWS Serverless Application Model (SAM) transform.","AWS CDK using the `aws-lambda` and `aws-apigateway` constructs.","AWS Systems Manager State Manager to configure Lambda functions.","AWS OpsWorks for Chef Automate to deploy serverless applications.","AWS Service Catalog to provision individual Lambda functions."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) provides a declarative syntax for defining serverless applications and their components (Lambda, API Gateway, DynamoDB) and transforms into CloudFormation, simplifying deployment. Option B (correct) allows developers to define their serverless infrastructure using familiar programming languages and high-level constructs for Lambda and API Gateway, offering strong abstraction and reusability. Option C (incorrect) is for configuration management of EC2 instances, not for defining or deploying serverless applications. Option D (incorrect) is a legacy configuration management service primarily for EC2 instances, not suitable for serverless applications. Option E (incorrect) is for creating a catalog of approved products for self-service, not for defining or deploying serverless applications directly.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  16,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_1_101',
  'A DevOps team is adopting AWS CDK for infrastructure definition and wants to ensure that their CDK applications are modular and reusable across different environments (e.g., dev, test, prod). They also need to define custom resources that are not natively supported by CloudFormation. Which CDK and CloudFormation capabilities are relevant here? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["CDK Stacks for organizing and deploying related resources as a unit.","CloudFormation Custom Resources for extending CloudFormation''s capabilities.","CloudFormation StackSets for multi-account, multi-Region deployments.","AWS Service Catalog for providing pre-approved CDK constructs.","CloudFormation Guard for policy-as-code validation."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is a fundamental CDK concept that allows organizing related resources into logical deployment units, promoting modularity and reusability across environments. Option B (correct) allows extending CloudFormation''s capabilities by integrating custom logic or third-party services, which is directly applicable when CDK needs to define resources not natively supported by CloudFormation. Option C (incorrect) is for deploying stacks across multiple accounts/Regions, which is a deployment mechanism, not directly related to modularity within a single CDK application or defining custom resource types. Option D (incorrect) is for providing a catalog of approved products, not for defining infrastructure modularity or custom resources within CDK. Option E (incorrect) is for policy validation, not for defining custom resources or modularity.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  17,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_1_102',
  'A DevOps team is implementing a CI/CD pipeline for their CloudFormation templates. They want to ensure that all templates adhere to organizational security policies, such as disallowing public S3 buckets or requiring encryption for databases, before deployment. Which AWS services or tools can be integrated into the pipeline for this pre-deployment policy validation? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS CloudFormation Guard for policy-as-code validation of templates.","AWS Config conformance packs for auditing deployed resources against policies.","AWS Systems Manager State Manager for enforcing runtime configurations.","AWS Service Catalog for providing pre-approved templates.","AWS CodeBuild to run custom scripts for template validation."]'::json,
  '{0,4}',
  'Option A (correct) is a policy-as-code tool that allows defining rules to validate CloudFormation templates against organizational security and compliance policies *before* they are deployed, ensuring adherence to standards like S3 bucket public access or database encryption. Option E (correct) can be used within a CI/CD pipeline to run custom scripts or tools that perform static analysis or validation on CloudFormation templates, enforcing policies programmatically. Option B (incorrect) is for auditing *deployed* resources for compliance, not for pre-deployment validation. Option C (incorrect) is for enforcing desired state on EC2 instances at runtime, not for validating infrastructure code. Option D (incorrect) is for providing a catalog of approved products for self-service, which might include compliant templates, but it''s not a direct policy validation engine for arbitrary templates in a pipeline.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  18,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_1_103',
  'A company is using AWS CloudFormation to manage its infrastructure. They want to ensure that their CloudFormation templates are modular and promote reusability across different projects. Additionally, they need to extend CloudFormation''s capabilities to integrate with a third-party service that is not natively supported. Which CloudFormation features can help achieve these goals? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["CloudFormation Nested Stacks for creating reusable components.","CloudFormation Custom Resources for integrating with unsupported services.","CloudFormation StackSets for deploying templates across multiple accounts.","CloudFormation Change Sets for previewing updates.","CloudFormation Guard for policy-as-code validation."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) allows for breaking down complex CloudFormation templates into smaller, reusable components, which can then be referenced by a root stack, promoting modularity and reusability. Option B (correct) provides a way to extend CloudFormation''s capabilities by allowing you to write custom provisioning logic for resources that are not natively supported by CloudFormation, enabling integration with third-party services. Option C (incorrect) is for deploying stacks across multiple accounts and Regions, which is a deployment strategy, not directly related to modularity or extending capabilities for unsupported services. Option D (incorrect) is for previewing changes before applying them, not for modularity or custom resource integration. Option E (incorrect) is for policy validation, not for modularity or custom resource integration.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  19,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_1_104',
  'A DevOps team is using AWS CDK to define their infrastructure. They need to ensure that the generated CloudFormation templates adhere to specific security policies, such as disallowing public access to certain resources or requiring specific tags. Which AWS services or tools can be used to validate these policies against the CDK-generated templates? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS CloudFormation Guard to define and enforce policy-as-code rules.","AWS Config conformance packs to audit deployed resources for compliance.","AWS Systems Manager State Manager to enforce runtime configurations on EC2 instances.","AWS Service Catalog to provide pre-approved CDK constructs.","AWS CodePipeline to automate the deployment process."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is a policy-as-code tool that can be integrated into the CI/CD pipeline to validate the CloudFormation templates synthesized by AWS CDK against predefined security and tagging policies before deployment. Option B (correct) can be used to define a set of AWS Config rules and remediation actions that specifically check for the desired security policies and tags on the *deployed* resources. While Guard is pre-deployment, Config conformance packs provide ongoing post-deployment validation and reporting, ensuring continuous adherence. Option C (incorrect) is for enforcing desired state on EC2 instances at runtime, not for validating infrastructure code. Option D (incorrect) is for providing a catalog of approved products for self-service, not for policy validation of arbitrary CDK-generated templates. Option E (incorrect) is an orchestration service for CI/CD, but it doesn''t perform the policy validation itself.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  20,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_1_105',
  'A company wants to provide its developers with a self-service portal to provision standardized development environments, including EC2 instances with specific software installed and pre-configured networking. The goal is to ensure consistency and compliance while empowering developers. Which AWS services are essential for building this self-service capability? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Service Catalog to manage and provision approved IT services.","AWS CloudFormation to define the templates for the standardized development environments.","AWS Systems Manager Patch Manager to keep the EC2 instances updated.","AWS Config to monitor the compliance of provisioned resources.","AWS CDK for programmatic infrastructure definition."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is specifically designed to enable organizations to create and manage catalogs of IT services that are approved for use on AWS, allowing developers to provision standardized environments in a self-service manner. Option B (correct) is used to define the infrastructure templates (products) for these standardized development environments, including EC2 instances, software installations, and networking configurations, which are then offered through Service Catalog. Option C (incorrect) is for patching instances, which is a post-provisioning operational task, not for building the self-service provisioning capability itself. Option D (incorrect) monitors the compliance of *deployed* resources, but doesn''t facilitate the initial self-service provisioning. Option E (incorrect) is a development framework for defining infrastructure, but Service Catalog is the service for providing a managed catalog for end-users.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  21,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_1_106',
  'A DevOps team needs to define and deploy infrastructure for a new application using a modern programming language, rather than YAML or JSON. They also want to ensure that their infrastructure code is modular and easily testable. Which AWS tools and concepts are best suited for this approach? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS CDK for defining infrastructure using familiar programming languages.","CloudFormation Nested Stacks for creating reusable and modular components.","AWS CloudFormation Guard for policy-as-code validation.","AWS Systems Manager State Manager for configuration management.","AWS Service Catalog for providing self-service provisioning."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) allows developers to define their cloud infrastructure using familiar programming languages (e.g., Python, TypeScript), providing higher-level abstractions and making the code modular and testable. Option B (correct) is a CloudFormation feature that enables breaking down complex templates into smaller, reusable stacks, which directly supports modularity and can be effectively used in conjunction with AWS CDK to organize infrastructure. Option C (incorrect) is for policy validation, not for defining infrastructure using programming languages or for modularity. Option D (incorrect) is for configuration management of EC2 instances, not for defining infrastructure as code. Option E (incorrect) is for providing a catalog of approved products, not for defining infrastructure using programming languages or for modularity.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  22,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_1_107',
  'A global software company needs to deploy a standardized set of security and monitoring resources (e.g., AWS Config rules, IAM roles, CloudWatch alarms) across 15 AWS accounts in 3 different AWS Regions. The deployment process must be automated, consistent, and allow for centralized management of the templates and parameters. Individual account teams should not be able to modify these core security resources independently. Which two AWS CloudFormation features should a DevOps engineer utilize to meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS CloudFormation custom resources to invoke Lambda functions for cross-account deployments.","AWS CloudFormation StackSets to deploy and manage a single template across multiple accounts and Regions.","AWS CloudFormation change sets to preview the impact of security resource updates before deployment.","AWS Service Catalog to allow account teams to provision security resources from a curated list.","AWS CloudFormation drift detection to identify unauthorized changes to the deployed security resources."]'::json,
  '{1,4}',
  'Option B (correct) describes AWS CloudFormation StackSets, which are designed for deploying and managing CloudFormation stacks across multiple AWS accounts and AWS Regions from a single administrator account. This provides centralized control and consistency with minimal operational overhead for large-scale deployments. Option E (correct) describes AWS CloudFormation drift detection. After deploying standardized security resources with StackSets, drift detection can be used to regularly identify any unauthorized manual modifications made by individual account teams, ensuring that the infrastructure remains compliant with the IaC definition. Option A (incorrect) describes CloudFormation custom resources. While custom resources can extend CloudFormation''s capabilities, using them for routine cross-account deployments of standard AWS services would introduce unnecessary complexity and higher operational overhead compared to the native functionality of StackSets. Option C (incorrect) describes CloudFormation change sets. While important for safe deployments, change sets are used to preview changes before applying updates to a single stack or a set of stacks. They do not provide the multi-account, multi-Region deployment and management capabilities of StackSets. Option D (incorrect) describes AWS Service Catalog. Service Catalog is for enabling self-service provisioning of approved products by end-users, not for centrally enforcing a baseline of mandatory security resources across accounts where individual teams should not be able to modify them independently.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  23,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_1_108',
  'A DevOps team frequently updates critical CloudFormation stacks that manage production infrastructure, including Amazon RDS instances and Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling groups. They have experienced unexpected resource replacements and service interruptions during previous deployments due to unforeseen changes in the CloudFormation template. The team needs a mechanism to preview the exact impact of a stack update before it is applied to production, ensuring stability and preventing unintended modifications. Which two actions should the DevOps team implement to achieve this goal and prevent unintended resource modifications or deletions during CloudFormation stack updates? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Implement AWS CloudFormation drift detection to identify manual changes after deployment.","Utilize AWS CloudFormation change sets to preview proposed changes before executing stack updates.","Define all resources within a single, monolithic CloudFormation stack to simplify management.","Use AWS CloudFormation Guard to validate template syntax and resource properties before deployment.","Adopt AWS CDK to define infrastructure, as it inherently prevents unexpected resource replacements."]'::json,
  '{1,3}',
  'Option B (correct) describes utilizing AWS CloudFormation change sets. Change sets allow you to preview the exact impact of a proposed stack update before you execute it. This shows which resources will be added, modified, or deleted, helping to prevent unexpected replacements or service interruptions, directly addressing the anti-pattern of not using change sets. Option D (correct) describes using AWS CloudFormation Guard. CloudFormation Guard is a policy-as-code language that can validate CloudFormation templates against predefined rules, ensuring that resource properties and configurations meet organizational standards before deployment. While not directly showing the ''impact'' of a change, it helps prevent non-compliant or potentially destructive configurations from even reaching the deployment stage, thus contributing to stability. Option A (incorrect) describes AWS CloudFormation drift detection. Drift detection is used to identify manual changes made to resources *after* they have been deployed, not to preview the impact of a *planned* CloudFormation update. Option C (incorrect) suggests defining all resources within a single, monolithic CloudFormation stack. This is an anti-pattern ([ap-2.1-001]) that leads to slow deployments, a larger blast radius if an update fails, and makes managing changes more complex, directly contradicting the goal of ensuring stability and preventing unintended modifications. Option E (incorrect) suggests adopting AWS CDK. While AWS CDK offers benefits like using familiar programming languages and strong typing, it synthesizes into CloudFormation templates. It does not inherently prevent unexpected resource replacements or negate the need for change sets when deploying to production; the underlying CloudFormation engine still behaves the same way.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  24,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_1_109',
  'A large organization has strict security and compliance policies that dictate resource configurations (e.g., S3 buckets must be encrypted, EC2 instances must not have public IPs). Before deploying CloudFormation templates to production, the DevOps team needs an automated way to validate that these templates adhere to the defined policies, preventing non-compliant infrastructure from being provisioned. This validation should occur early in the CI/CD pipeline. Which two AWS services or features should the team implement to MOST effectively enforce policy-as-code compliance for CloudFormation templates before deployment? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS CloudFormation change sets to review the policy violations.","AWS Config conformance packs to assess template compliance.","AWS CloudFormation Guard to validate templates against policy rules.","AWS Service Catalog to ensure only approved templates are used.","AWS Systems Manager State Manager to enforce desired configurations post-deployment."]'::json,
  '{2,3}',
  'Option C (correct) describes AWS CloudFormation Guard. CloudFormation Guard is a policy-as-code language that allows you to define rules to validate CloudFormation templates against organizational policies. It can be integrated into CI/CD pipelines to automatically check for non-compliant configurations *before* deployment, preventing them from being provisioned. Option D (correct) describes AWS Service Catalog. While CloudFormation Guard validates the template content, AWS Service Catalog provides a way to curate and manage a catalog of approved CloudFormation templates (products). By only allowing deployments from this catalog, the organization ensures that only templates that have already passed policy validation (e.g., via CloudFormation Guard checks during the Service Catalog product creation process) are available for provisioning, thus enforcing compliance at the provisioning stage. Option A (incorrect) describes AWS CloudFormation change sets. Change sets are used to preview the operational impact of a stack update (what resources will change), not to validate the template''s adherence to security policies. Option B (incorrect) describes AWS Config conformance packs. AWS Config conformance packs are used to assess the compliance of *deployed* resources against a set of rules. They do not validate CloudFormation templates *before* deployment in the CI/CD pipeline. Option E (incorrect) describes AWS Systems Manager State Manager. State Manager is used to *enforce* desired configurations on EC2 instances *after* they are deployed. It does not validate CloudFormation templates before deployment.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  25,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_1_110',
  'A central IT department in a large enterprise wants to empower various business units to provision their own AWS resources, such as specific EC2 instance types, Amazon RDS databases, or pre-configured application stacks. However, IT needs to ensure that all provisioned resources adhere to corporate governance, security standards, and cost controls. Business units should only be able to select from a curated list of approved and pre-configured products. Which two AWS services or features should a DevOps engineer implement to BEST enable self-service provisioning of approved AWS resources while maintaining central governance and compliance? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS CloudFormation StackSets to deploy standardized templates across business unit accounts.","AWS Systems Manager State Manager to enforce post-provisioning compliance on EC2 instances.","AWS Service Catalog to create and manage a portfolio of approved and governed products.","AWS CloudFormation nested stacks to modularize the application stacks for business units.","AWS CloudFormation Guard to validate business unit templates against corporate policies."]'::json,
  '{2,4}',
  'Option C (correct) describes AWS Service Catalog. Service Catalog is specifically designed to enable self-service provisioning of approved AWS resources (products) from a curated catalog (portfolio). This allows business units to provision resources while ensuring they adhere to corporate governance, security, and cost controls, as the underlying CloudFormation templates are pre-approved and managed by central IT. Option E (correct) describes AWS CloudFormation Guard. To ensure that the CloudFormation templates used to define the products in AWS Service Catalog adhere to corporate policies, CloudFormation Guard can be used during the product creation and update process. This validates the templates against policy-as-code rules, preventing non-compliant configurations from ever being made available for self-service provisioning. Option A (incorrect) describes AWS CloudFormation StackSets. StackSets are used for deploying stacks across multiple accounts and Regions, typically for central enforcement, not for enabling self-service provisioning where business units choose what to deploy. Option B (incorrect) describes AWS Systems Manager State Manager. State Manager is used for enforcing desired state configurations on EC2 instances *after* they are provisioned. While important for ongoing compliance, it doesn''t address the initial self-service provisioning and pre-validation of resources. Option D (incorrect) describes AWS CloudFormation nested stacks. Nested stacks are used for modularizing CloudFormation templates for reusability. While the products in Service Catalog might use nested stacks internally, nested stacks themselves don''t provide the self-service portal or governance layer required.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  26,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_1_111',
  'A DevOps team uses CloudFormation to manage its entire AWS infrastructure. They need to integrate a third-party monitoring tool that requires an API call to register new application endpoints after the application''s load balancer is provisioned. CloudFormation does not natively support this specific API call. The team wants to automate this registration as part of their CloudFormation stack deployment, ensuring the monitoring is set up immediately after the application is ready. Which two CloudFormation features should the team leverage to MOST effectively extend CloudFormation capabilities to provision or manage resources not natively supported by CloudFormation? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS CloudFormation change sets to manage the external API calls.","AWS CloudFormation custom resources to invoke Lambda functions for external API calls.","AWS CloudFormation nested stacks to encapsulate the external API integration logic.","AWS CloudFormation macros to transform the template with external API call logic.","AWS Systems Manager State Manager to execute the API calls post-deployment."]'::json,
  '{1,3}',
  'Option B (correct) describes AWS CloudFormation custom resources. Custom resources allow you to write custom provisioning logic, typically using an AWS Lambda function, to manage resources that are not natively supported by CloudFormation. The Lambda function can make the necessary API calls to the third-party monitoring tool during stack creation, update, and deletion events, integrating it seamlessly into the CloudFormation lifecycle. Option D (correct) describes AWS CloudFormation macros. CloudFormation macros allow you to process and transform templates before CloudFormation processes them. A macro could be used to inject the necessary custom resource definition or other logic that triggers the external API call based on a simplified declaration in the template, providing a more declarative way to integrate custom logic. Option A (incorrect) describes AWS CloudFormation change sets. Change sets are used to preview the impact of a stack update; they do not provide a mechanism to execute external API calls as part of the provisioning process. Option C (incorrect) describes AWS CloudFormation nested stacks. Nested stacks are used for modularizing and reusing CloudFormation templates. They do not extend CloudFormation''s ability to manage non-native resources or execute external API calls directly. Option E (incorrect) describes AWS Systems Manager State Manager. State Manager is used for enforcing desired state configurations on EC2 instances. While it could execute scripts post-deployment, it''s not integrated into the CloudFormation resource lifecycle for managing the creation/deletion of non-native resources, making custom resources a more appropriate and integrated solution for this scenario.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  27,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_1_112',
  'A development team is building a serverless application consisting of AWS Lambda functions, Amazon API Gateway endpoints, and Amazon DynamoDB tables. They are using CloudFormation to define their infrastructure but find the YAML syntax for defining serverless components to be verbose and repetitive. They want a simpler, more concise way to declare serverless resources within their CloudFormation templates, reducing boilerplate and improving readability. Which two AWS CloudFormation features should the team leverage to BEST simplify and condense the definition of serverless application resources within CloudFormation templates? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS CloudFormation custom resources to abstract serverless component definitions.","AWS CloudFormation change sets to manage serverless application deployments.","AWS CloudFormation macros to create custom transformations for serverless constructs.","AWS CloudFormation with the Serverless Application Model (SAM) transform.","AWS CloudFormation Guard to validate the syntax of serverless templates."]'::json,
  '{2,3}',
  'Option C (correct) describes AWS CloudFormation macros. Macros allow you to perform custom processing on CloudFormation templates before they are processed by CloudFormation. A custom macro could be developed to transform simplified serverless declarations into the full, verbose CloudFormation syntax, thereby reducing boilerplate and improving readability. Option D (correct) describes AWS CloudFormation with the Serverless Application Model (SAM) transform. SAM is a CloudFormation extension that provides a simplified, concise syntax for defining serverless resources. It uses a transform (`AWS::Serverless-2016-10-31`) to expand shorthand declarations into full CloudFormation syntax, directly addressing the verbosity and repetition issues for serverless applications. Option A (incorrect) describes AWS CloudFormation custom resources. Custom resources are used to manage resources not natively supported by CloudFormation. While they can encapsulate complex logic, they are not primarily designed to simplify the *declaration* of native serverless resources within the template itself. Option B (incorrect) describes AWS CloudFormation change sets. Change sets are used to preview the operational impact of a stack update; they do not simplify the template definition or reduce boilerplate. Option E (incorrect) describes AWS CloudFormation Guard. CloudFormation Guard is a policy-as-code tool for validating templates against security and compliance rules, not for simplifying the syntax or reducing boilerplate for serverless definitions.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  28,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_1_113',
  'A DevOps team is managing a complex application infrastructure defined using AWS CloudFormation. They need to ensure that any proposed changes to the stacks are reviewed for potential unintended resource replacements or deletions before being applied. Additionally, they want to identify any manual modifications made to resources outside of CloudFormation. Which AWS CloudFormation features help address these requirements? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["CloudFormation Change Sets","CloudFormation StackSets","CloudFormation Drift Detection","AWS Systems Manager State Manager","AWS Service Catalog"]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'Option A (CloudFormation Change Sets) is correct because it allows teams to preview the changes that will be made to a stack before applying them, highlighting any resources that will be added, modified, or deleted. This directly addresses the need to review proposed changes for unintended effects. Option C (CloudFormation Drift Detection) is correct because it identifies when a stack''s actual configuration deviates from its CloudFormation template, which helps in detecting manual modifications made outside of CloudFormation. Option B (CloudFormation StackSets) is used for deploying CloudFormation stacks across multiple AWS accounts and Regions, not for previewing changes or detecting drift within a single stack. Option D (AWS Systems Manager State Manager) is used for enforcing configuration compliance on EC2 instances and other resources, but it is not a direct feature of CloudFormation for managing stack updates or detecting template-to-resource drift. Option E (AWS Service Catalog) allows organizations to create and manage catalogs of IT services that are approved for use on AWS, focusing on provisioning, not change preview or drift detection for existing stacks.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  29,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_1_114',
  'A large organization wants to standardize the deployment of common infrastructure patterns, such as VPCs and security groups, across multiple development teams and environments. They need a way to define these patterns once and reuse them consistently, while also enabling self-service provisioning for authorized users. Which AWS services or features would best support these requirements? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Config conformance packs","AWS Service Catalog","AWS Systems Manager Inventory","AWS CloudFormation nested stacks","AWS CloudFormation Guard"]'::json,
  '{1,3}',
  'Option B (AWS Service Catalog) is correct because it enables organizations to create and manage catalogs of IT services that are approved for use on AWS. This allows development teams to self-service standardized infrastructure patterns while adhering to organizational policies. Option D (AWS CloudFormation nested stacks) is correct because they allow for the creation of reusable components (child stacks) that can be called from other CloudFormation templates (parent stacks). This promotes modularity and consistency in defining common infrastructure patterns, which can then be published through AWS Service Catalog. Option A (AWS Config conformance packs) are used for assessing compliance of AWS resources against a set of rules, not for provisioning or self-service. Option C (AWS Systems Manager Inventory) collects and displays information about instances and software, which is for operational visibility, not for standardized provisioning. Option E (AWS CloudFormation Guard) is a policy-as-code tool for validating CloudFormation templates against predefined rules, focusing on compliance checks during template authoring, not for self-service provisioning of infrastructure.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  30,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_1_115',
  'A DevOps team wants to define their AWS infrastructure using familiar programming languages like Python or TypeScript, leveraging object-oriented principles to create reusable constructs. The final infrastructure should still be provisioned and managed by AWS CloudFormation. Which AWS services or tools should the team utilize? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Systems Manager State Manager","AWS CDK","AWS Service Catalog","AWS CloudFormation","CloudFormation Guard"]'::json,
  '{1,3}',
  'Option B (AWS CDK) is correct because it allows developers to define cloud infrastructure using familiar programming languages (like Python, TypeScript, Java, etc.) and object-oriented principles. It compiles the code into AWS CloudFormation templates. Option D (AWS CloudFormation) is correct because the AWS CDK synthesizes its definitions into standard CloudFormation templates, which are then used by CloudFormation to provision and manage the actual AWS resources. Therefore, CloudFormation is the underlying service for deployment and management. Option A (AWS Systems Manager State Manager) is used for configuration management and enforcing desired state on instances, not for defining infrastructure as code. Option C (AWS Service Catalog) is for creating and managing catalogs of approved IT services for self-service provisioning, not for defining infrastructure using programming languages. Option E (CloudFormation Guard) is a policy-as-code tool used to validate CloudFormation templates against predefined rules, focusing on compliance, not on defining infrastructure with programming languages.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  31,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_1',
  'medium'
);

