INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_2_59',
  'A company needs to ensure that all EC2 instances in its fleet maintain a consistent operating system configuration and automatically remediate any deviations. Which two AWS Systems Manager capabilities are best suited for this task? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["State Manager","Patch Manager","Run Command","Session Manager","Parameter Store"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (State Manager) allows you to define and enforce a desired state for your EC2 instances and other AWS resources, ensuring configuration compliance and automatically remediating deviations. Option B (Patch Manager) automates the process of patching managed instances with security updates and other patches, which is a critical part of maintaining a consistent OS configuration. Option C (Run Command) executes commands on instances on-demand, but doesn''t enforce a continuous state. Option D (Session Manager) provides secure shell access to instances. Option E (Parameter Store) is for storing configuration data and secrets.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  32,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_2',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_2_60',
  'A security team needs to ensure that all EC2 instances are regularly updated with the latest security patches. Which two AWS Systems Manager capabilities are most relevant for automating and managing this process across a large fleet? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Patch Manager","Maintenance Windows","State Manager","Run Command","Inventory"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Patch Manager) automates the process of patching managed instances with security updates and other patches, making it ideal for managing security updates across a large fleet. Option B (Maintenance Windows) allows you to define a recurring schedule for performing potentially disruptive actions, such as patching, ensuring updates happen during approved times. Option C (State Manager) enforces desired configurations but is not specifically for patching. Option D (Run Command) executes commands on-demand. Option E (Inventory) collects data about instances, but doesn''t perform patching.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  33,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_2',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_2_61',
  'A security auditor requires a consolidated view of compliance status across multiple AWS accounts, based on a predefined set of rules. Which two AWS services or features are best suited for this requirement? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Config conformance packs","AWS Systems Manager State Manager","AWS CloudFormation Guard","AWS Service Catalog","AWS OpsWorks"]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'Option A (AWS Config conformance packs) provide a simple way to deploy and manage a collection of AWS Config rules and remediation actions as a single entity, giving a consolidated compliance view. Option C (AWS CloudFormation Guard) can be used to validate CloudFormation templates against policy-as-code rules, ensuring that infrastructure deployed is compliant from the start, which contributes to overall compliance status. Option B (AWS Systems Manager State Manager) enforces configuration on individual instances, not a consolidated view across accounts. Option D (AWS Service Catalog) is for provisioning approved products. Option E (AWS OpsWorks) is a legacy configuration management service.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  34,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_2',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_2_62',
  'Which two capabilities are provided by AWS Config to help maintain and assess the compliance of your AWS resources? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Continuous monitoring of resource configurations","Automated remediation of non-compliant resources","Executing commands on EC2 instances","Storing application secrets and configuration data","Deploying infrastructure across multiple accounts and Regions"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Continuous monitoring of resource configurations) is a core capability of AWS Config, which records configuration changes over time. Option B (Automated remediation of non-compliant resources) can be set up with AWS Config rules, allowing automatic actions to bring resources back into compliance. Option C (Executing commands on EC2 instances) is a function of AWS Systems Manager Run Command. Option D (Storing application secrets and configuration data) is handled by AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store or AWS Secrets Manager. Option E (Deploying infrastructure across multiple accounts and Regions) is a function of CloudFormation StackSets.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  35,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_2',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_2_63',
  'Which two core capabilities are offered by AWS Systems Manager to manage and automate operational tasks across your AWS resources? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Automating OS patching and software updates","Enforcing desired state configuration for instances","Defining infrastructure as code using programming languages","Centralized logging and monitoring of application metrics","Provisioning standardized IT services for end-users"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Automating OS patching and software updates) is a key capability provided by AWS Systems Manager Patch Manager. Option B (Enforcing desired state configuration for instances) is handled by AWS Systems Manager State Manager, ensuring consistency across your fleet. Option C (Defining infrastructure as code using programming languages) is done with AWS CDK. Option D (Centralized logging and monitoring of application metrics) is primarily handled by Amazon CloudWatch and AWS CloudTrail. Option E (Provisioning standardized IT services for end-users) is the role of AWS Service Catalog.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  36,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_2',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_2_64',
  'Which two statements accurately describe AWS OpsWorks? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["It is a configuration management service that supports Chef and Puppet.","It is considered a legacy service for configuration management.","It provides policy-as-code compliance checking for CloudFormation templates.","It is primarily used for serverless application deployment.","It automates patching and inventory collection for EC2 instances."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (It is a configuration management service that supports Chef and Puppet) is correct, as AWS OpsWorks provides managed instances of Chef Automate and Puppet Enterprise. Option B (It is considered a legacy service for configuration management) is also correct, as AWS recommends using AWS Systems Manager for most modern configuration management needs. Option C (It provides policy-as-code compliance checking for CloudFormation templates) describes CloudFormation Guard. Option D (It is primarily used for serverless application deployment) describes services like AWS Lambda or AWS Serverless Application Model (SAM). Option E (It automates patching and inventory collection for EC2 instances) describes AWS Systems Manager Patch Manager and Inventory.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  37,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_2',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_2_65',
  'A DevOps team uses AWS CloudFormation to manage its infrastructure. They want to identify any manual changes made to resources outside of CloudFormation templates. Which two AWS services or features can help detect and report on such deviations? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["CloudFormation Drift Detection","AWS Config","AWS Systems Manager State Manager","AWS Service Catalog","AWS CloudFormation Guard"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (CloudFormation Drift Detection) is specifically designed to identify when the actual configuration of stack resources differs from their expected configuration in the CloudFormation template. Option B (AWS Config) continuously records configuration changes of your AWS resources, allowing you to track changes and identify those made outside of CloudFormation. Option C (AWS Systems Manager State Manager) enforces desired state, but its primary role is not drift *detection* for CloudFormation stacks. Option D (AWS Service Catalog) is for provisioning. Option E (AWS CloudFormation Guard) is for policy validation *before* deployment.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  38,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_2',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_2_66',
  'A company requires automated remediation for non-compliant AWS resources detected by AWS Config rules. Which two services can be integrated with AWS Config to achieve this? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Systems Manager State Manager","AWS Systems Manager Automation","AWS CloudFormation","AWS Service Catalog","AWS OpsWorks"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (AWS Systems Manager State Manager) can be used to define and enforce a desired state for resources, and its capabilities can be triggered by AWS Config for remediation. Option B (AWS Systems Manager Automation) allows you to define runbooks for common operational tasks, which can be invoked by AWS Config rules to automatically remediate non-compliant resources. Option C (AWS CloudFormation) is for defining infrastructure, not for automated remediation of existing resources. Option D (AWS Service Catalog) is for provisioning. Option E (AWS OpsWorks) is a legacy configuration management service.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  39,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_2',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_2_67',
  'Which two AWS Systems Manager capabilities are essential for maintaining a secure and up-to-date fleet of EC2 instances by automating software updates and configuration baselines? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Patch Manager","State Manager","Run Command","Session Manager","Inventory"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Patch Manager) automates the process of scanning instances for missing patches and applying them, ensuring software updates are maintained. Option B (State Manager) allows you to define and enforce configuration baselines across your instances, ensuring they adhere to desired security and operational settings. Option C (Run Command) executes commands on-demand. Option D (Session Manager) provides secure shell access. Option E (Inventory) collects data about instances but doesn''t apply updates or enforce configurations.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  40,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_2',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_2_68',
  'A company needs to continuously monitor the compliance of its AWS resources against a set of internal security policies and industry regulations. They require a solution that can assess multiple rules simultaneously and provide a consolidated compliance report. Which AWS services are best suited for this purpose? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Config conformance packs to group multiple AWS Config rules and remediate non-compliant resources.","AWS CloudFormation Guard to define policy-as-code for pre-deployment validation.","AWS Systems Manager State Manager to enforce desired state configurations on EC2 instances.","AWS Config to record configuration changes and evaluate resource configurations against rules.","AWS Service Catalog to provision compliant resources."]'::json,
  '{0,3}',
  'Option D (correct) is fundamental for this requirement as it records configuration changes of AWS resources and continuously evaluates them against specified rules, providing a timeline of compliance status. Option A (correct) builds upon AWS Config by allowing the grouping of multiple AWS Config rules and remediation actions into a single pack, providing a consolidated view of compliance against a framework or standard, which is ideal for multi-rule assessment and reporting. Option B (incorrect) is used for policy-as-code validation *before* deployment, not for continuous monitoring of *deployed* resources. Option C (incorrect) is for enforcing desired state on EC2 instances, which is a configuration management tool, not a compliance monitoring and assessment service. Option E (incorrect) is for provisioning standardized resources, not for monitoring their compliance post-deployment.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  41,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_2_69',
  'A company needs to ensure that its AWS environment adheres to several compliance standards, such as PCI DSS and HIPAA. They require an automated way to continuously assess their resources against these standards and get a consolidated view of their compliance posture. Which AWS services are designed to address these requirements effectively? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Config to record resource configurations and evaluate them against compliance rules.","AWS Config conformance packs to deploy a collection of AWS Config rules and remediation actions for specific standards.","AWS CloudFormation Guard to enforce policy-as-code during template validation.","AWS Systems Manager Inventory to collect software and configuration data from instances.","AWS Service Catalog to provision compliant resources."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is the foundational service that continuously records configuration changes of AWS resources and evaluates them against desired configurations, providing a history of compliance status. Option B (correct) extends AWS Config by allowing the deployment of a collection of AWS Config rules and remediation actions as a single unit, specifically designed to assess compliance against industry standards like PCI DSS or HIPAA, providing a consolidated compliance view. Option C (incorrect) is for pre-deployment policy validation of CloudFormation templates, not for continuous assessment of deployed resources. Option D (incorrect) collects data from EC2 instances but does not assess overall resource compliance against standards. Option E (incorrect) is for provisioning standardized resources, not for continuously monitoring their compliance.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  42,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_2_70',
  'A large organization needs to deploy a common set of security configurations and monitoring tools across all new AWS accounts created within their AWS Organization. They require a highly automated and consistent approach to ensure compliance from day one. Which AWS services or features are best suited for this multi-account, baseline deployment? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS CloudFormation StackSets to deploy standardized CloudFormation templates across accounts.","AWS Config conformance packs to assess compliance across multiple accounts.","AWS Service Catalog to provide self-service provisioning of compliant resources.","AWS Systems Manager State Manager to enforce configuration on EC2 instances.","AWS CloudFormation Guard to validate templates before deployment."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is ideal for deploying common baseline resources, such as security configurations and monitoring tools, consistently across multiple AWS accounts and Regions from a central administrator account. Option B (correct) can be deployed across multiple accounts (often via StackSets) to continuously assess the compliance of resources against a defined set of rules, providing a consolidated view of the organization''s compliance posture. Option C (incorrect) is for enabling self-service provisioning of approved products, not for automated baseline deployment across all accounts. Option D (incorrect) is for configuration management of EC2 instances, not for deploying baseline infrastructure across accounts. Option E (incorrect) is for pre-deployment policy validation of templates, not for deploying resources across accounts.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  43,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_2_71',
  'A large consulting firm manages AWS environments for multiple clients, each in its own AWS account. The firm needs to enforce a baseline set of AWS Config rules and corresponding remediation actions across all client accounts to ensure continuous compliance with industry regulations. This enforcement must be scalable, automated, and centrally managed without requiring manual intervention in each client account. Which two AWS services or features should a DevOps engineer utilize to meet these requirements with the MOST efficiency and scalability? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Systems Manager State Manager to enforce configuration on EC2 instances in each account.","AWS CloudFormation StackSets to deploy and manage AWS Config conformance packs across client accounts.","AWS CloudFormation custom resources to trigger compliance checks in each client account.","AWS Service Catalog to allow clients to provision compliant resources.","AWS Config conformance packs to define the baseline set of rules and remediation actions."]'::json,
  '{1,4}',
  'Option B (correct) describes AWS CloudFormation StackSets. StackSets enable you to deploy and manage CloudFormation stacks across multiple AWS accounts and Regions from a single administrator account. This is the ideal mechanism for centrally deploying a standardized set of AWS Config rules or conformance packs to numerous client accounts, ensuring scalability and automation. Option E (correct) describes AWS Config conformance packs. Conformance packs allow you to define a collection of AWS Config rules and remediation actions as a single, deployable unit. This is the perfect way to package the ''baseline set of AWS Config rules and corresponding remediation actions'' for consistent deployment via StackSets across client accounts. Option A (incorrect) describes AWS Systems Manager State Manager. State Manager is used for enforcing desired state configurations on EC2 instances. While important for instance-level compliance, it doesn''t provide the capability to deploy and manage AWS Config rules themselves across multiple accounts. Option C (incorrect) describes AWS CloudFormation custom resources. While custom resources can extend CloudFormation, using them for deploying standard AWS Config rules or conformance packs across accounts would introduce unnecessary complexity and operational overhead compared to the native functionality of StackSets and Conformance Packs. Option D (incorrect) describes AWS Service Catalog. Service Catalog is for enabling self-service provisioning of approved products by end-users. It is not designed for centrally enforcing a mandatory baseline of compliance rules across client accounts.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  44,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_2_72',
  'A central platform team provides standardized infrastructure components (e.g., pre-configured VPCs, secure EC2 instances, approved Amazon RDS instances) to various development teams across different AWS accounts. These components must adhere to corporate security and cost policies. Development teams need a self-service portal to provision these approved resources without direct access to the underlying CloudFormation templates or requiring manual approval for every request. Which two AWS services or features should a DevOps engineer implement to BEST enable self-service provisioning of standardized, compliant infrastructure across multiple AWS accounts? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS CloudFormation Guard to validate the templates before making them available.","AWS CloudFormation StackSets to deploy the standardized components to all development accounts.","AWS Service Catalog to create a portfolio of approved products for self-service.","AWS Systems Manager Inventory to track provisioned resources for compliance.","AWS CDK to define the standardized components in a programmatic language."]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'Option A (correct) describes AWS CloudFormation Guard. Before making any standardized infrastructure components available for self-service, the underlying CloudFormation templates must be validated against corporate security and cost policies. CloudFormation Guard provides the policy-as-code mechanism to perform this validation, ensuring that only compliant templates are used. Option C (correct) describes AWS Service Catalog. AWS Service Catalog is the primary service for creating and managing a portfolio of approved IT services (products), which can be CloudFormation templates for standardized infrastructure components. It provides the self-service portal for development teams to provision these resources without direct access to the underlying templates, while enforcing governance and compliance. Option B (incorrect) describes AWS CloudFormation StackSets. StackSets are for centrally deploying and managing stacks across multiple accounts and Regions, typically for mandatory baselines. They do not provide a self-service portal where development teams can choose and provision resources on demand. Option D (incorrect) describes AWS Systems Manager Inventory. Inventory is used for collecting and centralizing detailed information about software and configurations on EC2 instances. While useful for post-provisioning auditing, it doesn''t enable the self-service provisioning process itself. Option E (incorrect) describes AWS CDK. AWS CDK is a framework for defining infrastructure using programming languages. While it can be used to create the CloudFormation templates for the standardized components, it doesn''t provide the self-service portal or governance layer for provisioning those components to end-users.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  45,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_2_73',
  'Before deploying any new CloudFormation templates into production environments across multiple AWS accounts, a security team requires a mechanism to automatically verify that these templates comply with organizational security policies. These policies include restrictions on resource types, encryption requirements, and network configurations. The validation process needs to be integrated into the CI/CD pipeline to prevent non-compliant infrastructure from ever reaching production. Which two AWS services or features should the team implement to MOST effectively enforce policy-as-code compliance for CloudFormation templates across a multi-account AWS environment? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS CloudFormation change sets to identify policy violations during deployment.","AWS Systems Manager State Manager to enforce compliance on deployed resources.","AWS CloudFormation Guard to validate templates against policy rules in the CI/CD pipeline.","AWS Config conformance packs to assess compliance of deployed resources.","AWS Service Catalog to ensure only pre-approved templates are used for provisioning."]'::json,
  '{2,4}',
  'Option C (correct) describes AWS CloudFormation Guard. CloudFormation Guard is a policy-as-code language that allows you to define rules to validate CloudFormation templates against organizational policies. Integrating it into the CI/CD pipeline enables automated checks *before* deployment, effectively preventing non-compliant infrastructure from reaching production environments across multiple accounts. Option E (correct) describes AWS Service Catalog. By making only pre-approved and policy-validated CloudFormation templates available as products in AWS Service Catalog, the organization can ensure that any infrastructure provisioned by development teams in their respective accounts adheres to corporate policies. This acts as a strong enforcement mechanism at the point of provisioning, complementing the pre-deployment validation provided by CloudFormation Guard. Option A (incorrect) describes AWS CloudFormation change sets. Change sets are used to preview the operational impact of a stack update (what resources will change), not to validate the template''s adherence to security policies. Option B (incorrect) describes AWS Systems Manager State Manager. State Manager is used for enforcing desired state configurations on EC2 instances *after* they are deployed. It does not validate CloudFormation templates before deployment. Option D (incorrect) describes AWS Config conformance packs. AWS Config conformance packs are used to assess the compliance of *deployed* resources against a set of rules. They do not validate CloudFormation templates *before* deployment in the CI/CD pipeline.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  46,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_2_74',
  'A large organization operates hundreds of Amazon EC2 instances across various AWS accounts and regions. They need to ensure that a specific set of security agents and configuration files are consistently installed and maintained on all these instances. Manual configuration is prone to errors and drift. The solution must provide centralized reporting on compliance status and automatically remediate any deviations. Which two AWS services or features should a DevOps engineer implement to provide the MOST scalable and automated solution for enforcing consistent configuration and compliance across a large fleet of EC2 instances in a multi-account environment? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS CloudFormation StackSets to deploy configuration scripts to each instance.","AWS Systems Manager State Manager to define and enforce desired state configurations.","AWS Config conformance packs to assess and report on the compliance of the EC2 instances.","AWS CloudFormation Guard to validate EC2 instance configurations before deployment.","AWS Service Catalog to provision pre-configured EC2 instances."]'::json,
  '{1,2}',
  'Option B (correct) describes AWS Systems Manager State Manager. State Manager allows you to define a desired state for your EC2 instances (e.g., specific software installed, configuration files present) and automatically enforces that state. It can be deployed across multiple accounts and regions, making it highly scalable for maintaining consistent configurations and remediating drift. Option C (correct) describes AWS Config conformance packs. Conformance packs allow you to define a collection of AWS Config rules and remediation actions as a single unit. These can be used to assess the compliance of the EC2 instances against the desired security and configuration standards, providing centralized reporting on compliance status, which is crucial for a large, multi-account environment. Option A (incorrect) describes AWS CloudFormation StackSets. StackSets are for deploying CloudFormation stacks across accounts/regions. While they could deploy resources that *enable* configuration management, they are not the direct tool for *enforcing* desired state configurations on running EC2 instances. Option D (incorrect) describes AWS CloudFormation Guard. CloudFormation Guard is for validating CloudFormation templates *before* deployment to ensure they adhere to policies. It doesn''t manage the configuration of *running* EC2 instances. Option E (incorrect) describes AWS Service Catalog. Service Catalog is for self-service provisioning of approved resources. While it can provision pre-configured EC2 instances, it doesn''t provide the ongoing enforcement and remediation of desired state configurations after the instance is launched.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  47,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_2_75',
  'A compliance team needs to assess and report on the adherence of AWS resources to several regulatory standards (e.g., PCI DSS, HIPAA) across 20 production AWS accounts. Manually checking individual AWS Config rules in each account is time-consuming and error-prone. They require a consolidated, high-level view of compliance status and the ability to deploy a predefined set of rules as a single entity. Which two AWS services or features should a DevOps engineer implement to provide the MOST efficient way to deploy and manage a collection of AWS Config rules and remediations as a single unit across multiple AWS accounts for compliance reporting? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Systems Manager State Manager to enforce compliance on EC2 instances.","AWS CloudFormation StackSets to deploy the compliance baseline.","AWS CloudFormation custom resources to integrate with external compliance tools.","AWS Config conformance packs to group and deploy related Config rules.","AWS Service Catalog to provide self-service compliance reporting."]'::json,
  '{1,3}',
  'Option B (correct) describes AWS CloudFormation StackSets. StackSets enable you to deploy and manage CloudFormation stacks across multiple AWS accounts and Regions from a single administrator account. This is the ideal mechanism for centrally deploying a standardized set of AWS Config conformance packs to numerous production accounts, ensuring scalability and automation. Option D (correct) describes AWS Config conformance packs. Conformance packs allow you to define a collection of AWS Config rules and remediation actions as a single, deployable unit. This is the perfect way to package the ''predefined set of rules'' for consistent deployment via StackSets across multiple accounts, providing a consolidated view of compliance status against regulatory standards. Option A (incorrect) describes AWS Systems Manager State Manager. State Manager is used for enforcing desired state configurations on EC2 instances. While important for instance-level compliance, it doesn''t provide the capability to deploy and manage AWS Config rules themselves across multiple accounts or provide a consolidated compliance view across various resource types. Option C (incorrect) describes AWS CloudFormation custom resources. While custom resources can extend CloudFormation, using them for deploying standard AWS Config rules or conformance packs across accounts would introduce unnecessary complexity and operational overhead compared to the native functionality of StackSets and Conformance Packs. Option E (incorrect) describes AWS Service Catalog. Service Catalog is for enabling self-service provisioning of approved products by end-users. It is not designed for centrally enforcing a mandatory baseline of compliance rules or providing consolidated compliance reporting across accounts.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  48,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_2_76',
  'An enterprise uses a custom, on-premises identity provider that needs to synchronize user groups with IAM roles in various AWS accounts. This synchronization must occur automatically whenever a new AWS account is provisioned or an application stack is deployed that requires specific IAM roles. CloudFormation doesn''t have a native resource type for this specific synchronization logic. Which two AWS CloudFormation features should a DevOps engineer leverage to MOST effectively integrate a custom, external identity synchronization process into CloudFormation deployments across multiple AWS accounts? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS CloudFormation change sets to manage the synchronization process.","AWS CloudFormation custom resources to invoke a Lambda function for synchronization.","AWS CloudFormation StackSets to deploy the synchronization logic to all accounts.","AWS CloudFormation Guard to validate the IAM role definitions.","AWS Systems Manager State Manager to enforce IAM role synchronization post-deployment."]'::json,
  '{1,2}',
  'Option B (correct) describes AWS CloudFormation custom resources. Custom resources allow you to extend CloudFormation''s capabilities to manage resources that are not natively supported. By invoking an AWS Lambda function, a custom resource can execute the necessary logic to interact with the on-premises identity provider and synchronize user groups with IAM roles during CloudFormation stack creation, update, and deletion events. Option C (correct) describes AWS CloudFormation StackSets. Once the custom resource (backed by a Lambda function) is defined within a CloudFormation template, AWS CloudFormation StackSets can be used to deploy this synchronization logic consistently across various AWS accounts. This ensures that the custom identity integration is automatically present and operational in all target accounts where it''s needed. Option A (incorrect) describes AWS CloudFormation change sets. Change sets are used to preview the impact of a stack update; they do not provide a mechanism to execute external synchronization logic as part of the provisioning process. Option D (incorrect) describes AWS CloudFormation Guard. CloudFormation Guard is a policy-as-code tool for validating CloudFormation templates against security and compliance rules. While important for IAM role definitions, it does not provide the mechanism for external identity synchronization. Option E (incorrect) describes AWS Systems Manager State Manager. State Manager is used for enforcing desired state configurations on EC2 instances. It is not designed for integrating custom identity synchronization logic into CloudFormation deployments across accounts.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  49,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_2_77',
  'A global company is expanding its operations into new AWS Regions. For each new Region, a foundational set of networking, logging, and monitoring resources must be established consistently across all existing and future AWS accounts. This baseline infrastructure needs to be deployed and updated centrally, ensuring uniformity and adherence to corporate standards across all active Regions. Which two AWS CloudFormation features should a DevOps engineer leverage to provide the MOST scalable and consistent approach to deploy and manage a baseline infrastructure across multiple AWS accounts and newly adopted AWS Regions? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS CloudFormation nested stacks to modularize the baseline components.","AWS CloudFormation StackSets to deploy the baseline template across accounts and Regions.","AWS Service Catalog to provide self-service provisioning of the baseline infrastructure.","AWS CloudFormation Guard to validate the baseline template against corporate policies.","AWS Systems Manager State Manager to configure resources after baseline deployment."]'::json,
  '{1,3}',
  'Option B (correct) describes AWS CloudFormation StackSets. StackSets are specifically designed for deploying and managing CloudFormation stacks across multiple AWS accounts and AWS Regions from a single administrator account. This is the most scalable and consistent approach for establishing a foundational baseline infrastructure across a global enterprise. Option D (correct) describes AWS CloudFormation Guard. Before deploying the baseline infrastructure template via StackSets, it is critical to ensure that the template adheres to corporate standards and policies. CloudFormation Guard can be integrated into the CI/CD pipeline to validate the baseline template against policy-as-code rules, preventing non-compliant infrastructure from being deployed across the enterprise. Option A (incorrect) describes AWS CloudFormation nested stacks. Nested stacks are used for modularizing CloudFormation templates within a single stack or for reuse within a single account/region context. While the baseline template itself might use nested stacks, they don''t provide the multi-account, multi-region deployment capabilities of StackSets. Option C (incorrect) describes AWS Service Catalog. Service Catalog is for enabling self-service provisioning of approved products by end-users. It is not designed for centrally enforcing a mandatory baseline infrastructure across all accounts and regions. Option E (incorrect) describes AWS Systems Manager State Manager. State Manager is used for enforcing desired state configurations on EC2 instances *after* they are deployed. It does not handle the initial deployment of foundational infrastructure across accounts and regions.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  50,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_2',
  'hard'
);

