INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_1_42',
  'A DevOps team is implementing a CI/CD pipeline using AWS CodePipeline. They need to ensure that the IAM role used by CodePipeline actions has only the necessary permissions for each stage, following the principle of least privilege. What is the recommended approach to achieve this for a complex pipeline with multiple stages and service interactions?',
  'mcq',
  '["Create a single IAM role with administrator access and attach it to the entire CodePipeline, simplifying permission management.","Define a single IAM role for the CodePipeline, and then use inline policies to grant specific permissions for each action within the pipeline.","Create separate IAM roles for each CodePipeline action, granting only the permissions required for that specific action, and configure CodePipeline to assume these roles.","Attach a managed policy with broad read-only access to the CodePipeline''s service role and rely on resource-based policies for write access."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is the recommended best practice for implementing least privilege in AWS CodePipeline. By creating separate IAM roles for each CodePipeline action and granting only the permissions required for that specific action, you minimize the blast radius if a role is compromised. CodePipeline can be configured to assume these specific roles for each action. This directly addresses anti-pattern [ap-1.1-001]. Option A (incorrect) is an anti-pattern ([ap-1.1-001]) as it grants overly broad permissions, creating a large blast radius if compromised. Option B (incorrect) is better than Option A but still attaches all permissions to a single role, making it harder to audit and potentially granting more permissions than necessary to the overall pipeline execution. It''s generally better to scope roles per action. Option D (incorrect) is insufficient, as many pipeline actions require write permissions (e.g., CodeBuild pushing artifacts, CodeDeploy deploying applications), and relying solely on resource-based policies for write access can be complex and less explicit than action-specific roles.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  12,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_1_43',
  'A company is using AWS CodeDeploy for blue/green deployments of its web application on Amazon EC2. The operations team requires that any deployment failure, such as high HTTP 5xx errors or increased latency, automatically triggers a rollback to the previously stable application version without manual intervention. How can this be implemented in CodeDeploy?',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure a manual approval step in AWS CodePipeline that allows the operations team to initiate a rollback if issues are detected.","Implement an AWS Lambda function that monitors Amazon CloudWatch alarms for application health and manually triggers a CodeDeploy rollback via the AWS SDK.","Configure Amazon CloudWatch alarms for relevant metrics (e.g., HTTP 5xx errors, latency) and associate them as rollback triggers in the CodeDeploy deployment group.","Set the CodeDeploy deployment group to automatically roll back if any instance fails to pass health checks during the deployment process."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is the most effective and automated way to implement automatic rollbacks based on application health metrics in AWS CodeDeploy. CodeDeploy deployment groups can be configured with rollback triggers that monitor Amazon CloudWatch alarms. If an alarm breaches its threshold (e.g., for HTTP 5xx errors or latency), CodeDeploy will automatically initiate a rollback to the last known good version. This directly addresses anti-pattern [ap-1.1-002]. Option A (incorrect) requires manual intervention, which goes against the requirement for automated rollback. Option B (incorrect) involves custom development and maintenance of a Lambda function, which is more complex and has higher operational overhead than using CodeDeploy''s native integration with CloudWatch alarms. Option D (incorrect) describes a basic health check rollback, which is useful but might not catch all application-level issues like increased 5xx errors or latency that occur even if instances are technically ''healthy'' but the application is performing poorly.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  13,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_1_44',
  'A DevOps team is setting up an AWS CodeBuild project for a Java application. They want to speed up build times by reusing dependencies and intermediate build outputs from previous builds. Which CodeBuild feature should they leverage to achieve this MOST effectively?',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure the build project to use a larger instance type for faster processing.","Implement a custom script in the buildspec.yml to download and cache dependencies from Amazon S3 before each build.","Enable CodeBuild caching, configuring it to use an Amazon S3 bucket or local caching for build artifacts and dependencies.","Split the build into multiple smaller CodeBuild projects and run them in parallel to reduce overall build time."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is the most effective and native CodeBuild feature for speeding up build times by reusing dependencies and intermediate outputs. CodeBuild caching allows you to specify a cache type (Amazon S3 or local) and paths to cache, which stores and retrieves build artifacts, dependencies, and other files between builds. This significantly reduces the time spent downloading dependencies or recompiling unchanged code. Option A (incorrect) might speed up individual steps but doesn''t address the core problem of repeatedly downloading or compiling the same dependencies. Option B (incorrect) involves custom scripting and manual management of the cache, which is less efficient and more complex than using CodeBuild''s built-in caching feature. Option D (incorrect) might reduce overall pipeline time but doesn''t specifically address reusing artifacts within a single build project to speed up that specific build.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  14,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_1_45',
  'A company needs to deploy an application to multiple AWS accounts and regions using a single, centralized AWS CodePipeline. The pipeline is hosted in a ''master'' AWS account. How can the CodePipeline securely deploy resources to target accounts and regions?',
  'mcq',
  '["Create IAM users in the master account and share their credentials with the target accounts for deployment actions.","Configure CodePipeline to use cross-account IAM roles, where the master account''s CodePipeline service role assumes roles in the target accounts, and use cross-region Amazon S3 buckets for artifacts.","Establish VPC peering connections between the master account and all target accounts to allow secure communication for deployments.","Use AWS Organizations to create a service control policy (SCP) that grants CodePipeline full access to all resources in member accounts."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the standard and most secure approach for cross-account and cross-region deployments with AWS CodePipeline. The CodePipeline service role in the master account is granted permission to assume specific IAM roles in the target accounts. These target account roles have least-privilege permissions for deployment actions (e.g., CodeDeploy, CloudFormation). For cross-region deployments, Amazon S3 buckets used for artifacts must also be configured for cross-region replication or separate buckets per region. Option A (incorrect) is highly insecure and violates AWS best practices by sharing IAM user credentials. Option C (incorrect) is for network connectivity between VPCs and does not directly address cross-account/cross-region deployment permissions for CodePipeline. Option D (incorrect) is too broad and insecure. While SCPs can restrict permissions, they cannot grant permissions. Granting full access via an SCP is an anti-pattern for least privilege.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  15,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_1_46',
  'A DevOps engineer is configuring an AWS CodeBuild project to run unit tests and generate test reports. The project needs to display test results, including success/failure rates and detailed test case information, directly within the CodeBuild console. What configuration is required in the buildspec.yml to achieve this?',
  'mcq',
  '["Include a `post_build` command to upload test results to an Amazon S3 bucket for later analysis.","Specify the `reports` section in the buildspec.yml, defining the test report format (e.g., JunitXml) and the path to the test results file.","Integrate an AWS Lambda function to parse CodeBuild logs and push test results to Amazon CloudWatch Logs.","Ensure the build environment includes the necessary testing framework, and CodeBuild will automatically detect and report test results."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the specific configuration required in the `buildspec.yml` to enable CodeBuild''s native test reporting feature. By defining a `reports` section, you tell CodeBuild where to find the test results file (e.g., `target/surefire-reports/TEST-*.xml` for Maven JUnit tests) and in what format (e.g., JunitXml). CodeBuild then processes these files and displays the test results, trends, and detailed information directly in the CodeBuild console. Option A (incorrect) stores reports in S3 but doesn''t integrate them into the CodeBuild console''s reporting features. Option C (incorrect) involves custom development and external integration, which is unnecessary given CodeBuild''s built-in reporting. Option D (incorrect) is false; while the testing framework is necessary, CodeBuild requires explicit configuration in the `buildspec.yml` to parse and display test reports.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  16,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_1_47',
  'A development team wants to deploy a new version of their application using AWS CodeDeploy with a blue/green strategy. During the deployment, they need to perform a series of custom validation steps, such as running integration tests and smoke tests, before traffic is fully shifted to the new environment. Where should these custom validation steps be defined?',
  'mcq',
  '["In the `ApplicationStop` lifecycle hook of the AppSpec file.","As a separate stage in AWS CodePipeline, placed after the CodeDeploy action.","Within the `BeforeAllowTraffic` or `AfterAllowTraffic` lifecycle hooks in the CodeDeploy AppSpec file.","As an AWS Lambda function triggered by Amazon CloudWatch events after the CodeDeploy deployment completes."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is the appropriate place to define custom validation steps for blue/green deployments in AWS CodeDeploy. The `BeforeAllowTraffic` and `AfterAllowTraffic` lifecycle hooks are specifically designed for blue/green deployments to run scripts or invoke Lambda functions at critical points during traffic shifting. `BeforeAllowTraffic` allows validation before any traffic is shifted, and `AfterAllowTraffic` allows validation after the initial traffic shift but before the old environment is terminated, making them ideal for integration and smoke tests. Option A (incorrect) is for stopping the application on instances and is not suitable for post-deployment validation. Option B (incorrect) is possible but less integrated with CodeDeploy''s specific traffic shifting logic. Defining these hooks directly in the AppSpec file provides a more granular and deployment-specific control. Option D (incorrect) is an external trigger and less tightly coupled with the CodeDeploy lifecycle, potentially leading to race conditions or less precise control over the traffic shift.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  17,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_1_48',
  'A company is using AWS CodePipeline for its CI/CD process. They need to integrate a manual approval step before deploying to production. This approval should be sent to a specific security team, and the pipeline should pause until approval is granted. How can this be configured within CodePipeline?',
  'mcq',
  '["Add an AWS Lambda function that sends an email to the security team and waits for a response before proceeding.","Insert a ''Manual Approval'' action type into the CodePipeline stage before the production deployment, configuring it with an SNS topic for notifications.","Use an AWS Step Functions workflow to orchestrate the approval process, integrating it as a custom action in CodePipeline.","Configure AWS CodeDeploy to require a manual confirmation before initiating the production deployment group."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the native and most straightforward way to implement a manual approval step in AWS CodePipeline. CodePipeline provides a ''Manual Approval'' action type that can be inserted into any stage. When this action is reached, the pipeline pauses, and a notification can be sent via an Amazon SNS topic to designated approvers. The pipeline resumes only after an approver manually approves it in the CodePipeline console. Option A (incorrect) requires custom development and maintenance of a Lambda function, which is unnecessary given the built-in feature. Option C (incorrect) is an overly complex solution for a standard manual approval step, increasing operational overhead. Option D (incorrect) is not a feature of AWS CodeDeploy itself; manual approvals are managed at the CodePipeline level.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  18,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_1_49',
  'A DevOps team needs to secure sensitive credentials, such as database passwords and API keys, used by AWS CodeBuild projects during the build process. These credentials must not be hardcoded in the buildspec.yml or source code. What is the MOST secure and recommended way to manage these secrets?',
  'mcq',
  '["Store secrets as environment variables directly in the CodeBuild project configuration.","Encrypt secrets and store them in an Amazon S3 bucket, then decrypt them within the buildspec.yml using a custom script.","Use AWS Secrets Manager or AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store to store secrets, and retrieve them securely within the buildspec.yml using their ARNs.","Hardcode secrets in a private Git repository (AWS CodeCommit) and ensure the repository has restricted access."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is the most secure and recommended approach for managing secrets in AWS CodeBuild. AWS Secrets Manager and AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store are purpose-built services for securely storing and retrieving sensitive information. CodeBuild can directly integrate with these services, allowing you to reference secrets by their ARNs in the `buildspec.yml` or as environment variables, and CodeBuild will securely inject them into the build environment. Option A (incorrect) is less secure because environment variables in CodeBuild are visible in the console and logs if not handled carefully, and they are not encrypted at rest by default in the same way as Secrets Manager/Parameter Store. Option B (incorrect) requires custom encryption/decryption logic, which increases complexity and the risk of misconfiguration, and it''s less secure than using dedicated secret management services. Option D (incorrect) is a major anti-pattern for security; hardcoding secrets in any version control system, even a private one, is highly insecure and makes secrets management and rotation extremely difficult.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  19,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_1_50',
  'A DevOps team is setting up an AWS CodePipeline for a new application. They need to define the sequence of actions, such as source, build, and deploy, and how artifacts flow between them. Which CodePipeline component is responsible for orchestrating these steps?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CodeBuild project definitions.","AWS CodeDeploy deployment groups.","CodePipeline stages and actions.","AWS CodeCommit repositories."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) refers to CodePipeline stages and actions. AWS CodePipeline orchestrates the entire release process by defining stages (e.g., Source, Build, Test, Deploy) and actions within each stage, specifying how artifacts flow between them. Option A (AWS CodeBuild project definitions) define how to build and test code within a single build action, not the overall pipeline flow. Option B (AWS CodeDeploy deployment groups) define the set of instances or environments where an application is deployed, not the pipeline orchestration. Option D (AWS CodeCommit repositories) are used for source code version control, which is typically the input for the first stage of a pipeline.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  20,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_1_51',
  'A development team uses AWS CodeBuild to compile their application code and run unit tests. They need to specify the commands for building, testing, and packaging their application. Which file is used by AWS CodeBuild to define these steps?',
  'mcq',
  '["appspec.yml","buildspec.yml","cloudformation.yaml","pipeline.json"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is buildspec.yml. The buildspec.yml file is a collection of build commands and related settings, in YAML format, that AWS CodeBuild uses to run a build. It specifies the build environment, commands to run, and artifacts to output. Option A (appspec.yml) is used by AWS CodeDeploy to manage deployments. Option C (cloudformation.yaml) is used for defining AWS infrastructure as code with AWS CloudFormation. Option D (pipeline.json) is not a standard configuration file used by AWS CodeBuild for defining build steps; CodePipeline definitions are often JSON or YAML, but CodeBuild itself uses buildspec.yml.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  21,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_1_52',
  'Which AWS service provides deployment strategies such as in-place and blue/green for applications running on Amazon EC2, AWS Lambda, and Amazon ECS?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CodeBuild","AWS CodePipeline","AWS CodeDeploy","AWS CodeCommit"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is AWS CodeDeploy. CodeDeploy is a deployment service that automates application deployments to various compute services, including Amazon EC2, AWS Lambda, and Amazon ECS. It supports different deployment strategies like in-place and blue/green. Option A (AWS CodeBuild) is a fully managed build service that compiles source code, runs tests, and produces software packages. Option B (AWS CodePipeline) is a continuous delivery service that automates release pipelines. Option D (AWS CodeCommit) is a fully managed source control service that hosts Git repositories.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  22,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_1_53',
  'A DevOps team uses AWS CodeDeploy for their application deployments. They want to ensure that if a new deployment introduces errors, the system automatically reverts to the last known good version without manual intervention. What should they configure in CodeDeploy?',
  'mcq',
  '["Manually monitor application metrics and trigger rollbacks if issues arise.","Implement a blue/green deployment strategy without any health checks.","Configure automated rollback triggers tied to Amazon CloudWatch alarms.","Use an in-place deployment strategy and rely on manual intervention for failures."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is to configure automated rollback triggers tied to Amazon CloudWatch alarms. AWS CodeDeploy allows you to set up rollback triggers based on CloudWatch alarms that monitor application health metrics (e.g., error rates, latency). If an alarm breaches its threshold during or after a deployment, CodeDeploy can automatically initiate a rollback to the previous successful version. Option A (Manually monitor application metrics and trigger rollbacks if issues arise) requires manual intervention, which is inefficient and prone to delays, failing the ''automatically reverts'' requirement. Option B (Implement a blue/green deployment strategy without any health checks) is insufficient; while blue/green helps minimize downtime, without health checks and rollback triggers, it won''t automatically revert on failure. Option D (Use an in-place deployment strategy and rely on manual intervention for failures) is an anti-pattern (ap-1.1-002) as it does not implement automated rollback triggers, leading to manual intervention for failed deployments.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  23,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_1_54',
  'A company requires a manual review and approval step in their CI/CD pipeline before deploying to production. Which AWS CodePipeline feature allows a designated person or team to approve or reject a deployment at a specific stage?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CodeBuild test reports.","AWS CodeDeploy lifecycle hooks.","CodePipeline manual approval actions.","AWS CodeCommit pull requests."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) refers to CodePipeline manual approval actions. AWS CodePipeline supports adding a manual approval action to any stage, which pauses the pipeline until an authorized user approves or rejects the action. This is specifically designed for scenarios requiring human review before proceeding. Option A (AWS CodeBuild test reports) are for reporting automated test results, not for manual approvals. Option B (AWS CodeDeploy lifecycle hooks) are scripts that run during various phases of a deployment (e.g., beforeInstall, AfterAllowTraffic), not for manual approval gates. Option D (AWS CodeCommit pull requests) are for code review and merging changes into a repository, which happens earlier in the development workflow, not typically as a final deployment gate in CodePipeline.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  24,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_1_55',
  'A DevOps team wants to optimize the build times for their AWS CodeBuild projects. Their builds frequently download the same dependencies and source code. Which CodeBuild feature can significantly reduce subsequent build durations by reusing previously downloaded data?',
  'mcq',
  '["Batch builds.","Buildspec.yml optimization.","Build caching.","Test reporting."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is build caching. AWS CodeBuild allows you to configure caching for your build projects. This feature stores dependencies and other build artifacts in a cache (either Amazon S3 or a local cache) that can be reused in subsequent builds, significantly reducing build times by avoiding repeated downloads. Option A (Batch builds) allows running multiple builds concurrently, which can improve throughput but doesn''t directly reduce the duration of a single build by reusing data. Option B (Buildspec.yml optimization) is a general practice to write efficient build commands, but ''build caching'' is a specific CodeBuild feature for dependency reuse. Option D (Test reporting) is for collecting and visualizing test results, not for accelerating build execution.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  25,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_1_56',
  'A company is implementing a CI/CD pipeline for a critical application. They want to ensure that all secrets, such as database credentials and API keys, used during the build process in AWS CodeBuild are handled securely without hardcoding them. Which AWS service should be integrated for secrets management?',
  'mcq',
  '["Store secrets directly in the buildspec.yml file.","Use environment variables within the CodeBuild project settings.","Integrate with AWS Secrets Manager or AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store.","Encrypt secrets with an Amazon S3 bucket policy."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is to integrate with AWS Secrets Manager or AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store. These services are designed for securely storing and managing secrets and configuration data, respectively. CodeBuild can securely retrieve secrets from these services during the build process, preventing them from being hardcoded or exposed. Option A (Store secrets directly in the buildspec.yml file) is a severe security anti-pattern; secrets should never be committed to source control. Option B (Use environment variables within the CodeBuild project settings) is better than hardcoding, but for highly sensitive data, Secrets Manager or Parameter Store provide stronger encryption, rotation, and access control. Option D (Encrypt secrets with an Amazon S3 bucket policy) is not a method for secrets management during a build process; S3 policies control access to objects, and while S3 can store encrypted data, it''s not designed as a secrets manager for application credentials.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  26,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_1_57',
  'What is the security best practice for managing IAM roles in AWS CodePipeline to prevent a broad blast radius if credentials are compromised?',
  'mcq',
  '["Use a single IAM role with full administrator access for the entire pipeline.","Grant the CodePipeline service role permissions to all necessary resources in all accounts.","Configure separate, least-privilege IAM roles for each action within a pipeline stage.","Store IAM user credentials directly in AWS CodeCommit for easy access by pipeline actions."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is to configure separate, least-privilege IAM roles for each action within a pipeline stage. This adheres to the principle of least privilege, ensuring that each action only has the specific permissions required to perform its task. This significantly limits the potential damage if a role''s credentials are compromised. Option A (Use a single IAM role with full administrator access for the entire pipeline) is an anti-pattern (ap-1.1-001) that creates a large blast radius if compromised, violating security best practices. Option B (Grant the CodePipeline service role permissions to all necessary resources in all accounts) also violates the principle of least privilege by granting overly broad permissions. Option D (Store IAM user credentials directly in AWS CodeCommit for easy access by pipeline actions) is a severe security anti-pattern; credentials should never be stored in source control. AWS Secrets Manager or AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store should be used for secrets management.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  27,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_1_58',
  'A DevOps team wants to ensure that their AWS CodeDeploy deployments automatically revert to a previous stable version if new application versions cause an increase in HTTP 5xx errors. How can this be implemented with minimal operational overhead?',
  'mcq',
  '["Manually monitor application logs for 5xx errors and trigger rollbacks.","Configure an Amazon CloudWatch alarm for 5xx errors and link it as a rollback trigger in CodeDeploy.","Implement a custom AWS Lambda function to check 5xx errors and call the CodeDeploy API.","Use an in-place deployment strategy and rely on manual intervention for failures."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is to configure an Amazon CloudWatch alarm for 5xx errors and link it as a rollback trigger in CodeDeploy. AWS CodeDeploy natively integrates with CloudWatch alarms to provide automated rollback capabilities. If a CloudWatch alarm (monitoring 5xx errors, for example) enters an ALARM state during or after a deployment, CodeDeploy can automatically initiate a rollback to the previous successful application version, requiring minimal operational overhead. Option A (Manually monitor application logs for 5xx errors and trigger rollbacks) requires manual intervention, which is not automated and can lead to slower recovery. Option C (Implement a custom AWS Lambda function to check 5xx errors and call the CodeDeploy API) is a custom solution that adds unnecessary operational overhead when CodeDeploy provides a native, integrated feature for this. Option D (Use an in-place deployment strategy and rely on manual intervention for failures) is an anti-pattern (ap-1.1-002) as it does not implement automated rollback triggers, leading to manual intervention for failed deployments.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  28,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_1',
  'medium'
);

