INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_3_31',
  'Which AWS service is specifically designed to store, manage, and deploy private container images for use with services like Amazon ECS and Amazon EKS?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon ECR","AWS CodeArtifact","Amazon S3","AWS CodeCommit"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (Amazon ECR) is the correct service. Amazon Elastic Container Registry (ECR) is a fully managed Docker container registry that makes it easy for developers to store, manage, and deploy Docker container images. Option B (AWS CodeArtifact) is for managing software packages like npm, Maven, or PyPI. Option C (Amazon S3) is object storage and while it can store files, it''s not optimized for container image management with Docker compatibility. Option D (AWS CodeCommit) is a source control service, not an artifact repository.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  29,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_3_32',
  'A development team needs a centralized, private repository for managing their npm, Maven, and PyPI packages used across multiple projects. Which AWS service should they use to achieve this with minimal operational overhead?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon S3","AWS CodeArtifact","Amazon ECR","AWS CodeCommit"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (AWS CodeArtifact) is the correct choice. AWS CodeArtifact is a fully managed artifact repository service that makes it easy for organizations to securely store, publish, and share software packages used in their software development process. It supports popular package managers like npm, Maven, and PyPI. Option A (Amazon S3) can store packages but lacks the native integration with package managers and repository features of CodeArtifact. Option C (Amazon ECR) is specifically for container images, not general software packages. Option D (AWS CodeCommit) is a version control service for source code, not a package repository.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  30,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_3_33',
  'A DevOps team is deploying containerized applications using AWS CodePipeline and Amazon ECR. They want to ensure that every deployment is uniquely identifiable and reproducible, preventing issues caused by changes to image tags. Which strategy should they implement?',
  'mcq',
  '["Use the ''latest'' tag for all production deployments to simplify updates.","Manually update container image tags in the deployment manifest for each release.","Employ immutable container image tags, such as a Git commit SHA or a unique build ID, for all deployments.","Configure Amazon ECR to automatically overwrite existing image tags with new builds."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Employ immutable container image tags, such as a Git commit SHA or a unique build ID, for all deployments) is the correct and recommended strategy. Immutable tags ensure that once an image is pushed with a specific tag, that tag always refers to the same image, making deployments reproducible and rollbacks predictable. Option A (Use the ''latest'' tag for all production deployments to simplify updates) is an anti-pattern (ap-1.3-001) because the ''latest'' tag is mutable and can refer to different images over time, making it impossible to audit or reliably roll back. Option B (Manually update container image tags in the deployment manifest for each release) introduces manual overhead and potential for human error. Option D (Configure Amazon ECR to automatically overwrite existing image tags with new builds) also leads to mutable tags and breaks reproducibility.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  31,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_3_34',
  'Which AWS service is commonly used as a highly durable and available storage location for artifacts generated and consumed by AWS CodePipeline stages?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CodeArtifact","Amazon ECR","AWS CodeCommit","Amazon S3"]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'Option D (Amazon S3) is the correct service. Amazon S3 buckets are commonly used as artifact stores for AWS CodePipeline because they offer high durability, availability, and scalability for storing build outputs, source code, and other pipeline artifacts. CodePipeline uses S3 buckets to pass artifacts between stages. Option A (AWS CodeArtifact) is for managing software packages, not general pipeline artifacts. Option B (Amazon ECR) is for container images. Option C (AWS CodeCommit) is a source control service, not an artifact storage service.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  32,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_3_35',
  'A company stores numerous container images in Amazon ECR. To manage costs and ensure compliance, they need to automatically remove old, unused image versions while retaining a certain number of recent images. Which ECR feature should they configure?',
  'mcq',
  '["Lifecycle policies","Image scanning","Repository policies","Cross-region replication"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (Lifecycle policies) is the correct feature. Amazon ECR lifecycle policies allow you to specify rules to automatically clean up unused or old images from your repositories. These rules can be based on image age or count, helping to manage storage costs and keep repositories tidy. Option B (Image scanning) is for identifying software vulnerabilities. Option C (Repository policies) control access permissions to the repository. Option D (Cross-region replication) is for copying images to other AWS Regions for disaster recovery or multi-region deployments, not for cleanup.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  33,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_3_36',
  'A company is implementing a multi-region CI/CD pipeline using AWS CodePipeline. They need to ensure that artifacts generated in one region are quickly available to deployment stages in another region for disaster recovery and global deployments. Which Amazon S3 feature should be used for this purpose?',
  'mcq',
  '["S3 Intelligent-Tiering","S3 Versioning","S3 Cross-Region Replication","S3 Lifecycle policies"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (S3 Cross-Region Replication) is the correct feature. Cross-Region Replication (CRR) automatically and asynchronously copies objects across Amazon S3 buckets in different AWS Regions. This is ideal for multi-region pipelines where artifacts need to be available in multiple geographies for deployment or disaster recovery. Option A (S3 Intelligent-Tiering) is for optimizing storage costs based on access patterns. Option B (S3 Versioning) keeps multiple versions of an object in the same bucket. Option D (S3 Lifecycle policies) are for managing object transitions to different storage classes or expiration, not for replicating across regions.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  34,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_3_37',
  'Which of the following package types can AWS CodeArtifact host and manage as a private repository?',
  'mcq',
  '["Docker images, Helm charts, and OCI artifacts","npm, Maven, and PyPI packages","Windows installers, macOS disk images, and Linux RPMs","Git repositories, Subversion repositories, and Mercurial repositories"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (npm, Maven, and PyPI packages) is correct. AWS CodeArtifact is designed to support popular package managers like npm (Node.js packages), Maven (Java packages), and PyPI (Python packages), allowing developers to store and retrieve these artifacts securely. Option A (Docker images, Helm charts, and OCI artifacts) are primarily managed by Amazon ECR. Option C (Windows installers, macOS disk images, and Linux RPMs) can be stored in Amazon S3 but are not natively managed as package types by CodeArtifact. Option D (Git repositories, Subversion repositories, and Mercurial repositories) are managed by version control systems like AWS CodeCommit or GitHub.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  35,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_3_38',
  'An organization requires a strategy to ensure that once a build artifact is created and stored, it cannot be modified or deleted, guaranteeing its integrity throughout the deployment pipeline. What is this approach known as?',
  'mcq',
  '["Immutable artifact strategy","Ephemeral artifact strategy","Mutable artifact strategy","Volatile artifact strategy"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (Immutable artifact strategy) is the correct term. An immutable artifact strategy ensures that once an artifact (like a container image or a build output) is created and stored, it is never modified. Any changes require creating a new artifact with a new identifier, ensuring traceability and reproducibility. Option B (Ephemeral artifact strategy) refers to artifacts that are short-lived and discarded after use. Option C (Mutable artifact strategy) allows artifacts to be changed after creation, which is an anti-pattern for reliable deployments. Option D (Volatile artifact strategy) is not a standard term in this context.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  36,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_3_39',
  'A company uses Amazon ECR to store Docker images. They need to restrict who can push and pull images to specific repositories based on IAM roles. Which mechanism should they use to define these permissions?',
  'mcq',
  '["S3 bucket policies","ECR repository policies","IAM user policies","VPC endpoint policies"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (ECR repository policies) is the correct mechanism. Amazon ECR repository policies are resource-based policies that allow you to control access to specific ECR repositories. You can define which IAM users or roles have permissions to push, pull, or delete images from a repository. Option A (S3 bucket policies) are for Amazon S3 buckets. Option C (IAM user policies) define permissions for IAM users, but repository policies provide a more granular, resource-specific control directly on the ECR repository. Option D (VPC endpoint policies) control access to AWS services via a VPC endpoint, not repository-level permissions.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  37,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_3_40',
  'Which AWS service allows you to store and manage private container images for Docker and Open Container Initiative (OCI) compatible images?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CodeArtifact","Amazon S3","Amazon ECR","AWS CodeCommit"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Amazon ECR) is the correct service. Amazon Elastic Container Registry (ECR) is a fully managed Docker container registry that makes it easy for developers to store, manage, and deploy Docker container images. It is compatible with OCI images. Option A (AWS CodeArtifact) is for software packages like npm, Maven, PyPI. Option B (Amazon S3) is object storage, not a specialized container registry. Option D (AWS CodeCommit) is a source control service.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  38,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_3_41',
  'What is the primary benefit of using AWS CodeArtifact in a CI/CD pipeline?',
  'mcq',
  '["To automate the deployment of applications to various environments.","To manage source code repositories securely.","To provide a centralized, private repository for software packages.","To build and test application code."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (To provide a centralized, private repository for software packages) is the primary benefit of AWS CodeArtifact. It acts as a fully managed artifact repository for common package types like npm, Maven, and PyPI, allowing organizations to securely store, publish, and share their software packages. Option A (To automate the deployment of applications to various environments) is handled by AWS CodeDeploy. Option B (To manage source code repositories securely) is handled by AWS CodeCommit. Option D (To build and test application code) is handled by AWS CodeBuild.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  39,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_3_42',
  'A company needs to store pipeline artifacts, such as build outputs and deployment manifests, in a highly durable and available manner for their CI/CD workflows. Which AWS service is the most cost-effective and appropriate choice for this general-purpose artifact storage?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CodeArtifact","Amazon ECR","Amazon S3","Amazon EBS"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Amazon S3) is the most cost-effective and appropriate choice for general-purpose pipeline artifact storage. Amazon S3 offers high durability, availability, and scalability at a low cost, making it ideal for storing build outputs, source bundles, and other files passed between pipeline stages. Option A (AWS CodeArtifact) is for specific package types (npm, Maven, PyPI) and would be overkill for general artifacts. Option B (Amazon ECR) is specifically for container images. Option D (Amazon EBS) provides block storage for EC2 instances and is not suitable for pipeline artifact storage.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  40,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_3_43',
  'A development team is managing container images for multiple microservices in Amazon ECR. They need to ensure that old, unused image versions are automatically cleaned up to reduce storage costs, but critical production images must be retained indefinitely. Which Amazon ECR feature should be configured to meet this requirement with the LEAST operational overhead?',
  'mcq',
  '["Implement a manual cleanup script that runs weekly and deletes images based on a predefined tag pattern.","Configure Amazon ECR lifecycle policies with rules to expire images based on age and untagged status, while excluding specific image tags.","Use AWS Lambda functions triggered by Amazon ECR events to identify and delete images that are no longer referenced by active Amazon ECS services.","Set up an AWS CodeBuild project to periodically scan ECR repositories and remove images older than 90 days, integrating with AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store for configuration."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the most efficient and automated way to manage Amazon ECR image lifecycle. ECR lifecycle policies allow you to define rules that automatically expire images based on various criteria, such as image age, status (tagged or untagged), and specific tag prefixes. You can create rules to retain specific tags (e.g., ''prod'', ''stable'') indefinitely while cleaning up others, directly addressing the requirement with minimal operational overhead. Option A (incorrect) involves manual intervention and scripting, which is high operational overhead and prone to human error. Option C (incorrect) uses AWS Lambda, which, while automated, requires developing, deploying, and maintaining custom code, leading to higher operational overhead compared to native ECR lifecycle policies. Option D (incorrect) involves setting up a CodeBuild project, which is also a custom solution requiring more setup and maintenance than built-in ECR lifecycle policies, increasing operational overhead.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  41,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_3_44',
  'A company uses AWS CodeArtifact to store private Maven and npm packages for its internal applications. Developers in different AWS accounts need to consume these packages. What is the MOST secure and scalable way to grant cross-account access to CodeArtifact repositories?',
  'mcq',
  '["Create individual IAM users in the CodeArtifact owner account for each developer in the consumer accounts and share credentials securely.","Configure resource-based policies directly on the CodeArtifact domain and repositories to grant access to specific IAM roles in the consumer accounts.","Generate temporary access credentials for each consumer account using AWS Security Token Service (STS) and distribute them manually.","Set up an AWS CodeBuild project in each consumer account that uses a shared IAM role with full CodeArtifact access to download packages."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the most secure and scalable approach for cross-account access to CodeArtifact. CodeArtifact supports resource-based policies (similar to Amazon S3 bucket policies) at both the domain and repository levels. These policies allow you to explicitly grant permissions to IAM roles or users in other AWS accounts, enabling secure and controlled access without sharing credentials. Option A (incorrect) is highly insecure and violates AWS best practices by sharing IAM user credentials across accounts. Option C (incorrect) involves manual distribution of temporary credentials, which is not scalable or automated for a large number of developers or frequent access needs. Option D (incorrect) is an overly complex and indirect method. While CodeBuild can interact with CodeArtifact, granting full CodeArtifact access to a shared role in CodeBuild is less granular and less secure than using resource-based policies directly on CodeArtifact for consumption.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  42,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_3_45',
  'A DevOps team is building a CI/CD pipeline using AWS CodePipeline. They need to ensure that all artifacts generated by AWS CodeBuild are stored securely and are immutable, preventing any accidental modification or deletion, and supporting auditing requirements. Which strategy should they implement for artifact storage?',
  'mcq',
  '["Store artifacts in an Amazon S3 bucket with versioning enabled and a lifecycle policy to transition old versions to S3 Glacier after 30 days.","Store artifacts in an Amazon S3 bucket with object versioning enabled, S3 Object Lock in compliance mode, and a WORM (Write Once Read Many) retention period.","Use AWS CodeArtifact to store all build artifacts, leveraging its built-in immutability features for package versions.","Store artifacts in an Amazon ECR repository, tagging each build with a unique identifier and enforcing tag immutability."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) provides the strongest guarantees for immutability and auditability. Enabling S3 Object Versioning ensures that every version of an object is preserved. S3 Object Lock in compliance mode prevents objects from being overwritten or deleted by any user, including the root user, for a specified retention period, effectively creating a WORM (Write Once Read Many) store. This directly addresses the requirement for immutable and undeletable artifacts for auditing. Option A (incorrect) enables versioning but does not prevent deletion of current versions or modification if Object Lock is not applied. Transitioning to S3 Glacier is for cost optimization, not immutability guarantees. Option C (incorrect) is for package management (Maven, npm, PyPI), not general build artifacts like ZIP files or compiled binaries from CodeBuild. While CodeArtifact provides immutability for package versions, it''s not the primary service for general CodePipeline artifacts. Option D (incorrect) is specifically for container images. While ECR supports tag immutability, it''s not suitable for storing arbitrary build artifacts like application binaries or deployment packages generated by CodeBuild for non-containerized applications.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  43,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_3_46',
  'A company has a multi-region application deployment and uses AWS CodePipeline for its CI/CD process. They need to ensure that artifacts generated in the primary region are available in a secondary region for disaster recovery and multi-region deployments with minimal latency and consistent access. What is the MOST effective approach for artifact management?',
  'mcq',
  '["Manually copy artifacts from the primary Amazon S3 bucket to the secondary region''s S3 bucket after each pipeline execution.","Configure cross-region replication on the Amazon S3 bucket used by AWS CodePipeline to automatically replicate artifacts to a bucket in the secondary region.","Use AWS CodeArtifact to store artifacts, configuring a separate repository in the secondary region and publishing artifacts to both.","Implement an AWS Lambda function triggered by S3 object creation events to copy artifacts to the secondary region, handling any replication failures."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the most effective and operationally efficient solution. Amazon S3 cross-region replication (CRR) automatically replicates objects uploaded to a source S3 bucket in one AWS Region to a destination S3 bucket in another Region. This ensures that artifacts are consistently available in the secondary region with minimal latency and without requiring manual intervention or custom code, directly supporting disaster recovery and multi-region deployments. Option A (incorrect) involves manual steps, which is prone to error, not scalable, and introduces significant operational overhead. Option C (incorrect) is designed for private package management (Maven, npm, PyPI), not general CodePipeline artifacts like build outputs. While CodeArtifact can be used across regions, it''s not the native solution for replicating CodePipeline''s S3-backed artifacts. Option D (incorrect) requires custom AWS Lambda development and maintenance, increasing operational overhead compared to the native S3 CRR feature, which handles replication, retries, and consistency automatically.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  44,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_3_47',
  'A DevOps team is developing a new microservice that uses container images. They want to ensure that once a container image is pushed to Amazon ECR with a specific tag (e.g., a Git commit SHA), that tag cannot be accidentally overwritten by a subsequent push, which could lead to non-reproducible deployments. How can they enforce this immutability for image tags?',
  'mcq',
  '["Implement a pre-push hook in their Git repository to prevent pushing images with existing tags.","Configure the Amazon ECR repository settings to enable the ''Tag immutability'' feature.","Use AWS CodeBuild to enforce unique image tags by appending a timestamp to every tag before pushing to ECR.","Set up an AWS Lambda function to monitor ECR push events and revert any attempts to overwrite existing tags."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the native and most straightforward way to enforce tag immutability in Amazon ECR. By enabling the ''Tag immutability'' setting on an ECR repository, you prevent image tags from being overwritten. Once an image is pushed with a specific tag, that tag cannot be used again for a different image, ensuring that each tag uniquely identifies a specific image version. This directly addresses the anti-pattern of using mutable container image tags. Option A (incorrect) relies on client-side hooks, which can be bypassed and do not provide a strong, centralized enforcement mechanism. Option C (incorrect) forces unique tags but doesn''t prevent an existing tag from being overwritten if the timestamp logic is flawed or bypassed; it''s a workaround rather than direct enforcement of tag immutability. Option D (incorrect) involves custom Lambda development and maintenance, which is a higher operational overhead and less reliable than using the built-in ECR feature for tag immutability.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  45,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_3_48',
  'A software development team uses AWS CodeArtifact to manage internal Python packages. They need to ensure that all packages consumed by their CI/CD pipelines and development environments are scanned for known vulnerabilities before being used. What is the MOST effective and integrated approach to achieve this?',
  'mcq',
  '["Integrate a third-party vulnerability scanning tool into their AWS CodeBuild projects to scan packages downloaded from CodeArtifact.","Manually download packages from CodeArtifact, scan them with an open-source tool, and then upload only approved packages to a separate, ''safe'' CodeArtifact repository.","Leverage AWS CodeGuru Reviewer to analyze the package source code for security vulnerabilities before publishing to CodeArtifact.","Configure CodeArtifact to integrate with AWS Security Hub, which automatically scans packages for vulnerabilities upon ingestion."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the most practical and integrated approach given current AWS service capabilities. While AWS CodeArtifact itself doesn''t have built-in vulnerability scanning, integrating a third-party vulnerability scanning tool (like Snyk, Mend, or Trivy) directly into the AWS CodeBuild stage of the CI/CD pipeline, *before* packages are consumed or deployed, is a common and effective pattern. This allows for automated scanning as part of the build process. Option B (incorrect) involves significant manual effort, is not scalable, and introduces delays, making it highly inefficient for a CI/CD pipeline. Option C (incorrect) is not applicable. AWS CodeGuru Reviewer is for static code analysis of application source code, not for scanning pre-built binary packages or dependencies for known vulnerabilities. Option D (incorrect) is not a current feature. AWS CodeArtifact does not natively integrate with AWS Security Hub for automatic vulnerability scanning of packages upon ingestion.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  46,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_3_49',
  'A company is migrating its on-premises package repositories to AWS CodeArtifact. They have a large number of existing Maven artifacts that need to be ingested into CodeArtifact. The migration must be efficient, minimize manual effort, and ensure all existing versions are preserved. Which approach is MOST suitable?',
  'mcq',
  '["Manually upload each artifact and its metadata to the CodeArtifact repository using the AWS Management Console.","Develop a custom script using the AWS SDK to iterate through the on-premises repository and programmatically publish artifacts to CodeArtifact.","Configure CodeArtifact to connect to the on-premises repository as an upstream source, allowing packages to be pulled on demand.","Use AWS CodeBuild to create a project that pulls artifacts from the on-premises repository and pushes them to CodeArtifact in a batch process."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the most suitable approach for an efficient and automated bulk migration of existing artifacts while preserving versions. Using the AWS SDK allows for programmatic interaction with CodeArtifact''s `publish-package-version` API, enabling the automation of iterating through existing on-premises artifacts and uploading them in a controlled manner, including their specific versions and metadata. Option A (incorrect) is highly impractical and error-prone for a large number of artifacts due to the manual effort involved. Option C (incorrect) is designed for ongoing consumption of new packages from public repositories or other CodeArtifact repositories, not for bulk migration of existing artifacts from an on-premises source into a new CodeArtifact repository. While it can pull, it doesn''t ''ingest'' the entire historical content directly. Option D (incorrect) could work, but developing a custom script with the AWS SDK offers more direct control over the publishing process, metadata, and error handling for a migration task compared to orchestrating it purely through CodeBuild, which is more geared towards CI/CD builds.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  47,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_3_50',
  'A DevOps team manages several containerized applications. They need to ensure that their Amazon ECR repositories are configured for high availability and disaster recovery across multiple AWS Regions. What is the recommended approach to achieve this for their container images?',
  'mcq',
  '["Manually push each container image to separate ECR repositories in different AWS Regions after every build.","Configure Amazon ECR replication rules to automatically replicate images to designated ECR repositories in other AWS Regions.","Store container images in an Amazon S3 bucket and use S3 Cross-Region Replication to copy them to other regions.","Utilize AWS CodePipeline to orchestrate the deployment of container images to ECR in multiple regions sequentially."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the native and most efficient solution for replicating Amazon ECR container images across regions for high availability and disaster recovery. ECR replication rules allow you to automatically replicate images from a source ECR repository to a destination ECR repository in another AWS Region or even another account. This ensures images are consistently available in multiple regions without manual effort. Option A (incorrect) involves manual steps, which is inefficient, error-prone, and not scalable for continuous integration. Option C (incorrect) is not a valid approach. Amazon ECR is the dedicated service for container image management; storing images directly in Amazon S3 is not how ECR operates and would lose all ECR-specific features like vulnerability scanning, lifecycle policies, and integration with container services. Option D (incorrect) describes orchestrating deployments, not the underlying replication of the images themselves. While CodePipeline can deploy images to multiple regions, it doesn''t inherently replicate the ECR repository content.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  48,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_3_51',
  'A company uses AWS CodeArtifact for private npm package management. Developers frequently publish new package versions. The security team requires that older, unused package versions are automatically removed from CodeArtifact to reduce the attack surface and storage costs, while ensuring that actively used versions are never deleted. How can this be achieved with the LEAST operational overhead?',
  'mcq',
  '["Implement a custom AWS Lambda function to periodically list CodeArtifact package versions and delete those not referenced in recent build logs.","Manually review and delete old package versions from the CodeArtifact repository on a monthly basis.","Configure CodeArtifact package retention policies to automatically delete package versions after a specified number of days or when a certain number of newer versions exist.","Use an AWS CodeBuild project to run a script that identifies and removes package versions older than 180 days from CodeArtifact."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is the most efficient and automated way to manage package versions in CodeArtifact. CodeArtifact provides package retention policies that allow you to define rules for automatically deleting older package versions based on criteria such as age (e.g., ''delete versions older than 90 days'') or count (e.g., ''keep the latest 10 versions''). This directly addresses the requirement to clean up unused versions while protecting active ones, with minimal operational overhead. Option A (incorrect) requires custom development, deployment, and maintenance of a Lambda function, increasing operational overhead. Option B (incorrect) is a manual process, which is not scalable, error-prone, and has high operational overhead. Option D (incorrect) involves setting up and maintaining a CodeBuild project for cleanup, which is also a custom solution requiring more setup and maintenance than the native CodeArtifact retention policies.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  49,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_3_52',
  'A DevOps team is setting up an AWS CodePipeline for a new application. The pipeline needs to store build artifacts, such as compiled code and deployment packages, in a highly durable and available manner. The artifacts must be accessible by subsequent stages in the pipeline, potentially across different AWS Regions. Which service is the most appropriate for storing these pipeline artifacts?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CodeArtifact, configured with a private repository for all build outputs.","Amazon ECR, used to store all compiled binaries and deployment scripts.","Amazon S3, configured with versioning and potentially cross-region replication.","AWS CodeCommit, storing artifacts as large binary files within the Git repository."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is the standard and most appropriate service for storing AWS CodePipeline artifacts. CodePipeline uses Amazon S3 buckets to store input and output artifacts between stages. Amazon S3 provides high durability, availability, and scalability. With versioning enabled, it ensures that previous versions of artifacts are retained, and cross-region replication can be configured for multi-region pipeline deployments or disaster recovery. Option A (incorrect) is designed for private package management (e.g., Maven, npm, PyPI) and is not the primary service for general CodePipeline build artifacts like compiled code or deployment packages. Option B (incorrect) is specifically for container images and is not suitable for storing arbitrary build outputs. Option D (incorrect) is a version control service. Storing large binary artifacts directly in a Git repository like CodeCommit is an anti-pattern, as it bloats the repository, slows down cloning, and is not optimized for artifact storage and retrieval in a pipeline context.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  50,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_3_53',
  'A DevOps team is managing container images for their microservices in Amazon ECR. They want to automatically clean up old, untagged images to reduce storage costs and maintain repository hygiene. Which Amazon ECR feature should they configure?',
  'mcq',
  '["Image scanning with AWS Security Hub integration","Repository policies for access control","Lifecycle policies for image management","Cross-region replication for high availability"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) refers to Amazon ECR lifecycle policies, which allow you to specify rules to automatically expire or delete images based on criteria such as age, tag status (e.g., untagged), or image count. This directly addresses the requirement to clean up old, untagged images to reduce storage costs and maintain repository hygiene. Option A (Image scanning with AWS Security Hub integration) is used for identifying vulnerabilities in container images, not for managing their lifecycle. Option B (Repository policies for access control) are used to define who can access the repository and perform actions, not for automated cleanup. Option D (Cross-region replication for high availability) is for replicating images to other regions for disaster recovery or global access, not for cleaning up old images.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  51,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_3_54',
  'What is the primary purpose of AWS CodeArtifact in a CI/CD pipeline?',
  'mcq',
  '["To store and manage container images for deployment.","To provide a secure, scalable, and cost-effective private package repository for application dependencies.","To automate the build and test processes for source code.","To orchestrate the end-to-end release process for software delivery."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) accurately describes AWS CodeArtifact''s primary purpose: to serve as a private package repository. It supports popular package managers like npm, Maven, and PyPI, allowing developers to store, publish, and share software packages securely within their organization. Option A (To store and manage container images for deployment) is the primary function of Amazon ECR. Option C (To automate the build and test processes for source code) is the role of AWS CodeBuild. Option D (To orchestrate the end-to-end release process for software delivery) is the function of AWS CodePipeline.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  52,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_3_55',
  'A development team uses AWS CodePipeline to automate their software releases. They need to store build artifacts, such as compiled code and deployment manifests, securely and durably between pipeline stages. Which AWS service is best suited for this purpose?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon EBS volumes attached to CodeBuild instances.","AWS CodeCommit repositories for version control.","Amazon S3 buckets configured for artifact storage.","AWS CodeArtifact for package management."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) states that Amazon S3 buckets are configured for artifact storage. AWS CodePipeline uses Amazon S3 as the default and recommended artifact store for passing build outputs, deployment packages, and other artifacts between different stages of a pipeline. S3 provides high durability, availability, and security for these artifacts. Option A (Amazon EBS volumes attached to CodeBuild instances) are ephemeral storage tied to specific instances and are not suitable for persistent artifact storage across pipeline stages. Option B (AWS CodeCommit repositories for version control) are for source code management, not for storing compiled build artifacts. Option D (AWS CodeArtifact for package management) is for managing software packages and dependencies, not for general pipeline artifacts like build outputs.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  53,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_3_56',
  'To ensure reproducibility and enable reliable rollbacks in a CI/CD pipeline, a DevOps engineer wants to implement an immutable artifact strategy for container images. Which approach aligns with this strategy?',
  'mcq',
  '["Using the ''latest'' tag for all container images in Amazon ECR.","Storing container images in a local Docker registry on development machines.","Tagging container images with a unique identifier, such as a Git commit SHA or build number.","Manually updating container images in Amazon ECR after each successful deployment."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) describes tagging container images with a unique identifier like a Git commit SHA or build number. This ensures that each image version is uniquely identifiable and immutable, making rollbacks reliable and deployments reproducible. This directly addresses the requirement for an immutable artifact strategy. Option A (Using the ''latest'' tag for all container images in Amazon ECR) is an anti-pattern (ap-1.3-001) because mutable tags make rollbacks and audits impossible, as ''latest'' can refer to different images over time. Option B (Storing container images in a local Docker registry on development machines) lacks centralized management, scalability, and security required for a production CI/CD pipeline. Option D (Manually updating container images in Amazon ECR after each successful deployment) introduces manual intervention, which is against CI/CD automation principles and does not guarantee immutability or reproducibility.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  54,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_3_57',
  'A company is using Amazon ECR to store container images. Developers frequently push new image versions, and old images are consuming excessive storage. The team wants to reduce costs by automatically deleting images older than 30 days, except for those tagged as ''production''. Which ECR feature should be configured?',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure a repository policy to deny pushes of old images.","Implement a custom AWS Lambda function to periodically clean up images.","Apply an Amazon ECR lifecycle policy with appropriate rules.","Enable versioning on the ECR repository to manage image history."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is to apply an Amazon ECR lifecycle policy with appropriate rules. ECR lifecycle policies are designed specifically for this purpose, allowing automated cleanup of images based on age, tag status, or count, with exceptions for specific tags like ''production''. Option A (Configure a repository policy to deny pushes of old images) is incorrect; repository policies control access permissions, not image lifecycle management. Option B (Implement a custom AWS Lambda function to periodically clean up images) is a manual and more operationally intensive approach when a native ECR feature (lifecycle policies) exists for this exact use case, increasing operational overhead. Option D (Enable versioning on the ECR repository to manage image history) is not a feature of ECR; image versions are managed by tags.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  55,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_3_58',
  'A software development team uses Node.js and Java for their microservices and needs a centralized, private repository to manage their npm and Maven dependencies. They want to integrate this repository seamlessly with AWS CodeBuild. Which AWS service should they use?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon S3 for storing package archives.","AWS CodeCommit for source code hosting.","AWS CodeArtifact for private package management.","Amazon ECR for container image storage."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is AWS CodeArtifact, which is specifically designed to provide a secure, scalable, and cost-effective private package repository for various package types, including npm and Maven. It integrates directly with AWS CodeBuild, allowing builds to fetch dependencies from and publish packages to CodeArtifact. Option A (Amazon S3 for storing package archives) could be used for storage, but it lacks the native package manager integration and features (like upstream repositories) that CodeArtifact provides, requiring more custom setup. Option B (AWS CodeCommit for source code hosting) is for version control of source code, not for managing compiled packages or dependencies. Option D (Amazon ECR for container image storage) is for Docker container images, not for language-specific packages like npm or Maven.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  56,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_3_59',
  'A DevOps team is building a multi-region CI/CD pipeline using AWS CodePipeline. They need to ensure that artifacts generated in one region are available for deployment stages in a different region with minimal latency and high durability. How should they configure artifact storage?',
  'mcq',
  '["Store artifacts in a single Amazon S3 bucket in the primary region and manually copy them.","Utilize Amazon S3 for artifact storage with cross-region replication enabled.","Use AWS CodeArtifact to store all pipeline artifacts across regions.","Attach Amazon EBS volumes to CodeBuild instances in each region for artifact storage."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) suggests utilizing Amazon S3 for artifact storage with cross-region replication enabled. AWS CodePipeline primarily uses Amazon S3 for storing artifacts. By enabling cross-region replication on the S3 bucket, artifacts are automatically and asynchronously copied to a bucket in a different region, ensuring high durability and availability for multi-region deployments with reduced latency compared to manual copies. Option A (Store artifacts in a single Amazon S3 bucket in the primary region and manually copy them) would introduce manual overhead and potential delays, failing the ''minimal latency'' and ''high durability'' requirements for an automated pipeline. Option C (Use AWS CodeArtifact to store all pipeline artifacts across regions) is incorrect; CodeArtifact is for software packages (npm, Maven, PyPI), not general pipeline artifacts like build outputs or deployment manifests. Option D (Attach Amazon EBS volumes to CodeBuild instances in each region for artifact storage) is unsuitable as EBS volumes are block storage tied to specific EC2 instances and are not designed for shared, cross-region artifact storage in a pipeline.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  57,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_3_60',
  'A CI/CD pipeline uses AWS CodeBuild to create Docker images. To ensure that every deployment is traceable and reproducible, the DevOps team must prevent accidental overwrites of image versions in Amazon ECR. Which artifact strategy should they enforce?',
  'mcq',
  '["Use the ''latest'' tag for all images to simplify deployments.","Implement a custom script to periodically delete old images from ECR.","Tag images with a unique, immutable identifier such as a Git commit hash or build ID.","Configure Amazon ECR repository policies to restrict image pushes to specific IAM roles."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is to tag images with a unique, immutable identifier such as a Git commit hash or build ID. This practice ensures that each image version is distinct and cannot be overwritten, making deployments fully traceable and reproducible, which is a core principle of immutable artifacts. Option A (Use the ''latest'' tag for all images to simplify deployments) is an anti-pattern (ap-1.3-001). Using mutable tags like ''latest'' means the content of the tag can change, leading to non-reproducible builds and making rollbacks unreliable. Option B (Implement a custom script to periodically delete old images from ECR) focuses on cleanup, not on ensuring the immutability or uniqueness of existing image versions. Option D (Configure Amazon ECR repository policies to restrict image pushes to specific IAM roles) is a security measure for access control, not an artifact strategy for ensuring image immutability or versioning.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  58,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_3_61',
  'A DevOps team wants to store Python packages for internal use in a private repository that integrates with pip. Which AWS service is designed for this requirement?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon S3","AWS CodeArtifact","Amazon ECR","AWS CodeCommit"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is AWS CodeArtifact. CodeArtifact is a fully managed artifact repository service that makes it easy for organizations to securely store, publish, and share software packages used in their development process. It supports popular package managers like pip (for Python), npm (for Node.js), and Maven (for Java). Option A (Amazon S3) is object storage and while it can store files, it doesn''t provide the native package manager integration and features of CodeArtifact. Option C (Amazon ECR) is for Docker container images. Option D (AWS CodeCommit) is for source code version control.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  59,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_3_62',
  'A company needs to ensure that their CI/CD pipeline artifacts stored in Amazon S3 are highly available and resilient to regional outages. What S3 feature should be enabled for the artifact bucket?',
  'mcq',
  '["S3 Versioning","S3 Lifecycle policies","S3 Cross-Region Replication (CRR)","S3 Storage Class Analysis"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is S3 Cross-Region Replication (CRR). CRR automatically and asynchronously replicates objects to a destination bucket in a different AWS Region, providing high availability and disaster recovery capabilities for pipeline artifacts. This ensures resilience to regional outages. Option A (S3 Versioning) keeps multiple versions of an object in the same bucket, protecting against accidental deletions or overwrites, but doesn''t provide cross-region resilience. Option B (S3 Lifecycle policies) automate the movement or deletion of objects based on age or other criteria, primarily for cost optimization and data retention, not cross-region availability. Option D (S3 Storage Class Analysis) helps analyze storage access patterns to optimize storage costs by identifying data that can be moved to a different storage class.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  60,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_3',
  'medium'
);

