INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_4_31',
  'Which AWS CodeDeploy deployment type replaces the entire set of instances in a deployment group with new instances that have the latest application revision, while maintaining the old instances until the new ones are fully operational?',
  'mcq',
  '["Blue/green deployment","In-place deployment","Rolling update deployment","All-at-once deployment"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (Blue/green deployment) is the correct deployment type. In a blue/green deployment, CodeDeploy provisions a completely new set of instances (the green environment) with the new application version. Traffic is then shifted from the old (blue) environment to the new (green) environment. The old environment is kept running for a specified period, allowing for easy rollback if issues arise, and then terminated. Option B (In-place deployment) updates the application on the existing instances. Option C (Rolling update deployment) is a strategy where instances are updated in batches, which CodeDeploy can facilitate but ''blue/green'' is the specific term for replacing the entire environment. Option D (All-at-once deployment) updates all instances simultaneously, leading to downtime.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  61,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_4',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_4_32',
  'A development team wants to deploy a new version of an AWS Lambda function with minimal risk. They need to gradually shift traffic to the new version and automatically roll back if any issues are detected. Which AWS service and feature should they leverage?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling with health checks.","AWS Lambda aliases with CodeDeploy traffic shifting.","Amazon S3 versioning with CloudFront distributions.","AWS CodeBuild with manual deployment."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (AWS Lambda aliases with CodeDeploy traffic shifting) is the correct solution. AWS Lambda supports versioning and aliases, which, when combined with AWS CodeDeploy, enable sophisticated traffic shifting strategies (like linear or canary deployments). CodeDeploy can automatically shift traffic to a new Lambda function version and monitor CloudWatch alarms to trigger an automatic rollback if errors occur. Option A (Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling with health checks) is for EC2 instances, not Lambda functions. Option C (Amazon S3 versioning with CloudFront distributions) is for static content and not for deploying serverless functions with traffic shifting. Option D (AWS CodeBuild with manual deployment) is a build service and manual deployment lacks automation and rollback capabilities.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  62,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_4',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_4_33',
  'A company is deploying a containerized application to Amazon ECS using AWS CodeDeploy. They require a deployment strategy that minimizes downtime and provides an easy rollback mechanism. Which CodeDeploy deployment type is best suited for this requirement?',
  'mcq',
  '["In-place deployment","Rolling update deployment","Blue/green deployment","All-at-once deployment"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Blue/green deployment) is best suited for minimizing downtime and providing an easy rollback mechanism for containerized applications on Amazon ECS. CodeDeploy''s blue/green deployment for ECS creates a new task set (green environment) with the updated application, shifts traffic gradually or instantly, and keeps the old task set (blue environment) running. If issues arise, traffic can be quickly rolled back to the blue environment. Option A (In-place deployment) is not typically used for ECS and would involve stopping and starting containers, causing downtime. Option B (Rolling update deployment) updates containers in batches, which can still cause brief periods of reduced capacity or issues during the update. Option D (All-at-once deployment) would cause significant downtime as all containers are updated simultaneously.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  63,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_4',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_4_34',
  'In AWS CodeDeploy, which deployment type updates the application on the existing instances in a deployment group by stopping the application, installing the latest revision, and then restarting the application?',
  'mcq',
  '["Blue/green deployment","Canary deployment","In-place deployment","Linear deployment"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (In-place deployment) is the correct deployment type. In an in-place deployment, the application on each instance in the deployment group is stopped, the latest application revision is installed, and the application is then restarted. This typically results in some downtime for the instances during the update process. Option A (Blue/green deployment) involves provisioning new instances. Options B (Canary deployment) and D (Linear deployment) are traffic-shifting strategies, often associated with blue/green or Lambda deployments, not the direct update of existing instances.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  64,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_4',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_4_35',
  'A DevOps team is deploying a new version of an Amazon ECS service. They want to ensure that if the new deployment introduces errors, it can be quickly reverted. Which CodeDeploy feature should be configured to enable automated rollbacks for ECS deployments?',
  'mcq',
  '["Manual approval stages in CodePipeline.","Automated rollback triggers based on CloudWatch alarms.","Disabling health checks on the ECS service.","Using mutable image tags in Amazon ECR."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (Automated rollback triggers based on CloudWatch alarms) is the correct feature. AWS CodeDeploy can be configured with rollback triggers that monitor Amazon CloudWatch alarms (e.g., for error rates, CPU utilization, or latency). If an alarm breaches its threshold during or after an ECS deployment, CodeDeploy can automatically initiate a rollback to the previous stable version. Option A (Manual approval stages in CodePipeline) requires human intervention and is not an automated rollback. Option C (Disabling health checks on the ECS service) would prevent detection of issues and is counterproductive. Option D (Using mutable image tags in Amazon ECR) is an anti-pattern (ap-1.3-001) that makes reliable rollbacks difficult due to lack of reproducibility.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  65,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_4',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_4_36',
  'What are CodeDeploy lifecycle hooks primarily used for in an EC2/on-premises deployment?',
  'mcq',
  '["To define the source code repository.","To specify the build environment for the application.","To run scripts at various stages of the deployment process.","To configure network load balancing for the application."]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'Option C (To run scripts at various stages of the deployment process) is the primary use of CodeDeploy lifecycle hooks. Lifecycle hooks are predefined events during a deployment (e.g., `BeforeInstall`, `AfterInstall`, `ApplicationStop`, `ApplicationStart`) where you can specify scripts to be run. These scripts can perform tasks like installing dependencies, configuring application settings, or validating the deployment. Option A (To define the source code repository) is for CodeCommit or other source control. Option B (To specify the build environment for the application) is for CodeBuild. Option D (To configure network load balancing for the application) is typically done with Elastic Load Balancing services.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  66,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_4',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_4_37',
  'A company needs to update an application running on Amazon EC2 instances with minimal downtime. They want to replace the existing instances with new ones that have the updated application, ensuring that the old instances remain available until the new ones are fully healthy. Which CodeDeploy deployment strategy should be chosen?',
  'mcq',
  '["Blue/green deployment","In-place deployment","All-at-once deployment","Rolling update deployment"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (Blue/green deployment) is the ideal CodeDeploy strategy for minimizing downtime during EC2 instance updates. It involves deploying the new application version to a completely new set of instances (green environment), performing validation, and then shifting traffic from the old (blue) environment to the new one. The old environment is kept running as a fallback. Option B (In-place deployment) updates existing instances, causing downtime during the update. Option C (All-at-once deployment) updates all instances simultaneously, leading to significant downtime. Option D (Rolling update deployment) updates instances in batches, which reduces downtime compared to all-at-once but can still have periods of reduced capacity or potential issues during the update phase.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  67,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_4',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_4_38',
  'What is a ''deployment group'' in AWS CodeDeploy?',
  'mcq',
  '["A collection of AWS CodePipeline stages.","A set of individual instances, Auto Scaling groups, or Amazon ECS services targeted for deployment.","A group of AWS CodeBuild projects that run concurrently.","A logical grouping of AWS Lambda functions for traffic shifting."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (A set of individual instances, Auto Scaling groups, or Amazon ECS services targeted for deployment) is the correct definition. A deployment group in AWS CodeDeploy specifies the target compute instances (Amazon EC2 instances, on-premises instances), Auto Scaling groups, or Amazon ECS services where an application revision will be deployed. Option A (A collection of AWS CodePipeline stages) describes a pipeline. Option C (A group of AWS CodeBuild projects that run concurrently) describes batch builds in CodeBuild. Option D (A logical grouping of AWS Lambda functions for traffic shifting) is related to Lambda aliases and traffic shifting, but a deployment group is a broader concept for CodeDeploy targets.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  68,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_4',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_4_39',
  'Which deployment strategy in AWS CodeDeploy updates applications on existing instances, one at a time or in batches, without provisioning new instances?',
  'mcq',
  '["Blue/green deployment","Canary deployment","In-place deployment","Linear deployment"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (In-place deployment) is the correct strategy. In an in-place deployment, CodeDeploy updates the application on the existing instances in the deployment group. This typically involves stopping the application, installing the new revision, and then restarting the application on the same instances. Option A (Blue/green deployment) provisions a new set of instances. Options B (Canary deployment) and D (Linear deployment) are traffic-shifting strategies, often used with blue/green or Lambda, but not the core mechanism of updating existing instances directly.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  69,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_4',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_4_40',
  'A company wants to deploy a new version of their application to Amazon EC2 instances with zero downtime. They need to ensure that the old version remains fully operational while the new version is being deployed and validated. Which CodeDeploy deployment type should they choose?',
  'mcq',
  '["In-place deployment","All-at-once deployment","Blue/green deployment","Rolling update deployment"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Blue/green deployment) is the correct choice for zero-downtime deployments to Amazon EC2 instances. With blue/green, a completely new environment (green) is provisioned with the new application version. Traffic is then shifted from the old (blue) environment to the new one, and the blue environment is kept as a fallback. This ensures continuous availability. Option A (In-place deployment) updates existing instances, causing downtime. Option B (All-at-once deployment) updates all instances simultaneously, leading to downtime. Option D (Rolling update deployment) updates instances in batches, which reduces downtime but might still have brief periods of reduced capacity or issues during the update.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  70,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_4',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_4_41',
  'A DevOps team wants to implement a deployment strategy for their containerized application on Amazon ECS that gradually shifts traffic to the new version while closely monitoring its performance. If any issues are detected, traffic should automatically revert to the old version. Which CodeDeploy deployment type supports this?',
  'mcq',
  '["In-place deployment","All-at-once deployment","Blue/green deployment with traffic shifting and automated rollbacks","Rolling update deployment"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Blue/green deployment with traffic shifting and automated rollbacks) is the correct CodeDeploy deployment type for this scenario. CodeDeploy''s blue/green deployments for Amazon ECS allow you to create a new task set (green), gradually shift traffic from the old (blue) task set, and integrate with CloudWatch alarms to automatically roll back traffic to the blue environment if issues are detected. This minimizes risk and downtime. Option A (In-place deployment) is not typically used for ECS and doesn''t support traffic shifting. Option B (All-at-once deployment) shifts all traffic immediately, which is risky. Option D (Rolling update deployment) updates containers in batches but lacks the robust traffic shifting and automated rollback capabilities of blue/green for ECS.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  71,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_4',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_4_42',
  'A DevOps team needs to deploy a serverless application consisting of AWS Lambda functions and Amazon API Gateway endpoints. They want to use AWS CodeDeploy for controlled deployments, specifically to gradually shift traffic to new Lambda function versions. Which deployment type and configuration should they use?',
  'mcq',
  '["Use an in-place deployment strategy with CodeDeploy, updating the Lambda function code directly.","Configure a blue/green deployment strategy with CodeDeploy, specifying a Lambda deployment type to shift traffic via Lambda aliases.","Manually update the Lambda function code and API Gateway integration, then monitor for errors before promoting.","Utilize AWS CodeBuild to deploy new Lambda versions, relying on its built-in traffic shifting capabilities."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the recommended approach for controlled serverless deployments with AWS CodeDeploy. CodeDeploy supports blue/green deployments specifically for AWS Lambda functions, leveraging Lambda aliases and versioning. This allows you to deploy a new version, test it, and then gradually shift traffic (e.g., linear, canary) from the old version to the new version, providing automated rollbacks if issues occur. Option A (incorrect) is an in-place deployment, which is generally not recommended for Lambda as it doesn''t provide the same level of safety, traffic shifting control, or easy rollback as blue/green. Option C (incorrect) involves manual steps, which is not automated and prone to human error, violating CI/CD principles. Option D (incorrect) is incorrect. AWS CodeBuild is for building and testing code, not for deploying Lambda functions with traffic shifting capabilities. CodeDeploy is the service designed for this purpose.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  72,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_4',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_4_43',
  'A company is deploying a critical microservice on Amazon ECS using the Fargate launch type. They need to implement a deployment strategy that minimizes downtime, allows for quick rollback, and enables A/B testing of new features with a small percentage of users before a full rollout. Which CodeDeploy deployment strategy is MOST suitable?',
  'mcq',
  '["In-place deployment, updating the existing ECS service tasks directly.","Rolling update deployment, gradually replacing old tasks with new ones within the ECS service.","Blue/green deployment, leveraging CodeDeploy''s integration with Amazon ECS and Application Load Balancer.","All-at-once deployment, replacing all existing tasks with new ones simultaneously."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is the most suitable CodeDeploy deployment strategy for this scenario. Blue/green deployments with CodeDeploy for Amazon ECS (Fargate or EC2 launch types) provide zero-downtime deployments by provisioning a completely new set of tasks (''green'' environment) alongside the existing (''blue'') environment. Traffic is then gradually shifted from blue to green via an Application Load Balancer, allowing for testing, quick rollbacks, and advanced traffic shifting patterns (e.g., canary, linear) for A/B testing. Option A (incorrect) is not a supported CodeDeploy strategy for ECS. Option B (incorrect) is the default ECS deployment strategy but causes downtime during task replacement and does not offer the same level of rollback control or A/B testing capabilities as blue/green. Option D (incorrect) would cause significant downtime and is not suitable for critical microservices.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  73,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_4',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_4_44',
  'A DevOps team needs to deploy a new version of an application hosted on Amazon EC2 instances. They want to minimize the risk of deployment failures impacting all users and ensure that if issues arise, traffic can be quickly reverted to the previous stable version. They also need to perform extensive testing on the new version before it receives full production traffic. Which CodeDeploy deployment strategy should be chosen?',
  'mcq',
  '["In-place deployment, updating agents on existing instances.","Rolling update deployment, replacing instances one by one.","Blue/green deployment, provisioning new instances and shifting traffic.","All-at-once deployment, updating all instances simultaneously."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is the ideal CodeDeploy strategy for this scenario. Blue/green deployments provision a completely new set of instances with the new application version (''green'' environment) alongside the existing (''blue'') environment. Traffic is then shifted from blue to green, allowing for extensive testing on the green environment before it receives full production traffic. This minimizes downtime, enables quick rollbacks by simply reverting traffic to the blue environment, and reduces risk. Option A (incorrect) updates the application directly on existing instances, which can cause downtime and makes rollbacks more complex. Option B (incorrect) replaces instances one by one, which minimizes downtime but doesn''t allow for pre-traffic testing on a separate environment or instant rollback to the entire previous environment. Option D (incorrect) updates all instances at once, leading to significant downtime and high risk if the deployment fails.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  74,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_4',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_4_45',
  'A development team is deploying a containerized application to an Amazon ECS service. They want to ensure that the deployment process gradually shifts traffic to the new task set, allowing for monitoring and automatic rollback if errors are detected during the shift. Which CodeDeploy deployment type and traffic shifting option should they configure?',
  'mcq',
  '["ECS (rolling update) deployment type with a linear traffic shifting option.","ECS (blue/green) deployment type with a canary traffic shifting option.","EC2/On-premises deployment type with an all-at-once traffic shifting option.","AWS Lambda deployment type with a gradual traffic shifting option."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the appropriate choice. For Amazon ECS, AWS CodeDeploy supports the blue/green deployment type. Within this type, you can configure different traffic shifting options, including ''Canary'', which shifts a small percentage of traffic to the new version first, monitors it, and then gradually increases the percentage. This allows for early detection of issues and automatic rollback, meeting the requirement for gradual traffic shifting with monitoring. Option A (incorrect) refers to the default ECS rolling update, which is not managed by CodeDeploy and doesn''t offer the same granular traffic shifting controls or automated rollback capabilities. Option C (incorrect) is for EC2 instances or on-premises servers, not ECS. Option D (incorrect) is for AWS Lambda functions, not ECS services.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  75,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_4',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_4_46',
  'A company needs to deploy a new version of an application to a fleet of Amazon EC2 instances. The deployment must be performed with zero downtime, and the new version should be thoroughly tested in a production-like environment before receiving any customer traffic. If tests fail, the deployment should be aborted, and the old version should remain active. Which CodeDeploy deployment strategy best meets these requirements?',
  'mcq',
  '["In-place deployment with a `HalfAtATime` configuration.","Rolling update deployment with a `OneAtATime` configuration.","Blue/green deployment, leveraging a load balancer to shift traffic.","All-at-once deployment, updating all instances simultaneously after a manual approval."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is the optimal strategy for zero-downtime deployments with pre-traffic testing and easy rollback. In a blue/green deployment, a new environment (''green'') is provisioned with the new application version alongside the existing (''blue'') environment. All testing can be performed on the green environment before any traffic is shifted. If tests fail, the green environment can be terminated without impacting the blue. Once validated, traffic is shifted, and the old blue environment is decommissioned. Option A (incorrect) involves updating existing instances, which can lead to downtime and complex rollbacks. Option B (incorrect) replaces instances one by one, minimizing downtime but not allowing for full pre-traffic testing on a separate environment or instant rollback of the entire environment. Option D (incorrect) would cause significant downtime and is high-risk.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  76,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_4',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_4_47',
  'A DevOps team is deploying a serverless application to AWS Lambda. They want to ensure that the new Lambda function version is deployed without any downtime and that traffic is gradually shifted to the new version, allowing for real-time monitoring and automatic rollback if errors occur. Which AWS service combination provides this capability?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CodeBuild for deploying Lambda functions and Amazon CloudWatch for monitoring.","AWS CodeDeploy with a Lambda deployment type, integrated with Amazon CloudWatch alarms.","AWS CodePipeline with a manual approval stage before updating the Lambda function alias.","Amazon API Gateway canary release deployments configured manually."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the most comprehensive and automated solution. AWS CodeDeploy supports blue/green deployments for AWS Lambda functions. It integrates with Lambda aliases and versions to shift traffic gradually (e.g., linear or canary options). By associating Amazon CloudWatch alarms with the CodeDeploy deployment group, you can automatically monitor for errors (e.g., invocation errors, throttles) during the traffic shift and trigger an automatic rollback to the previous stable version if thresholds are breached. Option A (incorrect) uses CodeBuild for deployment, which doesn''t provide native traffic shifting or rollback capabilities for Lambda. Option C (incorrect) provides manual approval but not automated traffic shifting, monitoring, or rollback for Lambda. Option D (incorrect) is for API Gateway, not directly for Lambda function versions, and would require manual configuration and monitoring for automated rollback.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  77,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_4',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_4_48',
  'A company is deploying a serverless application that uses AWS Lambda. They need to ensure that the deployment process minimizes the risk of introducing errors into production and provides an easy way to revert to a previous version if issues are detected. Which deployment strategy for Lambda functions is considered a best practice?',
  'mcq',
  '["Directly update the `$LATEST` version of the Lambda function with new code.","Use AWS CodeDeploy with a Lambda deployment type to manage traffic shifting between versions using aliases.","Manually create a new Lambda function for each deployment and update API Gateway to point to the new function.","Implement a custom script to deploy new Lambda versions and then use CloudWatch alarms to trigger a separate script for rollback."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) represents the best practice for deploying AWS Lambda functions. AWS CodeDeploy provides a Lambda deployment type that enables controlled, phased rollouts (e.g., linear, canary) by shifting traffic between different Lambda function versions using aliases. This strategy minimizes risk, allows for monitoring during the shift, and provides automated rollback capabilities if issues are detected, ensuring a safe deployment process. Option A (incorrect) is an anti-pattern. Directly updating the `$LATEST` version can cause downtime, makes rollbacks difficult, and doesn''t allow for gradual traffic shifting. Option C (incorrect) is a manual, error-prone process that lacks automation and proper traffic management. Option D (incorrect) involves custom scripting for both deployment and rollback, increasing operational overhead and complexity compared to CodeDeploy''s native capabilities.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  78,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_4',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_4_49',
  'A company is deploying a new version of its application to Amazon EC2 instances using AWS CodeDeploy. They want to ensure that if the deployment fails at any point, the system automatically reverts to the previous stable application version to minimize impact. What is the most effective way to configure this automatic rollback?',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure the CodeDeploy deployment group to automatically roll back if a deployment fails or if specified Amazon CloudWatch alarms are triggered.","Implement a custom script in the `AfterInstall` lifecycle hook of the AppSpec file to check for errors and manually trigger a rollback.","Set up an AWS Lambda function to monitor CodeDeploy deployment events and invoke a rollback if a failure status is detected.","Use AWS CodePipeline''s manual approval stage to allow an operator to initiate a rollback if an issue is observed."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the most effective and native way to configure automatic rollbacks in AWS CodeDeploy. CodeDeploy deployment groups have built-in functionality to automatically roll back if a deployment fails (e.g., an instance fails to install the application) or if specific Amazon CloudWatch alarms (e.g., high error rates, increased latency) are triggered during or after the deployment. This directly addresses anti-pattern [ap-1.1-002]. Option B (incorrect) requires custom scripting and manual error handling, which is less robust and more complex than CodeDeploy''s native rollback features. Option C (incorrect) involves custom Lambda development and maintenance, increasing operational overhead. Option D (incorrect) requires manual intervention, which contradicts the requirement for *automatic* rollback.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  79,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_4',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_4_50',
  'A DevOps team is deploying a new version of a containerized application to an Amazon ECS service using AWS CodeDeploy. They need to ensure that the deployment process is immutable, meaning that existing tasks are never modified in place, and new tasks are always created with the new image. Which deployment type naturally enforces this immutable deployment pattern?',
  'mcq',
  '["In-place deployment, updating the container image on running tasks.","Rolling update deployment, replacing existing tasks with new ones sequentially.","Blue/green deployment, creating an entirely new set of tasks for the new version.","All-at-once deployment, stopping all old tasks and starting all new tasks simultaneously."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) naturally enforces an immutable deployment pattern. In a blue/green deployment with AWS CodeDeploy for ECS, an entirely new set of tasks (the ''green'' environment) is provisioned with the new application version. The old tasks (the ''blue'' environment) remain untouched until traffic is fully shifted and the green environment is validated. This ensures that no existing tasks are modified, and every deployment creates a fresh, immutable environment. Option A (incorrect) is not a supported CodeDeploy strategy for ECS and would violate immutability by modifying existing tasks. Option B (incorrect) replaces tasks sequentially, but the *process* itself involves replacing, not necessarily creating an entirely separate, immutable environment before any traffic shift. Option D (incorrect) replaces all tasks, but it doesn''t provide the separate, immutable environment for testing and quick rollback that blue/green does.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  80,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_4',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_4_51',
  'Which AWS CodeDeploy deployment type replaces the entire set of instances in the original environment with a new set of instances that have the latest application version, typically involving a load balancer to shift traffic?',
  'mcq',
  '["In-place deployment","Rolling update deployment","Blue/green deployment","Canary deployment"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is Blue/green deployment. In a blue/green deployment, a completely new environment (green) is provisioned with the new application version, while the old environment (blue) remains active. Traffic is then shifted from the blue to the green environment, often using a load balancer. This strategy minimizes downtime and provides an easy rollback mechanism. Option A (In-place deployment) updates the application on the existing instances, which can lead to downtime or reduced capacity during the update. Option B (Rolling update deployment) is a type of in-place deployment where instances are updated in batches, but it doesn''t involve provisioning a completely new environment. Option D (Canary deployment) is a strategy where a small subset of traffic is routed to the new version before a full rollout, often a component of a blue/green strategy, but ''blue/green'' describes the full environment swap.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  81,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_4',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_4_52',
  'A DevOps team wants to deploy a new version of an application to Amazon EC2 instances with zero downtime. They need to provision a new set of instances with the updated application and then redirect traffic to them, keeping the old instances available for rollback. Which AWS CodeDeploy strategy should they use?',
  'mcq',
  '["In-place deployment","Rolling update","Blue/green deployment","All-at-once deployment"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is Blue/green deployment. This strategy provisions a completely new environment (green) with the updated application, while the existing environment (blue) remains operational. Traffic is then shifted from the blue to the green environment, ensuring zero downtime and providing an easy rollback by simply shifting traffic back to the blue environment if issues arise. Option A (In-place deployment) updates the application on the existing instances, which typically involves stopping services and can result in downtime. Option B (Rolling update) is a variation of in-place where instances are updated in batches, reducing downtime but not eliminating it entirely, and doesn''t keep the old environment fully active for quick rollback. Option D (All-at-once deployment) is an in-place strategy that updates all instances simultaneously, leading to significant downtime.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  82,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_4',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_4_53',
  'What is the primary purpose of the AppSpec file in AWS CodeDeploy?',
  'mcq',
  '["To define the build commands for AWS CodeBuild.","To specify the source code repository for AWS CodeCommit.","To manage application dependencies in AWS CodeArtifact.","To define the deployment actions and lifecycle hooks for AWS CodeDeploy."]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'Option D (correct) states that the AppSpec file defines the deployment actions and lifecycle hooks for AWS CodeDeploy. The AppSpec file (Application Specification file) is a YAML-formatted file used by CodeDeploy to manage the deployment. It specifies which source files should be copied where, what permissions to set, and which scripts (lifecycle hooks) to run at various stages of the deployment process. Option A (To define the build commands for AWS CodeBuild) is the purpose of the buildspec.yml file. Option B (To specify the source code repository for AWS CodeCommit) is incorrect; CodeCommit is a source control service, and its repositories are typically configured as the source stage in CodePipeline. Option C (To manage application dependencies in AWS CodeArtifact) is the purpose of AWS CodeArtifact, not the AppSpec file.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  83,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_4',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_4_54',
  'A DevOps team is deploying a serverless application consisting of AWS Lambda functions. They want to use AWS CodeDeploy to manage the deployment, including traffic shifting and automated rollbacks. Which CodeDeploy deployment type is best suited for AWS Lambda?',
  'mcq',
  '["In-place deployment","Blue/green deployment","Rolling update","All-at-once deployment"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is Blue/green deployment. For AWS Lambda, CodeDeploy implements a blue/green deployment by creating a new version of the Lambda function, shifting traffic to it gradually (or all at once), and providing automated rollback capabilities if issues are detected. This is the recommended strategy for Lambda deployments with CodeDeploy. Option A (In-place deployment) is not directly applicable to Lambda functions in the same way it is for EC2 instances, as Lambda functions are versioned and immutable. Option C (Rolling update) is typically associated with instance-based deployments. Option D (All-at-once deployment) is a deployment configuration that can be used within a blue/green strategy, but ''blue/green'' describes the overall approach for Lambda with CodeDeploy.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  84,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_4',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_4_55',
  'During an AWS CodeDeploy deployment, a DevOps engineer needs to run custom scripts at specific points, such as before installing the application or after traffic has been shifted. What mechanism does CodeDeploy provide for this?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Lambda triggers","Amazon CloudWatch Events","CodeDeploy lifecycle hooks","AWS Systems Manager Run Command"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) refers to CodeDeploy lifecycle hooks. Lifecycle hooks are predefined events during a CodeDeploy deployment (e.g., BeforeInstall, AfterInstall, ApplicationStop, BeforeAllowTraffic, AfterAllowTraffic) where you can specify scripts to run. These scripts are defined in the AppSpec file. Option A (AWS Lambda triggers) are used to invoke Lambda functions in response to events from other AWS services, not for custom actions within a CodeDeploy deployment. Option B (Amazon CloudWatch Events) can trigger actions based on AWS service events, but lifecycle hooks are the native mechanism for custom scripts within CodeDeploy. Option D (AWS Systems Manager Run Command) allows you to run commands on EC2 instances, but CodeDeploy''s lifecycle hooks are the integrated way to execute scripts specific to the deployment process.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  85,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_4',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_4_56',
  'A team is deploying an application to an Amazon ECS service using AWS CodeDeploy. They want to minimize downtime and quickly roll back if the new deployment fails. Which CodeDeploy deployment type should be chosen for this scenario?',
  'mcq',
  '["In-place deployment","Rolling update","Blue/green deployment","Linear deployment"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is Blue/green deployment. For Amazon ECS, AWS CodeDeploy integrates with Elastic Load Balancing to manage traffic shifting between the original (blue) task set and the new (green) task set. This strategy ensures zero downtime during deployment and provides a fast, reliable rollback mechanism by simply switching traffic back to the blue task set if issues are detected. Option A (In-place deployment) is not a direct deployment type for ECS with CodeDeploy in the same way it is for EC2 instances. Option B (Rolling update) is a strategy typically managed by ECS itself, but CodeDeploy''s blue/green offers more robust rollback capabilities and controlled traffic shifting. Option D (Linear deployment) is a traffic shifting option within a blue/green deployment, not the deployment type itself.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  86,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_4',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_4_57',
  'A company needs to update a critical microservice running on Amazon EC2 instances. They want to perform the update with minimal impact on users and ensure a quick recovery if the new version has issues. The update should be applied directly to the existing instances. Which CodeDeploy deployment type is most suitable for this, assuming some brief downtime per instance is acceptable?',
  'mcq',
  '["Blue/green deployment","In-place deployment","Canary deployment","Linear deployment"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is In-place deployment. An in-place deployment updates the application on the existing instances. While it can cause brief downtime per instance as the application is stopped, updated, and restarted, it''s suitable when minimal impact is desired without provisioning new environments, and existing instances are updated directly. Option A (Blue/green deployment) involves provisioning an entirely new set of instances, which is not what ''applied directly to the existing instances'' implies. Option C (Canary deployment) is a traffic shifting strategy often used with blue/green, not a primary deployment type for applying updates directly to existing instances. Option D (Linear deployment) is a traffic shifting option within blue/green, not a standalone deployment type for in-place updates.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  87,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_4',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_4_58',
  'A DevOps engineer has configured an AWS CodeDeploy deployment for an application running on Amazon EC2. To ensure application stability, they want to automatically roll back the deployment if the CPU utilization of the deployed instances exceeds 80% for five consecutive minutes. How can this be achieved?',
  'mcq',
  '["Manually monitor the CloudWatch metrics and initiate a rollback.","Configure a CodeDeploy deployment group with an associated Amazon CloudWatch alarm as a rollback trigger.","Implement a custom AWS Lambda function to check CPU utilization and call the CodeDeploy API for rollback.","Set a fixed timeout for the deployment, after which it automatically rolls back."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is to configure a CodeDeploy deployment group with an associated Amazon CloudWatch alarm as a rollback trigger. AWS CodeDeploy allows you to link CloudWatch alarms to a deployment group. If the specified alarm (e.g., for CPU utilization) goes into an ALARM state during or after a deployment, CodeDeploy can automatically initiate a rollback to the previous successful application version. Option A (Manually monitor the CloudWatch metrics and initiate a rollback) requires manual intervention, which is not automated and can lead to slower recovery. Option C (Implement a custom AWS Lambda function to check CPU utilization and call the CodeDeploy API for rollback) is a custom solution that adds unnecessary operational overhead when CodeDeploy provides a native, integrated feature for this. Option D (Set a fixed timeout for the deployment, after which it automatically rolls back) only addresses deployments that hang, not those that complete but introduce performance issues.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  88,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_4',
  'medium'
);

