INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_2_31',
  'Which AWS CodeBuild feature allows developers to view and analyze the results of unit, integration, and end-to-end tests directly within the CodeBuild console?',
  'mcq',
  '["Buildspec phases","Batch builds","Test reporting","Build caching"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Test reporting) is the correct CodeBuild feature. CodeBuild''s test reporting functionality allows you to configure your `buildspec.yml` to publish test results (e.g., JUnit XML reports). These reports are then aggregated and displayed in the CodeBuild console, providing insights into test success/failure rates, duration, and trends over time. Option A (Buildspec phases) defines the execution steps. Option B (Batch builds) runs multiple builds concurrently. Option D (Build caching) is for speeding up builds by reusing dependencies.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  89,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_2_32',
  'Which AWS service is specifically designed to provide automated static code analysis and intelligent recommendations to improve code quality and identify potential defects or security vulnerabilities?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CodeBuild","AWS CodePipeline","Amazon CodeGuru Reviewer","AWS X-Ray"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Amazon CodeGuru Reviewer) is the correct service. Amazon CodeGuru Reviewer uses machine learning to automatically detect hard-to-find defects and suggest improvements in your code during the development process, identifying issues like resource leaks, concurrency bugs, and security vulnerabilities. Option A (AWS CodeBuild) is a build service. Option B (AWS CodePipeline) orchestrates CI/CD. Option D (AWS X-Ray) is for distributed tracing and analyzing application performance, not static code analysis.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  90,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_2_33',
  'A development team is implementing a CI/CD pipeline for a critical application. They want to ensure that new features are thoroughly tested in an environment that closely mirrors production before being released to customers. Which pipeline stage is essential for this requirement?',
  'mcq',
  '["A dedicated staging environment stage.","Direct deployment to production after unit tests.","Manual code review before deployment.","Skipping all testing stages to accelerate delivery."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (A dedicated staging environment stage) is essential. A staging environment mirrors production and allows for comprehensive testing (integration, end-to-end, load testing) in a realistic setting before customer impact. This helps catch configuration, integration, and load-related issues. Option B (Direct deployment to production after unit tests) is an anti-pattern (ap-1.2-001) because production-only validation means issues first appear in the highest-stakes environment. Option C (Manual code review before deployment) is important but doesn''t replace environmental testing. Option D (Skipping all testing stages to accelerate delivery) is highly risky and detrimental to application quality.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  91,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_2_34',
  'What is the primary goal of a ''unit testing'' stage in a CI/CD pipeline?',
  'mcq',
  '["To verify the application''s performance under heavy load.","To test individual components or functions of the code in isolation.","To ensure that all integrated modules work together correctly.","To deploy the application to a production environment."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (To test individual components or functions of the code in isolation) is the primary goal of unit testing. Unit tests focus on the smallest testable parts of an application (units) to ensure they work as expected, typically without external dependencies. Option A (To verify the application''s performance under heavy load) is for load testing. Option C (To ensure that all integrated modules work together correctly) is for integration testing. Option D (To deploy the application to a production environment) is the purpose of a deployment stage.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  92,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_2_35',
  'A DevOps team wants to automatically identify potential bugs, security vulnerabilities, and code quality issues early in the development cycle, as part of their CI/CD pipeline. Which AWS service should they integrate into their build or test stage for this purpose?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CodeCommit","AWS CodePipeline","Amazon CodeGuru Reviewer","AWS CodeDeploy"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Amazon CodeGuru Reviewer) is the correct service for this purpose. CodeGuru Reviewer performs automated static code analysis and provides intelligent recommendations to improve code quality, identify potential defects, and detect security vulnerabilities. It integrates directly into the CI/CD pipeline. Option A (AWS CodeCommit) is a source control service. Option B (AWS CodePipeline) orchestrates the CI/CD process but doesn''t perform code analysis itself. Option D (AWS CodeDeploy) is for deploying applications, not for static code analysis.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  93,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_2_36',
  'What is the primary objective of integrating ''load testing'' into a CI/CD pipeline before production deployments?',
  'mcq',
  '["To ensure the application''s code compiles successfully.","To verify individual software components function correctly.","To identify security vulnerabilities in the application code.","To assess the application''s performance and stability under anticipated user traffic."]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'Option D (To assess the application''s performance and stability under anticipated user traffic) is the primary objective of load testing. Load testing simulates high user traffic to evaluate how the application performs, its response times, resource utilization, and stability under stress, ensuring it can handle production loads. Option A (To ensure the application''s code compiles successfully) is part of the build stage. Option B (To verify individual software components function correctly) is for unit testing. Option C (To identify security vulnerabilities in the application code) is for security testing or static code analysis.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  94,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_2_37',
  'A DevOps team wants to automate the testing of their infrastructure as code (IaC) templates, specifically AWS CloudFormation templates, for consistency and drift detection within their CI/CD pipeline. Which AWS service combination can help achieve this?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CloudFormation StackSets and AWS CloudFormation drift detection.","AWS CodeBuild with manual template validation.","Amazon CodeGuru Reviewer for CloudFormation templates.","AWS CodeDeploy with in-place deployments."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (AWS CloudFormation StackSets and AWS CloudFormation drift detection) is the correct combination. CloudFormation StackSets allow you to deploy CloudFormation templates across multiple accounts and regions consistently. CloudFormation drift detection can then be used to identify if the actual deployed resources deviate from their defined state in the CloudFormation template, which is crucial for infrastructure testing and maintaining consistency. Option B (AWS CodeBuild with manual template validation) lacks automation for drift detection. Option C (Amazon CodeGuru Reviewer for CloudFormation templates) is not its primary purpose; CodeGuru focuses on application code. Option D (AWS CodeDeploy with in-place deployments) is for application deployment, not for testing IaC templates or detecting infrastructure drift.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  95,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_2_38',
  'A DevOps team wants to monitor the quality of their builds over time by tracking the pass/fail rates and trends of their automated tests. Which AWS CodeBuild feature provides this capability?',
  'mcq',
  '["Buildspec caching","Batch build reports","Test result trends in CodeBuild reports","CodeBuild environment variables"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Test result trends in CodeBuild reports) is the correct feature. AWS CodeBuild''s test reporting functionality not only shows individual test results but also aggregates historical data to display trends in test pass/fail rates, duration, and coverage over multiple builds. This helps teams identify regressions or improvements in their test suite. Option A (Buildspec caching) is for speeding up builds. Option B (Batch build reports) shows results for concurrent builds but not necessarily trends over time. Option D (CodeBuild environment variables) are for configuring the build environment.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  96,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_2_39',
  'A DevOps team wants to ensure that their application code adheres to security best practices and common coding standards by automatically scanning it for potential issues during the CI/CD process. Which AWS service can be integrated into the pipeline for this purpose?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CodeDeploy","Amazon CodeGuru Reviewer","AWS CodePipeline","AWS CodeCommit"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (Amazon CodeGuru Reviewer) is the correct service. Amazon CodeGuru Reviewer provides automated static code analysis to identify potential bugs, security vulnerabilities, and code quality issues, offering intelligent recommendations to improve the code. It is designed to be integrated into CI/CD pipelines. Option A (AWS CodeDeploy) is for deploying applications. Option C (AWS CodePipeline) orchestrates the pipeline but doesn''t perform the analysis itself. Option D (AWS CodeCommit) is a source control service.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  97,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_2_40',
  'A development team wants to implement a CI/CD pipeline that includes automated testing for their application. Which AWS service is primarily responsible for running build commands, compiling code, and executing automated tests within the pipeline?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CodeDeploy","AWS CodePipeline","AWS CodeCommit","AWS CodeBuild"]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'Option D (AWS CodeBuild) is the correct service. AWS CodeBuild is a fully managed continuous integration service that compiles source code, runs unit tests, and produces artifacts. It is the component within a CI/CD pipeline responsible for executing the actual build and test commands. Option A (AWS CodeDeploy) handles deployments. Option B (AWS CodePipeline) orchestrates the entire pipeline. Option C (AWS CodeCommit) provides source control.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  98,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_2_41',
  'A company is implementing a CI/CD pipeline for a critical application. They want to ensure that all deployments to production are preceded by a comprehensive suite of automated tests, including unit, integration, and end-to-end tests, run in a staging environment that mirrors production. What is the anti-pattern they should avoid to prevent issues from appearing first in production?',
  'mcq',
  '["Using a single pipeline role with admin access instead of least-privilege per stage.","Not implementing automated rollback triggers in CodeDeploy.","Deploying to production without a staging environment stage in the pipeline.","Using mutable container image tags (e.g., ''latest'') in deployment pipelines."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) directly addresses the anti-pattern [ap-1.2-001]. Deploying directly to production without a dedicated staging environment means that configuration, integration, and load-related issues are first discovered in the highest-stakes environment, leading to potential customer impact. A staging environment mirrors production to catch these issues beforehand. Option A (incorrect) is an anti-pattern related to pipeline security ([ap-1.1-001]), not directly about testing environments. Option B (incorrect) is an anti-pattern related to CodeDeploy rollback mechanisms ([ap-1.1-002]), not about pre-production testing environments. Option D (incorrect) is an anti-pattern related to artifact management and reproducibility ([ap-1.3-001]), not about the existence of a staging environment.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  99,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_2_42',
  'A DevOps team is setting up an AWS CodeBuild project for a multi-module application. They need to ensure that unit and integration tests are executed as part of the build process, and the results are easily viewable and trackable over time within the AWS console. How should they configure CodeBuild to achieve this?',
  'mcq',
  '["Include commands in the `buildspec.yml` to run tests and output results to the console logs for manual review.","Configure the `reports` section in the `buildspec.yml` to specify the location and format of test result files (e.g., JUnit XML).","Integrate a third-party test reporting tool with CodeBuild by uploading test results to an external dashboard.","Use AWS CodeGuru Reviewer to automatically detect test failures and report them in the CodeBuild console."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the native and most effective way to integrate automated testing and view results within CodeBuild. By configuring the `reports` section in the `buildspec.yml`, you instruct CodeBuild to parse test result files (e.g., JUnit XML, Surefire reports) generated by your testing framework. CodeBuild then displays these results, including success/failure rates and detailed test case information, directly in the CodeBuild console and provides historical trends. Option A (incorrect) only outputs to logs, requiring manual parsing and lacking structured reporting. Option C (incorrect) involves external integration, which adds complexity and operational overhead compared to the native feature. Option D (incorrect) is incorrect; AWS CodeGuru Reviewer is for static code analysis and identifying code quality issues, not for executing tests or reporting their results.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  100,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_2_43',
  'A DevOps team needs to ensure that their application can handle expected production load before deploying to a production environment. They want to integrate automated load testing into their CI/CD pipeline using AWS services. At which stage of the CodePipeline should load testing typically be performed?',
  'mcq',
  '["In the ''Source'' stage, immediately after code is committed.","In the ''Build'' stage, alongside unit tests.","In a dedicated ''Test'' or ''Staging'' stage, after successful deployment to a production-like environment.","In the ''Deploy'' stage, as part of the production deployment process."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is the appropriate stage for load testing in a CI/CD pipeline. Load testing requires a deployed application instance, ideally in a staging environment that closely mirrors production, to accurately simulate real-world traffic. It should occur after successful unit and integration tests and a deployment to a non-production environment, but before the final production deployment. Option A (incorrect) is too early; load testing requires a deployable artifact. Option B (incorrect) is typically for unit and integration tests, not full-scale load testing. Option D (incorrect) is too late; load testing should precede production deployment to catch performance bottlenecks before customer impact.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  101,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_2_44',
  'A company is using AWS CodeBuild for its CI/CD pipeline. They want to ensure that code quality is consistently maintained by performing automated static code analysis on every code change. Which AWS service should be integrated into the CodeBuild project to achieve this goal?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CodeCommit, to store code and trigger builds.","Amazon CodeGuru Reviewer, to analyze code for defects and recommendations.","AWS CodeDeploy, to automate application deployments.","Amazon S3, to store static analysis reports."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the AWS service specifically designed for automated static code analysis. Amazon CodeGuru Reviewer uses machine learning to provide intelligent recommendations to improve code quality and identify hard-to-find defects. It integrates directly with CodeBuild, allowing you to run code analysis as part of your build process and receive feedback on every code change. Option A (incorrect) is a version control service, not a static analysis tool. Option C (incorrect) is for deployment, not code analysis. Option D (incorrect) is for storage, not for performing the analysis itself.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  102,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_2_45',
  'Which AWS service is used in a CI/CD pipeline to compile source code, run unit tests, and produce build artifacts?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CodeDeploy","AWS CodePipeline","AWS CodeCommit","AWS CodeBuild"]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'Option D (correct) is AWS CodeBuild. CodeBuild is a fully managed continuous integration service that compiles source code, runs tests, and produces software packages that are ready to deploy. Option A (AWS CodeDeploy) automates application deployments. Option B (AWS CodePipeline) orchestrates the entire release process, integrating various services. Option C (AWS CodeCommit) is a source control service for hosting Git repositories.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  103,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_2_46',
  'A DevOps team wants to automatically analyze their application''s source code for potential security vulnerabilities, code quality issues, and identify expensive lines of code within their CI/CD pipeline. Which AWS service is designed for this purpose?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon CloudWatch","Amazon Inspector","Amazon CodeGuru Reviewer","AWS X-Ray"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is Amazon CodeGuru Reviewer. CodeGuru Reviewer is an automated code review service that uses machine learning and automated reasoning to detect potential defects that are difficult to find, such as security vulnerabilities, resource leaks, concurrency issues, and expensive lines of code. It integrates directly into CI/CD pipelines. Option A (Amazon CloudWatch) is a monitoring and observability service. Option B (Amazon Inspector) is an automated security assessment service that helps improve the security and compliance of applications deployed on AWS, primarily focusing on EC2 instances and container images, not source code analysis during CI. Option D (AWS X-Ray) is a service for analyzing and debugging distributed applications, not for static code analysis.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  104,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_2_47',
  'Before deploying a new application version to production, a DevOps team wants to assess its behavior under anticipated peak traffic conditions to identify performance bottlenecks and ensure stability. Which type of testing should be integrated into the CI/CD pipeline?',
  'mcq',
  '["Unit testing","Integration testing","Static code analysis","Load testing"]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'Option D (correct) is Load testing. Load testing is specifically designed to simulate anticipated peak traffic conditions to evaluate an application''s performance, stability, and resource utilization under stress. This helps identify bottlenecks before production deployment. Option A (Unit testing) focuses on individual code components in isolation. Option B (Integration testing) verifies interactions between different modules or services. Option C (Static code analysis) analyzes source code for quality, style, and potential issues without executing it.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  105,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_2_48',
  'A development team uses AWS CodeBuild to execute their CI/CD pipeline. They want to integrate Amazon CodeGuru Reviewer to automatically scan their Java and Python code for defects and provide recommendations. How should CodeGuru Reviewer be integrated into the pipeline?',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure CodeGuru Reviewer as a post-build action in AWS CodeDeploy.","Add a CodeGuru Reviewer action directly into an AWS CodePipeline stage.","Manually upload code to CodeGuru Reviewer after each commit to AWS CodeCommit.","Run CodeGuru Reviewer as a local tool on developer workstations before pushing code."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is to add a CodeGuru Reviewer action directly into an AWS CodePipeline stage. CodeGuru Reviewer can be integrated as an action within a CodePipeline stage, typically after the source stage and before the build/deploy stages. This allows for automated code analysis as part of the continuous integration process. Option A (Configure CodeGuru Reviewer as a post-build action in AWS CodeDeploy) is incorrect; CodeDeploy is for deployment, not static code analysis. Option C (Manually upload code to CodeGuru Reviewer after each commit to AWS CodeCommit) introduces manual steps, which is against the principles of CI/CD automation. Option D (Run CodeGuru Reviewer as a local tool on developer workstations before pushing code) is a good practice but does not ensure consistent, automated analysis across all code changes within the centralized pipeline.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  106,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_2_49',
  'A company''s CI/CD pipeline currently deploys new application versions directly to production after passing automated unit tests. The DevOps team observes that issues frequently arise in production due to configuration mismatches or unexpected interactions. Which anti-pattern is being demonstrated, and what is the recommended solution?',
  'mcq',
  '["Anti-pattern: Lack of automated rollback triggers. Solution: Implement CloudWatch alarms for CodeDeploy rollbacks.","Anti-pattern: Using a single pipeline role with admin access. Solution: Implement least-privilege IAM roles per stage.","Anti-pattern: Deploying to production without a staging environment. Solution: Add a staging environment stage to the pipeline.","Anti-pattern: Using mutable container image tags. Solution: Enforce immutable image tags like Git commit SHAs."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) identifies the anti-pattern (ap-1.2-001) as ''Deploying to production without a staging environment'' and provides the correct solution: ''Add a staging environment stage to the pipeline.'' Staging environments mirror production and are crucial for catching configuration, integration, and load-related issues before customer impact. Option A (Anti-pattern: Lack of automated rollback triggers) is a valid anti-pattern (ap-1.1-002) but doesn''t address the root cause of issues appearing in production due to lack of pre-production validation. Option B (Anti-pattern: Using a single pipeline role with admin access) is a security anti-pattern (ap-1.1-001) related to IAM permissions, not directly to application quality issues in production. Option D (Anti-pattern: Using mutable container image tags) is an artifact management anti-pattern (ap-1.3-001) related to reproducibility, not the lack of a testing environment.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  107,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_2_50',
  'A DevOps engineer wants to configure an AWS CodeBuild project to publish detailed test results, including success/failure status, test duration, and individual test cases, to the CodeBuild console. Which section of the buildspec.yml file should be configured for this?',
  'mcq',
  '["artifacts","phases","reports","environment"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is the ''reports'' section. The `reports` section in the buildspec.yml file is specifically used to configure test reporting. It allows you to specify the location of test reports (e.g., JUnit XML files) and their format, enabling CodeBuild to parse and display detailed test results in the console. Option A (artifacts) is used to specify files or directories to be output as build artifacts. Option B (phases) defines the build commands to run during different stages of the build process (e.g., install, pre_build, build, post_build). Option D (environment) is used to define environment variables or runtime settings for the build.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  108,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_2_51',
  'A team is developing a new microservice and wants to ensure that all end-to-end user flows work correctly across multiple integrated services before deployment. Which type of testing should be a mandatory stage in their AWS CodePipeline?',
  'mcq',
  '["Unit testing","Static code analysis","End-to-end testing","Performance testing"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is End-to-end testing. End-to-end (E2E) testing verifies the complete application flow from start to finish, including interactions between all integrated services, databases, and external systems. This is crucial for ensuring that user flows work correctly in a multi-service environment. Option A (Unit testing) focuses on individual components in isolation. Option B (Static code analysis) examines code without executing it for quality and potential issues. Option D (Performance testing) assesses how the system behaves under specific workloads, not necessarily the functional correctness of user flows across services.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  109,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_2_52',
  'A DevOps team wants to ensure that all new code changes are automatically reviewed for common programming errors and best practices before being merged. Which AWS service can be integrated into the CI/CD pipeline for automated static code analysis?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon CloudWatch","AWS CodeCommit","Amazon CodeGuru Reviewer","AWS X-Ray"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is Amazon CodeGuru Reviewer. CodeGuru Reviewer is specifically designed for automated code reviews using machine learning to find defects, security vulnerabilities, and recommend improvements in code quality. It integrates directly into CI/CD pipelines, often triggered by pull requests in AWS CodeCommit. Option A (Amazon CloudWatch) is for monitoring and observability. Option B (AWS CodeCommit) is a source control service, not for code analysis. Option D (AWS X-Ray) is for analyzing and debugging distributed applications, focusing on runtime behavior rather than static code analysis.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  110,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_2_53',
  'A development team has integrated unit tests into their AWS CodeBuild project. They want to visualize the test results, including the number of passed, failed, and skipped tests, directly within the CodeBuild console. What capability of CodeBuild should they leverage?',
  'mcq',
  '["CodeBuild environment variables.","CodeBuild build caching.","CodeBuild test reporting.","CodeBuild batch builds."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is CodeBuild test reporting. AWS CodeBuild''s test reporting feature allows you to configure your buildspec.yml to publish test results from various test frameworks (e.g., JUnit, NUnit) directly to the CodeBuild console, providing detailed insights into test execution. Option A (CodeBuild environment variables) are used to pass configuration to the build environment. Option B (CodeBuild build caching) is used to speed up builds by reusing dependencies. Option D (CodeBuild batch builds) allows running multiple builds concurrently, but not for visualizing test results.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  111,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_2',
  'medium'
);

