  'm_38_2',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_2_61',
  'A company is deploying a new global application that requires its NoSQL database to handle high volumes of read and write traffic with low latency across multiple AWS Regions. Which two characteristics of Amazon DynamoDB Global Tables are crucial for meeting these requirements? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Automatic multi-master replication","Automatic scaling of throughput capacity","Support for complex relational queries","Manual failover to a secondary region","Strong consistency for all cross-region reads"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Automatic multi-master replication) is a core feature of DynamoDB Global Tables, allowing applications to perform reads and writes in any designated Region, with changes automatically propagated to all other replica tables. This enables low-latency global access. Option B (Automatic scaling of throughput capacity) is a fundamental capability of DynamoDB, ensuring that Global Tables can handle fluctuating high volumes of read and write traffic without manual intervention. Option C (Support for complex relational queries) is incorrect, as DynamoDB is a NoSQL database and does not support complex SQL queries or joins. Option D (Manual failover to a secondary region) is not applicable in an active-active multi-master setup; traffic is simply directed to healthy regions. Option E (Strong consistency for all cross-region reads) is incorrect; DynamoDB Global Tables provide eventual consistency across regions, optimizing for availability and performance over immediate strong consistency.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  46,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_2_62',
  'A global e-commerce platform needs to ensure its relational database can scale to handle increasing read traffic from users worldwide, while also providing fast disaster recovery capabilities. Which two features of Amazon Aurora Global Database address these requirements? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Read replicas in secondary regions for local read scaling","Low RPO and RTO for cross-region failover","Automatic scaling of compute capacity based on read load","Active-active multi-master writes across all regions","Support for complex SQL queries and stored procedures"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Read replicas in secondary regions for local read scaling) is a key feature of Aurora Global Database, allowing applications to serve read traffic from the closest secondary Region, significantly improving performance and scalability for global users. Option B (Low RPO and RTO for cross-region failover) is a primary benefit, as Aurora Global Database is designed for rapid disaster recovery, typically achieving an RPO under 1 second and an RTO under 1 minute. Option C (Automatic scaling of compute capacity based on read load) is a general feature of Amazon Aurora, but not specific to the ''Global'' aspect for cross-Region scalability. Option D (Active-active multi-master writes across all regions) is incorrect; Aurora Global Database operates with a single primary Region for writes and up to five secondary read-only Regions. Option E (Support for complex SQL queries and stored procedures) is a general characteristic of relational databases, not a unique scaling feature of Aurora Global Database.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  47,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_2_63',
  'A company is launching a new application globally and needs to ensure that users are routed to the closest healthy endpoint to minimize latency and improve overall application performance. Which two AWS services can be used to intelligently direct user traffic across multiple regions? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Global Accelerator","Amazon Route 53 with latency routing","Amazon DynamoDB Global Tables","Amazon Aurora Global Database","AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery (DRS)"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (AWS Global Accelerator) uses the AWS global network to route user traffic to the closest healthy endpoint, significantly reducing latency and improving performance for global applications. It provides static IP addresses as entry points. Option B (Amazon Route 53 with latency routing) routes user requests to the AWS Region that provides the lowest latency for the user, based on health checks. This helps in directing users to the closest healthy endpoint. Option C (Amazon DynamoDB Global Tables) and Option D (Amazon Aurora Global Database) are database services, not traffic routing services. Option E (AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery (DRS)) is for server replication and disaster recovery, not for optimizing live traffic routing.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  48,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_2_64',
  'To ensure an application can handle sudden spikes in global traffic and maintain low latency for users worldwide, a DevOps team is considering a globally distributed NoSQL database. Which two features of Amazon DynamoDB Global Tables are critical for this scenario? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Automatic, active-active replication across selected regions","Automatic scaling of read and write capacity based on demand","Strong consistency for all reads across all regions","Manual provisioning of replica tables in each region","Support for complex SQL transactions and joins"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Automatic, active-active replication across selected regions) allows DynamoDB Global Tables to distribute read and write traffic across multiple AWS Regions, providing low-latency access for global users and improving resilience against regional outages. Option B (Automatic scaling of read and write capacity based on demand) ensures that the database can dynamically adjust its throughput to accommodate sudden spikes in traffic without manual intervention, maintaining performance. Option C (Strong consistency for all reads across all regions) is incorrect; DynamoDB Global Tables provide eventual consistency across regions. Option D (Manual provisioning of replica tables in each region) is incorrect; Global Tables are fully managed, and replication is automatically handled once configured. Option E (Support for complex SQL transactions and joins) is incorrect, as DynamoDB is a NoSQL database and does not support SQL.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  49,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_2_65',
  'A company needs to implement a highly scalable and resilient relational database solution for a global application. The solution must support rapid failover to a secondary region in case of a disaster. Which two capabilities of Amazon Aurora Global Database are key to meeting these requirements? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Cross-region data replication with minimal lag","Ability to promote a secondary region to primary for writes","Active-active multi-master write capabilities across regions","Automatic scaling of storage and compute independently","Support for multiple database engines within a single cluster"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Cross-region data replication with minimal lag) is a core feature of Aurora Global Database, using dedicated infrastructure to replicate data with very low latency, which is essential for low RPO. Option B (Ability to promote a secondary region to primary for writes) is a critical disaster recovery capability, allowing a secondary region to quickly take over write operations with an RTO of less than 1 minute. Option C (Active-active multi-master write capabilities across regions) is incorrect; Aurora Global Database operates with a single primary region for writes. Option D (Automatic scaling of storage and compute independently) is a general feature of Amazon Aurora, not specific to the ''Global'' aspect for cross-Region resilience. Option E (Support for multiple database engines within a single cluster) is incorrect; Aurora supports specific engines (MySQL and PostgreSQL compatible) but not multiple engines within one cluster.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  50,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_2_66',
  'For a globally distributed application, a DevOps engineer wants to ensure that users consistently connect to the optimal application endpoint, even during peak load times. They also need to maintain static IP addresses for the application''s entry points. Which two AWS services can facilitate this? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Global Accelerator","Amazon Route 53 with weighted routing","Amazon DynamoDB Global Tables","Amazon Aurora Global Database","AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery (DRS)"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (AWS Global Accelerator) provides static IP addresses that serve as fixed entry points for your application. It routes traffic over the AWS global network to the closest healthy endpoint, optimizing performance and availability, especially during peak loads. Option B (Amazon Route 53 with weighted routing) can distribute traffic across multiple endpoints based on specified weights, allowing for load distribution and controlled scaling. When combined with health checks, it ensures traffic goes to healthy endpoints. Option C (Amazon DynamoDB Global Tables) and Option D (Amazon Aurora Global Database) are database services, not traffic routing services. Option E (AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery (DRS)) is for server replication, not for optimizing live traffic routing or providing static IP addresses.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  51,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_2_67',
  'A company is building a new global application that requires a highly scalable NoSQL database with low-latency access for users in different geographical regions. Which two aspects of Amazon DynamoDB Global Tables contribute to meeting these scalability requirements? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Automatic creation and management of replica tables","Consistent low-latency access to data from any region","Manual sharding of data across multiple tables","Synchronous replication for strong consistency across all regions","Support for complex joins and subqueries"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Automatic creation and management of replica tables) simplifies the deployment and operation of a globally distributed database, allowing the application to scale without manual intervention for replication. Option B (Consistent low-latency access to data from any region) is a key benefit of DynamoDB Global Tables, as users can read and write to the closest replica table, minimizing network latency. Option C (Manual sharding of data across multiple tables) is incorrect; DynamoDB handles data partitioning automatically. Option D (Synchronous replication for strong consistency across all regions) is incorrect; Global Tables use asynchronous replication, providing eventual consistency. Option E (Support for complex joins and subqueries) is incorrect, as DynamoDB is a NoSQL database.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  52,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_2_68',
  'To improve the scalability and resilience of a globally distributed application, a DevOps team needs to ensure that DNS resolution can handle high query volumes and route traffic efficiently. Which two Amazon Route 53 features are beneficial for this scenario? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["High availability and global distribution of DNS servers","Support for various routing policies (e.g., latency, geolocation)","Automatic scaling of compute resources for applications","Real-time data streaming and processing","Centralized backup and restore capabilities"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (High availability and global distribution of DNS servers) is a core characteristic of Amazon Route 53, ensuring that DNS resolution is highly resilient and can handle massive query volumes globally. Option B (Support for various routing policies (e.g., latency, geolocation)) allows Route 53 to intelligently direct user traffic to the most appropriate endpoint based on factors like user location or network latency, improving scalability and user experience. Option C (Automatic scaling of compute resources for applications) is handled by services like Auto Scaling Groups or AWS Lambda, not Route 53. Option D (Real-time data streaming and processing) is a function of services like Amazon Kinesis, not Route 53. Option E (Centralized backup and restore capabilities) is provided by AWS Backup, not Route 53.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  53,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_2_69',
  'A company needs to provide consistent, low-latency access to its relational database for users in multiple geographical regions, while also ensuring high availability and scalability of read operations. Which two features of Amazon Aurora Global Database address these requirements? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Cross-region read replicas for local read scaling","Dedicated, low-latency replication infrastructure","Active-active multi-master write capabilities across regions","Automatic scaling of write capacity based on demand","Support for a wide range of NoSQL data models"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Cross-region read replicas for local read scaling) allows Aurora Global Database to serve read traffic from secondary regions, providing low-latency access for users closer to those regions and scaling read capacity globally. Option B (Dedicated, low-latency replication infrastructure) ensures that data changes from the primary region are replicated to secondary regions with minimal lag, which is crucial for both read consistency and disaster recovery. Option C (Active-active multi-master write capabilities across regions) is incorrect; Aurora Global Database has a single primary region for writes. Option D (Automatic scaling of write capacity based on demand) is a general Aurora feature but not specific to the ''Global'' aspect for cross-Region read scaling. Option E (Support for a wide range of NoSQL data models) is incorrect; Aurora is a relational database.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  54,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_2_70',
  'A DevOps team is deploying a new global application and needs to improve its performance and availability for users worldwide by leveraging the AWS global network. They also want to ensure that the application''s entry points are stable. Which two benefits does AWS Global Accelerator provide for this scenario? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Static IP addresses that don''t change","Traffic acceleration over the AWS global backbone","Content caching at edge locations","DNS-based load balancing for HTTP/S traffic","Automatic scaling of backend compute resources"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Static IP addresses that don''t change) is a key feature of AWS Global Accelerator, providing fixed entry points for your application, which simplifies client configuration and improves resilience. Option B (Traffic acceleration over the AWS global backbone) is a primary benefit, as Global Accelerator routes user traffic through the optimized AWS network, bypassing congested public internet paths, leading to improved performance and reduced latency for global users. Option C (Content caching at edge locations) is a function of Amazon CloudFront, not Global Accelerator. Option D (DNS-based load balancing for HTTP/S traffic) is a capability of Amazon Route 53 and Application Load Balancers, not Global Accelerator directly. Option E (Automatic scaling of backend compute resources) is handled by services like Auto Scaling Groups or AWS Lambda, not Global Accelerator.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  55,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_2_71',
  'For an application that requires a globally distributed, highly available NoSQL database with automatic scaling, which two features of Amazon DynamoDB Global Tables are most relevant? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Automatic provisioning of replicas in chosen regions","Eventual consistency for cross-region data synchronization","Strong consistency for all reads across all regions","Manual management of data partitions","Support for complex SQL queries"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Automatic provisioning of replicas in chosen regions) simplifies the deployment and management of a globally distributed database, allowing the application to scale globally without manual setup of replication. Option B (Eventual consistency for cross-region data synchronization) is a characteristic of DynamoDB Global Tables, where updates are propagated asynchronously across regions, optimizing for availability and performance. Option C (Strong consistency for all reads across all regions) is incorrect; Global Tables provide eventual consistency. Option D (Manual management of data partitions) is incorrect; DynamoDB automatically manages data partitioning. Option E (Support for complex SQL queries) is incorrect, as DynamoDB is a NoSQL database.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  56,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_2_72',
  'A DevOps team is designing a scalable architecture for a global web application. They need to ensure that DNS resolution is highly available and can route traffic based on user location to minimize latency. Which two Amazon Route 53 features are best suited for this? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Geolocation routing policy","Health checks for endpoint monitoring","Private hosted zones for internal DNS","Simple routing policy for single resources","Resolver endpoints for on-premises integration"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Geolocation routing policy) allows Route 53 to route traffic to resources based on the geographic location of your users, which helps in minimizing latency by directing them to the closest regional endpoint. Option B (Health checks for endpoint monitoring) are crucial to ensure that traffic is only routed to healthy endpoints, preventing users from being directed to unavailable application instances. Option C (Private hosted zones for internal DNS) are used for DNS resolution within VPCs, not for public global traffic routing. Option D (Simple routing policy for single resources) does not provide location-based routing or health check integration. Option E (Resolver endpoints for on-premises integration) are used for hybrid DNS resolution, not for global traffic distribution based on user location.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  57,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_2_73',
  'To enhance the scalability and performance of a global application''s relational database, a DevOps team is considering Amazon Aurora Global Database. Which two aspects of this service contribute to its global scalability? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Ability to create up to 5 secondary read-only regions","Dedicated replication infrastructure for high throughput","Automatic scaling of read replicas based on traffic","Active-active multi-master write capabilities across regions","Support for sharding data across multiple clusters"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Ability to create up to 5 secondary read-only regions) allows Aurora Global Database to distribute read traffic globally, improving performance and scalability by serving users from the closest region. Option B (Dedicated replication infrastructure for high throughput) ensures efficient and low-latency data replication between regions, which is essential for maintaining data currency in secondary regions and supporting global read scaling. Option C (Automatic scaling of read replicas based on traffic) is a general feature of Aurora, but not specific to the ''Global'' aspect for cross-Region scalability. Option D (Active-active multi-master write capabilities across regions) is incorrect; Aurora Global Database has a single primary region for writes. Option E (Support for sharding data across multiple clusters) is not a native feature of Aurora Global Database; sharding is a separate architectural pattern.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  58,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_2_74',
  'A company needs to ensure its global application can handle increasing user traffic efficiently and maintain low latency. They want to use a service that provides a single entry point and routes traffic over the AWS global network. Which two benefits does AWS Global Accelerator offer in this scenario? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Static IP addresses for consistent entry points","Improved performance for all TCP/UDP traffic","Automatic scaling of application servers","DNS-based routing for HTTP/S requests","Caching of dynamic content at edge locations"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Static IP addresses for consistent entry points) is a core feature of AWS Global Accelerator, providing fixed IP addresses that simplify client configuration and offer a stable interface for your application. Option B (Improved performance for all TCP/UDP traffic) is a key benefit, as Global Accelerator routes traffic over the optimized AWS global network, reducing latency and improving throughput for both HTTP/S and non-HTTP/S applications. Option C (Automatic scaling of application servers) is handled by services like Auto Scaling Groups, not Global Accelerator. Option D (DNS-based routing for HTTP/S requests) is primarily a function of Amazon Route 53 or Application Load Balancers. Option E (Caching of dynamic content at edge locations) is not a primary function of Global Accelerator; it''s typically handled by Amazon CloudFront.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  59,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_2_75',
  'For a globally distributed application requiring a highly scalable NoSQL database, a DevOps team chooses Amazon DynamoDB Global Tables. Which two features enable the database to scale effectively across regions? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Automatic propagation of data changes to all replica tables","Ability to scale read and write capacity independently per region","Manual sharding of data across multiple DynamoDB tables","Strongly consistent reads across all global regions by default","Support for complex joins and aggregations"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Automatic propagation of data changes to all replica tables) is a fundamental feature of DynamoDB Global Tables, ensuring that data is synchronized across all chosen regions without manual intervention, which is key for global scalability. Option B (Ability to scale read and write capacity independently per region) allows each replica table to be configured with its own read and write capacity settings, enabling the database to handle varying workloads and scale efficiently in each region. Option C (Manual sharding of data across multiple DynamoDB tables) is incorrect; DynamoDB automatically manages data partitioning. Option D (Strongly consistent reads across all global regions by default) is incorrect; Global Tables provide eventual consistency across regions. Option E (Support for complex joins and aggregations) is incorrect, as DynamoDB is a NoSQL database.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  60,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_2_76',
  'A company is planning to deploy a new application that needs to serve users globally with low latency and high availability. They are using Amazon Route 53 for DNS management. Which two Route 53 routing policies are most suitable for directing users to the optimal endpoint based on their geographic location or network performance? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Geolocation routing policy","Latency routing policy","Simple routing policy","Weighted routing policy","Failover routing policy"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Geolocation routing policy) allows you to route traffic based on the geographic location of your users, enabling you to serve content from specific regions to users in those regions, which can reduce latency. Option B (Latency routing policy) routes user requests to the AWS Region that provides the lowest latency for the user, improving application performance. Option C (Simple routing policy) is for single resources and does not offer location or latency-based routing. Option D (Weighted routing policy) distributes traffic based on specified weights, useful for A/B testing or gradual rollouts, but not primarily for location/latency optimization. Option E (Failover routing policy) is for disaster recovery, switching traffic to a secondary endpoint if the primary becomes unhealthy, which is different from optimizing for location or latency.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  61,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_2_77',
  'To ensure a highly scalable and performant relational database for a global application, a DevOps team implements Amazon Aurora Global Database. Which two architectural characteristics contribute to its ability to scale reads globally? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Asynchronous, continuous replication to secondary regions","Ability to add up to 15 read replicas per secondary region","Active-active multi-master write capabilities across regions","Automatic scaling of storage capacity based on data growth","Support for sharding data across multiple database instances"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Asynchronous, continuous replication to secondary regions) ensures that data changes from the primary region are continuously and efficiently propagated to secondary regions, allowing read replicas in those regions to serve up-to-date data. Option B (Ability to add up to 15 read replicas per secondary region) allows for significant read scaling within each secondary region, distributing the read load and providing low-latency access to global users. Option C (Active-active multi-master write capabilities across regions) is incorrect; Aurora Global Database has a single primary region for writes. Option D (Automatic scaling of storage capacity based on data growth) is a general feature of Amazon Aurora, not specific to its global read scaling. Option E (Support for sharding data across multiple database instances) is not a native feature of Aurora Global Database.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  62,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_2',
  'medium'
);

