INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_3_67',
  'Which disaster recovery strategies typically involve maintaining a minimal, non-production environment in a secondary Region? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Pilot light","Warm standby","Backup and restore","Multi-site active/active","Cold backup"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Pilot light) maintains a small, scaled-down version of the application in the secondary Region, ready to be scaled up. Option B (Warm standby) keeps a fully functional but scaled-down version of the application running in the secondary Region. Both strategies involve a minimal, non-production environment. Backup and restore (Option C) involves restoring data and infrastructure from backups. Multi-site active/active (Option D) runs a full production environment in multiple Regions simultaneously. Cold backup (Option E) is a simpler form of backup and restore with longer recovery times.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  63,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_3',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_3_68',
  'Which capabilities does AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery (DRS) provide for server replication and recovery? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Continuous data replication from source servers","Automated machine conversion for AWS","Database-specific replication for Amazon RDS","DNS failover management","Serverless application deployment"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Continuous data replication from source servers) ensures minimal data loss by constantly replicating data to a staging area in AWS. Option B (Automated machine conversion for AWS) handles the process of converting source servers into AWS-compatible instances during recovery. Database-specific replication for Amazon RDS (Option C) is handled by RDS itself. DNS failover management (Option D) is a function of Amazon Route 53. Serverless application deployment (Option E) is unrelated to DRS.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  64,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_3',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_3_69',
  'Which principles are central to Chaos Engineering and can be implemented using AWS Fault Injection Service (FIS)? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Proactively identify weaknesses in system resilience","Build confidence in system behavior during failures","Prevent all failures from occurring","Optimize application cost through resource reduction","Automate code deployments to production"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Proactively identify weaknesses in system resilience) and Option B (Build confidence in system behavior during failures) are core tenets of Chaos Engineering. By intentionally injecting faults, teams can discover vulnerabilities before they impact customers and validate their recovery mechanisms. Chaos Engineering does not aim to prevent all failures (Option C) but rather to understand and prepare for them. Optimizing application cost (Option D) and automating code deployments (Option E) are separate DevOps concerns.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  65,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_3',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_3_70',
  'A DevOps team wants to regularly test their disaster recovery plan to ensure it meets defined RTO and RPO objectives. Which AWS services or practices are most effective for this? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Fault Injection Service (FIS) experiments","GameDay exercises","AWS Backup for daily snapshots","Amazon Route 53 latency routing","AWS Global Accelerator"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (AWS Fault Injection Service (FIS) experiments) allows teams to simulate real-world outages and test their system''s resilience. Option B (GameDay exercises) are structured events where teams simulate a disaster and practice their recovery procedures. Both are highly effective for validating RTO/RPO. AWS Backup (Option C) is for data protection, not active DR testing. Amazon Route 53 latency routing (Option D) is for traffic distribution, not a DR testing tool. AWS Global Accelerator (Option E) is for global traffic routing, not for testing DR plans.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  66,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_3',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_3_71',
  'Which factors are critical for defining and evaluating disaster recovery solutions? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Recovery Time Objective (RTO)","Recovery Point Objective (RPO)","Cost Optimization Objective (COO)","Operational Efficiency Objective (OEO)","Security Compliance Objective (SCO)"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Recovery Time Objective (RTO)) defines the maximum acceptable delay between the interruption of service and restoration of service. Option B (Recovery Point Objective (RPO)) defines the maximum acceptable amount of data loss measured in time. Both RTO and RPO are fundamental metrics for designing and evaluating any disaster recovery solution. The other options, while important business objectives, are not specific to disaster recovery planning.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  67,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_3',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_3_72',
  'Which types of AWS resources can be centrally managed and backed up using AWS Backup? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon RDS databases","Amazon DynamoDB tables","AWS Lambda functions","Amazon CloudWatch logs","Amazon Route 53 hosted zones"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Amazon RDS databases) and Option B (Amazon DynamoDB tables) are among the many AWS services that AWS Backup can centrally manage and back up. AWS Backup provides a unified way to configure backup policies for various AWS resources. AWS Lambda functions (Option C) are serverless compute and don''t have traditional backups. Amazon CloudWatch logs (Option D) are a logging service. Amazon Route 53 hosted zones (Option E) are DNS configurations, not data that is backed up by AWS Backup.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  68,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_3',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_3_73',
  'A company needs to migrate its on-premises servers to AWS while also setting up a disaster recovery solution for them. They require continuous replication and minimal downtime during failover. Which AWS service capabilities are best suited for this? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery (DRS) continuous data replication","Automated server conversion by AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery","AWS Backup for daily snapshots","Amazon Aurora Global Database for server data","AWS Fault Injection Service for server replication"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery (DRS) continuous data replication) ensures that data from on-premises servers is constantly replicated to AWS, minimizing data loss. Option B (Automated server conversion by AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery) streamlines the process of preparing replicated servers to run as EC2 instances in AWS, enabling minimal downtime during failover. AWS Backup (Option C) provides snapshots but not continuous replication for servers. Amazon Aurora Global Database (Option D) is a relational database service, not for server replication. AWS Fault Injection Service (Option E) is for testing, not replication.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  69,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_3',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_3_74',
  'What are the primary benefits of conducting GameDay exercises for disaster recovery? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Validate runbooks and automation scripts","Train teams for real-world incident response","Automatically recover all systems without manual intervention","Eliminate all potential system failures","Reduce infrastructure costs in the disaster recovery environment"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Validate runbooks and automation scripts) ensures that documented procedures and automated recovery processes work as expected. Option B (Train teams for real-world incident response) provides hands-on experience for engineers to react effectively under pressure. GameDay exercises do not automatically recover systems (Option C) or eliminate all failures (Option D), but rather prepare teams for them. While cost optimization is important, it''s not a primary benefit of conducting a GameDay exercise (Option E).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  70,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_3',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_3_75',
  'Which types of fault injection experiments can AWS Fault Injection Service (FIS) perform to test resilience? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Terminate Amazon EC2 instances","Inject API errors into AWS services","Automatically re-architect applications for resilience","Deploy new application versions to production","Optimize database queries for performance"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Terminate Amazon EC2 instances) and Option B (Inject API errors into AWS services) are examples of common fault injection actions that AWS FIS can perform to simulate real-world failures and test how applications respond. AWS FIS is a testing tool; it does not automatically re-architect applications (Option C), deploy new versions (Option D), or optimize database queries (Option E).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  71,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_3',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_3_76',
  'A company has designed a disaster recovery plan but has not yet tested it. They want to identify potential weaknesses before a real incident. Which approaches would effectively expose gaps in their DR strategy? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Performing GameDay exercises","Implementing AWS Fault Injection Service experiments","Relying solely on AWS Backup for data recovery","Using Amazon Route 53 latency routing for failover","Documenting the DR plan thoroughly without execution"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Performing GameDay exercises) simulates real-world disaster scenarios, forcing teams to execute their DR plan and exposing any gaps in procedures or automation. Option B (Implementing AWS Fault Injection Service experiments) allows for controlled injection of faults to test specific failure modes and system responses. Relying solely on AWS Backup (Option C) provides data recovery but doesn''t test the entire DR process. Using Amazon Route 53 latency routing (Option D) is an anti-pattern if used as a primary DR mechanism without health checks. Documenting the DR plan thoroughly without execution (Option E) is an anti-pattern as untested plans often fail when needed most.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  72,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_3',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_3_77',
  'Which of the following capabilities are primary features of AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery (DRS)? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Continuous block-level replication of servers","Automated conversion of source servers to AWS EC2 instances","Database-specific point-in-time recovery","Cross-Region data archiving for long-term retention","Application-level load balancing across multiple Regions"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Continuous block-level replication of servers) is a core feature of AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery (DRS), ensuring minimal data loss (low RPO) by continuously replicating data changes. Option B (Automated conversion of source servers to AWS EC2 instances) is another key capability, simplifying the recovery process by preparing replicated servers to run as EC2 instances during failover. Option C (Database-specific point-in-time recovery) is typically handled by services like Amazon RDS or Amazon Aurora, not DRS itself. Option D (Cross-Region data archiving for long-term retention) is a function of services like AWS Backup or Amazon S3. Option E (Application-level load balancing across multiple Regions) is handled by services such as AWS Global Accelerator or Amazon Route 53.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  73,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_3_78',
  'Which of the following are core capabilities of AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery (DRS) for migrating and recovering servers to AWS? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Continuous, block-level replication of source servers.","Automated conversion of source servers to Amazon EC2 instances during recovery.","Orchestration of multi-tier application failover across Regions.","Real-time monitoring of application performance metrics.","Centralized management of database backups for various engines."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is a fundamental feature of DRS, ensuring that data changes on source servers are continuously replicated to a low-cost staging area in AWS. Option B (correct) highlights DRS''s ability to prepare and launch EC2 instances from the replicated data during a recovery or drill, making the process efficient. Option C is incorrect; while DRS handles server recovery, orchestrating complex multi-tier application failover often requires additional services like AWS Systems Manager Automation. Option D is incorrect; DRS focuses on replication and recovery, not real-time application performance monitoring, which is handled by services like Amazon CloudWatch. Option E is incorrect; centralized database backup management is a function of AWS Backup, not DRS.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  74,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_3_79',
  'A company is evaluating different disaster recovery strategies to minimize both recovery time objective (RTO) and recovery point objective (RPO) for its critical applications. Which strategies offer the lowest RTO and RPO among the common DR approaches? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Multi-site active/active","Warm standby","Pilot light","Backup and restore","Cold standby"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) provides the lowest RTO and RPO because the application is fully functional in multiple Regions simultaneously, allowing immediate failover. Option B (correct) keeps a scaled-down but functional version of the application running in the DR Region, offering significantly lower RTO/RPO than backup and restore or pilot light. Option C, Pilot light, has a higher RTO/RPO than warm standby or multi-site as it requires more resources to be provisioned during a disaster. Option D, Backup and restore, typically has the highest RTO and RPO due to the time required to restore data and provision infrastructure. Option E, Cold standby, is similar to backup and restore with even higher RTO/RPO as infrastructure is not pre-provisioned.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  75,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_3_80',
  'Which characteristics accurately describe a ''warm standby'' disaster recovery strategy? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["A scaled-down but fully functional version of the application is running in the recovery Region.","Data is continuously replicated to the recovery Region.","All infrastructure is provisioned only after a disaster is declared.","Only essential core services are running in the recovery Region, with data replicated.","Recovery involves restoring from backups and provisioning all infrastructure."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is a defining characteristic of warm standby, where a minimal but operational environment is maintained. Option B (correct) is essential for warm standby to achieve lower RPO, ensuring data is up-to-date in the recovery Region. Option C is incorrect; this describes a cold standby or backup and restore approach. Option D describes a pilot light strategy, where only the core database or minimal services are running. Option E describes a backup and restore strategy, which has higher RTO/RPO.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  76,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_3_81',
  'Which types of fault injection experiments can be effectively performed using AWS Fault Injection Service (FIS) to test the resilience of an application? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Injecting CPU or memory stress on Amazon EC2 instances.","Simulating network latency or packet loss between services.","Performing automated code deployments to production environments.","Analyzing application logs for security vulnerabilities.","Generating synthetic user traffic to test scalability."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is a common use case for FIS, allowing engineers to test how applications respond to resource constraints. Option B (correct) is another core capability, enabling the simulation of network impairments to understand their impact on distributed systems. Option C is incorrect; FIS is for fault injection, not automated code deployments. Option D is incorrect; FIS is not a security analysis tool. Option E is incorrect; FIS is for injecting faults, not for generating synthetic user traffic, which would typically be done with load testing tools.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  77,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_3_82',
  'A company is preparing for a disaster recovery GameDay exercise. Which of the following are primary objectives of conducting such exercises? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["To validate the recovery time objective (RTO) and recovery point objective (RPO).","To identify gaps in runbooks and automation scripts.","To reduce the overall operational cost of the disaster recovery solution.","To perform a full-scale migration of existing workloads to AWS.","To train new employees on routine operational tasks."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is a critical objective, as GameDays provide real-world data on whether the DR plan can meet business-defined RTO/RPO. Option B (correct) is another primary goal, as practical execution often reveals flaws or missing steps in documentation and automation, addressing anti-pattern [ap-3.2-001]. Option C is incorrect; while DR solutions can be cost-optimized, reducing cost is not the primary objective of a GameDay exercise. Option D is incorrect; GameDays are for testing DR, not for full-scale migrations. Option E is incorrect; while some training may occur, the main focus is on DR validation, not general operational task training.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  78,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_3_83',
  'Which of the following are key benefits of implementing runbook automation for disaster recovery procedures? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Reduces human error during complex recovery processes.","Accelerates the recovery time objective (RTO) by automating manual steps.","Eliminates the need for regular DR testing and GameDay exercises.","Provides real-time application performance monitoring capabilities.","Automatically generates detailed cost reports for DR infrastructure."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is a major benefit, as automation removes the potential for human mistakes during stressful disaster scenarios. Option B (correct) directly contributes to meeting RTOs by executing recovery steps much faster than manual intervention. Option C (incorrect) represents anti-pattern [ap-3.2-001]; automation enhances DR, but regular testing is still crucial to validate the automation itself and ensure it works as expected. Option D is incorrect; runbook automation focuses on execution, not real-time performance monitoring. Option E is incorrect; while cost can be tracked, runbook automation doesn''t automatically generate detailed cost reports.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  79,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_3_84',
  'A company needs to centralize and automate its backup and recovery operations across various AWS services and on-premises workloads. Which of the following are key features provided by AWS Backup? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Centralized backup console for multiple AWS services.","Support for creating immutable backups for compliance.","Real-time data replication for active-active multi-Region databases.","Automated server migration from on-premises to AWS.","Advanced analytics and reporting on backup content."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is a primary feature of AWS Backup, allowing users to manage backups for various services from a single console. Option B (correct) enables users to create backups that cannot be altered or deleted, which is crucial for regulatory compliance. Option C is incorrect; real-time active-active replication is handled by services like Aurora Global Database or DynamoDB Global Tables, not AWS Backup. Option D is incorrect; automated server migration is a function of AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery (DRS). Option E is incorrect; while AWS Backup provides basic reporting, advanced analytics on backup content are not a core feature.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  80,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_3_85',
  'Which AWS resources can be centrally managed and protected using AWS Backup for automated backup and recovery? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon RDS databases","Amazon EC2 instances","AWS Lambda functions","Amazon CloudWatch logs","Amazon SQS queues"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) and Option B (correct) are among the many AWS services supported by AWS Backup, allowing for automated, policy-driven backups. AWS Backup supports services like Amazon EBS volumes, Amazon RDS databases, Amazon DynamoDB tables, Amazon EC2 instances, Amazon EFS file systems, AWS Storage Gateway volumes, and Amazon S3 buckets. Option C, AWS Lambda functions, are serverless compute and typically managed through code and configuration, not directly backed up by AWS Backup in the same way as persistent data stores. Option D, Amazon CloudWatch logs, are a monitoring service and their data is retained based on log group settings, not backed up by AWS Backup. Option E, Amazon SQS queues, are message queues and do not have a backup mechanism in AWS Backup.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  81,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_3_86',
  'When designing a disaster recovery strategy, a DevOps team wants to ensure that the DR plan is regularly tested and validated to prevent failures during an actual disaster. Which actions are crucial for validating the effectiveness of a DR plan? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Conducting regular GameDay exercises.","Implementing AWS Fault Injection Service (FIS) experiments.","Relying solely on documentation reviews without execution.","Performing annual security audits.","Maintaining separate, untested DR environments."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) involves simulating a disaster to test the recovery process end-to-end, which is vital for validating RTO/RPO and team readiness. Option B (correct) allows for controlled injection of faults to test specific failure scenarios and application resilience, directly addressing anti-pattern [ap-3.2-001] by proactively testing. Option C (incorrect) is a common anti-pattern ([ap-3.2-001]) as documentation alone does not guarantee a plan''s effectiveness in a real disaster. Option D, performing security audits, is important for security but not for validating DR plan execution. Option E (incorrect) suggests an untested plan, which is precisely what anti-pattern [ap-3.2-001] warns against.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  82,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_3_87',
  'Which of the following DR strategies involve maintaining a minimal, continuously updated environment in a secondary Region, requiring some manual effort to fully scale up during a disaster? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Pilot light","Warm standby","Backup and restore","Multi-site active/active","Cold standby"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) describes a pilot light strategy where a minimal core infrastructure (like databases) is running and continuously replicated, but the full application environment needs to be provisioned and scaled up. Option B (correct) describes a warm standby strategy, which is a scaled-down but functional version of the application running, also requiring some scaling up. Both require some manual effort to fully scale up. Option C, Backup and restore, involves restoring from backups and provisioning all infrastructure, which is a higher RTO/RPO. Option D, Multi-site active/active, has fully operational environments in multiple Regions and requires minimal manual effort for failover. Option E, Cold standby, involves provisioning all infrastructure only after a disaster, leading to the highest RTO.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  83,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_3',
  'medium'
);

