INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_2_90',
  'An organization is setting up a new multi-account AWS environment and wants to establish a strong security baseline from the start. They plan to use AWS Control Tower to provision and govern these accounts. Order the steps to successfully set up AWS Control Tower in a new AWS Organization.',
  'ordering',
  '["Enroll existing or provision new AWS accounts into the Control Tower landing zone.","Configure the necessary AWS Organizations structure, including the management account.","Launch AWS Control Tower and define the landing zone configuration (e.g., Regions, OUs).","Review and enable recommended guardrails to enforce compliance policies."]'::json,
  '{1,2,3,0}',
  'Setting up AWS Control Tower in a new AWS Organization requires a specific sequence of foundational and configuration steps. Option B (Configure the necessary AWS Organizations structure, including the management account) is the prerequisite, as Control Tower operates within an Organization. Next, Option C (Launch AWS Control Tower and define the landing zone configuration) establishes the core Control Tower environment. Option D (Review and enable recommended guardrails to enforce compliance policies) then activates the preventative and detective controls. Finally, Option A (Enroll existing or provision new AWS accounts into the Control Tower landing zone) brings individual accounts under Control Tower''s governance, ensuring they adhere to the defined baseline.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  54,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_2_91',
  'A security team needs to ensure that all newly provisioned AWS accounts in their organization adhere to a set of compliance rules, such as requiring encryption for S3 buckets and EC2 EBS volumes. They decide to use AWS Config conformance packs for multi-rule compliance assessment. Order the steps to deploy a conformance pack across multiple accounts in an AWS Organization.',
  'ordering',
  '["Deploy the conformance pack as an AWS CloudFormation StackSet from the management account.","Define a YAML template for the conformance pack, specifying the AWS Config rules and remediation actions.","Ensure AWS Config is enabled in all target accounts and Regions.","Create an IAM role in the management account with permissions to deploy StackSets and AWS Config resources."]'::json,
  '{3,2,1,0}',
  'Deploying an AWS Config conformance pack across multiple accounts in an AWS Organization using StackSets requires careful preparation. Option D (Create an IAM role in the management account with permissions to deploy StackSets and AWS Config resources) is the initial step, providing the necessary administrative privileges. Next, Option C (Ensure AWS Config is enabled in all target accounts and Regions) is a prerequisite for the conformance pack to function. Option B (Define a YAML template for the conformance pack) then specifies the compliance rules and desired state. Finally, Option A (Deploy the conformance pack as an AWS CloudFormation StackSet from the management account) leverages the StackSet capability to provision the conformance pack consistently across the target accounts and Regions.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  55,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_2_92',
  'An enterprise wants to automate the provisioning of new AWS accounts for development teams, ensuring each account comes with a predefined security baseline and access controls. They plan to use AWS Service Catalog to offer ''Account Provisioning'' as a product. Order the steps to create and offer this automated account provisioning product.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create a Service Catalog product linked to the CloudFormation template.","Define an AWS CloudFormation template that provisions a new AWS account, applies SCPs, and sets up initial IAM roles.","Create a portfolio in Service Catalog, associate the product, and grant access to an ''Account Creator'' IAM role.","Test the Service Catalog product by provisioning a new account and verifying its configuration."]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}',
  'Automating account provisioning with AWS Service Catalog involves defining the account structure, making it available, and then testing. Option B (Define an AWS CloudFormation template that provisions a new AWS account, applies SCPs, and sets up initial IAM roles) is the core definition of the ''product''. Next, Option A (Create a Service Catalog product linked to the CloudFormation template) registers this definition within Service Catalog. Option C (Create a portfolio in Service Catalog, associate the product, and grant access to an ''Account Creator'' IAM role) makes the product discoverable and provisionable by authorized users. Finally, Option D (Test the Service Catalog product by provisioning a new account and verifying its configuration) ensures the automation works as expected before broader release.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  56,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_2_93',
  'A company operating a multi-account AWS environment needs to enforce strict security policies across all accounts, such as preventing the creation of unencrypted Amazon S3 buckets or disallowing specific AWS Regions. They decide to use Service Control Policies (SCPs) within AWS Organizations. Order the steps to effectively implement SCPs.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create the SCPs with the desired permissions or denial statements.","Attach the SCPs to the organizational units (OUs) or individual accounts.","Enable all features in AWS Organizations.","Test the SCPs by attempting to perform restricted actions in target accounts."]'::json,
  '{2,0,1,3}',
  'Implementing Service Control Policies (SCPs) in AWS Organizations requires a specific order to ensure they are effective. Option C (Enable all features in AWS Organizations) is a prerequisite, as SCPs are only available when all features are enabled. Next, Option A (Create the SCPs with the desired permissions or denial statements) defines the actual policies. Option B (Attach the SCPs to the organizational units (OUs) or individual accounts) then applies these policies to the target scope. Finally, Option D (Test the SCPs by attempting to perform restricted actions in target accounts) is crucial to verify that the policies are enforced as intended and do not inadvertently block legitimate operations.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  57,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_2_94',
  'A large enterprise wants to centralize identity management for its multi-account AWS environment, allowing users to sign in once and access all authorized accounts and applications. They plan to implement AWS IAM Identity Center (successor to AWS SSO). Order the steps to set up IAM Identity Center for multi-account access.',
  'ordering',
  '["Configure permission sets to define access levels for users and groups in target accounts.","Enable AWS IAM Identity Center in the AWS Organizations management account.","Assign users and groups to permission sets and specific AWS accounts.","Connect your identity source (e.g., AWS IAM Identity Center directory, Active Directory, Okta)."]'::json,
  '{1,3,0,2}',
  'Setting up AWS IAM Identity Center for centralized multi-account access follows a clear sequence. Option B (Enable AWS IAM Identity Center in the AWS Organizations management account) is the foundational step to activate the service. Next, Option D (Connect your identity source) integrates your existing user directory or uses the built-in directory. Option A (Configure permission sets to define access levels for users and groups in target accounts) then defines what users can do in which accounts. Finally, Option C (Assign users and groups to permission sets and specific AWS accounts) grants the actual access, linking identities to their authorized roles across the organization.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  58,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_2_95',
  'A security operations team needs to centrally monitor security findings and compliance status across all accounts in their AWS Organization. They decide to enable and configure AWS Security Hub to aggregate and analyze security data. Order the steps to deploy AWS Security Hub across a multi-account environment.',
  'ordering',
  '["Enable Security Hub in the designated administrator account.","Delegate a Security Hub administrator account in AWS Organizations.","Enable Security Hub in all member accounts.","Configure standards (e.g., AWS Foundational Security Best Practices) and custom insights."]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}',
  'Deploying AWS Security Hub in a multi-account environment requires a hierarchical setup. Option B (Delegate a Security Hub administrator account in AWS Organizations) is the initial and crucial step, establishing a central point for management. Next, Option A (Enable Security Hub in the designated administrator account) activates the service in the central account. Option C (Enable Security Hub in all member accounts) then extends the service''s coverage across the entire organization. Finally, Option D (Configure standards and custom insights) customizes the security checks and reporting to meet specific organizational requirements, ensuring comprehensive security monitoring.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  59,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_2_96',
  'A company wants to ensure that all AWS resources provisioned across their multi-account environment are consistently tagged for cost allocation and operational management. They plan to use AWS Config rules with remediation actions to enforce tagging policies. Order the steps to implement automated tagging enforcement.',
  'ordering',
  '["Deploy the AWS Config rule and remediation action across target accounts using CloudFormation StackSets.","Define the desired tagging policy (e.g., ''Environment'' tag required for all EC2 instances).","Create an AWS Config custom rule (e.g., using a Lambda function) to evaluate resource compliance against the tagging policy.","Develop an AWS Systems Manager Automation document for the remediation action (e.g., adding a default ''Environment'' tag)."]'::json,
  '{1,2,3,0}',
  'Implementing automated tagging enforcement with AWS Config and Systems Manager involves defining the policy, creating detection mechanisms, and then setting up remediation. Option B (Define the desired tagging policy) is the foundational step, clearly stating the tagging requirements. Next, Option C (Create an AWS Config custom rule) provides the mechanism to detect non-compliant resources. Option D (Develop an AWS Systems Manager Automation document for the remediation action) then defines how non-compliant resources will be automatically corrected. Finally, Option A (Deploy the AWS Config rule and remediation action across target accounts using CloudFormation StackSets) ensures consistent enforcement across the entire multi-account environment.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
  60,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_37_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_37_m_37_2_97',
  'A security team needs to deploy Amazon GuardDuty across their entire AWS Organization to continuously monitor for malicious activity and unauthorized behavior. They want to centralize GuardDuty management in a delegated administrator account. Order the steps to enable and configure GuardDuty across a multi-account environment.',
  'ordering',
  '["Enable GuardDuty in the designated delegated administrator account.","Delegate a GuardDuty administrator account in AWS Organizations.","Enable GuardDuty in all member accounts through the delegated administrator account.","Review GuardDuty findings in the administrator account and integrate with other security services."]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}',
  'Deploying Amazon GuardDuty in a multi-account environment requires a specific hierarchical setup. Option B (Delegate a GuardDuty administrator account in AWS Organizations) is the initial and crucial step, establishing a central point for management. Next, Option A (Enable GuardDuty in the designated delegated administrator account) activates the service in the central account. Option C (Enable GuardDuty in all member accounts through the delegated administrator account) then extends the service''s coverage across the entire organization. Finally, Option D (Review GuardDuty findings in the administrator account and integrate with other security services) allows for centralized security monitoring and response.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain37_8',
  NOW(),
