INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_1_59',
  'A DevOps team is designing a CI/CD pipeline using AWS CodePipeline. Which components are essential for defining the build process and executing custom commands? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS CodeBuild","AWS CodeDeploy","AWS CodeCommit","AWS Lambda","Amazon S3"]'::json,
  '{0,3}',
  'Option A (AWS CodeBuild) is a fully managed continuous integration service that compiles source code, runs tests, and produces software packages that are ready to deploy, making it essential for defining and executing build processes. Option D (AWS Lambda) can be used within a CodePipeline as a custom action to execute arbitrary code or commands, offering flexibility for specific build or processing steps. Option B (AWS CodeDeploy) is for automating application deployments, not for the build process itself. Option C (AWS CodeCommit) is a source control service. Option E (Amazon S3) is typically used for storing artifacts, not for executing build commands.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  25,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_1',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_1_60',
  'When implementing CI/CD pipelines with AWS CodePipeline, which elements are fundamental for structuring the workflow and managing data flow between stages? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Pipeline stages","AWS CodeCommit repositories","Actions","AWS CodeDeploy deployment groups","AWS CodeBuild buildspecs"]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'Option A (Pipeline stages) are logical divisions in a pipeline, such as Source, Build, Test, and Deploy, that organize the workflow. Option C (Actions) are operations performed in a stage, such as compiling code, running tests, or deploying an application, and they process input artifacts and produce output artifacts. Option B (AWS CodeCommit repositories) are used as a source for the pipeline but are not structural elements of the pipeline workflow itself. Option D (AWS CodeDeploy deployment groups) are part of CodeDeploy, which is an action provider, not a fundamental structural element of CodePipeline. Option E (AWS CodeBuild buildspecs) define the build process within a CodeBuild action, but are not CodePipeline''s structural elements.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  26,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_1',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_1_61',
  'To enhance the security of an AWS CodePipeline, a DevOps engineer needs to ensure that each stage has only the necessary permissions. Which IAM best practices should be applied? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Using a single IAM role with administrator access for the entire pipeline","Granting least privilege permissions to each CodePipeline action''s IAM role","Attaching inline policies directly to IAM users for pipeline access","Creating separate IAM roles for each CodePipeline stage with scoped permissions","Relying solely on security groups to restrict pipeline access"]'::json,
  '{1,3}',
  'Option B (Granting least privilege permissions to each CodePipeline action''s IAM role) is a fundamental security best practice, ensuring that roles only have the permissions required for their specific tasks. Option D (Creating separate IAM roles for each CodePipeline stage with scoped permissions) further refines least privilege by isolating permissions per stage, reducing the blast radius if a role is compromised. Option A (Using a single IAM role with administrator access for the entire pipeline) is an anti-pattern (ap-1.1-001) that creates a large blast radius and violates the principle of least privilege. Option C (Attaching inline policies directly to IAM users for pipeline access) is not the recommended way to manage pipeline permissions, which should be done via roles assumed by CodePipeline. Option E (Relying solely on security groups to restrict pipeline access) is incorrect as security groups control network traffic, not IAM permissions.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  27,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_1',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_1_62',
  'A development team uses AWS CodeBuild for their CI/CD pipeline. Which features can they leverage to optimize build times and manage dependencies efficiently? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Batch builds","Buildspec.yml caching","Manual approvals","Cross-account deployments","CodeDeploy lifecycle hooks"]'::json,
  '{1,0}',
  'Option B (Buildspec.yml caching) allows CodeBuild to cache dependencies and intermediate build artifacts, significantly reducing subsequent build times. Option A (Batch builds) enables running multiple builds in parallel or in a sequence, which can optimize overall pipeline execution time for projects with many components. Option C (Manual approvals) are for pipeline gates, not build optimization. Option D (Cross-account deployments) is a pipeline capability, not a CodeBuild build optimization feature. Option E (CodeDeploy lifecycle hooks) are specific to CodeDeploy, not CodeBuild.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  28,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_1',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_1_63',
  'When configuring AWS CodeDeploy, which elements are used to define the deployment process and specify actions to be taken during different phases of a deployment? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AppSpec file","Buildspec.yml","Lifecycle hooks","AWS CodeCommit triggers","Amazon S3 bucket policies"]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'Option A (AppSpec file) is a YAML-formatted file that CodeDeploy uses to manage a deployment. It specifies the source files to be copied, where to copy them, and permissions. Option C (Lifecycle hooks) are scripts or functions defined in the AppSpec file that CodeDeploy runs at specific points during the deployment process (e.g., BeforeInstall, AfterInstall, ApplicationStop, ApplicationStart). Option B (Buildspec.yml) is used by AWS CodeBuild to define build commands, not CodeDeploy. Option D (AWS CodeCommit triggers) are for initiating pipelines based on source code changes. Option E (Amazon S3 bucket policies) are for access control to S3 buckets, not for defining deployment processes.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  29,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_1',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_1_64',
  'A company needs to deploy applications to multiple AWS accounts and regions using a single CI/CD pipeline. Which CodePipeline capabilities support this requirement? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Cross-account pipeline deployments","Manual approval actions","Cross-region pipeline deployments","AWS CodeBuild batch builds","AWS CodeDeploy in-place deployments"]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'Option A (Cross-account pipeline deployments) allows CodePipeline to deploy resources to target AWS accounts different from the account where the pipeline is defined, typically by assuming IAM roles. Option C (Cross-region pipeline deployments) enables deploying applications to different AWS Regions from a single pipeline, often utilizing S3 cross-region replication for artifacts. Option B (Manual approval actions) are for human gates in the pipeline, not for multi-account/region deployment. Option D (AWS CodeBuild batch builds) optimize CodeBuild execution but don''t directly enable cross-account/region deployment. Option E (AWS CodeDeploy in-place deployments) is a deployment strategy, not a cross-account/region capability of CodePipeline.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  30,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_1',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_1_65',
  'Which AWS services are commonly used to store and retrieve sensitive information, such as API keys and database credentials, for use within an AWS CodeBuild project? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Secrets Manager","AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store","Amazon S3","AWS CodeCommit","Amazon CloudWatch Logs"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (AWS Secrets Manager) is a dedicated service for securely storing and managing secrets, offering features like automatic rotation and fine-grained access control. Option B (AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store) provides secure, hierarchical storage for configuration data management and secrets, also supporting encryption. Both services integrate well with CodeBuild to inject secrets into the build environment. Option C (Amazon S3) is object storage and not designed for managing secrets securely within a pipeline. Option D (AWS CodeCommit) is a source control service. Option E (Amazon CloudWatch Logs) is for logging, not secret management.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  31,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_1',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_1_66',
  'A DevOps team is designing a CI/CD pipeline using AWS CodePipeline. They need to define the build steps, including compiling code, running unit tests, and packaging artifacts. Which AWS services and configurations are essential for achieving these requirements? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS CodeBuild with a buildspec.yml file.","AWS CodeDeploy for managing the build environment.","AWS Lambda functions to execute compilation tasks.","Amazon S3 for storing source code prior to build.","AWS CodeCommit for storing build logs and reports."]'::json,
  '{0,3}',
  'Option A (correct) is the primary service for running build and test jobs in a CI/CD pipeline. The `buildspec.yml` file defines all the commands and instructions for the build process, including compilation, testing, and artifact packaging. Option D (correct) is typically used as the artifact store for CodePipeline, where source code from CodeCommit (or other sources) is placed before being consumed by CodeBuild, and where CodeBuild places its output artifacts. Option B (incorrect) is used for deploying applications, not for managing the build environment or executing build steps. Option C (incorrect) can be used for small, event-driven tasks, but CodeBuild is specifically designed and optimized for comprehensive build and test workloads within a CI/CD pipeline. Option E (incorrect) is a source code repository; build logs and reports are typically stored in Amazon S3 or streamed to Amazon CloudWatch Logs.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  32,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_1_67',
  'A company uses AWS CodeDeploy for its application deployments. To minimize manual intervention during failed deployments and ensure application stability, which configurations should the DevOps team implement? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Configuring automated rollback triggers based on Amazon CloudWatch alarms.","Implementing manual approval stages in AWS CodePipeline before each deployment.","Using an in-place deployment strategy for all critical production applications.","Defining lifecycle hooks in the AppSpec file to validate application health.","Disabling health checks during deployment to speed up the process."]'::json,
  '{0,3}',
  'Option A (correct) directly addresses anti-pattern ap-1.1-002 by enabling CodeDeploy to automatically roll back a deployment if specified Amazon CloudWatch alarms (e.g., for error rates, latency, or CPU utilization) breach thresholds, preventing prolonged service disruption. Option D (correct) allows custom scripts to be run at various points during a deployment, which can include health checks or validation steps. If these hooks fail, CodeDeploy can be configured to stop or roll back the deployment. Option B (incorrect) adds a manual gate, which increases operational overhead and slows down the pipeline, rather than automating recovery from failures. Option C (incorrect) is a deployment strategy, but it''s not inherently about minimizing manual intervention during failures; blue/green deployments often offer better rollback capabilities. Option E (incorrect) is a dangerous practice that can lead to deploying faulty applications to production without detection, increasing the risk of outages.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  33,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_1_68',
  'When securing AWS CodePipeline, which practices align with the principle of least privilege for pipeline roles and secrets management? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Creating separate IAM roles for each CodePipeline stage with only necessary permissions.","Storing all secrets, including database credentials, directly in the CodeBuild buildspec.yml file.","Using AWS Secrets Manager or AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store to manage sensitive credentials.","Granting a single, broad IAM role with administrator access to the entire CodePipeline.","Allowing CodeBuild projects to access all S3 buckets in the account by default."]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'Option A (correct) adheres to the principle of least privilege by ensuring that each stage of the pipeline (e.g., source, build, deploy) has only the permissions required for its specific actions, limiting the blast radius if a role is compromised. Option C (correct) is the recommended best practice for secrets management, providing secure storage and retrieval of sensitive information, which can then be injected into CodeBuild environments at runtime. Option B (incorrect) is a significant security vulnerability as it exposes sensitive data in plain text within the build definition, making it easily accessible and compromising security. Option D (incorrect) represents anti-pattern ap-1.1-001. Granting a single role with administrator access violates the principle of least privilege and creates a large blast radius if compromised. Option E (incorrect) also violates the principle of least privilege by granting overly broad access to S3, which is a security risk.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  34,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_1_69',
  'A DevOps team needs to set up a CI/CD pipeline for an application that requires building Docker images, running integration tests, and deploying to an Amazon ECS cluster. Which AWS services are best suited for the build and deployment stages of this pipeline? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS CodeBuild for building Docker images and running tests.","AWS CodeDeploy for deploying containerized applications to Amazon ECS.","AWS Lambda for orchestrating the entire build and deployment process.","Amazon S3 for executing build commands and managing dependencies.","AWS CodeCommit for storing Docker images after they are built."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is designed for compiling source code, running tests, and producing software packages, including building Docker images. It uses a `buildspec.yml` file to define these steps. Option B (correct) supports deployments to Amazon ECS, enabling various deployment strategies like blue/green for containerized applications. Option C (incorrect) is a serverless compute service suitable for short-lived, event-driven functions, not for orchestrating a full CI/CD pipeline or managing complex build processes. AWS CodePipeline is the orchestration service. Option D (incorrect) is an object storage service; it stores artifacts but does not execute build commands or manage dependencies. Option E (incorrect) is a source code repository; Docker images are stored in Amazon ECR.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  35,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_1_70',
  'To ensure high availability and disaster recovery for a critical application, a company plans to deploy its application to multiple AWS regions using AWS CodePipeline. Which considerations are important for implementing cross-region pipeline deployments? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Configuring cross-region replication for Amazon S3 buckets storing pipeline artifacts.","Using separate AWS CodeCommit repositories in each region for source code.","Ensuring IAM roles for CodePipeline and CodeBuild have permissions to resources in all target regions.","Implementing AWS CodeArtifact to manage cross-region replication of container images.","Manually triggering deployments in each region to ensure proper synchronization."]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'Option A (correct) is crucial for making pipeline artifacts (like build outputs or deployment packages) available in all target regions, ensuring that deployments can proceed even if the primary region is unavailable. Option C (correct) is essential for security and functionality. The IAM roles used by CodePipeline and its integrated services (like CodeBuild and CodeDeploy) must have the necessary permissions to interact with resources in all AWS regions where the application is being deployed. Option B (incorrect) is generally not recommended; a single CodeCommit repository can be used, and source code is typically pulled from the primary region. Replicating source code itself across regions is often handled by the version control system or through a single source of truth. Option D (incorrect) is wrong; AWS CodeArtifact manages application packages, not container images, and does not provide native cross-region replication for container images. Amazon ECR is for container images, and cross-region replication for ECR is not a native feature. Option E (incorrect) introduces manual steps, which defeats the purpose of an automated CI/CD pipeline and increases the risk of human error and delays.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  36,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_1_71',
  'When designing an AWS CodePipeline for deploying applications, which components are fundamental for defining the workflow, executing actions, and managing artifacts? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Pipeline stages, which group actions into logical phases.","CodeDeploy AppSpec files for defining the pipeline structure.","Actions, which perform tasks like building, testing, or deploying.","AWS CodeCommit for orchestrating pipeline execution.","AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store for storing pipeline artifacts."]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'Option A (correct) are the logical divisions of a pipeline, such as Source, Build, Test, and Deploy, that organize actions into sequential phases. Option C (correct) are the specific tasks performed within each stage, such as fetching source code from CodeCommit, building with CodeBuild, or deploying with CodeDeploy. Option B (incorrect) is used by AWS CodeDeploy to manage deployments to instances, not for defining the overall CodePipeline structure. Option D (incorrect) is a source code repository; AWS CodePipeline itself orchestrates pipeline execution. Option E (incorrect) is for storing configuration data and secrets, not for storing pipeline artifacts, which are typically stored in Amazon S3.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  37,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_1_72',
  'A DevOps team is configuring an AWS CodeDeploy deployment group for an Amazon EC2 application. To ensure that the application remains available during deployments and can automatically recover from failures, which CodeDeploy features should they leverage? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Implementing a blue/green deployment strategy to shift traffic gradually.","Defining lifecycle hooks in the AppSpec file to perform validation and health checks.","Using an in-place deployment strategy for minimal infrastructure changes.","Disabling automatic rollbacks to allow manual intervention for critical issues.","Configuring a single deployment group for all environments (dev, test, prod)."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is a deployment strategy that minimizes downtime by deploying the new version to a separate set of instances (green environment) and then shifting traffic from the old (blue) environment to the new one. This allows for easy rollback if issues are detected. Option B (correct) allows custom scripts to be run at various points during the deployment lifecycle (e.g., beforeInstall, ApplicationStart, ValidateService). These hooks can be used to perform health checks and validation, and if a hook fails, CodeDeploy can automatically stop or roll back the deployment. Option C (incorrect) is a deployment strategy where the application is updated directly on existing instances. While it has minimal infrastructure changes, it typically involves downtime and has higher risk during failures compared to blue/green. Option D (incorrect) represents anti-pattern ap-1.1-002. Disabling automatic rollbacks increases the time to recovery and requires manual intervention, which is not ideal for ensuring application stability. Option E (incorrect) is generally not recommended as it mixes environments with different requirements and risks, making it harder to manage and secure.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  38,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_1_73',
  'When configuring AWS CodePipeline for cross-account deployments, which security and access management considerations are crucial to ensure secure and efficient operations? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Defining IAM roles in the target account with trust policies allowing the source account''s CodePipeline role to assume them.","Granting the source account''s CodePipeline role full administrator access to all target accounts.","Using AWS Secrets Manager to store cross-account IAM credentials directly in the pipeline definition.","Configuring Amazon S3 bucket policies in the target account to allow artifact access from the source account.","Manually deploying artifacts to target accounts to avoid complex IAM configurations."]'::json,
  '{0,3}',
  'Option A (correct) is the standard and most secure method for cross-account access. The target account defines IAM roles with specific permissions, and a trust policy allows the CodePipeline service role from the source account to assume these roles, enabling cross-account actions. Option D (correct) is necessary for CodePipeline to access and store artifacts (e.g., build outputs, deployment packages) in S3 buckets located in the target account. The bucket policy must grant permissions to the source account''s CodePipeline or assumed role. Option B (incorrect) represents anti-pattern ap-1.1-001. Granting full administrator access violates the principle of least privilege and creates a significant security risk across accounts. Option C (incorrect) is generally not recommended. While Secrets Manager is for secrets, using it to store cross-account IAM credentials that are then directly used by the pipeline is less secure than IAM role assumption. Option E (incorrect) defeats the purpose of an automated CI/CD pipeline, increases operational overhead, and is prone to human error.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  39,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_1_74',
  'A DevOps team is setting up a new CI/CD pipeline using AWS CodePipeline for a mission-critical application. The pipeline involves multiple stages: Source, Build, Test, and Deploy. The team needs to ensure that the pipeline has the necessary permissions to interact with various AWS services (e.g., AWS CodeCommit for source, AWS CodeBuild for building, AWS CodeDeploy for deployment) while adhering to the principle of least privilege. Additionally, they want to prevent a single compromised role from granting excessive access across the entire pipeline. Which two IAM best practices should the team implement? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Create a separate IAM service role for each CodePipeline action, granting only the specific permissions required for that action.","Configure a single IAM role with administrator access for the entire CodePipeline to simplify permission management.","Utilize AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store to manage all IAM role ARNs and dynamically assign them during pipeline execution.","Attach IAM policies directly to individual AWS CodePipeline users, granting them permissions to manage pipeline resources.","Define resource-level permissions in IAM policies, restricting access to specific CodeCommit repositories, CodeBuild projects, and CodeDeploy applications."]'::json,
  '{0,4}',
  'Option A (correct) is a core best practice for securing CodePipeline. By creating a separate IAM service role for each CodePipeline action (e.g., a role for the CodeBuild action, a role for the CodeDeploy action), and granting only the specific permissions required for that action, the principle of least privilege is enforced. This significantly reduces the blast radius if any single role is compromised. Option D (correct) complements the least privilege principle by further restricting access. Defining resource-level permissions in IAM policies ensures that even if a role has permission for an action (e.g., `codebuild:StartBuild`), it can only perform that action on specified resources (e.g., a particular CodeBuild project ARN), rather than all CodeBuild projects. This granular control is crucial for multi-service and multi-team environments. Option B (incorrect) directly violates the anti-pattern ap-1.1-001, which states that using a single pipeline role with admin access instead of least-privilege per stage creates a large blast radius if compromised. This approach is a significant security risk and should be avoided. Option C (incorrect) While AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store can be used for configuration management, it is not the primary mechanism for managing IAM role assignments within CodePipeline. CodePipeline''s action configurations directly specify the IAM role to assume. Storing ARNs in Parameter Store doesn''t inherently enforce least privilege or prevent broad access. Option E (incorrect) Attaching IAM policies directly to individual AWS CodePipeline users grants them permissions to *manage* pipeline resources, but it does not define the *runtime* permissions for the pipeline itself when it executes actions. The pipeline''s execution permissions are governed by the service roles assumed by each action.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  40,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_1_75',
  'A development team uses AWS CodeBuild within their AWS CodePipeline for building and testing their applications. They are experiencing slow build times due to repeated downloads of dependencies and long-running test suites. The team wants to optimize their CodeBuild projects to reduce build duration and improve efficiency. Which two CodeBuild features should the team implement to achieve these goals? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Configure build caching to store dependencies and intermediate build artifacts in an Amazon S3 bucket.","Utilize batch builds to run multiple buildspecs or test phases in parallel within a single build project.","Implement AWS CodeDeploy deployment strategies to speed up the deployment phase after the build.","Use AWS CodeArtifact to host private package repositories and proxy public ones, reducing external dependency fetches.","Increase the compute capacity of the CodeBuild environment by selecting a larger instance type."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) directly addresses the issue of slow build times due to repeated dependency downloads. CodeBuild''s caching feature allows dependencies and intermediate build artifacts to be stored in an Amazon S3 bucket or locally within the build environment. This significantly reduces subsequent build times by reusing previously downloaded or generated files. Option B (correct) addresses the issue of long-running test suites. CodeBuild batch builds allow a single build project to run multiple builds concurrently. This is particularly useful for parallelizing test phases (e.g., unit tests, integration tests) or building multiple components simultaneously, thus reducing the overall time taken for the build stage. Option C (incorrect) AWS CodeDeploy deployment strategies (like blue/green or in-place) are relevant for the *deployment* phase of the CI/CD pipeline, not the *build* phase. While they optimize deployment, they do not impact the duration of the CodeBuild process itself. Option D (incorrect) While AWS CodeArtifact can help with dependency management and reduce external fetches, it''s a separate service that integrates with CodeBuild. The question asks for CodeBuild features specifically. CodeArtifact helps with dependency availability and security, which indirectly speeds up builds, but CodeBuild''s native caching is a more direct feature for optimizing build duration within the CodeBuild project itself. Option E (incorrect) Increasing the compute capacity (e.g., using `build.general1.large` instead of `build.general1.small`) can indeed speed up CPU-bound tasks within a build. However, it is often a more expensive solution and doesn''t address the root causes of slow builds like repeated dependency downloads or sequential test execution as effectively as caching and batch builds do.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  41,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_1_76',
  'A DevOps team manages a critical web application deployed on Amazon EC2 instances using AWS CodeDeploy. They have implemented an in-place deployment strategy. Recently, several deployments have failed due to application errors detected shortly after traffic was shifted to the new version, requiring manual rollback procedures. The team wants to automate rollbacks to minimize downtime and manual intervention when application health issues are detected post-deployment. Which two configurations should the team implement in AWS CodeDeploy? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Configure CodeDeploy deployment groups to use Amazon CloudWatch alarms as rollback triggers based on application health metrics.","Implement a blue/green deployment strategy instead of in-place deployments to simplify manual rollbacks.","Define CodeDeploy lifecycle hooks in the AppSpec file to execute pre-traffic and post-traffic validation scripts.","Integrate AWS Lambda functions to monitor deployment status and manually initiate rollbacks if errors occur.","Set up AWS Systems Manager Automation documents to automatically revert to the previous application version upon deployment failure."]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'Option A (correct) is a direct and effective way to automate rollbacks. CodeDeploy deployment groups can be configured with rollback triggers that monitor Amazon CloudWatch alarms. By setting up alarms on critical application health metrics (e.g., high error rates, increased latency, CPU utilization) that indicate a deployment failure, CodeDeploy can automatically initiate a rollback to the previous stable version when an alarm state is breached. This directly addresses the need for automated rollbacks to minimize downtime. Option C (correct) provides the mechanism to detect issues early and potentially prevent them, which complements automated rollbacks. CodeDeploy lifecycle hooks, defined in the AppSpec file, allow custom scripts to be run at various stages of a deployment. By implementing pre-traffic and post-traffic validation scripts (e.g., health checks, integration tests), the team can verify application health before and after traffic is shifted. If these scripts fail, CodeDeploy can be configured to automatically roll back, preventing unhealthy deployments from reaching users. Option B (incorrect) While a blue/green deployment strategy is excellent for minimizing downtime and simplifying rollbacks (by simply terminating the new environment), the question specifically asks for solutions within the context of *automating rollbacks* for an *in-place* deployment strategy and detecting issues *post-deployment*. Blue/green is a different deployment strategy, not a direct configuration for automating rollbacks within an existing in-place setup, although it''s a good alternative for future consideration. Option D (incorrect) Integrating AWS Lambda functions for manual rollback initiation contradicts the goal of *automating* rollbacks and minimizing *manual intervention*. The scenario explicitly states that manual rollbacks are already an issue. Option E (incorrect) AWS Systems Manager Automation documents can be used for various operational tasks, including rollbacks. However, CodeDeploy has built-in features (CloudWatch triggers and lifecycle hooks) specifically designed for automated rollbacks during deployments, which are more tightly integrated and efficient for this particular use case than a separate Automation document.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  42,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_1_77',
  'A global software company operates CI/CD pipelines for various applications across multiple AWS accounts and regions. Their main development account hosts the AWS CodePipeline definitions, while applications are deployed to separate production accounts in different regions. The DevOps team needs to ensure secure and efficient cross-account and cross-region deployments, specifically for the deployment stage using AWS CodeDeploy. Which two configurations are essential to enable this setup while adhering to security best practices? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Configure IAM roles in the target production accounts with trust policies allowing the CodePipeline service role from the development account to assume them.","Create a single IAM role with administrator access in the development account and replicate it across all production accounts.","Use AWS Organizations Service Control Policies (SCPs) to grant CodePipeline cross-account access to CodeDeploy in all target accounts.","Ensure CodePipeline artifact buckets in the development account have bucket policies granting read access to CodeDeploy service roles in target accounts.","Deploy a dedicated AWS CodePipeline instance in each production account and manually synchronize pipeline definitions."]'::json,
  '{0,3}',
  'Option A (correct) is a fundamental security mechanism for cross-account access. In the target production accounts, IAM roles must be created with trust policies that explicitly allow the CodePipeline service role (or a specific action role) from the development account to assume them. These roles in the target account would then have the necessary permissions to interact with CodeDeploy and other resources within that production account, adhering to the principle of least privilege. Option D (correct) is crucial for cross-account and cross-region deployments. CodePipeline stores build artifacts in an Amazon S3 bucket, typically in the same account as the pipeline. For CodeDeploy in a different account/region to access these artifacts, the S3 artifact bucket in the development account must have a bucket policy that explicitly grants read permissions to the CodeDeploy service role (or the assumed role) in the target production account. This ensures CodeDeploy can retrieve the deployment package. Option B (incorrect) directly violates the anti-pattern ap-1.1-001, which states that using a single pipeline role with admin access instead of least-privilege per stage creates a large blast radius if compromised. Replicating an admin role across accounts is a severe security anti-pattern. Each role should have least privilege for its specific task and target account. Option C (incorrect) AWS Organizations Service Control Policies (SCPs) define maximum permissions for accounts within an organization. They do not grant permissions; rather, they restrict them. Therefore, SCPs cannot be used to grant CodePipeline cross-account access to CodeDeploy. IAM roles and policies are the correct mechanisms for granting permissions. Option E (incorrect) Deploying a dedicated AWS CodePipeline instance in each production account and manually synchronizing definitions would introduce significant operational overhead, complexity, and potential for inconsistencies. The goal of CodePipeline is to orchestrate deployments from a central point, often across accounts and regions, rather than duplicating pipelines.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  43,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_1_78',
  'A DevOps team is implementing a CI/CD pipeline for a new serverless application using AWS Lambda, managed by AWS CodePipeline and AWS CodeDeploy. The application requires sensitive API keys and database credentials during the build and deployment phases. The team needs a secure and automated way to inject these secrets into the CodeBuild environment and make them available to CodeDeploy without hardcoding them in the source code or buildspec files. Which two AWS services should the team leverage for secure secrets management in this pipeline? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Secrets Manager to store and retrieve sensitive credentials during CodeBuild and CodeDeploy actions.","AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store to store non-sensitive configuration data and potentially encrypted secrets.","AWS CodeCommit to store encrypted secret files directly within the application''s source code repository.","Amazon S3 to store encrypted secret files in a private bucket, accessible via IAM roles.","AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS) to encrypt secrets stored directly in the buildspec.yml file."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is a dedicated service for managing, retrieving, and rotating secrets. AWS Secrets Manager is ideal for storing sensitive API keys and database credentials. CodeBuild can directly integrate with Secrets Manager to retrieve secrets and inject them as environment variables during the build process. CodeDeploy can also be configured to access secrets from Secrets Manager for deployment-specific tasks, ensuring that sensitive information is never hardcoded. Option B (correct) AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store can store both plain-text and encrypted parameters. For secrets, it can store them as `SecureString` parameters, encrypted using AWS KMS. CodeBuild can retrieve these parameters and inject them as environment variables. While Secrets Manager is generally preferred for highly sensitive secrets requiring rotation, Parameter Store is a robust and often simpler solution for many secret management needs within CI/CD pipelines, especially for non-rotating secrets or configuration that needs to be encrypted. Option C (incorrect) Storing encrypted secret files directly within an AWS CodeCommit (Git) repository is an anti-pattern. Even if encrypted, secrets should not reside in source control, as it complicates rotation, audit, and access control, and increases the risk of exposure if the repository is compromised. Dedicated secret management services are preferred. Option D (incorrect) While Amazon S3 can store encrypted files, it is not a dedicated secret management service. It lacks features like automatic rotation, fine-grained access policies specifically for secrets, and native integration for injecting secrets into build environments as easily as Secrets Manager or Parameter Store. Custom logic would be required to retrieve and decrypt. Option E (incorrect) AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS) is used for encryption, but it''s not a secret storage service itself. Encrypting secrets directly in the `buildspec.yml` file is insecure and an anti-pattern, as the encrypted secret would still be exposed in the build definition, and the KMS key ID would also need to be managed. Secrets should be stored in a dedicated secret management service and retrieved at runtime.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  44,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_1_79',
  'A financial services company requires strict control over software deployments to its production environment. All changes must undergo a final manual review and approval by a security officer before being deployed to production. Their existing CI/CD pipeline uses AWS CodePipeline for orchestration. The DevOps team needs to integrate this manual approval step into the pipeline while ensuring that the process is auditable and prevents unauthorized deployments. Which two CodePipeline features should the team implement? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Add a manual approval action to the CodePipeline stage immediately preceding the production deployment stage.","Configure AWS CodeDeploy to automatically pause deployments and wait for manual confirmation from a security officer.","Integrate AWS Lambda functions to send notifications for approval requests and process approval responses.","Utilize AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store to store an approval token that must be manually updated before deployment.","Set up an IAM policy that requires multi-factor authentication (MFA) for the CodePipeline service role to execute the deployment action."]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'Option A (correct) is the primary way to implement manual approvals in AWS CodePipeline. By adding a ''Manual approval'' action to a stage (e.g., ''Pre-Production-Approval'') immediately before the production deployment stage, the pipeline will pause and wait for an authorized user to manually approve or reject the change. This directly addresses the requirement for a final manual review. Option C (correct) enhances the manual approval process. While CodePipeline handles the approval action itself, integrating AWS Lambda functions (often triggered by Amazon SNS notifications from the approval action) allows for custom notification mechanisms (e.g., Slack, email to a specific group) and can process approval responses (e.g., via a custom web interface or chatbot). This makes the approval workflow more efficient and auditable, ensuring security officers are promptly notified and can act. Option B (incorrect) AWS CodeDeploy does not have a native feature to automatically pause deployments and wait for manual confirmation from a security officer as part of its deployment strategies. CodeDeploy focuses on the execution of the deployment itself. The manual approval mechanism is handled at the CodePipeline orchestration level. Option D (incorrect) While AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store can store configuration, using it for an ''approval token'' that needs manual updating is a less integrated and auditable approach compared to CodePipeline''s native manual approval action. It would require custom logic to check the token and pause the pipeline, adding unnecessary complexity. Option E (incorrect) An IAM policy requiring MFA for the CodePipeline service role is not a mechanism for manual approval of *deployments*. It would require the *service role* itself to perform MFA, which is not how service roles operate. MFA is typically for human users accessing the console or making API calls, not for service-to-service communication or automated pipeline execution.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  45,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_1_80',
  'A DevOps team is developing a CI/CD pipeline for a new application using AWS CodePipeline. The application consists of several independent microservices, each with its own build and test requirements. To optimize resource utilization and accelerate the overall pipeline execution time, the team wants to run the build and unit test phases for these microservices concurrently within the CodeBuild stage. Which two CodeBuild features should the team leverage to achieve this parallel execution? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Utilize CodeBuild batch builds to run multiple buildspecs or commands in parallel within a single build project.","Configure separate CodeBuild projects for each microservice and integrate them as parallel actions in the CodePipeline build stage.","Increase the compute capacity of the CodeBuild environment by selecting a larger instance type to handle concurrent tasks.","Implement CodeBuild caching to store intermediate build artifacts and dependencies, reducing the need for parallel execution.","Use AWS Lambda functions to orchestrate parallel execution of individual build commands within a CodeBuild project."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is a direct CodeBuild feature designed for this purpose. CodeBuild batch builds allow a single build project to run multiple builds concurrently, each with its own `buildspec.yml` or a specific set of commands. This is ideal for microservices where each can be built and tested independently, significantly reducing the overall build phase duration. Option B (correct) is another effective strategy for parallelizing builds. By creating separate CodeBuild projects for each microservice, the team can then add these projects as parallel actions within a single stage of AWS CodePipeline. CodePipeline will then trigger all these CodeBuild projects simultaneously, achieving concurrent execution of builds and tests for different microservices. Option C (incorrect) Increasing the compute capacity of a single CodeBuild environment primarily speeds up sequential tasks within that environment. While it might help if a single build is CPU-bound, it doesn''t inherently enable parallel execution of *multiple* independent microservice builds/tests in the same way batch builds or parallel CodePipeline actions do. It''s a scaling solution for a single task, not a parallelization solution for multiple tasks. Option D (incorrect) CodeBuild caching helps reduce build times by reusing dependencies and artifacts from previous builds. While it optimizes individual build durations, it does not enable or facilitate the *parallel execution* of multiple microservice builds or test phases. It''s a performance optimization, not a parallelization strategy. Option E (incorrect) Using AWS Lambda functions to orchestrate parallel execution *within* a CodeBuild project is overly complex and unnecessary. CodeBuild''s native batch build feature (Option A) is specifically designed for this purpose and is much simpler to implement and manage.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  46,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_1_81',
  'When designing an AWS CodePipeline, which components are essential for defining the workflow and passing data between stages? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Stages","Artifacts","Buildspec.yml","AppSpec file","Lifecycle hooks"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) represents the logical divisions in a CodePipeline, such as Source, Build, Test, and Deploy, which define the sequence of actions. Option B (correct) are the files or data produced by an action in one stage and consumed by an action in a subsequent stage, ensuring data flow throughout the pipeline. Option C (incorrect) is a configuration file specific to AWS CodeBuild, defining build commands and settings. Option D (incorrect) is a configuration file specific to AWS CodeDeploy, defining deployment parameters. Option E (incorrect) are scripts or commands executed at specific points during a CodeDeploy deployment lifecycle.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  47,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_1_82',
  'Which AWS CodeDeploy features are designed to minimize downtime and provide automated recovery from deployment failures? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["In-place deployment strategy","Blue/Green deployment strategy","Manual approval actions","Automated rollback triggers","Single pipeline role with admin access"]'::json,
  '{1,3}',
  'Option B (correct) allows deploying a new version of an application to a separate set of instances while the old version continues to serve traffic. Once validated, traffic is shifted, enabling zero-downtime deployments and easy rollback by simply shifting traffic back to the old environment. Option D (correct) is crucial for automated recovery. CodeDeploy can be configured with rollback triggers tied to Amazon CloudWatch alarms, automatically initiating a rollback if error rates or latency breach predefined thresholds (anti-pattern ap-1.1-002 if not implemented). Option A (incorrect) involves deploying the new application version directly onto existing instances, which typically incurs downtime during the deployment process. Option C (incorrect) introduces a manual gate, which delays recovery and increases operational overhead, rather than automating it. Option E (incorrect) is a security anti-pattern (ap-1.1-001) related to IAM permissions, not a deployment or recovery feature.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  48,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_1_83',
  'To enhance the security of an AWS CodePipeline, which practices should be implemented to manage permissions and sensitive data? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Use a single pipeline role with admin access for simplicity.","Store sensitive credentials directly in the buildspec.yml file.","Implement least-privilege IAM roles for each pipeline stage.","Utilize AWS Secrets Manager to store and retrieve secrets securely.","Rely solely on manual approvals for critical deployment stages."]'::json,
  '{2,3}',
  'Option C (correct) ensures that each AWS CodePipeline action and stage has only the necessary permissions to perform its specific task, adhering to the principle of least privilege. This significantly reduces the blast radius if a role is compromised (anti-pattern ap-1.1-001 if not followed). Option D (correct) is the recommended AWS service for securely storing, managing, and retrieving sensitive information like database credentials, API keys, and other secrets, integrating seamlessly with CodePipeline and CodeBuild. Option A (incorrect) is an anti-pattern (ap-1.1-001) as a single role with broad permissions creates a large blast radius if compromised. Option B (incorrect) is a severe security vulnerability, as hardcoding secrets exposes them in plain text within your source code repository. Option E (incorrect) is a valid practice for certain scenarios but does not directly address managing permissions or sensitive data within the pipeline''s execution context.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  49,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_1',
  'medium'
);

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_36_4
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_4_59',
  'A DevOps team wants to deploy a new version of an AWS Lambda function with minimal risk. Which AWS CodeDeploy features can help achieve this by gradually shifting traffic? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["In-place deployment","Linear deployment","All-at-once deployment","Canary deployment","Rolling update"]'::json,
  '{1,3}',
  'Option B (Linear deployment) is a CodeDeploy deployment type for Lambda functions that shifts traffic in equal increments with a wait time between each increment. Option D (Canary deployment) is another CodeDeploy deployment type for Lambda functions that shifts a small percentage of traffic first, then after a specified interval, shifts the remaining traffic. Both strategies allow for gradual traffic shifting and monitoring, minimizing risk. Option A (In-place deployment) is not applicable to Lambda functions as they are serverless. Option C (All-at-once deployment) shifts all traffic immediately, which is high risk. Option E (Rolling update) is typically for instance-based deployments, not Lambda.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  50,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_4',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_4_60',
  'When deploying containerized applications to Amazon ECS using AWS CodeDeploy, which deployment types allow for testing the new version before fully committing to it? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["In-place deployment","Blue/green deployment","All-at-once deployment","Canary deployment","Rolling update"]'::json,
  '{1,3}',
  'Option B (Blue/green deployment) for Amazon ECS with CodeDeploy allows you to deploy a new task set and test it thoroughly before routing production traffic to it, minimizing downtime and risk. Option D (Canary deployment) for Amazon ECS with CodeDeploy allows you to gradually shift a small portion of traffic to the new task set, monitor its performance, and then progressively increase traffic. Option A (In-place deployment) is not a valid deployment type for ECS with CodeDeploy. Option C (All-at-once deployment) immediately replaces all tasks, which is high risk. Option E (Rolling update) is a default ECS deployment strategy but CodeDeploy offers more advanced traffic shifting for ECS.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  51,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_4',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_4_61',
  'A DevOps team wants to ensure that failed deployments to Amazon EC2 instances are automatically reverted to a stable state. Which AWS CodeDeploy features enable automated rollback? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Manual approval actions in CodePipeline","Configuring rollback triggers based on CloudWatch alarms","Implementing a blue/green deployment strategy","Using AWS CodeBuild for post-deployment validation","Disabling application health checks"]'::json,
  '{1,2}',
  'Option B (Configuring rollback triggers based on CloudWatch alarms) allows CodeDeploy to automatically initiate a rollback if specified CloudWatch alarms (e.g., high error rates, high CPU utilization) are breached during or after a deployment. Option C (Implementing a blue/green deployment strategy) inherently supports easy rollbacks by simply shifting traffic back to the original, stable environment if issues are detected in the new ''green'' environment. Option A (Manual approval actions in CodePipeline) requires human intervention and is not automated. Option D (Using AWS CodeBuild for post-deployment validation) is for testing, not for automated rollback. Option E (Disabling application health checks) would prevent detection of issues, making rollbacks harder.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  52,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_4',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_4_62',
  'Which of the following are key benefits of using a blue/green deployment strategy with AWS CodeDeploy for applications on Amazon EC2? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Minimizing downtime during deployments","Reducing the number of EC2 instances required","Simplifying the rollback process in case of issues","Allowing for in-place updates of application code","Eliminating the need for application testing"]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'Option A (Minimizing downtime during deployments) is a primary benefit of blue/green, as traffic is shifted from the old environment to the new one only after the new environment is fully operational. Option C (Simplifying the rollback process in case of issues) is another key advantage, as you can quickly revert to the previous stable ''blue'' environment by simply shifting traffic back. Option B (Reducing the number of EC2 instances required) is incorrect; blue/green typically requires at least double the instances temporarily. Option D (Allowing for in-place updates of application code) describes an in-place deployment, not blue/green. Option E (Eliminating the need for application testing) is false; testing is still crucial, often performed in the ''green'' environment before traffic shift.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  53,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_4',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_4_63',
  'A DevOps engineer is configuring an AWS CodeDeploy deployment for an application running on Amazon EC2. Which sections are typically included in the AppSpec file to define the deployment? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["artifacts","hooks","resources","build","test"]'::json,
  '{1,2}',
  'Option B (hooks) is a critical section in the AppSpec file that defines scripts to run at various stages of the deployment lifecycle (e.g., BeforeInstall, ApplicationStart). Option C (resources) is another essential section that specifies the target resources for the deployment, such as an Amazon EC2 instance or an Amazon ECS service. Option A (artifacts) is not a standard top-level section in the AppSpec file for EC2 deployments; artifacts are typically managed by CodePipeline. Option D (build) and Option E (test) sections are typically found in a buildspec.yml file for AWS CodeBuild, not an AppSpec file for CodeDeploy.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  54,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_4',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_4_64',
  'When deploying AWS Lambda functions using AWS CodeDeploy, which types of traffic shifting configurations are available to gradually roll out new versions? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["All-at-once","Linear","In-place","Canary","Rolling"]'::json,
  '{1,3}',
  'Option B (Linear) is a CodeDeploy traffic shifting option for Lambda that shifts traffic in equal increments with a wait time between each increment. Option D (Canary) is another CodeDeploy traffic shifting option for Lambda that shifts a small percentage of traffic first, then after a specified interval, shifts the remaining traffic. Option A (All-at-once) is a traffic shifting option, but it is not gradual. Option C (In-place) and Option E (Rolling) are deployment types typically associated with instance-based deployments, not Lambda functions.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  55,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_4',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_4_65',
  'A DevOps team wants to implement automated monitoring for their AWS CodeDeploy deployments to detect issues quickly. Which AWS services can be integrated with CodeDeploy to trigger rollbacks based on application health? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon CloudWatch","AWS Lambda","Amazon S3","AWS CodeCommit","AWS Systems Manager"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Amazon CloudWatch) is directly integrated with CodeDeploy to provide metrics and alarms. CloudWatch alarms can be configured as rollback triggers for CodeDeploy deployments. Option B (AWS Lambda) can be used as a custom function within CodeDeploy lifecycle hooks or invoked by CloudWatch alarms to perform custom validation or initiate rollbacks programmatically. Option C (Amazon S3) is for object storage, not monitoring. Option D (AWS CodeCommit) is for source control. Option E (AWS Systems Manager) offers various operational tools but is not the primary service for direct health-based rollback triggers in CodeDeploy.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  56,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_4',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_4_66',
  'A company wants to deploy a new version of its application to Amazon EC2 instances using AWS CodeDeploy. They need to ensure minimal downtime and the ability to quickly roll back if the new version introduces issues. Which deployment strategies offered by CodeDeploy are best suited for these requirements? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Blue/Green deployment strategy.","In-place deployment strategy.","All-at-once deployment strategy.","Canary deployment strategy.","Rolling update deployment strategy."]'::json,
  '{0,3}',
  'Option A (correct) involves deploying the new application version to a completely separate set of instances, then redirecting traffic from the old environment to the new one. This provides minimal downtime and an easy, fast rollback by simply reverting traffic to the old environment. Option D (correct) is a controlled way to release a new version to a small subset of users or instances first, monitoring its performance, and then gradually rolling it out to the rest. This minimizes impact and allows for quick rollback if issues arise during the initial rollout. Option B (incorrect) updates the application directly on the existing instances, which typically involves downtime during the update process and can be risky if a rollback is needed. Option C (incorrect) is a type of in-place deployment where all instances are updated simultaneously, leading to downtime and high risk during failures. Option E (incorrect) is a generic term for updating instances in batches; while it reduces downtime compared to all-at-once, blue/green and canary offer superior rollback capabilities and risk mitigation.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  57,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_4',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_4_67',
  'A DevOps team is deploying a new version of a containerized application to an Amazon ECS cluster using AWS CodeDeploy. They need to ensure that the deployment process updates the task definition and shifts traffic to the new tasks with minimal disruption. Which CodeDeploy deployment types are designed for this scenario? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Blue/Green deployment for Amazon ECS.","In-place deployment for Amazon ECS.","Rolling update with Amazon ECS native capabilities.","Canary deployment for Amazon ECS.","All-at-once deployment for Amazon ECS."]'::json,
  '{0,3}',
  'Option A (correct) is a deployment type specifically supported by AWS CodeDeploy for Amazon ECS. It deploys the new application version to a new set of ECS tasks, shifts traffic, and allows for easy rollback, ensuring minimal downtime. Option D (correct) is another advanced deployment type supported by CodeDeploy for Amazon ECS. It gradually shifts traffic to the new version, allowing for phased rollouts and monitoring before a full deployment, minimizing the blast radius of potential issues. Option B (incorrect) is not a supported deployment type for Amazon ECS with CodeDeploy; CodeDeploy manages ECS deployments by creating new task sets. Option C (incorrect) refers to native ECS rolling updates, which are different from CodeDeploy''s advanced deployment strategies and typically involve updating tasks in batches without the advanced traffic shifting and rollback capabilities of CodeDeploy''s blue/green or canary. Option E (incorrect) is a basic deployment approach that updates all tasks at once, which can lead to downtime and higher risk during failures.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  58,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_4',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_4_68',
  'When deploying AWS Lambda functions using AWS CodeDeploy, which aspects of the deployment process are managed by CodeDeploy to ensure a controlled rollout and easy rollback? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Traffic shifting between different versions of the Lambda function.","Automatic creation and management of Lambda aliases.","Direct modification of the Lambda function''s code in place.","Defining the Lambda function''s runtime and memory settings.","Executing unit tests on the Lambda function during deployment."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is a core feature of CodeDeploy for Lambda deployments. It allows for controlled traffic shifting (e.g., linear, canary) between the old and new versions of a Lambda function, minimizing risk during deployment. Option B (correct) is also managed by CodeDeploy. It automatically creates and updates Lambda aliases to point to the correct function versions during traffic shifting, simplifying version management. Option C (incorrect) is not how CodeDeploy works for Lambda; it deploys new versions of the function, rather than modifying the code in place. Option D (incorrect) is part of the Lambda function''s configuration, typically defined in the CloudFormation template or directly in the Lambda console, not directly managed by CodeDeploy during the deployment process. Option E (incorrect) is typically handled in a preceding build or test stage (e.g., using AWS CodeBuild) in the CI/CD pipeline, not directly by CodeDeploy during the deployment phase.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  59,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_4',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_4_69',
  'A DevOps team is planning a blue/green deployment for an Amazon EC2 application using AWS CodeDeploy. Which steps are crucial for successfully implementing this strategy with minimal downtime and a robust rollback mechanism? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Provisioning a new set of instances (green environment) for the new application version.","Updating the application directly on the existing production instances (blue environment).","Configuring a load balancer to shift traffic between the blue and green environments.","Disabling health checks on the green environment to speed up traffic shifting.","Manually rolling back the deployment if issues are detected in the green environment."]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'Option A (correct) is fundamental to blue/green deployments, as it ensures the new application version is deployed to a completely isolated environment, preventing interference with the current production system. Option C (correct) is essential for managing traffic flow. A load balancer (e.g., Application Load Balancer) is used to gradually or instantly shift traffic from the old (blue) environment to the new (green) environment, and to revert traffic in case of issues. Option B (incorrect) describes an in-place deployment, which is contrary to the blue/green strategy. Option D (incorrect) is a risky practice that could lead to deploying a faulty application to production; health checks are critical for validating the new environment before traffic is shifted. Option E (incorrect) is a manual step; CodeDeploy can be configured with automated rollback triggers based on CloudWatch alarms, which is a best practice (anti-pattern ap-1.1-002).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  60,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_4',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_4_70',
  'A DevOps team needs to ensure that their application deployments to Amazon ECS clusters are highly resilient and can automatically revert to a stable state if issues are detected. Which AWS CodeDeploy features support these requirements? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Automated rollback triggers based on Amazon CloudWatch alarms.","Blue/Green deployment type for Amazon ECS.","In-place deployment strategy for ECS tasks.","Manual approval steps in CodePipeline to prevent automatic rollbacks.","Disabling health checks in the AppSpec file to speed up deployments."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is a critical feature for resilience. CodeDeploy can be configured to automatically roll back a deployment if specified Amazon CloudWatch alarms (e.g., for error rates, latency) breach thresholds, ensuring a quick return to a stable state. This directly addresses anti-pattern ap-1.1-002. Option B (correct) is a deployment type for Amazon ECS that deploys the new application version to a new set of tasks, shifts traffic, and allows for easy and quick rollback to the previous stable version if issues are detected. Option C (incorrect) is not a supported deployment strategy for Amazon ECS with CodeDeploy; CodeDeploy manages ECS deployments by creating new task sets. Option D (incorrect) introduces manual intervention and delays recovery, which is contrary to ensuring automatic recovery. Option E (incorrect) is a dangerous practice that can lead to deploying faulty applications without detection, increasing the risk of outages.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  61,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_4',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_4_71',
  'A company requires immutable deployments for its Amazon EC2 applications, meaning that new versions are deployed to entirely new instances, and old instances are terminated. Which AWS services and practices facilitate this approach? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS CodeDeploy with a blue/green deployment strategy.","AWS CodeBuild to create new Amazon Machine Images (AMIs) for each deployment.","AWS CodeDeploy with an in-place deployment strategy.","Manually provisioning new EC2 instances for each deployment.","Using AWS Systems Manager to update existing EC2 instances."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is a deployment strategy that deploys the new application version to a completely new set of instances (green environment). Once validated, traffic is shifted, and the old instances (blue environment) can be terminated, achieving immutability. Option B (correct) can be used to automate the creation of new AMIs containing the latest application code and dependencies. These AMIs can then be used to launch new, immutable EC2 instances for deployments. Option C (incorrect) updates the application directly on existing instances, which is a mutable deployment approach. Option D (incorrect) is a manual process that increases operational overhead and is prone to human error, which is an anti-pattern for automated CI/CD. Option E (incorrect) is used for managing and patching existing instances, which supports mutable infrastructure, not immutable deployments.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  62,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_4',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_4_72',
  'A DevOps team is deploying a new version of a Lambda function using AWS CodeDeploy. They want to ensure that the new version is rolled out gradually and automatically rolled back if any errors are detected. Which CodeDeploy features enable this functionality? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Configuring a canary or linear traffic shifting deployment for the Lambda function.","Setting up automated rollback triggers based on Amazon CloudWatch alarms.","Manually updating the Lambda function alias to point to the new version.","Using an in-place deployment strategy for the Lambda function.","Disabling pre-traffic and post-traffic hooks to speed up the deployment."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) allows for a controlled rollout of the new Lambda function version by gradually shifting a percentage of traffic to it over time (linear) or in stages (canary), minimizing the impact of potential issues. Option B (correct) is crucial for automatic recovery. CodeDeploy can be configured to automatically roll back to the previous stable Lambda version if Amazon CloudWatch alarms (e.g., for error rates, invocations errors) are triggered, directly addressing anti-pattern ap-1.1-002. Option C (incorrect) is a manual process that bypasses the controlled traffic shifting and rollback capabilities of CodeDeploy, increasing risk and operational overhead. Option D (incorrect) is not a concept applicable to Lambda functions in the same way as EC2 instances; Lambda deployments involve creating new versions and shifting aliases. Option E (incorrect) removes critical validation steps that ensure the health of the new Lambda version before and after traffic shifting, increasing the risk of deploying faulty code.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  63,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_4',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_4_73',
  'A DevOps team is deploying a new containerized application to Amazon ECS using AWS CodeDeploy. The application is critical, and any downtime during deployment must be minimized. The team also needs a robust rollback mechanism in case of deployment failures or issues detected post-deployment. Which two capabilities of AWS CodeDeploy should the team leverage to meet these requirements for their Amazon ECS deployments? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Implement a blue/green deployment strategy to shift traffic gradually and maintain the old environment for easy rollback.","Utilize in-place deployment to update containers directly on existing ECS tasks, minimizing new resource provisioning.","Configure CodeDeploy lifecycle hooks to run validation tests and automatically roll back if tests fail.","Use AWS Systems Manager Automation documents to manually trigger rollbacks when issues are identified.","Integrate Amazon CloudWatch alarms with CodeDeploy to automatically roll back based on application health metrics."]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'Option A (correct) is a key capability of AWS CodeDeploy for Amazon ECS that directly addresses minimizing downtime and providing a robust rollback mechanism. A blue/green deployment strategy involves deploying the new version to a separate, identical environment (green) while the old version (blue) remains active. Traffic is then shifted gradually or instantly to the green environment. If issues arise, traffic can be instantly reverted to the stable blue environment, providing near-zero downtime rollbacks. Option C (correct) enhances the reliability of deployments and enables automated rollbacks. CodeDeploy allows defining lifecycle hooks in the AppSpec file for Amazon ECS deployments. These hooks can execute custom AWS Lambda functions at various stages (e.g., `BeforeAllowTraffic`, `AfterAllowTraffic`) to run validation tests or health checks. If a lifecycle hook fails, CodeDeploy can be configured to automatically roll back the deployment, preventing unhealthy versions from becoming fully active. Option B (incorrect) In-place deployment is a strategy where the application is updated directly on the existing instances or tasks. While CodeDeploy supports in-place deployments for EC2, it is not the primary or recommended strategy for Amazon ECS when minimizing downtime and robust rollbacks are critical. Blue/green (Option A) is specifically designed for these goals with containers. In-place deployments for ECS are less common and typically less robust for critical applications. Option D (incorrect) Using AWS Systems Manager Automation documents for *manual* rollbacks contradicts the goal of *automating* rollbacks to minimize downtime and manual intervention. CodeDeploy offers built-in automation for rollbacks. Option E (incorrect) While integrating Amazon CloudWatch alarms for automated rollbacks is a valid and highly recommended practice for CodeDeploy (as seen in EC2 deployments), the prompt asks for *two capabilities of AWS CodeDeploy*. CloudWatch alarms are an external service that CodeDeploy integrates with, not an inherent *capability* of CodeDeploy itself in the same way blue/green strategy or lifecycle hooks are. However, it''s a very strong distractor as it''s a common and effective pattern.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  64,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_4',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_4_74',
  'A development team is deploying a new version of their microservice to Amazon ECS using AWS CodeDeploy. They want to perform a controlled rollout where a small percentage of production traffic is shifted to the new version first, allowing them to monitor its performance and error rates before fully committing to the deployment. If any issues are detected, they need to be able to automatically roll back. Which two CodeDeploy deployment types and configurations should the team utilize for this scenario? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Configure a blue/green deployment with a linear traffic shifting strategy for the Amazon ECS service.","Implement an in-place deployment strategy with a custom rollback trigger based on Amazon CloudWatch alarms.","Set up a canary deployment using the `CodeDeployDefault.ECSLinear10PercentEvery1Minute` traffic shifting option.","Define CodeDeploy lifecycle hooks to run extensive integration tests on the new task set before any traffic is shifted.","Use an all-at-once deployment type and rely on manual monitoring for issues before a full cutover."]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'Option A (correct) describes the core mechanism for controlled rollouts with Amazon ECS. AWS CodeDeploy''s blue/green deployment type for ECS inherently supports traffic shifting. A linear traffic shifting strategy allows the team to gradually route a small percentage of production traffic to the new task set, observe its behavior, and then incrementally increase traffic. This is a form of canary release and enables controlled rollouts. Option C (correct) is a specific example of a canary deployment strategy that directly fits the requirement. CodeDeploy provides predefined traffic shifting options, such as `CodeDeployDefault.ECSLinear10PercentEvery1Minute`, which shifts traffic in 10% increments every minute. This allows for a controlled, gradual rollout to a small percentage of users, enabling monitoring and automatic rollback if issues are detected, which is the essence of a canary deployment. Option B (incorrect) In-place deployment is not suitable for this scenario. It updates existing tasks directly, making controlled traffic shifting to a *new* version difficult and rollbacks less seamless compared to blue/green or canary strategies for ECS. While CloudWatch alarms can trigger rollbacks, the underlying deployment strategy doesn''t meet the controlled rollout requirement. Option D (incorrect) Defining CodeDeploy lifecycle hooks to run extensive integration tests on the new task set *before* any traffic is shifted is a good practice for validating the new environment. However, it addresses pre-traffic validation, not the controlled *shifting of traffic* to a small percentage of users for real-world monitoring, which is the core of the question. The question focuses on the traffic shifting aspect. Option E (incorrect) An all-at-once deployment type shifts all traffic to the new version immediately. This directly contradicts the requirement for a controlled rollout with a small percentage of traffic shifted first. Relying on manual monitoring for issues in an all-at-once deployment increases the risk of widespread impact if problems occur.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  65,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_4',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_4_75',
  'A DevOps team is deploying a new version of an AWS Lambda-based microservice using AWS CodeDeploy. The team needs to ensure that the new Lambda function version is thoroughly validated with real traffic before it fully replaces the old version. They also require a mechanism to automatically revert to the previous stable version if any errors or performance degradation are detected during the traffic shifting process. Which two CodeDeploy features should the team implement for this serverless deployment? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Configure CodeDeploy to use a canary or linear traffic shifting strategy for the Lambda alias.","Define CodeDeploy lifecycle hooks to invoke validation Lambda functions during traffic shifting.","Implement an in-place deployment strategy for the Lambda function to minimize resource provisioning.","Use AWS Systems Manager Automation documents to manually trigger rollbacks based on monitoring data.","Set up Amazon CloudWatch alarms to monitor Lambda error rates and latency, triggering automatic rollbacks."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is a core capability of AWS CodeDeploy for AWS Lambda. CodeDeploy enables controlled traffic shifting for Lambda functions using aliases. The team can choose a canary (e.g., `Canary10Percent5Minutes`) or linear (e.g., `Linear10PercentEvery1Minute`) traffic shifting strategy. This allows a small percentage of invocations to be routed to the new function version, enabling real-time validation before a full cutover, directly addressing the requirement for thorough validation with real traffic. Option B (correct) complements traffic shifting by providing automated validation. CodeDeploy allows defining lifecycle hooks (e.g., `BeforeAllowTraffic`, `AfterAllowTraffic`) for Lambda deployments. These hooks can invoke other Lambda functions (validation functions) to perform checks on the new version during the traffic shifting process. If a validation function fails, CodeDeploy can be configured to automatically roll back the deployment, ensuring that only healthy versions are fully promoted. Option C (incorrect) In-place deployment is not applicable to AWS Lambda functions in the same way it is for EC2 instances. Lambda functions are versioned, and CodeDeploy manages traffic shifting between versions using aliases, not by modifying existing function code directly in place. Option D (incorrect) Using AWS Systems Manager Automation documents for *manual* rollbacks contradicts the requirement for *automatic* reversion upon error detection. CodeDeploy provides built-in automation for rollbacks during traffic shifting. Option E (incorrect) While setting up Amazon CloudWatch alarms to monitor Lambda error rates and latency is a critical part of detecting issues and *triggering* rollbacks, the question asks for *CodeDeploy features* to implement. The alarms themselves are a CloudWatch feature, which CodeDeploy *integrates* with for rollback triggers, but they are not a CodeDeploy feature in the same sense as traffic shifting strategies or lifecycle hooks.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  66,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_4',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_4_76',
  'A DevOps team is deploying a critical microservice to Amazon ECS using AWS CodeDeploy. They need to ensure that the deployment process is robust, with minimal impact on users, and includes automated mechanisms to detect and revert failed deployments. The team wants to implement a strategy that allows them to validate the new version with actual traffic before a full cutover and automatically roll back if performance metrics degrade. Which two CodeDeploy configurations should the team implement? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Configure a blue/green deployment strategy with a linear or canary traffic shifting option.","Define CodeDeploy lifecycle hooks to run validation tests on the new task set during the deployment process.","Implement an in-place deployment strategy and rely on manual monitoring for post-deployment issues.","Set up AWS Systems Manager State Manager to automatically revert to the previous ECS task definition on failure.","Use Amazon CloudWatch alarms to monitor application performance metrics and trigger automatic rollbacks via CodeDeploy."]'::json,
  '{0,4}',
  'Option A (correct) is the most effective strategy for achieving minimal impact and validating with actual traffic for Amazon ECS. A blue/green deployment strategy allows the new version to be deployed to a separate environment. CodeDeploy then facilitates traffic shifting, which can be configured as linear (e.g., 10% every 1 minute) or canary (e.g., 10% for 5 minutes). This enables the team to gradually expose the new version to users, monitor its behavior, and then decide to fully shift traffic or roll back. Option E (correct) is crucial for automated detection and rollback based on performance degradation. AWS CodeDeploy integrates with Amazon CloudWatch alarms. By setting up CloudWatch alarms to monitor critical application performance metrics (e.g., error rates, latency, CPU utilization) for the new task set, CodeDeploy can be configured to automatically trigger a rollback to the previous stable version if any of these alarms breach their thresholds during or after traffic shifting. Option B (incorrect) Defining CodeDeploy lifecycle hooks to run validation tests is an excellent practice for ensuring the new task set is functional *before* traffic is shifted. However, the question specifically asks for validating with *actual traffic* and rolling back if *performance metrics degrade*. While lifecycle hooks can trigger rollbacks, CloudWatch alarms (Option E) are more directly tied to real-time performance monitoring and degradation. Option C (incorrect) An in-place deployment strategy updates existing tasks directly, which typically involves more downtime and does not provide the controlled traffic shifting or easy rollback capabilities of a blue/green strategy. Relying on manual monitoring for post-deployment issues contradicts the goal of automated detection and reversion. Option D (incorrect) AWS Systems Manager State Manager is used for configuring and maintaining the state of EC2 instances or other resources. It is not the primary service for orchestrating application deployments or managing automated rollbacks for Amazon ECS services. CodeDeploy''s built-in rollback mechanisms are more appropriate.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  67,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_4',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_4_77',
  'A DevOps team is managing a critical serverless application built with AWS Lambda. They need to implement a robust deployment strategy that minimizes the risk of introducing errors into production and allows for quick recovery. Specifically, they want to ensure that a new Lambda function version is thoroughly tested with a small portion of live traffic before a full rollout, and that automated rollbacks occur if any issues are detected. Which two CodeDeploy features should the team configure for their AWS Lambda deployments? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Configure a linear or canary traffic shifting deployment preference for the Lambda alias.","Define CodeDeploy lifecycle hooks to invoke validation Lambda functions at various stages of traffic shifting.","Implement an all-at-once deployment strategy for the Lambda function to ensure rapid updates.","Use AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store to store the previous Lambda version ARN for manual rollbacks.","Set up AWS CodeGuru Reviewer to analyze the Lambda function code for security vulnerabilities before deployment."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is a fundamental capability of AWS CodeDeploy for AWS Lambda. By configuring a linear (e.g., `Linear10PercentEvery1Minute`) or canary (e.g., `Canary10Percent5Minutes`) traffic shifting deployment preference, CodeDeploy gradually routes a small percentage of live invocations to the new Lambda function version. This allows the team to monitor its performance and error rates with real traffic before a full cutover, minimizing risk. Option B (correct) complements traffic shifting by enabling automated validation and rollback. CodeDeploy allows defining lifecycle hooks (e.g., `BeforeAllowTraffic`, `AfterAllowTraffic`) for Lambda deployments. These hooks can invoke other Lambda functions (validation functions) to perform health checks, integration tests, or custom metrics analysis on the new version during the traffic shifting process. If a validation function fails, CodeDeploy can automatically roll back the deployment, preventing unhealthy versions from reaching full production. Option C (incorrect) An all-at-once deployment strategy shifts 100% of traffic to the new Lambda version immediately. This directly contradicts the requirement for testing with a *small portion of live traffic* first and minimizes the ability to detect issues gradually, thus increasing risk. Option D (incorrect) While AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store can store configuration, using it for manual rollbacks is not an automated solution and doesn''t leverage CodeDeploy''s built-in capabilities for automatic rollbacks during traffic shifting. CodeDeploy handles version management and rollback automatically when configured with traffic shifting and alarms. Option E (incorrect) AWS CodeGuru Reviewer is a service for static code analysis to find security vulnerabilities and code quality issues. While valuable for code quality, it is performed *before* deployment and does not directly address the requirements for validating with *live traffic* or *automated rollbacks* during the deployment process itself.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  68,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_4',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_4_78',
  'Which AWS services can be directly targeted by AWS CodeDeploy for automated application deployments? (Select THREE.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon EC2 instances","Amazon ECS services","AWS Lambda functions","Amazon S3 buckets","AWS CodeCommit repositories"]'::json,
  '{0,1,2}',
  'Option A (correct) allows CodeDeploy to deploy applications to Amazon EC2 instances, including those managed by Auto Scaling groups. Option B (correct) enables CodeDeploy to manage deployments for applications running on Amazon Elastic Container Service (ECS). Option C (correct) supports deploying new versions of AWS Lambda functions, including traffic shifting capabilities. Option D (incorrect) is primarily for static website hosting or object storage, not for deploying dynamic applications that run on compute resources. Option E (incorrect) is a source control service and not a deployment target for applications.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  69,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_4',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_4_79',
  'Which are key advantages of implementing a Blue/Green deployment strategy with AWS CodeDeploy? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Reduced infrastructure costs due to fewer instances.","Zero downtime deployments for end-users.","Faster application build times.","Simplified and rapid rollback capabilities.","Automatic scaling of application instances."]'::json,
  '{1,3}',
  'Option B (correct) is a primary benefit of Blue/Green deployments, as the new version is deployed to a separate environment while the old version continues to serve traffic, eliminating service interruption. Option D (correct) is another significant advantage; if issues arise with the new version, traffic can be instantly shifted back to the stable ''blue'' environment, providing a quick and safe rollback. Option A (incorrect) is generally not true; Blue/Green deployments often require maintaining two full environments temporarily, which can increase infrastructure costs during the deployment phase. Option C (incorrect) relates to the build stage of a CI/CD pipeline and is not directly influenced by the deployment strategy. Option E (incorrect) is a feature of services like Auto Scaling groups or ECS, not an inherent benefit of the Blue/Green deployment strategy itself, although these services can be used in conjunction with Blue/Green deployments.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  70,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_4',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_4_80',
  'When deploying an application to Amazon EC2 instances using AWS CodeDeploy, which essential components are defined within the AppSpec file? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Build commands for the application.","Lifecycle hooks for deployment events.","Pipeline stages and actions.","Source files to be deployed to the instances.","IAM roles for CodeDeploy service permissions."]'::json,
  '{1,3}',
  'Option B (correct) defines scripts or commands that AWS CodeDeploy should execute at specific points during the deployment lifecycle (e.g., BeforeInstall, AfterInstall, ApplicationStart). Option D (correct) specifies which files from the application revision should be copied to the target instances and where they should be placed. Option A (incorrect) is typically defined in a buildspec.yml file for AWS CodeBuild, not the AppSpec file for CodeDeploy. Option C (incorrect) are defined in AWS CodePipeline, orchestrating the overall CI/CD workflow. Option E (incorrect) are configured in AWS IAM and assigned to the CodeDeploy service role, not defined within the AppSpec file.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  71,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_4',
  'medium'
);

