INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_3_88',
  'Order the following steps to set up AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery (DRS) for a server.',
  'ordering',
  '["Install the AWS Replication Agent on the source server","Configure replication settings for the source server","Add the source server to AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery","Launch recovery instances in the target Region"]'::json,
  '{2,0,1,3}',
  'The correct sequence for setting up AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery (DRS) begins with adding the source server to the DRS console (Option C). Once added, the AWS Replication Agent must be installed on the source server (Option A) to enable data replication. After the agent is installed and communicating, replication settings need to be configured (Option B) to define how data is replicated. Finally, in the event of a disaster or for testing, recovery instances can be launched in the target Region (Option D) from the replicated data.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  1,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_3',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_3_89',
  'Arrange the steps in the correct order to create a new backup plan using AWS Backup.',
  'ordering',
  '["Assign resources to the backup plan","Define backup rules, including frequency and retention","Create a backup vault to store backups","Name the new backup plan"]'::json,
  '{2,3,1,0}',
  'To create a backup plan in AWS Backup, you first need a destination for your backups, which is a backup vault (Option C). After the vault is ready, you name your backup plan (Option D) to identify it. Next, you define the backup rules (Option B), specifying details like backup frequency, retention period, and lifecycle policies. Finally, you assign the specific AWS resources (e.g., Amazon EC2 instances, Amazon RDS databases) that this plan should protect (Option A).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  2,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_3',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_3_90',
  'Order the basic steps to conduct a chaos engineering experiment using AWS Fault Injection Service (FIS).',
  'ordering',
  '["Define target resources for the experiment","Start the experiment template to inject faults","Create an experiment template in FIS","Choose fault actions to be injected"]'::json,
  '{0,3,2,1}',
  'The process of conducting a chaos engineering experiment with AWS Fault Injection Service (FIS) starts by defining the specific target resources (Option A) that will be affected by the experiment. Next, you choose the fault actions (Option D), such as stopping EC2 instances or throttling API calls, that you want to inject. These definitions are then combined into an experiment template (Option C), which is a reusable configuration. Finally, you start the experiment template (Option B) to execute the fault injection.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  3,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_3',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_3_91',
  'Place the following steps in the typical order for a disaster recovery (DR) testing cycle.',
  'ordering',
  '["Review and update DR runbooks and automation","Execute the planned DR drill","Define RTO (Recovery Time Objective) and RPO (Recovery Point Objective) objectives","Analyze results and identify gaps or areas for improvement"]'::json,
  '{2,1,3,0}',
  'A robust disaster recovery (DR) testing cycle begins with clearly defining the RTO and RPO objectives (Option C), which are critical business requirements for recovery. Once objectives are set, the DR drill is executed (Option B) to simulate a disaster and test the recovery process. After the drill, the results are analyzed to identify any gaps, failures, or areas for improvement (Option D). Based on this analysis, the DR runbooks and automation are reviewed and updated (Option A) to incorporate lessons learned and improve future recovery efforts.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  4,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_3',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_3_92',
  'Order the conceptual steps for implementing a pilot light disaster recovery strategy on AWS.',
  'ordering',
  '["Restore full application functionality and scale out","Maintain core infrastructure in the recovery region in a minimal, running state","Scale up resources in the recovery region when a disaster occurs","Continuously replicate data to the recovery region"]'::json,
  '{3,1,2,0}',
  'A pilot light disaster recovery strategy starts with continuously replicating data to a recovery region (Option D) to minimize data loss. Concurrently, core infrastructure components are maintained in the recovery region in a minimal, running state (Option B), ready to be scaled up. When a disaster occurs, the next step is to scale up the resources in the recovery region (Option C) from the pilot light state. Finally, after scaling, full application functionality is restored and scaled out to handle production load (Option A).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  5,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_3',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_3_93',
  'Arrange the conceptual steps for a warm standby disaster recovery strategy on AWS.',
  'ordering',
  '["Scale up the environment to full production capacity","Replicate data to a continuously running, scaled-down environment in the recovery region","Redirect user traffic to the recovery region''s environment","Perform regular testing of the scaled-down standby environment"]'::json,
  '{1,3,2,0}',
  'A warm standby disaster recovery strategy begins with replicating data to a continuously running, scaled-down environment in the recovery region (Option B), ensuring a near real-time copy of data and minimal infrastructure. Regular testing of this scaled-down standby environment (Option D) is crucial to ensure it functions as expected. In a disaster, user traffic is redirected to this recovery region (Option C). Finally, the environment is scaled up to full production capacity (Option A) to handle the workload.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  6,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_3',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_3_94',
  'Order the steps to validate the Recovery Point Objective (RPO) and Recovery Time Objective (RTO) for an application.',
  'ordering',
  '["Measure the actual recovery time","Define the acceptable RPO and RTO values for the application","Initiate a simulated disaster event or failover","Verify data consistency and integrity after recovery"]'::json,
  '{1,2,0,3}',
  'Validating RPO and RTO starts with clearly defining the acceptable RPO and RTO values for the application (Option B), which are the targets for recovery. Next, a simulated disaster event or failover is initiated (Option C) to test the recovery process. During and after this event, the actual recovery time is measured (Option A) to compare against the RTO. Finally, data consistency and integrity are verified after recovery (Option D) to ensure the RPO is met and no critical data is lost or corrupted.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  7,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_3',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_3_95',
  'Arrange the steps to restore a protected resource using AWS Backup.',
  'ordering',
  '["Select the desired recovery point (backup)","Choose the restore location or target resource","Initiate the restore job","Identify the protected resource to be restored"]'::json,
  '{3,0,1,2}',
  'To restore a resource using AWS Backup, the first step is to identify the specific protected resource that needs to be restored (Option D). Once identified, you select the desired recovery point or backup (Option A) from which to restore. Next, you choose the restore location or target resource (Option B), which could be the original location or a new one. Finally, you initiate the restore job (Option C) to begin the data recovery process.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  8,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_3',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_3_96',
  'Order the steps to automate a disaster recovery runbook using AWS Systems Manager.',
  'ordering',
  '["Document the manual recovery steps for the application","Convert the documented steps into AWS Systems Manager Automation documents","Test the automated runbook to ensure it functions correctly","Integrate the automation with monitoring for automatic invocation"]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'Automating a disaster recovery runbook begins by thoroughly documenting the existing manual recovery steps for the application (Option A). These documented steps are then converted into structured AWS Systems Manager Automation documents (Option B), which define the automated actions. After creation, the automated runbook must be rigorously tested (Option C) to verify its correctness and reliability. Finally, for proactive recovery, the automation can be integrated with monitoring systems (Option D) to trigger automatically upon detecting a disaster event.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  9,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_3',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_3_97',
  'Order the steps to configure a basic backup plan for an Amazon RDS database using AWS Backup.',
  'ordering',
  '["Assign resources to the backup plan.","Define a backup plan with rules.","Monitor backup jobs and recovery points.","Create a backup vault."]'::json,
  '{3,1,0,2}',
  'To configure AWS Backup for an Amazon RDS database, you first need to create a backup vault (Option D) to store your backups. Next, you define a backup plan with rules (Option B) that specify how often and when backups should occur, and their retention period. After defining the plan, you assign the Amazon RDS database as a resource (Option A) to this plan. Finally, it is crucial to monitor backup jobs and recovery points (Option C) to ensure successful backups and readiness for recovery.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  10,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_3_98',
  'A company wants to implement a pilot light disaster recovery strategy for its critical application running on Amazon EC2. Order the essential steps to establish this strategy.',
  'ordering',
  '["Set up DNS failover using Amazon Route 53 to the DR Region.","Configure cross-Region replication for data (e.g., Amazon S3, Amazon RDS).","Regularly test the recovery process and validate RTO/RPO.","Deploy a scaled-down version of the application infrastructure in the DR Region."]'::json,
  '{1,3,0,2}',
  'The pilot light strategy begins with configuring cross-Region replication for data (Option B) to the disaster recovery (DR) Region, ensuring data is continuously available. Next, a scaled-down version of the application infrastructure (Option D) is deployed in the DR Region, ready to be scaled up. When a disaster occurs, DNS failover (Option A) is set up using Amazon Route 53 to redirect traffic to the DR Region. Finally, regularly testing the recovery process and validating RTO/RPO (Option C) is vital to ensure the plan''s effectiveness.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  11,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_3_99',
  'A large enterprise needs to migrate several critical on-premises Windows and Linux servers to AWS, establishing a robust disaster recovery solution with minimal RTO and RPO. They have decided to use AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery (DRS). Order the steps required to successfully implement DRS for these servers, ensuring continuous replication and readiness for failover.',
  'ordering',
  '["Install the AWS Replication Agent on each source server to initiate data replication.","Perform a test drill by launching instances in the recovery Region and validating application functionality.","Configure AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery in the AWS Management Console, specifying the source and target Regions.","Configure launch settings for the replicated servers, including instance type, subnet, and security groups for recovery instances."]'::json,
  '{2,0,3,1}',
  'The correct sequence for implementing AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery begins with configuring DRS in the console (Option C) to set up the replication infrastructure. Next, the AWS Replication Agent must be installed on each source server (Option A) to begin the continuous data replication to the target Region. After replication is established, launch settings for the recovery instances must be configured (Option D) to define how the servers will be launched during a recovery event. Finally, a test drill should be performed (Option B) to validate the recovery process and ensure applications function correctly, which is a critical step for any DR plan.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  12,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_3_100',
  'A company needs to implement a warm standby disaster recovery strategy for its critical application running on Amazon EC2 instances. The goal is to minimize recovery time objective (RTO) while keeping costs manageable. Arrange the following steps in the correct sequence to establish this strategy.',
  'ordering',
  '["Deploy a scaled-down version of the application in the DR Region.","Configure cross-Region replication for data stores.","Set up DNS failover using Amazon Route 53 to redirect traffic.","Regularly test the DR environment and update runbooks."]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}',
  'To establish a warm standby DR strategy, the first crucial step is to ensure data availability in the DR Region. Option B (Configure cross-Region replication for data stores) achieves this by replicating databases or object storage. Once data is available, Option A (Deploy a scaled-down version of the application in the DR Region) sets up the ''warm'' standby environment, ready to be scaled up. After the standby environment is ready, Option C (Set up DNS failover using Amazon Route 53 to redirect traffic) is configured to automatically or manually redirect users to the DR Region during an incident. Finally, Option D (Regularly test the DR environment and update runbooks) is an ongoing operational task vital for validating the DR plan and ensuring its effectiveness, addressing the anti-pattern of not testing DR plans.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  13,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_3_101',
  'An organization is migrating its on-premises servers to AWS and wants to implement a robust disaster recovery solution for them. Order the steps required to set up AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery (DRS) for continuous replication and recovery of these servers.',
  'ordering',
  '["Install the AWS Replication Agent on the source servers.","Configure replication settings and launch templates in the DRS console.","Add source servers to the AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery console.","Monitor replication status and perform recovery drills."]'::json,
  '{2,0,1,3}',
  'The initial step to set up AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery (DRS) is to onboard the source servers. Option C (Add source servers to the AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery console) registers the servers with the service. Following this, Option A (Install the AWS Replication Agent on the source servers) is necessary to enable continuous data replication from the source to the AWS staging area. Once the agent is installed and replication begins, Option B (Configure replication settings and launch templates in the DRS console) allows customization of how the replicated data is stored and how recovered instances will be launched. Finally, Option D (Monitor replication status and perform recovery drills) is an ongoing operational task to ensure the DR solution is working correctly and to validate RTO/RPO objectives.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  14,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_3_102',
  'A DevOps team wants to proactively identify weaknesses in their application''s resilience by simulating real-world failures. Order the steps to create and execute a basic fault injection experiment using AWS Fault Injection Service (FIS).',
  'ordering',
  '["Define the actions and targets for the experiment.","Start the experiment and monitor its impact.","Create an FIS experiment template.","Specify stop conditions and roles for the experiment."]'::json,
  '{2,0,3,1}',
  'The process of using AWS Fault Injection Service (FIS) begins with defining the experiment''s blueprint. Option C (Create an FIS experiment template) is the foundational step where you define the overall structure. Within this template, Option A (Define the actions and targets for the experiment) specifies what faults to inject (e.g., terminate EC2 instances) and which resources to target. Next, Option D (Specify stop conditions and roles for the experiment) is crucial for safety, defining criteria to automatically stop the experiment if issues arise and granting necessary permissions. Finally, Option B (Start the experiment and monitor its impact) initiates the fault injection, allowing the team to observe system behavior and identify resilience gaps.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  15,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_3_103',
  'A company is planning a GameDay exercise to test its disaster recovery procedures and validate its runbooks. This exercise aims to simulate a critical outage and evaluate the team''s response. Order the following activities for a successful GameDay exercise.',
  'ordering',
  '["Execute the simulated disaster scenario and observe system behavior.","Review and update runbooks based on lessons learned.","Define clear objectives and scope for the GameDay.","Gather participants, assign roles, and provide necessary access."]'::json,
  '{2,3,0,1}',
  'A successful GameDay exercise starts with careful planning. Option C (Define clear objectives and scope for the GameDay) ensures everyone understands what is being tested and why. Next, Option D (Gather participants, assign roles, and provide necessary access) prepares the team and resources for the exercise. Once preparations are complete, Option A (Execute the simulated disaster scenario and observe system behavior) is where the actual testing occurs. Finally, Option B (Review and update runbooks based on lessons learned) is a critical post-exercise step to incorporate findings and improve the DR plan, directly addressing the anti-pattern of not testing DR plans.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  16,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_3_104',
  'A development team needs to implement a backup and restore disaster recovery strategy for their Amazon EC2 instances and associated data. Order the steps to configure and utilize AWS Backup for this purpose.',
  'ordering',
  '["Assign resources to the backup plan.","Create a backup plan with a backup rule.","Define a backup vault.","Initiate a restore job from a recovery point."]'::json,
  '{2,1,0,3}',
  'The initial step in configuring AWS Backup is to establish where your backups will be stored. Option C (Define a backup vault) creates the logical container for recovery points. Following this, Option B (Create a backup plan with a backup rule) defines the schedule, frequency, and retention policy for backups. Once the plan is defined, Option A (Assign resources to the backup plan) links specific AWS resources (like EC2 instances, EBS volumes, RDS databases) to be protected by the plan. Finally, Option D (Initiate a restore job from a recovery point) is the process of recovering data when needed, which is typically performed during a disaster or a test.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  17,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_3_105',
  'An organization is designing a pilot light disaster recovery strategy for its application, which includes Amazon EC2 instances and an Amazon RDS database. This strategy aims for a faster recovery than backup and restore, with lower costs than warm standby. Order the steps to implement this approach.',
  'ordering',
  '["Maintain a minimal, non-functional set of core resources in the DR Region.","Replicate critical data to the DR Region.","Scale up the application and database instances in the DR Region during a disaster.","Configure DNS failover to redirect traffic to the DR Region."]'::json,
  '{1,0,3,2}',
  'The foundation of any disaster recovery strategy is data availability. Option B (Replicate critical data to the DR Region) ensures that the essential data is present in the recovery region. Once data is replicated, Option A (Maintain a minimal, non-functional set of core resources in the DR Region) establishes the ''pilot light'' – a small, running infrastructure that can be quickly scaled up. After the pilot light is in place, Option D (Configure DNS failover to redirect traffic to the DR Region) prepares the mechanism for directing users to the recovery site. Finally, Option C (Scale up the application and database instances in the DR Region during a disaster) is the actual recovery action taken when a disaster occurs, bringing the full application online.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  18,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_3_106',
  'A DevOps team is setting up an AWS Fault Injection Service (FIS) experiment to test the resilience of their Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling group against instance termination. Order the steps to define an FIS experiment template.',
  'ordering',
  '["Select the target resources (e.g., EC2 instances in an Auto Scaling group).","Define the actions to be performed (e.g., ''aws:ec2:terminate-instances'').","Specify the duration and stop conditions for the experiment.","Create a new experiment template in the FIS console."]'::json,
  '{3,1,0,2}',
  'The initial step in defining an AWS FIS experiment template is to create the template itself. Option D (Create a new experiment template in the FIS console) serves as the starting point. Within this template, Option B (Define the actions to be performed (e.g., ''aws:ec2:terminate-instances'')) specifies the type of fault to inject. Next, Option A (Select the target resources (e.g., EC2 instances in an Auto Scaling group)) identifies which specific resources will be affected by the defined actions. Finally, Option C (Specify the duration and stop conditions for the experiment) is crucial for controlling the experiment''s lifecycle and ensuring it halts if predefined thresholds are met, preventing unintended prolonged impact.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  19,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_3_107',
  'To ensure a company''s disaster recovery plan is effective, the DevOps team plans a GameDay exercise focusing on a critical application''s failover process. Order the preparatory steps required before the actual GameDay execution.',
  'ordering',
  '["Communicate the GameDay schedule and objectives to stakeholders.","Identify the specific application and disaster scenario to test.","Verify that all necessary monitoring and logging are in place.","Prepare a rollback plan in case of unexpected issues during the exercise."]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}',
  'Effective GameDay preparation begins with clearly defining what will be tested. Option B (Identify the specific application and disaster scenario to test) sets the focus. Following this, Option A (Communicate the GameDay schedule and objectives to stakeholders) ensures transparency and manages expectations. Before execution, it''s vital to have visibility into the system''s behavior, so Option C (Verify that all necessary monitoring and logging are in place) is essential. Finally, Option D (Prepare a rollback plan in case of unexpected issues during the exercise) is a critical safety measure to revert changes if the exercise causes unintended disruptions, reinforcing responsible chaos engineering practices.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  20,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_3_108',
  'A global e-commerce company requires a multi-site active/active disaster recovery strategy to ensure continuous availability and minimal data loss across multiple AWS Regions. Order the key architectural considerations for implementing such a solution.',
  'ordering',
  '["Implement a global load balancing solution like AWS Global Accelerator or Amazon Route 53.","Ensure data synchronization and conflict resolution across all active Regions.","Design applications to be stateless or use globally consistent data stores.","Deploy identical application stacks in multiple AWS Regions."]'::json,
  '{2,3,1,0}',
  'For a multi-site active/active strategy, the application architecture must first support distributed operation. Option C (Design applications to be stateless or use globally consistent data stores) is fundamental to avoid session affinity issues and ensure data integrity across regions. Once the application is designed for multi-region operation, Option D (Deploy identical application stacks in multiple AWS Regions) establishes the active presence in each region. A critical challenge in active/active setups is data consistency, so Option B (Ensure data synchronization and conflict resolution across all active Regions) is vital for services like Amazon DynamoDB Global Tables or Amazon Aurora Global Database. Finally, Option A (Implement a global load balancing solution like AWS Global Accelerator or Amazon Route 53) is necessary to distribute user traffic efficiently across the active regions, providing a single entry point.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  21,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_3_109',
  'A DevOps engineer needs to perform a recovery drill for an application protected by AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery (DRS) to validate the recovery time objective (RTO). Order the steps to initiate a recovery drill in the DRS console.',
  'ordering',
  '["Select the source server(s) for the drill.","Launch the recovery instance(s) into a test subnet.","Verify the recovered application functionality.","Choose a recovery point (snapshot) for the drill."]'::json,
  '{0,3,1,2}',
  'To initiate a recovery drill with AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery (DRS), the first step is to identify the scope of the drill. Option A (Select the source server(s) for the drill) specifies which servers'' recovery will be tested. Next, Option D (Choose a recovery point (snapshot) for the drill) selects the specific point in time from which the recovery will be performed. Once the server and recovery point are chosen, Option B (Launch the recovery instance(s) into a test subnet) provisions the instances in an isolated environment. Finally, Option C (Verify the recovered application functionality) is the crucial step to confirm that the application starts correctly and operates as expected, validating the RTO.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  22,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_3_110',
  'A team wants to simulate an Availability Zone outage to test their application''s resilience. Order the steps to configure and run an AWS Fault Injection Service (FIS) experiment that targets resources in a specific Availability Zone.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create an FIS experiment template with a specific action.","Define the scope of the experiment to include resources in a target Availability Zone.","Specify the IAM role and stop conditions for the experiment.","Start the experiment and observe the application''s behavior."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'The process of setting up an AWS FIS experiment begins with defining its blueprint. Option A (Create an FIS experiment template with a specific action) is the initial step, where you choose the type of fault to inject (e.g., ''aws:ec2:stop-instances''). Next, Option B (Define the scope of the experiment to include resources in a target Availability Zone) refines the targets to ensure only resources within the desired AZ are affected. Following this, Option C (Specify the IAM role and stop conditions for the experiment) configures permissions and safety mechanisms to control the experiment''s execution. Finally, Option D (Start the experiment and observe the application''s behavior) initiates the fault injection, allowing the team to assess resilience.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  23,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_3_111',
  'After a successful failover to a warm standby environment, a DevOps team needs to scale up the resources to handle full production load. Order the steps to effectively transition the warm standby environment to a fully operational state.',
  'ordering',
  '["Perform post-recovery data synchronization and integrity checks.","Scale up Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling groups and Amazon RDS database instances.","Update DNS records in Amazon Route 53 to point to the scaled-up resources.","Verify full application functionality and performance under load."]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}',
  'Once a warm standby environment is active, the immediate next step is to increase its capacity to handle production traffic. Option B (Scale up Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling groups and Amazon RDS database instances) ensures that compute and database resources are adequate. Following the scaling, Option A (Perform post-recovery data synchronization and integrity checks) is crucial to ensure all data is consistent and complete before full traffic redirection. Next, Option C (Update DNS records in Amazon Route 53 to point to the scaled-up resources) directs all user traffic to the now fully provisioned environment. Finally, Option D (Verify full application functionality and performance under load) confirms that the application is stable and performing optimally, completing the recovery process.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  24,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_3_112',
  'A company needs to centralize its backup strategy for various AWS resources, including Amazon EBS volumes and Amazon RDS databases. Order the steps to create a comprehensive backup plan using AWS Backup.',
  'ordering',
  '["Define a backup rule specifying frequency, retention, and lifecycle policies.","Create a backup vault to store recovery points.","Assign specific AWS resources to the backup plan.","Monitor backup jobs and compliance reports."]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}',
  'The initial step in establishing an AWS Backup strategy is to designate a storage location for your backups. Option B (Create a backup vault to store recovery points) provides this secure, encrypted repository. Following this, Option A (Define a backup rule specifying frequency, retention, and lifecycle policies) outlines how and when backups will be taken and how long they will be kept. Once the rules are set, Option C (Assign specific AWS resources to the backup plan) links the resources you want to protect (e.g., EBS volumes, RDS databases) to the defined plan. Finally, Option D (Monitor backup jobs and compliance reports) is an ongoing operational task to ensure backups are successful and meet regulatory requirements.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  25,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_3_113',
  'A DevOps team is implementing a pilot light disaster recovery strategy for a web application. During a disaster, they need to restore the application from the minimal resources in the DR Region. Order the steps to perform this restoration.',
  'ordering',
  '["Scale up the application and database instances.","Verify application functionality and data integrity.","Promote the pilot light database replica to primary.","Update Amazon Route 53 DNS records to point to the DR environment."]'::json,
  '{2,0,3,1}',
  'In a pilot light disaster recovery scenario, the first step during an actual disaster is to activate the core database. Option C (Promote the pilot light database replica to primary) makes the replicated database in the DR region the active one. Once the database is ready, Option A (Scale up the application and database instances) provisions the necessary compute resources to handle the full production load. After the infrastructure is scaled, Option D (Update Amazon Route 53 DNS records to point to the DR environment) redirects user traffic to the newly activated environment. Finally, Option B (Verify application functionality and data integrity) confirms that the application is fully operational and data is consistent.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  26,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_3_114',
  'After a GameDay exercise, a DevOps team needs to ensure that the lessons learned are incorporated into their disaster recovery processes. Order the post-GameDay activities to maximize the value of the exercise and improve resilience.',
  'ordering',
  '["Identify and prioritize action items based on observations.","Update runbooks, automation scripts, and documentation.","Conduct a post-mortem review with all participants.","Implement the identified improvements and retest if necessary."]'::json,
  '{2,0,1,3}',
  'Following a GameDay exercise, the most immediate and crucial step is to gather feedback and analyze performance. Option C (Conduct a post-mortem review with all participants) facilitates this, allowing for open discussion and identification of strengths and weaknesses. Based on this review, Option A (Identify and prioritize action items based on observations) translates insights into concrete tasks. Next, Option B (Update runbooks, automation scripts, and documentation) ensures that the DR plan reflects the latest best practices and lessons learned, directly addressing the anti-pattern of not testing DR plans. Finally, Option D (Implement the identified improvements and retest if necessary) closes the loop, applying the changes and verifying their effectiveness.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  27,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_3_115',
  'A DevOps engineer is performing a planned cutover of an on-premises application to AWS using AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery (DRS). Order the steps to execute a successful cutover to the recovered instances in AWS.',
  'ordering',
  '["Initiate the cutover for the selected source server(s).","Verify application functionality on the launched instances.","Configure DNS to point to the newly launched AWS instances.","Launch test instances from the latest recovery point."]'::json,
  '{3,0,1,2}',
  'Before performing a cutover with AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery (DRS), it''s best practice to ensure the recovery process works as expected. Option D (Launch test instances from the latest recovery point) allows for validation in an isolated environment. Once satisfied, Option A (Initiate the cutover for the selected source server(s)) formally begins the migration to the AWS environment. After the cutover instances are launched, Option B (Verify application functionality on the launched instances) is critical to confirm that the application is fully operational. Finally, Option C (Configure DNS to point to the newly launched AWS instances) redirects production traffic to the AWS environment, completing the cutover.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  28,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_3_116',
  'A critical application relies on an Amazon RDS database. To meet strict recovery time objective (RTO) and recovery point objective (RPO) requirements, a backup and restore strategy is in place. Order the steps to recover the database during a disaster scenario.',
  'ordering',
  '["Restore the Amazon RDS database from the latest available snapshot.","Verify data integrity and application connectivity to the restored database.","Update application configuration to point to the new database endpoint.","Identify the most recent recovery point (snapshot) for restoration."]'::json,
  '{3,0,2,1}',
  'In a backup and restore scenario, the first step is to determine the point in time for recovery. Option D (Identify the most recent recovery point (snapshot) for restoration) ensures minimal data loss (RPO). Once the recovery point is identified, Option A (Restore the Amazon RDS database from the latest available snapshot) initiates the database recovery process. After the database is restored, Option C (Update application configuration to point to the new database endpoint) is necessary to connect the application to the newly recovered database instance. Finally, Option B (Verify data integrity and application connectivity to the restored database) confirms that the recovery was successful and the application can resume normal operations.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  29,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_3_117',
  'A company is planning its disaster recovery strategy. Order the general phases of implementing a disaster recovery strategy.',
  'ordering',
  '["Implement the chosen DR solution (e.g., pilot light, warm standby).","Define Recovery Time Objective (RTO) and Recovery Point Objective (RPO).","Regularly test and refine the DR plan.","Identify critical applications and data."]'::json,
  '{3,1,0,2}',
  'The initial phase of any disaster recovery strategy is to identify critical applications and data (Option D) to understand what needs protection. Following this, defining the Recovery Time Objective (RTO) and Recovery Point Objective (RPO) (Option B) sets the targets for recovery. Once objectives are clear, the chosen DR solution is implemented (Option A). Crucially, the DR plan must be regularly tested and refined (Option C) to ensure it works as expected when a real disaster occurs, addressing the anti-pattern of not testing DR plans.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  30,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_3_118',
  'A DevOps team needs to conduct a GameDay exercise to validate the resilience of their application''s failover mechanism. Order the actions to prepare for a GameDay exercise for a critical application.',
  'ordering',
  '["Define the scope, objectives, and success criteria for the GameDay.","Gather relevant stakeholders and assign roles (e.g., incident commander, observers).","Develop a detailed runbook for the GameDay scenario.","Communicate the GameDay schedule and expectations to all involved teams."]'::json,
  '{0,2,1,3}',
  'Effective GameDay preparation starts with defining the scope, objectives, and success criteria (Option A) to ensure clear goals. Next, a detailed runbook for the GameDay scenario is developed (Option C), outlining the steps and expected outcomes. After the plan is ready, relevant stakeholders are gathered, and roles are assigned (Option B) to ensure everyone knows their responsibilities. Finally, the GameDay schedule and expectations are communicated to all involved teams (Option D) to ensure coordination and awareness. This structured approach helps in addressing the anti-pattern of untested DR plans by ensuring thorough preparation.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  31,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_3_119',
  'A company wants to implement a warm standby DR strategy for its EC2-based application. Order the steps to achieve this using AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery (DRS).',
  'ordering',
  '["Launch recovery instances from the replicated data in the target Region.","Install the AWS Replication Agent on the source EC2 instances.","Configure the Elastic Disaster Recovery service in the AWS Management Console.","Perform a drill or test recovery to validate the setup."]'::json,
  '{2,1,0,3}',
  'To implement a warm standby DR strategy with AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery, the first step is to configure the DRS service in the AWS Management Console (Option C), setting up the replication settings. Next, the AWS Replication Agent is installed on the source EC2 instances (Option B) to begin continuous data replication. When a disaster occurs or during a drill, recovery instances are launched from the replicated data in the target Region (Option A). Finally, it is crucial to perform a drill or test recovery (Option D) to validate the setup and ensure the RTO/RPO objectives can be met, directly addressing the anti-pattern of untested DR plans.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  32,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
