INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_1_115',
  'A DevOps team wants to enable multi-region active-active capabilities for an existing Amazon DynamoDB table to improve global availability and performance. Order the steps to convert a single-region DynamoDB table into a global table.',
  'ordering',
  '["Enable DynamoDB Streams with new and old images for the table.","Add replica Regions to the global table.","Verify that the table has a single primary key.","Monitor replication status and latency across Regions."]'::json,
  '{2,0,1,3}',
  'Before enabling DynamoDB Global Tables, the table structure must meet specific requirements. Option C (Verify that the table has a single primary key) is a prerequisite, as global tables require a single partition key and an optional sort key. Next, Option A (Enable DynamoDB Streams with new and old images for the table) is essential, as Global Tables use streams to replicate data changes across regions. Once streams are enabled, Option B (Add replica Regions to the global table) converts the table into a global table by creating replicas in other specified regions. Finally, Option D (Monitor replication status and latency across Regions) is an ongoing operational task to ensure data consistency and performance.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  43,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_1_116',
  'A company needs to improve the performance and availability of its internet-facing applications for a global user base. Order the steps to set up AWS Global Accelerator for multi-region traffic routing.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create an accelerator and configure listeners.","Add endpoint groups for each AWS Region.","Configure endpoints within each endpoint group.","Update DNS records to point to the accelerator''s static IP addresses."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'The initial step to use AWS Global Accelerator is to provision the accelerator itself. Option A (Create an accelerator and configure listeners) establishes the global entry points (static IP addresses) and defines the ports and protocols for incoming traffic. Next, Option B (Add endpoint groups for each AWS Region) defines the regions where your application endpoints reside. Within these regional groups, Option C (Configure endpoints within each endpoint group) specifies the actual resources (e.g., Application Load Balancers, EC2 instances) that Global Accelerator will direct traffic to. Finally, Option D (Update DNS records to point to the accelerator''s static IP addresses) directs user traffic to the Global Accelerator, leveraging its optimized network path.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  44,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_1_117',
  'During a regional outage affecting a primary application endpoint, Amazon Route 53 needs to automatically failover traffic to a healthy secondary endpoint in another region. Order the sequence of operations that Route 53 performs during such a DNS failover event.',
  'ordering',
  '["Route 53 health checks detect the primary endpoint is unhealthy.","Route 53 updates its internal records to point to the secondary endpoint.","Client DNS resolvers query Route 53 for the application''s IP address.","Clients receive the IP address of the healthy secondary endpoint."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'The failover process in Amazon Route 53 begins with the detection of an issue. Option A (Route 53 health checks detect the primary endpoint is unhealthy) is the trigger. Once unhealthiness is confirmed, Option B (Route 53 updates its internal records to point to the secondary endpoint) changes the DNS resolution logic. Subsequently, Option C (Client DNS resolvers query Route 53 for the application''s IP address) represents users attempting to access the application. Finally, Option D (Clients receive the IP address of the healthy secondary endpoint) completes the failover, directing users to the operational region, assuming DNS TTLs have expired.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  45,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_1_118',
  'A company wants to optimize the network path for its global users accessing an application deployed across multiple AWS Regions. Order the typical traffic flow through AWS Global Accelerator from a user''s perspective.',
  'ordering',
  '["User''s client connects to the nearest AWS edge location.","Global Accelerator routes traffic over the AWS global network.","Traffic reaches the optimal endpoint in an AWS Region.","User''s client resolves DNS to Global Accelerator''s static IP address."]'::json,
  '{3,0,1,2}',
  'The process of a user accessing an application through AWS Global Accelerator begins with DNS resolution. Option D (User''s client resolves DNS to Global Accelerator''s static IP address) directs the client to Global Accelerator''s entry points. Next, Option A (User''s client connects to the nearest AWS edge location) establishes the initial connection at the closest point of presence. From there, Option B (Global Accelerator routes traffic over the AWS global network) leverages AWS''s optimized backbone network. Finally, Option C (Traffic reaches the optimal endpoint in an AWS Region) delivers the traffic to the healthiest and closest application endpoint, bypassing congested public internet paths.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  46,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_1_119',
  'A global enterprise needs to implement a multi-region active-passive architecture for a critical internal application to meet stringent recovery time objectives (RTOs). Order the steps to establish this architecture.',
  'ordering',
  '["Deploy the full application stack in the primary AWS Region.","Configure cross-Region data replication for the primary database.","Deploy a minimal, scaled-down version of the application in the secondary Region.","Set up Amazon Route 53 failover routing policy for automatic redirection."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'The initial step for any application deployment is to establish the primary operational environment. Option A (Deploy the full application stack in the primary AWS Region) sets up the main production site. For disaster recovery, data consistency is paramount, so Option B (Configure cross-Region data replication for the primary database) ensures that data is continuously copied to the secondary region. Next, Option C (Deploy a minimal, scaled-down version of the application in the secondary Region) creates the ''passive'' standby environment, ready for rapid activation. Finally, Option D (Set up Amazon Route 53 failover routing policy for automatic redirection) provides the mechanism to automatically switch traffic to the secondary region during an outage.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  47,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_1_120',
  'A DevOps team wants to monitor the health of their application endpoints and integrate this monitoring with DNS failover. Order the steps to create a basic Amazon Route 53 health check.',
  'ordering',
  '["Specify the endpoint to monitor (IP address or domain name).","Choose the protocol and port for the health check.","Define the health check parameters (e.g., request interval, failure threshold).","Create a new health check in the Amazon Route 53 console."]'::json,
  '{3,0,1,2}',
  'The process of creating an Amazon Route 53 health check begins with initiating the creation process. Option D (Create a new health check in the Amazon Route 53 console) is the starting point. Next, Option A (Specify the endpoint to monitor (IP address or domain name)) identifies the specific resource whose health will be tracked. Following this, Option B (Choose the protocol and port for the health check) defines how Route 53 will attempt to connect to the endpoint (e.g., HTTP on port 80). Finally, Option C (Define the health check parameters (e.g., request interval, failure threshold)) customizes the behavior of the health check, determining how quickly it detects and reacts to endpoint status changes.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  48,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_1_121',
  'A company is using an Amazon Aurora Global Database for its mission-critical application and needs to perform a point-in-time restore operation. Order the steps to restore an Aurora Global Database from a snapshot.',
  'ordering',
  '["Choose the desired recovery point (snapshot) or point in time.","Restore the primary Aurora cluster from the selected recovery point.","Re-establish the Aurora Global Database configuration.","Verify data integrity and application connectivity to the restored database."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'When performing a point-in-time restore for an Amazon Aurora Global Database, the first step is to identify the specific recovery point. Option A (Choose the desired recovery point (snapshot) or point in time) ensures that data is restored to the correct state. Next, Option B (Restore the primary Aurora cluster from the selected recovery point) creates a new primary cluster based on the chosen snapshot. After the primary is restored, Option C (Re-establish the Aurora Global Database configuration) is necessary to link any secondary clusters back to the new primary or create new secondary clusters. Finally, Option D (Verify data integrity and application connectivity to the restored database) confirms that the restoration was successful and the application can resume operations.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  49,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_1_122',
  'A DevOps team is designing an application that leverages Amazon DynamoDB Global Tables for multi-region active-active operation. Order the key considerations for ensuring data consistency and conflict resolution across regions.',
  'ordering',
  '["Understand DynamoDB''s last-writer-wins conflict resolution mechanism.","Design application logic to handle eventual consistency.","Ensure all writes are directed to a local replica for optimal performance.","Monitor replication lag and conflicts using Amazon CloudWatch."]'::json,
  '{2,0,1,3}',
  'For an application leveraging Amazon DynamoDB Global Tables, the primary goal is often low-latency writes. Option C (Ensure all writes are directed to a local replica for optimal performance) achieves this by writing to the closest regional table. When multiple regions can write concurrently, conflict resolution becomes critical. Option A (Understand DynamoDB''s last-writer-wins conflict resolution mechanism) is essential for predicting how conflicts will be resolved. Given this, Option B (Design application logic to handle eventual consistency) is necessary, as data changes propagate asynchronously across regions. Finally, Option D (Monitor replication lag and conflicts using Amazon CloudWatch) is an ongoing operational task to ensure the global table is performing as expected and to identify any potential issues.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  50,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_1_123',
  'A company needs to update the endpoints for an application served by AWS Global Accelerator. Order the steps to modify an existing endpoint group to include new or updated application endpoints.',
  'ordering',
  '["Navigate to the specific endpoint group in the Global Accelerator console.","Add or remove endpoints (e.g., Application Load Balancers, EC2 instances).","Configure endpoint weights and health check settings.","Save the changes to the endpoint group."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'To modify an existing endpoint group in AWS Global Accelerator, the first step is to access the relevant configuration. Option A (Navigate to the specific endpoint group in the Global Accelerator console) directs you to the correct section. Next, Option B (Add or remove endpoints (e.g., Application Load Balancers, EC2 instances)) allows you to update the list of application resources that Global Accelerator can route traffic to. Following this, Option C (Configure endpoint weights and health check settings) enables fine-tuning of traffic distribution and health monitoring for the individual endpoints. Finally, Option D (Save the changes to the endpoint group) applies the modifications, ensuring Global Accelerator uses the updated configuration.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  51,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_1_124',
  'A DevOps team is setting up a new Amazon RDS for MySQL database and wants to ensure high availability within a single AWS Region. Order the steps to configure an Amazon RDS Multi-AZ deployment.',
  'ordering',
  '["Choose the Multi-AZ deployment option during database instance creation.","Select the desired database instance class and storage.","Configure network and security settings (VPC, subnets, security groups).","Monitor the Multi-AZ status and automatic failover capabilities."]'::json,
  '{1,2,0,3}',
  'When creating an Amazon RDS database, the initial step is to define its core specifications. Option B (Select the desired database instance class and storage) determines the compute and storage capacity. Next, Option C (Configure network and security settings (VPC, subnets, security groups)) ensures the database is securely placed within your virtual network. Crucially, Option A (Choose the Multi-AZ deployment option during database instance creation) enables the high-availability feature, automatically provisioning a standby replica in a different Availability Zone. Finally, Option D (Monitor the Multi-AZ status and automatic failover capabilities) is an ongoing operational task to ensure the high-availability setup is functioning as expected.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  52,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_1_125',
  'A DevOps team needs to establish a highly available relational database solution across multiple Availability Zones. Order the typical steps to configure a multi-AZ Amazon RDS database instance.',
  'ordering',
  '["Select the desired instance class and storage type.","Choose the database engine and version.","Specify network and security settings, including VPC and subnet group.","Enable Multi-AZ deployment option."]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}',
  'To configure a multi-AZ Amazon RDS database instance, the logical sequence is to first choose the database engine and version (Option B), then select the instance class and storage (Option A). After that, network and security settings like VPC and subnet group are specified (Option C), as these are foundational for database connectivity. Finally, the Multi-AZ deployment option is enabled (Option D) to ensure high availability across Availability Zones. This order ensures that the fundamental database and infrastructure configurations are in place before enabling the high-availability feature.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  53,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_1_126',
  'A company is deploying a new web application globally and wants to ensure users are routed to the nearest healthy endpoint for optimal performance and availability. Order the steps to configure AWS Global Accelerator for this purpose.',
  'ordering',
  '["Add endpoint groups for each AWS Region where the application is deployed.","Configure listeners to process incoming connections on specific ports and protocols.","Create a new accelerator.","Register endpoints (e.g., Application Load Balancers, EC2 instances) to their respective endpoint groups."]'::json,
  '{2,1,0,3}',
  'To configure AWS Global Accelerator, the logical first step is to create a new accelerator (Option C), which provides static IP addresses. Next, listeners are configured (Option B) to define how Global Accelerator accepts incoming client connections. After listeners, endpoint groups are added for each AWS Region where the application is deployed (Option A) to define the regional targets. Finally, specific endpoints like Application Load Balancers or EC2 instances are registered to their respective endpoint groups (Option D), allowing Global Accelerator to route traffic to them based on health and proximity.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  54,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_1_127',
  'A DevOps engineer needs to enable a DynamoDB global table for an existing table to support multi-region active-active replication. Order the actions to achieve this.',
  'ordering',
  '["Add additional replica regions to the global table.","Ensure the existing DynamoDB table has DynamoDB Streams enabled with the ''New and old images'' view type.","Create the global table.","Verify the table''s capacity mode (on-demand or provisioned) is suitable for global table replication."]'::json,
  '{1,3,2,0}',
  'Before creating a DynamoDB global table, it''s crucial to ensure that DynamoDB Streams are enabled on the existing table with the ''New and old images'' view type (Option B), as this is how changes are propagated. Next, verify the table''s capacity mode (on-demand or provisioned) is suitable for global table replication (Option D), as capacity needs to be managed across regions. Then, the global table can be created (Option C), which initially sets up the table in one region. Finally, additional replica regions are added to the global table (Option A) to achieve multi-region active-active replication.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  55,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_38_m_38_1_128',
  'A DevOps team needs to migrate an on-premises application to AWS, ensuring high availability across multiple Availability Zones for its EC2 instances. Order the general steps for this migration and HA setup.',
  'ordering',
  '["Deploy application components across multiple Availability Zones.","Set up an Auto Scaling group and Application Load Balancer.","Migrate application data and code to AWS.","Provision a Virtual Private Cloud (VPC) with public and private subnets in multiple Availability Zones."]'::json,
  '{3,2,0,1}',
  'The foundational step for any AWS deployment is to provision a Virtual Private Cloud (VPC) with public and private subnets across multiple Availability Zones (Option D) to provide the network infrastructure. After the network is ready, the application data and code are migrated to AWS (Option C). Once the application components are in AWS, they should be deployed across multiple Availability Zones (Option A) to ensure resilience. Finally, an Auto Scaling group and an Application Load Balancer are set up (Option B) to distribute traffic, handle scaling, and maintain high availability.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain38_8',
  NOW(),
  56,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_38_1',
  'medium'
);

