INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_39_m_39_1_48',
  'A company needs to aggregate logs from hundreds of Amazon EC2 instances across multiple AWS accounts into a central location for long-term retention and analysis. The solution must minimize operational overhead and scale automatically. Which approach is MOST cost-effective?',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure each EC2 instance to send logs directly to CloudWatch Logs in its respective account, then use CloudWatch Logs subscription filters to forward logs to a central Amazon S3 bucket.","Deploy a custom log forwarding agent on each EC2 instance to push logs to a central Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose delivery stream, which then delivers them to a central Amazon S3 bucket.","Manually configure an Amazon S3 bucket in each account to receive logs, then use S3 replication to copy logs to a central S3 bucket in a dedicated logging account.","Install the CloudWatch Agent on each EC2 instance to send logs to CloudWatch Logs in each account, then use CloudWatch cross-account observability to link log groups to a central monitoring account for querying."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) describes a centralized logging architecture using Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose. This approach minimizes operational overhead by using a managed service for ingestion and delivery, scales automatically, and is cost-effective for high-volume log aggregation to S3. Option A represents an anti-pattern (sending all logs directly from EC2 to CloudWatch without centralized log routing), as it creates per-account silos and requires complex routing for aggregation, increasing operational overhead and making unified analysis difficult. Option C requires manual configuration and management of S3 replication across many accounts, which is not scalable or low operational overhead. Option D allows for cross-account querying but doesn''t centralize the raw log storage in a single S3 bucket for long-term retention and analysis by other services like OpenSearch, which is often a requirement for ''centralized logging''.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain39_8',
  NOW(),
  9,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_39_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_39_m_39_1_49',
  'A DevOps team wants to collect application logs from AWS Lambda functions and containerized applications, store them centrally, and enable real-time search and visualization. Which combination of services would provide this with LEAST operational overhead?',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure Lambda functions to send logs to CloudWatch Logs, then use CloudWatch Logs subscription filters to stream logs to an Amazon EC2 instance running self-managed OpenSearch.","Configure Lambda functions and containerized applications to send logs to CloudWatch Logs, then use a Kinesis Data Firehose delivery stream to send logs from CloudWatch Logs to Amazon OpenSearch Service.","Configure Lambda functions to write logs directly to an Amazon S3 bucket, and use Amazon Athena for querying.","Configure Lambda functions and containerized applications to send logs to CloudWatch Logs, then manually export logs to Amazon S3 for archival and use CloudWatch Logs Insights for search."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) provides the most managed and low-overhead solution. AWS Lambda and containerized applications (e.g., ECS/EKS) natively integrate with CloudWatch Logs. Kinesis Data Firehose can then be configured to automatically stream these logs to Amazon OpenSearch Service, which provides managed real-time search and visualization capabilities. Option A involves self-managing OpenSearch on EC2, which significantly increases operational overhead. Option C stores logs in S3, but Athena is not designed for real-time search and visualization, and direct writing from Lambda to S3 might require custom code. Option D involves manual export and while Logs Insights is good for search, it doesn''t offer the same visualization capabilities as OpenSearch, and manual export adds overhead.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain39_8',
  NOW(),
  10,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_39_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_39_m_39_1_50',
  'A security team requires a centralized logging solution for all AWS CloudTrail logs from multiple accounts. The logs must be immutable, highly durable, and accessible for auditing for several years. Which service is best for storing these logs?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon S3","Amazon DynamoDB","Amazon RDS","Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is Amazon S3. CloudTrail logs are typically delivered to an S3 bucket, which provides high durability, immutability (especially with S3 Object Lock), and cost-effective long-term storage, meeting the requirements for security auditing. Option B (Amazon DynamoDB) is a NoSQL database, not ideal for raw log storage due to cost and access patterns. Option C (Amazon RDS) is a relational database, also not suitable for raw, high-volume log storage. Option D (Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose) is an ingestion service, not a long-term storage solution itself, though it can deliver to S3.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain39_8',
  NOW(),
  11,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_39_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_39_m_39_1_51',
  'An application generates high-volume, streaming logs that need to be processed, transformed, and delivered to Amazon OpenSearch Service for near real-time analytics. Which AWS service should be used for reliable ingestion and delivery?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CloudTrail Lake","Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose","Amazon EventBridge","AWS X-Ray"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose, which is specifically designed for reliably capturing, transforming, and loading streaming data, including high-volume logs, into various destinations such as Amazon OpenSearch Service, with built-in transformation capabilities. Option A (AWS CloudTrail Lake) is for querying CloudTrail events. Option C (Amazon EventBridge) is for event-driven automation, not streaming data ingestion. Option D (AWS X-Ray) is for distributed tracing.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain39_8',
  NOW(),
  12,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_39_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_39_m_39_1_52',
  'A large enterprise operates hundreds of Amazon EC2 instances across multiple AWS accounts, generating terabytes of application logs daily. The DevOps team needs a highly scalable, cost-effective, and operationally simple solution to centralize these logs for real-time analysis, search, and long-term archival. They require a managed service that minimizes administrative overhead for ingestion and allows for complex querying and visualization.',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure the CloudWatch agent on each EC2 instance to send logs to CloudWatch Logs, then use CloudWatch Logs subscription filters to stream logs to an AWS Lambda function, which then pushes them to a self-managed OpenSearch cluster on EC2.","Deploy Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose in a central logging account, configure it to ingest logs from all EC2 instances, and deliver them to an Amazon OpenSearch Service domain for real-time analysis and Amazon S3 for long-term archival.","Implement a custom log forwarding solution using Fluentd or Logstash agents on each EC2 instance, sending logs directly to a dedicated Amazon OpenSearch Service domain for analysis and S3 for archival.","Set up CloudWatch Logs across all accounts, enable cross-account log aggregation in CloudWatch, and use CloudWatch Logs Insights for querying and CloudWatch dashboards for visualization."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) describes a robust, managed, and scalable centralized logging architecture. Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose is a fully managed service that automatically scales to handle log ingestion, converts and transforms data, and delivers it to destinations like Amazon OpenSearch Service for real-time analytics and Amazon S3 for cost-effective long-term archival, all with minimal operational overhead. This approach aligns with best practices for centralized logging at scale. Option A (incorrect) introduces significant operational overhead by requiring a self-managed OpenSearch cluster and a custom Lambda function for routing, which contradicts the "LEAST operational overhead" constraint. Option C (incorrect) involves managing custom agents (Fluentd/Logstash) on each EC2 instance and configuring them to send directly to OpenSearch, which adds administrative complexity compared to Firehose''s managed ingestion. Option D (incorrect) uses CloudWatch Logs Insights, which is good for querying within CloudWatch, but it doesn''t provide the advanced search, visualization, and analytics capabilities of Amazon OpenSearch Service, nor does it inherently offer the same cost-effectiveness for long-term archival as S3 for raw logs, especially for terabytes of data. It also doesn''t leverage a dedicated streaming service like Firehose for ingestion.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain39_8',
  NOW(),
  13,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_39_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_39_m_39_1_53',
  'A DevOps team needs to collect logs from various AWS services and custom applications, process them, and then store them in Amazon S3 for archival and Amazon OpenSearch Service for real-time analysis. Which service can efficiently stream these logs to both destinations with minimal operational overhead?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose","AWS CloudTrail","Amazon EventBridge","AWS X-Ray"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose, which is a fully managed service that can capture, transform, and load streaming data into Amazon S3, Amazon OpenSearch Service, and other analytics services. It provides automatic scaling and handles data buffering, encryption, and compression, making it ideal for centralized logging with minimal operational overhead. Option B (incorrect) is AWS CloudTrail, which primarily records AWS API calls and related events, not general application logs. Option C (incorrect) is Amazon EventBridge, an event bus service for routing events between AWS services and applications, not for streaming high-volume logs to storage and analytics platforms. Option D (incorrect) is AWS X-Ray, used for distributed tracing and analyzing application performance, not for log aggregation and storage.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain39_8',
  NOW(),
  14,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_39_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_39_m_39_1_54',
  'What is the primary purpose of Amazon CloudWatch Logs Insights?',
  'mcq',
  '["To store application logs indefinitely in Amazon S3.","To interactively search and analyze log data in Amazon CloudWatch Logs.","To create custom metrics from log events for dashboards.","To stream log data to external Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) systems."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) states that Amazon CloudWatch Logs Insights is used to interactively search and analyze log data in Amazon CloudWatch Logs. It provides a powerful query language to perform queries on your log data, helping you to troubleshoot operational issues and identify trends. Option A (incorrect) describes a function of Amazon S3, often used as a long-term storage destination for logs, but not the primary purpose of Logs Insights itself. Option C (incorrect) describes the functionality of CloudWatch metric filters, which can extract metrics from log events, but is not the main purpose of Logs Insights. Option D (incorrect) describes a potential use case for CloudWatch Logs subscription filters, which can stream logs to other services for SIEM integration, but not Logs Insights.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain39_8',
  NOW(),
  15,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_39_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_39_m_39_1_55',
  'A DevOps team needs to centralize logs from various Amazon EC2 instances and AWS Lambda functions across multiple accounts into a single location for unified search and long-term retention. The solution must be highly scalable and integrate with Amazon OpenSearch Service for analytics. Which approach is MOST effective for this requirement?',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure CloudWatch Logs subscription filters to stream logs directly from each account to a central Amazon OpenSearch Service domain.","Deploy a custom log forwarding agent on each Amazon EC2 instance to send logs directly to a central Amazon S3 bucket, then use AWS Glue to import into Amazon OpenSearch Service.","Utilize Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose in each account to ingest logs and deliver them to a central Amazon OpenSearch Service domain, enabling cross-account log aggregation.","Set up individual CloudWatch Logs groups for each application in every account and manually export logs to Amazon S3 for archival, then import into Amazon OpenSearch Service."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) describes the recommended centralized logging architecture. Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose is designed for streaming data to various destinations, including Amazon OpenSearch Service, and supports cross-account delivery. This provides a scalable, managed service for log aggregation, simplifying unified search and retention. Option A (incorrect) describes a method for streaming logs, but managing subscription filters across many accounts and log groups can become complex and less scalable for true centralized aggregation, especially for direct delivery to OpenSearch without an intermediate buffer or transformation layer. Option B (incorrect) introduces significant operational overhead by requiring custom agents and an additional ETL process (AWS Glue) which is not the most direct or managed way to stream logs to OpenSearch for real-time analytics. Option D (incorrect) is an anti-pattern (ap-4.1-001) as it involves manual export and creates per-account silos, complicating cross-account analysis and requiring significant operational effort for aggregation and import into Amazon OpenSearch Service.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain39_8',
  NOW(),
  16,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_39_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_39_m_39_1_56',
  'Which AWS service is specifically designed to enable interactive querying and analysis of log data in Amazon CloudWatch Logs using a SQL-like syntax?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon OpenSearch Service","AWS X-Ray","Amazon CloudWatch Logs Insights","AWS CloudTrail Lake"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is Amazon CloudWatch Logs Insights, which allows users to interactively search and analyze their log data in CloudWatch Logs using a powerful, SQL-like query language. This service is optimized for quick analysis of log events. Option A (incorrect) is Amazon OpenSearch Service, which is a search and analytics engine often used for log analytics but typically requires logs to be streamed to it, rather than directly querying CloudWatch Logs. Option B (incorrect) is AWS X-Ray, used for distributed tracing and analyzing application performance, not for querying raw log data. Option D (incorrect) is AWS CloudTrail Lake, which is used for auditing and analyzing AWS API activity logs, not general application logs stored in CloudWatch Logs.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain39_8',
  NOW(),
  17,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_39_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_39_m_39_1_57',
  'A development team has deployed a microservices application on AWS and needs to visualize the end-to-end request flow, identify performance bottlenecks, and analyze latency across different service components. Which AWS service should they use to achieve this?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon CloudWatch Logs Insights","AWS X-Ray","Amazon OpenSearch Service","AWS CloudTrail Lake"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is AWS X-Ray, which provides end-to-end visibility into the request flow of distributed applications. It generates a service map that visualizes application components and their interactions, helping to identify performance bottlenecks and latency issues across microservices. Option A (incorrect) is Amazon CloudWatch Logs Insights, which is used for querying and analyzing log data, not for distributed tracing or service maps. Option C (incorrect) is Amazon OpenSearch Service, a search and analytics engine primarily used for log analytics and visualization. Option D (incorrect) is AWS CloudTrail Lake, which focuses on auditing and analyzing AWS API activity, not application performance tracing.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain39_8',
  NOW(),
  18,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_39_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_39_m_39_1_58',
  'A company operates a custom application that processes financial transactions and needs to monitor specific business metrics, such as the number of successful transactions per minute and the average transaction processing time. These metrics are not automatically collected by default AWS services. Which CloudWatch feature should be used to collect and monitor this data?',
  'mcq',
  '["CloudWatch Logs Insights","CloudWatch Alarms","CloudWatch Custom Metrics","CloudWatch Contributor Insights"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is CloudWatch Custom Metrics, which allows users to publish their own metrics to CloudWatch. This is ideal for application-specific business metrics like transaction counts or processing times that are not provided by standard AWS service metrics. Option A (incorrect) is CloudWatch Logs Insights, used for querying and analyzing log data, not for publishing custom metrics. Option B (incorrect) is CloudWatch Alarms, which are used to trigger actions based on metric thresholds, but do not collect the metrics themselves. Option D (incorrect) is CloudWatch Contributor Insights, which helps identify top contributors to log data, not for defining and publishing new metrics.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain39_8',
  NOW(),
  19,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_39_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_39_m_39_1_59',
  'Which AWS service is best suited for performing real-time log analytics, full-text search, and creating interactive dashboards for operational data, often used as part of an ELK stack pattern?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CloudTrail Lake","Amazon OpenSearch Service","Amazon CloudWatch Logs Insights","AWS X-Ray"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is Amazon OpenSearch Service (formerly Amazon Elasticsearch Service), which is a fully managed service that makes it easy to deploy, operate, and scale OpenSearch clusters. It is commonly used for log analytics, full-text search, and creating interactive dashboards, often as a component in an ELK (Elasticsearch, Logstash, Kibana) stack pattern. Option A (incorrect) is AWS CloudTrail Lake, which is for auditing and analyzing AWS API activity logs. Option C (incorrect) is Amazon CloudWatch Logs Insights, which is for querying CloudWatch Logs, but not a full-fledged search and analytics engine like OpenSearch. Option D (incorrect) is AWS X-Ray, used for distributed tracing of application requests.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain39_8',
  NOW(),
  20,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_39_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_39_m_39_1_60',
  'A large enterprise has multiple AWS accounts, each with various applications generating logs. The security and operations teams require a centralized view of all logs for auditing, troubleshooting, and compliance across these accounts. Which CloudWatch feature enables this consolidated view?',
  'mcq',
  '["CloudWatch Logs subscription filters to a central Amazon S3 bucket.","CloudWatch cross-account observability for log aggregation.","Manually configuring CloudWatch Logs exports from each account.","Using AWS Organizations to manage CloudWatch permissions."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is CloudWatch cross-account observability, which allows you to monitor and troubleshoot applications and infrastructure across multiple AWS accounts from a single monitoring account. This includes centralizing logs for aggregation and analysis. Option A (incorrect) involves setting up subscription filters, which can be complex to manage at scale for true cross-account aggregation and doesn''t provide the unified console experience of cross-account observability. Option C (incorrect) is a manual and inefficient process that does not scale for continuous, real-time log aggregation. Option D (incorrect) describes a method for managing permissions, but does not directly provide the mechanism for centralizing logs or a unified view.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain39_8',
  NOW(),
  21,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_39_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_39_m_39_1_61',
  'Which CloudWatch feature allows users to create customizable visual interfaces to monitor their AWS resources and applications, displaying metrics, logs, and alarms in a unified view?',
  'mcq',
  '["CloudWatch Logs Insights","CloudWatch Dashboards","CloudWatch Contributor Insights","CloudWatch Alarms"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is CloudWatch Dashboards, which enable users to create custom, interactive dashboards to monitor their AWS resources and applications. These dashboards can display metrics, logs, and alarms, providing a unified operational view. Option A (incorrect) is CloudWatch Logs Insights, used for querying log data. Option C (incorrect) is CloudWatch Contributor Insights, which helps identify top contributors to log data. Option D (incorrect) is CloudWatch Alarms, used for triggering actions based on metric thresholds, not for visual display of multiple data types.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain39_8',
  NOW(),
  22,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_39_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_39_m_39_1_62',
  'A company needs to store application logs for seven years to meet compliance requirements. The logs are generated continuously and need to be cost-effectively archived with infrequent access. Which centralized logging architecture component should be used for long-term, low-cost storage?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon OpenSearch Service","AWS X-Ray","Amazon S3","CloudWatch Logs"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is Amazon S3, which is an object storage service known for its durability, scalability, and cost-effectiveness for long-term archival, especially with infrequent access tiers like S3 Glacier. It''s a common destination for logs streamed via Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose for compliance and long-term retention. Option A (incorrect) is Amazon OpenSearch Service, which is optimized for real-time analytics and search, not for cost-effective long-term archival of infrequently accessed data. Option B (incorrect) is AWS X-Ray, a distributed tracing service. Option D (incorrect) is CloudWatch Logs, which has configurable retention but becomes less cost-effective than S3 for very long-term, cold archival.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain39_8',
  NOW(),
  23,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_39_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_39_m_39_1_63',
  'Which CloudWatch feature helps identify the top talkers, top contributors, or top consumers in your log data, making it easier to pinpoint sources of operational issues or anomalies?',
  'mcq',
  '["CloudWatch Logs Insights","CloudWatch Dashboards","CloudWatch Contributor Insights","CloudWatch Alarms"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is CloudWatch Contributor Insights, which analyzes log data to identify the top contributors to log events, such as the top N IP addresses, user IDs, or resource IDs generating errors or traffic. This helps in quickly pinpointing the root cause of operational issues. Option A (incorrect) is CloudWatch Logs Insights, used for querying log data. Option B (incorrect) is CloudWatch Dashboards, used for visualizing metrics and logs. Option D (incorrect) is CloudWatch Alarms, used for setting thresholds on metrics.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain39_8',
  NOW(),
  24,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_39_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_39_m_39_1_64',
  'A company''s microservices architecture is experiencing intermittent performance degradation. The DevOps team needs to quickly identify which specific service calls are contributing to increased latency and where bottlenecks occur within the distributed application. Which AWS service provides the most efficient way to achieve this?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon CloudWatch Logs Insights to analyze service logs for error rates and latency metrics.","AWS X-Ray to trace requests across services and visualize the service map to pinpoint latency.","Amazon OpenSearch Service to index all application logs and perform full-text searches for performance keywords.","AWS CloudTrail Lake to audit API calls and identify any unauthorized or slow API interactions across services."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is AWS X-Ray, which is specifically designed for distributed tracing. It allows the DevOps team to visualize the end-to-end request flow, identify latency issues, and pinpoint performance bottlenecks across different microservices. Its service map provides a clear overview of component interactions. Option A (incorrect) can help analyze logs, but it doesn''t provide the integrated, end-to-end tracing and service map visualization that X-Ray offers for distributed applications. Option C (incorrect) is suitable for general log analysis and search, but less efficient for visualizing complex request flows and identifying specific bottlenecks within a distributed system compared to X-Ray. Option D (incorrect) is AWS CloudTrail Lake, which is for auditing AWS API calls, not for tracing application performance within a microservices architecture.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain39_8',
  NOW(),
  25,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_39_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_39_m_39_1_65',
  'To collect operating system logs and custom application logs from Amazon EC2 instances and send them to CloudWatch Logs, which component should be installed and configured on the instances?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Systems Manager Agent (SSM Agent)","AWS X-Ray Daemon","CloudWatch Agent","Amazon Kinesis Agent"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is the CloudWatch Agent, which is specifically designed to collect system-level metrics, operating system logs, and custom application logs from Amazon EC2 instances and on-premises servers, and send them to CloudWatch. Option A (incorrect) is the AWS Systems Manager Agent (SSM Agent), used for managing EC2 instances and on-premises systems, but not primarily for collecting and sending logs to CloudWatch. Option B (incorrect) is the AWS X-Ray Daemon, used for collecting trace data for AWS X-Ray. Option D (incorrect) is the Amazon Kinesis Agent, used for sending data to Kinesis Data Streams or Kinesis Data Firehose, which could be an alternative for log collection but the CloudWatch Agent is the direct solution for CloudWatch Logs.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain39_8',
  NOW(),
  26,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_39_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_39_m_39_1_66',
  'A company needs to stream high volumes of application logs from various sources to a centralized analytics platform for real-time processing and visualization. The solution must be fully managed and scale automatically. Which AWS service should be used to ingest and deliver these logs to Amazon OpenSearch Service?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CloudTrail Lake","Amazon S3","Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose","Amazon CloudWatch Logs Insights"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose, a fully managed service for delivering real-time streaming data to destinations like Amazon S3, Amazon Redshift, Amazon OpenSearch Service, and Splunk. It scales automatically and requires minimal administrative effort, making it ideal for high-volume log ingestion to an analytics platform. Option A (incorrect) is AWS CloudTrail Lake, which is for auditing AWS API activity, not for general application log streaming. Option B (incorrect) is Amazon S3, an object storage service that can be a destination for logs, but not the service that ingests and streams data to OpenSearch in real-time. Option D (incorrect) is Amazon CloudWatch Logs Insights, which is for querying logs already in CloudWatch Logs, not for ingesting and streaming them.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain39_8',
  NOW(),
  27,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_39_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_39_m_39_1_67',
  'What is the primary purpose of the Service Map feature in AWS X-Ray?',
  'mcq',
  '["To display aggregated metrics for all services in a CloudWatch Dashboard.","To visualize the components of a distributed application and their connections, showing latency and errors.","To provide a graphical interface for querying CloudWatch Logs data.","To map AWS API calls to specific AWS resources for auditing purposes."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) accurately describes the primary purpose of the AWS X-Ray Service Map: to visualize the components of a distributed application, their connections, and highlight performance data such as latency and errors. This helps in understanding the application''s architecture and identifying bottlenecks. Option A (incorrect) describes a function of CloudWatch Dashboards. Option C (incorrect) describes a function of CloudWatch Logs Insights. Option D (incorrect) describes a function related to AWS CloudTrail.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain39_8',
  NOW(),
  28,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_39_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_39_m_39_1_68',
  'A DevOps engineer needs to quickly identify common error patterns and trends across various application logs stored in Amazon CloudWatch Logs. The goal is to perform ad-hoc queries to troubleshoot issues without setting up a separate analytics platform. Which CloudWatch feature is best suited for this task?',
  'mcq',
  '["CloudWatch Alarms","CloudWatch Dashboards","CloudWatch Logs Insights","CloudWatch Contributor Insights"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is CloudWatch Logs Insights, which allows DevOps engineers to interactively search and analyze log data in CloudWatch Logs using a powerful, SQL-like query language. This is ideal for ad-hoc troubleshooting, identifying error patterns, and understanding log trends without needing to export logs or set up another analytics service. Option A (incorrect) is CloudWatch Alarms, which are used to trigger actions based on metric thresholds, not for interactive log querying. Option B (incorrect) is CloudWatch Dashboards, used for visualizing metrics and logs, but not for interactive querying. Option D (incorrect) is CloudWatch Contributor Insights, which identifies top contributors to log data but doesn''t provide the flexible querying capabilities of Logs Insights.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain39_8',
  NOW(),
  29,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_39_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_39_m_39_1_69',
  'What is the primary purpose of CloudWatch cross-account observability?',
  'mcq',
  '["To allow a single AWS account to manage IAM roles and policies for multiple other accounts.","To enable a monitoring account to seamlessly view and interact with metrics, logs, and traces from source accounts.","To automatically replicate CloudWatch alarms and dashboards across all accounts in an AWS Organization.","To provide a centralized billing view for CloudWatch usage across multiple AWS accounts."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) accurately describes CloudWatch cross-account observability, which allows you to monitor and troubleshoot applications and infrastructure across multiple AWS accounts from a single monitoring account. This includes viewing and interacting with metrics, logs, and traces (from X-Ray) from source accounts. Option A (incorrect) describes a function of AWS Organizations and IAM, not specifically CloudWatch observability. Option C (incorrect) is not an automatic feature of cross-account observability; dashboards and alarms need to be configured or shared. Option D (incorrect) describes a function of AWS Billing and Cost Management, not CloudWatch observability.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain39_8',
  NOW(),
  30,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_39_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_39_m_39_1_70',
  'A company currently sends all application logs directly from Amazon EC2 instances to CloudWatch Logs in each respective account. The operations team is struggling with cross-account analysis and unified compliance reporting. To address this, which solution should the DevOps team implement to centralize logs and simplify analysis?',
  'mcq',
  '["Continue sending logs directly to CloudWatch Logs, but use CloudWatch Logs Insights in each account for individual analysis.","Configure Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose in each account to stream logs to a central Amazon S3 bucket, then use CloudWatch cross-account observability.","Deploy a custom log aggregation service on an EC2 instance in a central account to pull logs from all other CloudWatch Logs groups.","Create CloudWatch Logs subscription filters in each account to forward logs to a central CloudWatch Logs group for consolidated viewing."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the recommended approach to overcome the anti-pattern (ap-4.1-001) of siloed logs. By configuring Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose in each account to stream logs to a central Amazon S3 bucket (or Amazon OpenSearch Service), logs are aggregated centrally. Combined with CloudWatch cross-account observability, this enables unified search, retention, and compliance reporting. Option A (incorrect) perpetuates the anti-pattern by keeping logs siloed in individual accounts, making cross-account analysis difficult. Option C (incorrect) introduces significant operational overhead and complexity by requiring a custom service to manage log pulling, which is less scalable and reliable than managed AWS services. Option D (incorrect) can work for some consolidation, but managing numerous subscription filters can become complex at scale, and it doesn''t offer the same flexibility for diverse destinations or long-term cost-effective storage as Kinesis Data Firehose to S3.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain39_8',
  NOW(),
  31,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_39_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_39_m_39_1_71',
  'A DevOps team needs to establish a centralized logging solution for multiple Amazon EC2 instances across several AWS accounts. The primary goal is to aggregate logs into a single destination for analysis and long-term retention with the LEAST operational overhead. Which AWS service is best suited for collecting and delivering these logs to a central Amazon S3 bucket?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose","AWS CloudWatch Logs Agent","AWS Lambda with custom S3 upload logic","Amazon SQS with a custom consumer"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose, which is a fully managed service that automatically scales to deliver streaming data to destinations like Amazon S3, Amazon OpenSearch Service, and Amazon Redshift. It requires minimal operational overhead as it handles scaling, data buffering, and delivery without needing to manage servers or write custom code for log aggregation. Option B (incorrect) is the AWS CloudWatch Logs Agent. While it can collect logs from EC2 instances, sending logs directly from each EC2 instance to CloudWatch Logs in different accounts, and then aggregating them, creates per-account silos and complicates cross-account analysis, increasing operational overhead for centralized management. This aligns with anti-pattern ap-4.1-001. Option C (incorrect) involves using AWS Lambda with custom S3 upload logic. While feasible, it requires developing, deploying, and maintaining custom Lambda functions, which introduces more operational overhead compared to a fully managed service like Kinesis Data Firehose. Option D (incorrect) suggests Amazon SQS with a custom consumer. This approach would require building a custom producer to send logs to SQS and a custom consumer to process messages and store them in S3, significantly increasing development and operational overhead.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain39_8',
  NOW(),
  32,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_39_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_39_m_39_1_72',
  'Which AWS service is specifically designed for real-time log analytics, full-text search, and interactive visualization of operational data?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CloudTrail Lake","Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose","Amazon OpenSearch Service","Amazon CloudWatch Logs"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is Amazon OpenSearch Service, which is a fully managed service that makes it easy to deploy, operate, and scale OpenSearch clusters. It is ideal for real-time application monitoring, log analytics, and website search, providing powerful full-text search and interactive visualization capabilities. Option A (incorrect) is AWS CloudTrail Lake, which is used for auditing and security analysis of AWS API activity, not general application log analytics. Option B (incorrect) is Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose, which is a data delivery service used for streaming data to various destinations, including Amazon OpenSearch Service, but it does not provide analytics or visualization capabilities itself. Option D (incorrect) is Amazon CloudWatch Logs, which is a service for monitoring, storing, and accessing log files from AWS services and applications. While it allows for basic querying with Logs Insights, it is not primarily designed for the advanced, real-time, full-text search and visualization capabilities offered by Amazon OpenSearch Service.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain39_8',
  NOW(),
  33,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_39_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_39_m_39_1_73',
  'A development team wants to collect custom metrics from their application running on Amazon EC2 instances, such as the number of user logins per minute. Which AWS service should they use to publish and store these custom metrics?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CloudTrail","Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose","AWS X-Ray","Amazon CloudWatch"]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'Option D (correct) is Amazon CloudWatch. It is the monitoring and observability service for AWS resources and applications. It allows users to publish custom metrics using the PutMetricData API, which can then be visualized on dashboards, used for alarms, and analyzed. Option A (incorrect) is AWS CloudTrail, which records AWS API calls and related events for auditing and security analysis, not for publishing custom application metrics. Option B (incorrect) is Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose, a service for delivering streaming data to various destinations, not for publishing or storing custom metrics directly. Option C (incorrect) is AWS X-Ray, which helps developers analyze and debug distributed applications by providing tracing capabilities, but it does not handle general custom application metrics.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain39_8',
  NOW(),
  34,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_39_1',
  'medium'
);

