  'm_36_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_1_101',
  'A development team needs to set up a basic CI/CD pipeline using AWS CodePipeline to automate the build and deployment of their application. Order the fundamental stages of a CodePipeline.',
  'ordering',
  '["Add a Build stage using AWS CodeBuild to compile code and create artifacts.","Add a Source stage to pull code from a version control repository like AWS CodeCommit.","Add a Deploy stage using AWS CodeDeploy to deploy the application to target environments.","Create a new AWS CodePipeline."]'::json,
  '{3,1,0,2}',
  'The logical flow for setting up an AWS CodePipeline starts with creating the pipeline itself. Option D (Create a new AWS CodePipeline) is the initial action. Following this, Option B (Add a Source stage to pull code from a version control repository) is always the first stage in any CI/CD pipeline. Next, Option A (Add a Build stage using AWS CodeBuild to compile code and create artifacts) processes the source code. Finally, Option C (Add a Deploy stage using AWS CodeDeploy to deploy the application to target environments) pushes the built artifacts to the infrastructure.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  49,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_1_102',
  'A developer is creating a `buildspec.yml` file for an AWS CodeBuild project. Order the standard phases that typically appear in a `buildspec.yml` file for a successful build process.',
  'ordering',
  '["The `install` phase for installing dependencies.","The `build` phase for running build commands.","The `pre_build` phase for commands to execute before the build.","The `post_build` phase for commands to execute after the build."]'::json,
  '{0,2,1,3}',
  'The standard execution order of phases in an AWS CodeBuild `buildspec.yml` file is sequential. Option A (The `install` phase for installing dependencies) is typically the first execution phase after cloning the source. Next, Option C (The `pre_build` phase for commands to execute before the build) runs preparatory commands. Following this, Option B (The `build` phase for running build commands) executes the core build logic. Finally, Option D (The `post_build` phase for commands to execute after the build) handles cleanup or artifact processing.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  50,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_1_103',
  'A team wants to implement a Blue/Green deployment strategy using AWS CodeDeploy for an application running on Amazon EC2 instances to minimize downtime and risk. Order the steps to configure this deployment type.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create a new deployment group in CodeDeploy configured for Blue/Green deployments, specifying the original and replacement environments.","Define the `AppSpec` file to manage the deployment process, including hooks for traffic shifting.","Create an application in AWS CodeDeploy.","Initiate a deployment to the CodeDeploy application, which will provision the new ''green'' environment and shift traffic."]'::json,
  '{2,0,1,3}',
  'Configuring a Blue/Green deployment with AWS CodeDeploy starts with the application definition. Option C (Create an application in AWS CodeDeploy) is the initial setup. Next, Option A (Create a new deployment group in CodeDeploy configured for Blue/Green deployments) defines the target environment and strategy. Following this, Option B (Define the `AppSpec` file to manage the deployment process) specifies the deployment logic and lifecycle hooks. Finally, Option D (Initiate a deployment to the CodeDeploy application) triggers the actual Blue/Green deployment process.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  51,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_1_104',
  'A critical application deployment pipeline requires manual approval before deploying to the production environment. Order the steps to add a manual approval action to an existing AWS CodePipeline.',
  'ordering',
  '["Add an ''Approval'' action to the new stage, specifying an SNS topic for notifications and an IAM role.","Edit the AWS CodePipeline and add a new stage before the production deployment stage.","Configure the SNS topic to send notifications to the appropriate approvers.","Deploy a change through the pipeline and wait for the manual approval notification."]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}',
  'To add a manual approval to an AWS CodePipeline, you first need to modify the pipeline structure. Option B (Edit the AWS CodePipeline and add a new stage before the production deployment stage) creates the necessary container for the approval. Next, Option A (Add an ''Approval'' action to the new stage) configures the approval mechanism. Following this, Option C (Configure the SNS topic to send notifications to the appropriate approvers) ensures that the right people are alerted for approval. Finally, Option D (Deploy a change through the pipeline and wait for the manual approval notification) tests the configured approval process.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  52,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_1_105',
  'A DevOps team wants to ensure that failed AWS CodeDeploy deployments automatically roll back to the last known good version to minimize service disruption. Order the steps to configure automated rollback triggers.',
  'ordering',
  '["Configure the CodeDeploy deployment group to use the created CloudWatch alarm as a rollback trigger.","Create a CloudWatch alarm that monitors a relevant metric (e.g., error rate, latency) for the deployed application.","Deploy a new application version that intentionally causes the CloudWatch alarm to breach its threshold.","Ensure the IAM role used by CodeDeploy has permissions to interact with CloudWatch alarms."]'::json,
  '{1,3,0,2}',
  'To configure automated rollback triggers in AWS CodeDeploy, you first need a mechanism to detect deployment failures. Option B (Create a CloudWatch alarm that monitors a relevant metric) establishes this detection. Next, Option D (Ensure the IAM role used by CodeDeploy has permissions to interact with CloudWatch alarms) is a critical security step. Following this, Option A (Configure the CodeDeploy deployment group to use the created CloudWatch alarm as a rollback trigger) links the alarm to the deployment process, directly addressing anti-pattern ap-1.1-002. Finally, Option C (Deploy a new application version that intentionally causes the CloudWatch alarm to breach its threshold) is a way to test and verify the rollback functionality.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  53,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_1_106',
  'To speed up subsequent builds, a development team wants to configure caching for their AWS CodeBuild project. Order the steps to enable and configure a cache for a CodeBuild project.',
  'ordering',
  '["Specify the cache configuration (e.g., S3 bucket or local cache) in the CodeBuild project settings.","Update the `buildspec.yml` to define which paths should be cached.","Create a new AWS CodeBuild project or modify an existing one.","Run a build to populate the cache and observe improved build times on subsequent runs."]'::json,
  '{2,0,1,3}',
  'Configuring caching for an AWS CodeBuild project begins with the project itself. Option C (Create a new AWS CodeBuild project or modify an existing one) is the initial step. Next, Option A (Specify the cache configuration in the CodeBuild project settings) defines where the cache will be stored. Following this, Option B (Update the `buildspec.yml` to define which paths should be cached) tells CodeBuild what content to store in the cache. Finally, Option D (Run a build to populate the cache and observe improved build times on subsequent runs) validates the caching setup.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  54,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_1_107',
  'A company needs to deploy applications to a production AWS account from a development AWS account using AWS CodePipeline. Order the initial steps to set up cross-account pipeline deployments.',
  'ordering',
  '["In the production account, create an IAM role that the development account''s CodePipeline can assume, granting necessary deployment permissions.","In the development account, create an IAM role for CodePipeline with permissions to access source, build, and deploy resources, and to assume the production account''s role.","Configure the CodePipeline in the development account to include a cross-account deployment action.","Establish trust relationships between the IAM roles in both accounts."]'::json,
  '{0,1,3,2}',
  'Setting up cross-account CodePipeline deployments requires careful IAM configuration across both accounts. Option A (In the production account, create an IAM role that the development account''s CodePipeline can assume) establishes the target permissions. Next, Option B (In the development account, create an IAM role for CodePipeline) defines the pipeline''s execution permissions. Following this, Option D (Establish trust relationships between the IAM roles in both accounts) is crucial for allowing the development account''s pipeline role to assume the production role. Finally, Option C (Configure the CodePipeline in the development account to include a cross-account deployment action) integrates this setup into the pipeline.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  55,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_1_108',
  'A team is performing an in-place deployment of an application to Amazon EC2 instances using AWS CodeDeploy. Order the typical lifecycle events that occur during an in-place deployment.',
  'ordering',
  '["DownloadBundle: The new application revision is downloaded to the instances.","Install: The new application revision is installed on the instances.","ApplicationStop: The application is stopped on the instances.","BeforeInstall: Scripts run before the new application is installed."]'::json,
  '{2,0,3,1}',
  'During an AWS CodeDeploy in-place deployment, the lifecycle events follow a specific order. Option C (ApplicationStop) is typically the first step to gracefully shut down the running application. Next, Option A (DownloadBundle) fetches the new application revision to the instances. Following this, Option D (BeforeInstall) runs preparatory scripts before installation. Finally, Option B (Install) places the new application files onto the instances.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  56,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_1_109',
  'A DevOps team is reviewing their AWS CodePipeline IAM roles and identifies that a single role with broad administrative access is being used across all stages. Order the steps to refine these permissions to adhere to the principle of least privilege.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create separate, granular IAM roles for each CodePipeline stage (Source, Build, Deploy), granting only the specific permissions required for that stage''s actions.","Update the CodePipeline stage configurations to use their respective least-privilege IAM roles.","Identify the specific AWS services and actions required by each stage of the CodePipeline.","Remove the overly permissive IAM role from the CodePipeline and verify pipeline functionality."]'::json,
  '{2,0,1,3}',
  'To refine IAM permissions for AWS CodePipeline to adhere to the principle of least privilege, you must first understand the requirements. Option C (Identify the specific AWS services and actions required by each stage) is the initial analysis. Next, Option A (Create separate, granular IAM roles for each CodePipeline stage) implements the least-privilege design. Following this, Option B (Update the CodePipeline stage configurations to use their respective least-privilege IAM roles) applies these new roles to the pipeline, addressing the anti-pattern of using a single pipeline role with admin access. Finally, Option D (Remove the overly permissive IAM role from the CodePipeline and verify pipeline functionality) is a crucial step to ensure the new configuration works as expected and the old, insecure role is no longer in use.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  57,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_36_m_36_1_110',
  'An application is being updated using an AWS CodeDeploy in-place deployment strategy on Amazon EC2 instances. Arrange the following lifecycle event hooks in the order they are typically executed during a successful deployment.',
  'ordering',
  '["`ApplicationStop`","`BeforeInstall`","`AfterInstall`","`ApplicationStart`"]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'During an AWS CodeDeploy in-place deployment, lifecycle event hooks are executed in a specific order. The first hook to run is typically `ApplicationStop` (Option A), which stops the currently running application on the instance. Next, `BeforeInstall` (Option B) runs any scripts needed before the new application revision files are copied. After the new files are installed, `AfterInstall` (Option C) executes scripts for tasks like configuring the application. Finally, `ApplicationStart` (Option D) starts the newly deployed application on the instance.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain36_8',
  NOW(),
  58,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_36_1',
  'medium'
);

