INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_40_m_40_1_67',
  'A company wants to receive notifications for critical AWS Health events that impact their specific resources and trigger an automated response, such as creating a support ticket. Which integration pattern should they use?',
  'mcq',
  '["Polling the AWS Health Dashboard periodically","AWS Health events routed to Amazon EventBridge, then to AWS Lambda","AWS CloudTrail logs analyzed by Amazon CloudWatch Alarms","Manual subscription to AWS Health RSS feeds"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is AWS Health events routed to Amazon EventBridge, then to AWS Lambda. AWS Health publishes events to EventBridge, which allows for real-time, automated processing. An EventBridge rule can filter for specific Health events and trigger an AWS Lambda function to create a support ticket or perform other automated actions. Option A, polling the dashboard, is inefficient and not real-time. Option C, CloudTrail logs API activity, not service health events. Option D, manual RSS feeds, is not an automated response mechanism.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain40_8',
  NOW(),
  30,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_40_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_40_m_40_1_68',
  'A company needs to route events from various AWS services to multiple target services, such as AWS Lambda functions, Amazon SQS queues, and Amazon SNS topics, based on specific event patterns. Which AWS service is purpose-built for this event-driven architecture with content-based filtering?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon EventBridge","Amazon SQS","Amazon SNS","AWS CloudTrail"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) Amazon EventBridge is a serverless event bus that makes it easy to connect applications together using data from your own applications, integrated SaaS applications, and AWS services. It supports content-based filtering using event patterns to route events to specific targets, making it ideal for the described scenario. Option B (incorrect) Amazon SQS is a message queuing service used for decoupling and scaling microservices, not for content-based event routing to multiple diverse targets. Option C (incorrect) Amazon SNS is a notification service that uses a publish/subscribe model, primarily for fan-out messaging, but it has limited content-based filtering capabilities compared to EventBridge for routing to different target types. Option D (incorrect) AWS CloudTrail is a service that enables governance, compliance, and operational and risk auditing of your AWS account by logging actions, but it is not an event routing service.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain40_8',
  NOW(),
  31,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_40_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_40_m_40_1_69',
  'A security team wants to centralize all security findings from Amazon GuardDuty and automatically trigger a remediation workflow in a separate security account. Which is the MOST efficient way to route these findings to the central account for processing?',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure Amazon EventBridge with cross-account event routing to send GuardDuty findings to an event bus in the security account.","Set up an AWS Lambda function in each account to poll GuardDuty findings and push them to an Amazon SQS queue in the security account.","Use Amazon SNS topics in each account to publish GuardDuty findings and subscribe an Amazon SQS queue in the security account to all topics.","Manually configure AWS CloudWatch Logs to export GuardDuty findings to an Amazon S3 bucket in the security account for periodic processing."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the most efficient and recommended approach. Amazon EventBridge supports cross-account event routing, allowing you to centralize events, including Amazon GuardDuty findings, from multiple source accounts to a single event bus in a central security account. This provides native pattern matching and routing without custom code. Option B (incorrect) involves building custom polling logic with AWS Lambda in each account, which introduces significant operational overhead and complexity compared to EventBridge''s native capabilities. This represents the anti-pattern ap-5.1-001, building custom event routing logic instead of using EventBridge rules. Option C (incorrect) using Amazon SNS topics and Amazon SQS queues can facilitate event distribution, but EventBridge provides more robust content-based filtering and routing capabilities specifically designed for event-driven architectures, making it more efficient for complex routing scenarios like this. Option D (incorrect) manually configuring AWS CloudWatch Logs and Amazon S3 for periodic processing is a less real-time and more operationally intensive approach, failing the ''MOST efficient'' and automated remediation requirements.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain40_8',
  NOW(),
  32,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_40_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_40_m_40_1_70',
  'Which AWS service provides personalized views into the performance and availability of the AWS services underlying your AWS resources, and can integrate with Amazon EventBridge for automated responses?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Health","Amazon CloudWatch","AWS CloudTrail","AWS Systems Manager OpsCenter"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) AWS Health provides personalized information about events that can affect your AWS resources, such as scheduled maintenance, service degradations, or account notifications. It integrates directly with Amazon EventBridge, allowing you to create rules to capture AWS Health events and trigger automated actions, such as sending notifications or initiating remediation workflows. Option B (incorrect) Amazon CloudWatch is a monitoring and observability service that collects metrics, logs, and events from your AWS resources and applications, but it doesn''t provide personalized service health information like AWS Health. Option C (incorrect) AWS CloudTrail records API calls and related events made in your AWS account, providing an audit trail, but it does not offer service health notifications. Option D (incorrect) AWS Systems Manager OpsCenter is for tracking and resolving operational issues, not for providing personalized AWS service health information.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain40_8',
  NOW(),
  33,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_40_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_40_m_40_1_71',
  'A company needs to distribute a single event message to multiple subscribers, including an AWS Lambda function, an Amazon SQS queue, and an email address, to trigger different actions in parallel. Which AWS service pattern is best suited for this fan-out scenario?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon SNS fan-out pattern","Amazon SQS FIFO queue","Amazon EventBridge direct invocation","AWS Lambda with multiple destinations"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) The Amazon SNS fan-out pattern is specifically designed for distributing messages to multiple subscribers in parallel. An Amazon SNS topic can publish a message once, and that message will be delivered to all subscribed endpoints, which can include AWS Lambda functions, Amazon SQS queues, HTTP/S endpoints, and email addresses. Option B (incorrect) Amazon SQS FIFO (First-In, First-Out) queue is designed for strict message ordering and exactly-once processing, not for fan-out to multiple diverse targets. Option C (incorrect) Amazon EventBridge direct invocation would typically route an event to a single target or multiple targets based on rules, but the SNS fan-out pattern is more idiomatic for simple ''one-to-many'' message distribution to different types of subscribers. Option D (incorrect) AWS Lambda with multiple destinations can send invocation results to multiple targets, but it''s for the *result* of a Lambda function, not for initial event distribution to diverse targets from a single source.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain40_8',
  NOW(),
  34,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_40_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_40_m_40_1_72',
  'What is the primary function of Amazon GuardDuty?',
  'mcq',
  '["To continuously monitor for malicious activity and unauthorized behavior to protect AWS accounts and workloads.","To provide a centralized dashboard for managing security groups and network ACLs.","To encrypt data at rest and in transit across all AWS services.","To perform automated penetration testing on AWS infrastructure."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) Amazon GuardDuty is a threat detection service that continuously monitors for malicious activity and unauthorized behavior to protect your AWS accounts, workloads, and data stored in Amazon S3. It uses machine learning, anomaly detection, and integrated threat intelligence to identify and prioritize potential threats. Option B (incorrect) Managing security groups and network ACLs is typically done through Amazon VPC or AWS Firewall Manager, not GuardDuty. Option C (incorrect) Encryption is handled by services like AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS) and implemented across various AWS services, not GuardDuty. Option D (incorrect) Automated penetration testing is not a feature of GuardDuty; it is a threat detection service.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain40_8',
  NOW(),
  35,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_40_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_40_m_40_1_73',
  'A DevOps team wants to automatically respond to AWS Health events, such as scheduled maintenance for Amazon EC2 instances, by triggering an AWS Lambda function to perform pre-maintenance tasks. Which service combination should be used to achieve this?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Health integrated with Amazon EventBridge to trigger an AWS Lambda function.","Amazon CloudWatch Alarms monitoring EC2 instance health and invoking an AWS Lambda function.","AWS Systems Manager Automation runbooks scheduled to run periodically and check AWS Health Dashboard.","Amazon SNS topic subscribed to AWS Health notifications and then manually invoking an AWS Lambda function."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the most direct and automated approach. AWS Health events can be published to Amazon EventBridge. An EventBridge rule can then be configured to match specific AWS Health event patterns (e.g., EC2 scheduled maintenance) and trigger an AWS Lambda function directly, allowing for automated pre-maintenance actions. Option B (incorrect) Amazon CloudWatch Alarms monitor metrics and logs, not directly AWS Health events. While you could create custom metrics, it''s not the native integration path for AWS Health. Option C (incorrect) AWS Systems Manager Automation runbooks can be scheduled, but polling the AWS Health Dashboard periodically is less efficient and reactive than an event-driven approach. Option D (incorrect) An Amazon SNS topic can receive AWS Health notifications, but ''manually invoking an AWS Lambda function'' defeats the purpose of automation and introduces operational overhead.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain40_8',
  NOW(),
  36,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_40_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_40_m_40_1_74',
  'Which AWS service is commonly used in a fan-out pattern to send the same message to multiple subscribers, including email, SMS, and AWS Lambda functions?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon SNS","Amazon SQS","Amazon Kinesis Data Streams","Amazon EventBridge"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) Amazon SNS (Simple Notification Service) is a highly available, durable, secure, fully managed pub/sub messaging service that enables you to decouple microservices, distributed systems, and serverless applications. It is ideal for fan-out scenarios where a single message needs to be delivered to multiple subscribers, such as email, SMS, HTTP/S endpoints, and AWS Lambda functions. Option B (incorrect) Amazon SQS (Simple Queue Service) is a message queuing service primarily used for decoupling and scaling microservices, ensuring messages are processed by a single consumer or a group of consumers, not for fan-out to diverse endpoints simultaneously. Option C (incorrect) Amazon Kinesis Data Streams is a real-time data streaming service used for processing large streams of data records, not for general-purpose fan-out messaging to various subscriber types. Option D (incorrect) Amazon EventBridge is an event bus service for routing events between AWS services, SaaS applications, and your own applications, offering more advanced filtering and routing than simple fan-out, but SNS is the classic fan-out service.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain40_8',
  NOW(),
  37,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_40_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_40_m_40_1_75',
  'A security team detects a critical Amazon GuardDuty finding indicating a compromised Amazon EC2 instance. They need to automatically isolate the instance by modifying its security group and notify the incident response team. Which service combination can achieve this automated remediation with the LEAST latency?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon EventBridge rule capturing GuardDuty findings, triggering an AWS Lambda function to modify the security group and send an Amazon SNS notification.","AWS Systems Manager OpsCenter creating an OpsItem for the GuardDuty finding, which a human operator then manually acts upon.","AWS CloudTrail logs monitored by Amazon CloudWatch Logs, which then triggers an AWS Lambda function after a delay.","Periodic AWS Config rules checking for GuardDuty findings and initiating remediation through AWS Systems Manager Automation."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) provides the most direct and low-latency automated response. Amazon GuardDuty findings are published as events to Amazon EventBridge. An EventBridge rule can be configured to immediately capture specific GuardDuty findings and trigger an AWS Lambda function. The Lambda function can then execute custom code to modify the EC2 instance''s security group for isolation and send an Amazon SNS notification to the incident response team, all in near real-time. Option B (incorrect) involves AWS Systems Manager OpsCenter, which creates an OpsItem for human review, failing the ''automated remediation with the LEAST latency'' requirement. Option C (incorrect) relies on AWS CloudTrail logs and Amazon CloudWatch Logs, which can have a delay in log delivery and processing, making it less immediate than EventBridge''s direct integration with GuardDuty. Option D (incorrect) involves periodic checks by AWS Config, which introduces latency as remediation is not immediate upon detection, failing the ''LEAST latency'' requirement.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain40_8',
  NOW(),
  38,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_40_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_40_m_40_1_76',
  'What is the primary benefit of using an Amazon SQS queue in an event-driven architecture for event distribution?',
  'mcq',
  '["To decouple components and buffer messages, ensuring reliable delivery even if consumers are unavailable.","To enable real-time processing of streaming data with low latency.","To route events to multiple targets based on content-based filtering.","To provide a centralized audit trail of all API calls made in an AWS account."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the primary benefit of Amazon SQS. It acts as a buffer between producers and consumers, decoupling components of a distributed system. Messages are reliably stored in the queue until a consumer processes them, ensuring that events are not lost even if the consuming application is temporarily unavailable or overloaded. Option B (incorrect) describes services like Amazon Kinesis Data Streams or Amazon Managed Streaming for Apache Kafka (Amazon MSK), not SQS. Option C (incorrect) describes Amazon EventBridge, which is designed for content-based event routing. Option D (incorrect) describes AWS CloudTrail, which provides an audit trail of API calls.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain40_8',
  NOW(),
  39,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_40_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_40_m_40_1_77',
  'A DevOps team wants to implement an event-driven architecture where events from various AWS services are processed by different AWS Lambda functions based on specific attributes within the event payload. Which AWS service should be used to achieve this content-based routing efficiently?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon EventBridge","Amazon SQS","Amazon SNS","AWS Step Functions"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) Amazon EventBridge is the ideal service for content-based routing of events. It allows you to define event patterns that match specific attributes within an event''s payload and then route those events to different targets, such as AWS Lambda functions, based on those patterns. This provides powerful and flexible event filtering and routing capabilities. Option B (incorrect) Amazon SQS is a message queuing service primarily for decoupling and buffering messages, not for content-based routing to multiple distinct targets. Option C (incorrect) Amazon SNS is a publish/subscribe service for fan-out messaging, with limited content-based filtering compared to EventBridge. Option D (incorrect) AWS Step Functions is a serverless workflow service used for orchestrating complex multi-step processes, but it''s not primarily an event router for initial event distribution and filtering.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain40_8',
  NOW(),
  40,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_40_1',
  'medium'
);

