  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_41_m_41_3_56',
  'A company needs to ensure that all changes to critical AWS resources are logged for auditing purposes and that these logs are centrally stored and protected. Which service should they enable to record API calls and related events?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon CloudWatch Logs","AWS Config","AWS CloudTrail","AWS Security Hub"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (AWS CloudTrail) is the correct service. AWS CloudTrail records API calls and related events made by or on behalf of an AWS account and delivers log files to an Amazon S3 bucket. This provides an audit trail of actions taken within the AWS environment. Option A (Amazon CloudWatch Logs) is a service for monitoring and storing logs from various sources, but CloudTrail is specifically for AWS API activity. Option B (AWS Config) records resource configurations and changes, but not API calls. Option D (AWS Security Hub) aggregates security findings, not raw API call logs.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain41_8',
  NOW(),
  49,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_41_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_41_m_41_3_57',
  'A security team wants to ensure continuous compliance monitoring and near real-time detection of configuration drift from established security baselines across their multi-account AWS environment. They also need a centralized view of compliance status. Which combination of services best achieves this?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CloudTrail and Amazon GuardDuty","AWS Config and AWS Security Hub","Amazon Inspector v2 and AWS Audit Manager","AWS IAM Access Analyzer and AWS Organizations"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (AWS Config and AWS Security Hub) is the correct combination. AWS Config continuously monitors resource configurations for compliance against defined rules and detects configuration drift. AWS Security Hub then aggregates these compliance findings, along with other security findings, into a centralized dashboard, providing a near real-time overview of the compliance status. Option A (AWS CloudTrail and Amazon GuardDuty) are for auditing API calls and threat detection, respectively, not continuous configuration compliance. Option C (Amazon Inspector v2 and AWS Audit Manager) are for vulnerability scanning and audit evidence collection, which are different concerns. Option D (AWS IAM Access Analyzer and AWS Organizations) are for identifying external access and managing accounts, not continuous configuration compliance monitoring.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain41_8',
  NOW(),
  50,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_41_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_41_m_41_3_58',
  'A large enterprise has a strict compliance requirement to audit all resource configurations against internal security policies and industry benchmarks (e.g., PCI DSS, HIPAA) across hundreds of AWS accounts. The security team needs a scalable and automated way to deploy and manage a collection of AWS Config rules and remediation actions consistently across all accounts within specific Organizational Units (OUs). They also need to generate aggregated compliance reports for auditors. Which AWS service feature is designed to simplify the deployment and management of these compliance controls at scale?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Audit Manager assessments","AWS Config conformance packs","AWS Security Hub custom insights","AWS Organizations Service Control Policies (SCPs)"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (AWS Config conformance packs) is the correct solution. Conformance packs are a collection of AWS Config rules and remediation actions that can be easily deployed as a single entity across an AWS Organization or specific accounts. They simplify the management and deployment of compliance baselines at scale, providing a summary compliance score and aggregated reports, directly addressing the need for consistent deployment and reporting across many accounts. This also helps to avoid the anti-pattern of manual, ad-hoc compliance audits. Option A (AWS Audit Manager assessments) helps automate the collection of audit evidence for compliance, but it doesn''t deploy or manage Config rules. Option C (AWS Security Hub custom insights) allows you to define custom filters for security findings but doesn''t deploy compliance rules. Option D (AWS Organizations Service Control Policies (SCPs)) are preventive controls that restrict actions, not detective controls for auditing resource configurations or deploying Config rules.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain41_8',
  NOW(),
  51,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_41_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_41_m_41_3_59',
  'A compliance officer at a regulated company needs to prepare for an upcoming external audit (e.g., PCI DSS, SOC 2). The process of manually gathering evidence from various AWS services (e.g., AWS CloudTrail logs, AWS Config compliance reports, IAM policies) is time-consuming and error-prone. The officer requires an automated solution that continuously collects and organizes audit-related evidence from their AWS accounts, maps it to specific control requirements, and facilitates the generation of audit-ready reports. Which AWS service is specifically designed to streamline this evidence collection and audit preparation process?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Config","AWS Audit Manager","AWS Security Hub","Amazon Macie"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (AWS Audit Manager) is the correct service. AWS Audit Manager helps you continuously audit your AWS usage to simplify how you assess risk and compliance with regulations and industry standards. It automates the collection of evidence from various AWS services, maps it to control frameworks (like PCI DSS, HIPAA, SOC 2), and helps you manage audit projects and generate audit-ready reports. This directly addresses the anti-pattern of performing compliance audits manually. Option A (AWS Config) assesses, audits, and evaluates the configurations of your AWS resources for compliance, but it doesn''t automate the evidence collection and report generation specifically for external audits in the same way Audit Manager does. Option C (AWS Security Hub) aggregates security findings and checks against security standards, but it''s not primarily an audit evidence collection and management tool. Option D (Amazon Macie) is for data discovery and classification, specifically for sensitive data like PII, not for general audit evidence collection.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain41_8',
  NOW(),
  52,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_41_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_41_m_41_3_60',
  'A media company stores vast amounts of unstructured data, including customer-generated content and internal documents, in Amazon S3. The compliance team is concerned about the presence of personally identifiable information (PII) within this data, which could lead to regulatory violations if not properly managed. They need an automated service that can discover, classify, and report on sensitive data, such as names, addresses, and credit card numbers, across their S3 buckets. The solution should also provide insights into the security posture of these buckets. Which AWS service is designed for this specific data classification and protection task?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Config","Amazon GuardDuty","Amazon Macie","AWS IAM Access Analyzer"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Amazon Macie) is the correct service. Amazon Macie is a data security and data privacy service that uses machine learning and pattern matching to discover, classify, and protect sensitive data in AWS, primarily in Amazon S3. It identifies PII, financial data, and other sensitive information, and provides visibility into data access patterns and security alerts. Option A (AWS Config) assesses, audits, and evaluates the configurations of your AWS resources, not the content of the data itself. Option B (Amazon GuardDuty) is a threat detection service that monitors for malicious activity and unauthorized behavior, not for sensitive data discovery. Option D (AWS IAM Access Analyzer) helps identify resources shared with an external entity, focusing on access policies, not data content.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain41_8',
  NOW(),
  53,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_41_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_41_m_41_3_61',
  'A DevOps team is responsible for maintaining continuous compliance for their applications running on AWS. They need to ensure that all Amazon EC2 instances are launched with specific Amazon Machine Images (AMIs) and that Amazon EBS volumes are always encrypted. Currently, they perform manual checks quarterly, which often results in discovering non-compliant resources months after they were provisioned. The team needs to automate the detection of non-compliant resources in near real-time and integrate these findings into a centralized security dashboard. Which AWS service should be used to achieve continuous compliance monitoring and reporting?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Audit Manager","AWS Config","AWS CloudTrail","Amazon EventBridge"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (AWS Config) is the correct service for continuous compliance monitoring. AWS Config continuously monitors and records your AWS resource configurations and allows you to evaluate them against desired configurations using predefined or custom rules. It can detect non-compliant resources in near real-time, which directly addresses the anti-pattern of performing manual, quarterly compliance audits. Findings can be integrated with AWS Security Hub for centralized reporting. Option A (AWS Audit Manager) helps automate the collection of audit evidence for compliance, but it''s not primarily for continuous, real-time configuration monitoring. Option C (AWS CloudTrail) records API calls and related events, which is crucial for auditing but doesn''t evaluate resource configurations against rules. Option D (Amazon EventBridge) is a serverless event bus that can route events, useful for automated responses, but it doesn''t perform the compliance evaluation itself.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain41_8',
  NOW(),
  54,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_41_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_41_m_41_3_62',
  'A security team needs to establish a robust security posture across their multi-account AWS environment. They want to aggregate security findings from various AWS services like Amazon GuardDuty, Amazon Inspector, and Amazon Macie into a single pane of glass. Furthermore, they need to continuously monitor their compliance against industry standards and best practices, and trigger automated responses to critical findings. The solution should provide a consolidated view of security alerts and compliance status. Which AWS service is designed to centralize and manage security findings and compliance status?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CloudTrail","Amazon CloudWatch","AWS Security Hub","AWS Systems Manager"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (AWS Security Hub) is the correct service. AWS Security Hub provides a comprehensive view of your security posture across your AWS accounts by aggregating security findings from various AWS services (like GuardDuty, Inspector, Macie, Config) and partner solutions. It also allows you to check your environment against security industry standards and best practices (e.g., CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark) and facilitates automated responses to security incidents. Option A (AWS CloudTrail) records API calls and related events, which is crucial for auditing but doesn''t aggregate findings or provide compliance checks. Option B (Amazon CloudWatch) is a monitoring and observability service for resources and applications, but it doesn''t specifically aggregate security findings or provide compliance standard checks. Option D (AWS Systems Manager) helps you gain operational insights and automate operational tasks, but it is not a centralized security findings management platform.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain41_8',
  NOW(),
  55,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_41_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_41_m_41_3_63',
  'A large e-commerce company needs to detect unusual and potentially malicious activity within their AWS environment, such as unauthorized access to S3 buckets, unusual API call patterns, or compromised EC2 instances. The security team requires a service that continuously monitors for these threats, uses machine learning to identify anomalies, and provides actionable security findings. The solution must operate with minimal configuration and integrate with a centralized security dashboard. Which AWS service is purpose-built for continuous threat detection and monitoring?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Config","Amazon Inspector v2","Amazon GuardDuty","AWS IAM Access Analyzer"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Amazon GuardDuty) is the correct service. Amazon GuardDuty is a threat detection service that continuously monitors for malicious activity and unauthorized behavior to protect your AWS accounts and workloads. It uses machine learning, anomaly detection, and integrated threat intelligence to identify potential threats such as unusual API calls, compromised EC2 instances, and unauthorized access to S3 buckets. It operates with minimal configuration and sends findings to AWS Security Hub. Option A (AWS Config) assesses, audits, and evaluates the configurations of your AWS resources for compliance, not for threat detection. Option B (Amazon Inspector v2) performs continuous vulnerability scanning of EC2 instances and container images, which is different from threat detection. Option D (AWS IAM Access Analyzer) helps identify resources shared with an external entity, focusing on access policies, not active threat detection.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain41_8',
  NOW(),
  56,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_41_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_41_m_41_3_64',
  'A security architect is designing a solution to ensure that no AWS resources (such as Amazon S3 buckets, AWS KMS keys, or IAM roles) are inadvertently configured to allow public or cross-account access that is not explicitly authorized. The architect needs a service that continuously analyzes resource policies and identifies any external access, providing findings that can be reviewed and acted upon. This is crucial for maintaining a strong security posture and preventing data breaches. Which AWS service should be used to proactively identify external access to resources?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CloudTrail","AWS Config","AWS IAM Access Analyzer","Amazon GuardDuty"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (AWS IAM Access Analyzer) is the correct service. AWS IAM Access Analyzer helps you identify the resources in your organization and accounts, such as Amazon S3 buckets or AWS KMS keys, that are shared with an external entity. It continuously monitors resource policies (e.g., S3 bucket policies, IAM role trust policies, KMS key policies) and generates findings for any public or cross-account access, allowing the security team to review and remediate unintended access. Option A (AWS CloudTrail) records API calls and related events, which is for auditing actions, not for analyzing resource policies for external access. Option B (AWS Config) assesses, audits, and evaluates the configurations of your AWS resources for compliance, but it''s not specifically designed for the deep analysis of resource policies for external access that Access Analyzer provides. Option D (Amazon GuardDuty) is a threat detection service that monitors for malicious activity and unauthorized behavior, not for static analysis of resource policies.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain41_8',
  NOW(),
  57,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_41_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_41_m_41_3_65',
  'A DevOps team is implementing an automated remediation workflow for non-compliant resources. For example, if an Amazon S3 bucket is created without encryption, an AWS Config rule detects this violation. The team needs to automatically trigger an AWS Lambda function to encrypt the bucket and then report the remediation status to a central security dashboard. Which combination of AWS services will allow for the detection of non-compliance, triggering of an automated remediation, and centralized reporting of the outcome?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CloudTrail for detection, Amazon EventBridge for triggering, and Amazon CloudWatch for reporting.","AWS Config for detection, Amazon EventBridge for triggering a Lambda function, and AWS Security Hub for reporting.","Amazon GuardDuty for detection, AWS Systems Manager Automation for remediation, and AWS Audit Manager for reporting.","AWS IAM Access Analyzer for detection, AWS Step Functions for triggering, and Amazon SNS for reporting."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (AWS Config for detection, Amazon EventBridge for triggering a Lambda function, and AWS Security Hub for reporting) is the correct and most efficient combination. AWS Config continuously monitors resource configurations and detects non-compliance. When a Config rule is violated, it can send an event to Amazon EventBridge. EventBridge can then trigger an AWS Lambda function to perform the automated remediation (e.g., encrypting the S3 bucket). The findings from AWS Config, including remediation status, are aggregated into AWS Security Hub, providing a centralized dashboard for monitoring and reporting. Option A (AWS CloudTrail for detection, Amazon EventBridge for triggering, and Amazon CloudWatch for reporting) is incorrect because CloudTrail records API calls, but AWS Config is specifically designed for continuous configuration compliance detection. Option C (Amazon GuardDuty for detection, AWS Systems Manager Automation for remediation, and AWS Audit Manager for reporting) is incorrect because GuardDuty is for threat detection, not configuration compliance, and Audit Manager is for evidence collection, not real-time reporting of remediation status. Option D (AWS IAM Access Analyzer for detection, AWS Step Functions for triggering, and Amazon SNS for reporting) is incorrect because IAM Access Analyzer detects external access, not general configuration non-compliance.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain41_8',
  NOW(),
  58,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_41_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_41_m_41_3_66',
  'A global media company uses Amazon S3 to store large volumes of digital assets, some of which contain sensitive intellectual property. The security team needs to ensure that these sensitive assets are always encrypted with customer-managed keys (CMKs) and that access to these keys is strictly controlled. Additionally, they need to identify if any of these S3 buckets are inadvertently configured for public access or shared with unauthorized external entities. The solution must integrate with a centralized security and compliance dashboard. Which combination of AWS services provides the necessary capabilities for CMK management, sensitive data access analysis, and centralized security reporting?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon Macie for data discovery, AWS KMS for CMKs, and AWS Security Hub for reporting.","AWS Secrets Manager for key storage, AWS Config for public access detection, and Amazon GuardDuty for threat detection.","AWS CloudTrail for logging, Amazon CloudWatch for monitoring, and AWS IAM Access Analyzer for external access.","AWS Audit Manager for evidence collection, CloudFormation Guard for IaC validation, and Amazon EventBridge for automation."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (Amazon Macie for data discovery, AWS KMS for CMKs, and AWS Security Hub for reporting) is the most appropriate combination. Amazon Macie can discover and classify sensitive data within S3 buckets, helping to identify which assets require CMK encryption. AWS KMS is the service for creating and managing customer-managed encryption keys (CMKs) for S3. AWS Security Hub then aggregates findings from Macie (for sensitive data and bucket security posture) and can integrate with AWS Config rules (which can check for KMS encryption and public access) to provide a centralized dashboard for security and compliance. Option B (AWS Secrets Manager for key storage, AWS Config for public access detection, and Amazon GuardDuty for threat detection) is incorrect because Secrets Manager is for credentials, not encryption keys. Option C (AWS CloudTrail for logging, Amazon CloudWatch for monitoring, and AWS IAM Access Analyzer for external access) are valuable services, but they don''t cover CMK management or sensitive data discovery directly. Option D (AWS Audit Manager for evidence collection, CloudFormation Guard for IaC validation, and Amazon EventBridge for automation) are for audit, IaC, and automation respectively, not directly for CMK management, sensitive data discovery, and centralized security reporting.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain41_8',
  NOW(),
  59,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_41_3',
  'hard'
);

