INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_41_m_41_1_46',
  'A company wants to delegate administrative tasks to development teams within their AWS Organization, but they need to ensure that these teams cannot inadvertently or intentionally exceed certain security guardrails. Which IAM feature should be used to set the maximum permissions that an IAM role can grant?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Organizations Service Control Policies (SCPs)","IAM permission boundaries","AWS IAM Access Analyzer","IAM roles with least privilege policies"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (IAM permission boundaries) is the correct feature. A permission boundary is an advanced feature for using a managed policy to set the maximum permissions that an identity-based policy can grant to an IAM entity (user or role). This allows for delegation of administration while ensuring that the delegated roles cannot exceed the defined boundary. Option A (AWS Organizations Service Control Policies (SCPs)) apply to all accounts in an organization and define maximum permissions at the account level, not for individual IAM roles within an account. Option C (AWS IAM Access Analyzer) identifies external access, not sets maximum permissions. Option D (IAM roles with least privilege policies) is a best practice, but a permission boundary adds an additional, higher-level guardrail.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain41_8',
  NOW(),
  68,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_41_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_41_m_41_1_47',
  'A company wants to enforce a policy across all accounts in their AWS Organization that prevents any IAM user or role from creating Amazon S3 buckets with public read/write access. This policy must apply even to account administrators. Which AWS Organizations feature should be used?',
  'mcq',
  '["IAM permission boundaries","AWS IAM Access Analyzer","AWS Organizations Service Control Policies (SCPs)","AWS Config rules with auto-remediation"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (AWS Organizations Service Control Policies (SCPs)) is the correct feature. SCPs are powerful preventive controls that can restrict the maximum permissions for all IAM users and roles, including the root user, within affected accounts in an AWS Organization. This ensures that no entity can create publicly accessible S3 buckets if the SCP denies that action. Option A (IAM permission boundaries) apply to individual IAM entities within an account, not across all accounts or to the root user. Option B (AWS IAM Access Analyzer) identifies existing external access but doesn''t prevent actions. Option D (AWS Config rules with auto-remediation) are detective and reactive controls; they detect non-compliance after it occurs, whereas SCPs prevent it from happening in the first place.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain41_8',
  NOW(),
  69,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_41_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_41_m_41_1_48',
  'A DevOps team manages several microservices, each using a dedicated IAM role. They want to ensure that even if an administrator mistakenly grants overly permissive policies to these roles, the roles cannot perform actions outside a predefined set of safe operations. Which IAM feature should be applied to these roles?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Organizations Service Control Policies (SCPs)","IAM permission boundaries","AWS IAM Access Analyzer findings","AWS Config conformance packs"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (IAM permission boundaries) is the correct feature. A permission boundary sets the maximum permissions that an identity-based policy can grant to an IAM entity. This means that even if an administrator attaches a policy that grants broader permissions, the effective permissions of the role will be limited by the permission boundary, acting as an additional guardrail. Option A (AWS Organizations Service Control Policies (SCPs)) apply at the account level and affect all entities within the account, not specific roles with granular control. Option C (AWS IAM Access Analyzer findings) identify existing external access, they don''t enforce permissions. Option D (AWS Config conformance packs) are for compliance monitoring and deployment of rules, not for setting maximum permissions on individual IAM roles.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain41_8',
  NOW(),
  70,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_41_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_41_m_41_1_49',
  'A company needs to automate the rotation of database credentials for an Amazon RDS instance every 90 days to meet security compliance requirements. This rotation should occur without requiring manual intervention or application downtime. Which AWS services should be used together to achieve this?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CloudTrail and Amazon EventBridge","AWS Secrets Manager and AWS Lambda","AWS Config and AWS Security Hub","Amazon GuardDuty and Amazon Inspector v2"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (AWS Secrets Manager and AWS Lambda) is the correct combination. AWS Secrets Manager can automatically rotate credentials for supported databases like Amazon RDS. For custom rotation logic or unsupported services, Secrets Manager integrates with AWS Lambda, allowing a Lambda function to perform the actual rotation steps. This automates the process, addressing the anti-pattern of manual credential rotation. Option A (AWS CloudTrail and Amazon EventBridge) are for auditing and event-driven automation, but not direct secret rotation. Option C (AWS Config and AWS Security Hub) are for configuration compliance and security findings aggregation. Option D (Amazon GuardDuty and Amazon Inspector v2) are for threat detection and vulnerability scanning.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain41_8',
  NOW(),
  71,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_41_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_41_m_41_1_50',
  'Which AWS service is specifically designed to store, manage, and automatically rotate secrets such as database credentials, API keys, and other sensitive information?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS KMS","AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store","AWS Secrets Manager","Amazon S3"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (AWS Secrets Manager) is the correct service. It is purpose-built for storing, managing, and automatically rotating secrets. This helps improve the security posture by reducing the risk of hardcoded credentials. Option A (AWS KMS) is for managing encryption keys. Option B (AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store) can store configuration data and secrets, but its primary focus is not automatic rotation. Option D (Amazon S3) is object storage and not designed for secret management and rotation.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain41_8',
  NOW(),
  72,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_41_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_41_m_41_1_51',
  'A security auditor needs to identify all IAM users and roles in an AWS account that have not been used for an extended period, to implement a policy of least privilege and reduce the attack surface. Which AWS service can provide these ''unused access'' findings?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CloudTrail","AWS Config","AWS IAM Access Analyzer","Amazon GuardDuty"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (AWS IAM Access Analyzer) is the correct service. In addition to identifying external access, IAM Access Analyzer also provides ''unused access'' findings, which highlight IAM users, roles, and access keys that have not been used for a specified period. This helps administrators identify and remove unnecessary permissions, enforcing the principle of least privilege. Option A (AWS CloudTrail) records API calls but doesn''t directly identify unused access. Option B (AWS Config) monitors resource configurations. Option D (Amazon GuardDuty) is a threat detection service.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain41_8',
  NOW(),
  73,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_41_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_41_m_41_1_52',
  'A company has sensitive data stored in Amazon S3 buckets. They want to ensure that these buckets are never inadvertently made public or shared with external AWS accounts. They need a proactive mechanism to prevent such configurations from being deployed. Which combination of AWS services and features provides the strongest preventive control?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Config rules for S3 public access and AWS Security Hub findings.","AWS Organizations Service Control Policies (SCPs) and S3 Block Public Access settings.","AWS IAM Access Analyzer for external access findings and Amazon GuardDuty.","IAM permission boundaries on all IAM roles and AWS CloudTrail logging."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (AWS Organizations Service Control Policies (SCPs) and S3 Block Public Access settings) provides the strongest preventive control. SCPs can deny actions that would make S3 buckets public or share them externally at the organizational level, preventing any account member from overriding this. S3 Block Public Access settings, when enabled at the account or bucket level, provide another layer of preventive control to block public access. Option A (AWS Config rules and AWS Security Hub) are detective controls; they identify non-compliance after it occurs. Option C (AWS IAM Access Analyzer and Amazon GuardDuty) are for identifying existing external access and threat detection, not preventing configurations. Option D (IAM permission boundaries and AWS CloudTrail logging) are important for least privilege and auditing, but SCPs and S3 Block Public Access are more direct and robust for preventing public S3 access across an organization.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain41_8',
  NOW(),
  74,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_41_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_41_m_41_1_53',
  'A DevOps team is responsible for managing credentials for various applications and databases. To enhance security and reduce operational burden, they want to automate the rotation of these credentials on a regular schedule. Which AWS service is specifically designed for this purpose, often integrating with AWS Lambda for custom rotation logic?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS KMS","AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store","AWS Secrets Manager","Amazon EventBridge"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (AWS Secrets Manager) is the correct service. AWS Secrets Manager is designed to store, manage, and automatically rotate credentials such as database passwords, API keys, and other secrets. It has built-in integrations for many AWS services and can use AWS Lambda functions for custom rotation logic, addressing the anti-pattern of manual credential rotation. Option A (AWS KMS) is for managing encryption keys, not secret rotation. Option B (AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store) can store secrets but does not offer automatic rotation capabilities. Option D (Amazon EventBridge) is an event bus service for building event-driven architectures, not for secret management.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain41_8',
  NOW(),
  75,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_41_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_41_m_41_1_54',
  'A large enterprise uses AWS Organizations to manage its multi-account environment. The security team needs to enforce a global policy that prevents any IAM user or role from accessing AWS services outside of a specific region (e.g., ''us-east-1''). This policy must apply to all accounts within a particular Organizational Unit (OU) and override any individual IAM policies that might grant broader regional access. The goal is to minimize the attack surface and ensure data residency compliance. Which AWS Organizations feature should be implemented to achieve this preventive control?',
  'mcq',
  '["IAM permission boundaries","AWS Config rules","Service Control Policies (SCPs)","AWS IAM Access Analyzer"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Service Control Policies (SCPs)) are the correct AWS Organizations feature. SCPs are powerful preventive controls that allow you to specify the maximum permissions for an OU or account. They can be used to deny access to specific AWS regions for all IAM users and roles within the affected accounts, overriding any local IAM policies. This provides a centralized and effective way to enforce region restrictions. Option A (IAM permission boundaries) set the maximum permissions for individual IAM entities, which would be cumbersome to manage across an entire OU and might not cover all access paths. Option B (AWS Config rules) are detective controls that monitor for non-compliance after resources are provisioned, not preventive controls that restrict actions. Option D (AWS IAM Access Analyzer) helps identify resources shared with an external entity, which is a detective control, not a preventive one for region restriction.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain41_8',
  NOW(),
  76,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_41_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_41_m_41_1_55',
  'A security architect needs to audit all AWS resources in a multi-account environment to identify any policies that grant public or cross-account access to sensitive resources like Amazon S3 buckets, AWS KMS keys, or Amazon SQS queues. The goal is to proactively detect and remediate unintended external access to prevent data leakage or unauthorized operations. The solution must provide a consolidated view of these findings across all accounts. Which AWS service is specifically designed to analyze resource policies and report external access?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon GuardDuty","AWS CloudTrail","AWS IAM Access Analyzer","AWS Config"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (AWS IAM Access Analyzer) is the correct service. AWS IAM Access Analyzer helps you identify the resources in your organization and accounts, such as Amazon S3 buckets, AWS KMS keys, or Amazon SQS queues, that are shared with an external entity. It continuously monitors resource policies and generates findings for any public or cross-account access, allowing the security team to review and remediate unintended access. Option A (Amazon GuardDuty) is a threat detection service that monitors for malicious activity and unauthorized behavior, not for static analysis of resource policies for external access. Option B (AWS CloudTrail) records API calls and related events, which is for auditing actions, not for analyzing resource policies for external access. Option D (AWS Config) assesses, audits, and evaluates the configurations of your AWS resources for compliance, but it''s not specifically designed for the deep analysis of resource policies for external access that Access Analyzer provides.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain41_8',
  NOW(),
  77,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_41_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_41_m_41_1_56',
  'A DevOps team manages several applications that connect to Amazon RDS databases. The database credentials for these applications are currently stored in plaintext within application configuration files, requiring manual updates and restarts for rotation. The security team has mandated an automated solution that securely stores these credentials, rotates them every 60 days, and allows applications to retrieve them dynamically. The solution must also support different database engines and integrate with AWS Lambda for custom rotation logic. Which AWS service is purpose-built to address this automated credential rotation requirement?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store","AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS)","AWS Secrets Manager","Amazon DynamoDB"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (AWS Secrets Manager) is the correct service. AWS Secrets Manager is designed for storing, managing, and automatically rotating secrets such as database credentials, API keys, and other sensitive data. It integrates with AWS Lambda to execute custom rotation logic for various database types, ensuring credentials are rotated on a defined schedule without downtime or manual intervention. This directly addresses the anti-pattern of manual credential rotation. Option A (AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store) provides secure storage for parameters, including secrets, but its built-in rotation capabilities are more limited than Secrets Manager, and it doesn''t offer the same level of automated integration for database credential rotation. Option B (AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS)) is for managing encryption keys, not for storing or rotating secrets themselves. Option D (Amazon DynamoDB) is a NoSQL database service and is not designed for secret management or automated rotation.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain41_8',
  NOW(),
  78,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_41_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_41_m_41_1_57',
  'A large enterprise needs to centralize the management of various sensitive credentials, including database passwords, API keys for third-party services, and SSH keys for bastion hosts. These secrets are currently scattered across different systems and managed inconsistently. The security team requires a highly secure, auditable, and easily accessible service that can store these secrets, control access to them using IAM, and integrate with applications for dynamic retrieval. The service should also support versioning of secrets. Which AWS service is best suited for this centralized secret management?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon S3","AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store","AWS Secrets Manager","AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS)"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (AWS Secrets Manager) is the best service for centralized secret management. It is designed to store, manage, and retrieve secrets securely. It offers features like automatic rotation, fine-grained access control with IAM, auditing with AWS CloudTrail, and integration with applications for dynamic retrieval. It also supports versioning of secrets. Option A (Amazon S3) is object storage and, while it can store encrypted files, it''s not purpose-built for secret management with features like rotation and dynamic retrieval. Option B (AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store) can store secure strings, but Secrets Manager offers more advanced features specifically for secrets, such as automated rotation and direct integration with various services. Option D (AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS)) is for managing encryption keys, not for storing the secrets themselves.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain41_8',
  NOW(),
  79,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_41_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_41_m_41_1_58',
  'A security team manages a multi-account AWS environment under AWS Organizations. They want to ensure that all accounts adhere to a strict security policy that prevents the creation of any Amazon S3 buckets with public read/write access. Additionally, they need to identify any existing S3 buckets that might inadvertently grant public access. The solution requires both preventive controls at the organizational level and continuous monitoring for existing resources. Which combination of AWS services provides the most effective approach for both preventing and detecting public S3 access across the organization?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Config rules for detection and AWS CloudTrail for logging.","AWS Organizations Service Control Policies (SCPs) for prevention and AWS IAM Access Analyzer for detection.","Amazon GuardDuty for threat detection and AWS Security Hub for findings aggregation.","IAM permission boundaries for prevention and Amazon Macie for sensitive data discovery."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (AWS Organizations Service Control Policies (SCPs) for prevention and AWS IAM Access Analyzer for detection) is the most effective combination. SCPs can be used to prevent the creation of public S3 buckets at the organizational level, acting as a strong preventive control. AWS IAM Access Analyzer continuously analyzes resource policies, including S3 bucket policies, to identify any existing buckets that grant public or cross-account access, serving as a powerful detective control. This combination provides both proactive prevention and continuous detection. Option A (AWS Config rules for detection and AWS CloudTrail for logging) are detective controls but don''t prevent the initial creation of public buckets. Option C (Amazon GuardDuty for threat detection and AWS Security Hub for findings aggregation) are for threat detection and findings aggregation, not specifically for preventing or detecting public S3 access via policy analysis. Option D (IAM permission boundaries for prevention and Amazon Macie for sensitive data discovery) is incorrect because permission boundaries are for individual IAM entities, not organizational-level prevention of public S3 buckets, and Macie is for sensitive data discovery, not public access detection.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain41_8',
  NOW(),
  80,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_41_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_41_m_41_1_59',
  'A central security team needs to consolidate all security findings related to external access to resources across their multi-account AWS environment. They are currently using AWS IAM Access Analyzer to identify publicly accessible S3 buckets and roles with external trust policies. The team requires a centralized dashboard that aggregates these findings, allows for prioritization, and integrates with incident response workflows. Which AWS service should be used to centralize the findings from AWS IAM Access Analyzer and other security services?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon CloudWatch Logs","AWS Systems Manager Explorer","AWS Security Hub","AWS Audit Manager"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (AWS Security Hub) is the correct service for centralizing security findings. AWS Security Hub aggregates security findings from various AWS services, including AWS IAM Access Analyzer, Amazon GuardDuty, Amazon Inspector, and Amazon Macie, into a single, consolidated view. This allows the security team to prioritize findings, view their security posture, and integrate with incident response tools. Option A (Amazon CloudWatch Logs) is a logging service and can store logs, but it doesn''t provide the aggregation, prioritization, and dashboarding capabilities of Security Hub for security findings. Option B (AWS Systems Manager Explorer) provides an operational dashboard for your AWS resources but is not specifically designed for aggregating security findings from various security services. Option D (AWS Audit Manager) helps automate the collection of audit evidence for compliance, but it''s not a centralized security findings aggregation platform.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain41_8',
  NOW(),
  81,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_41_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_41_m_41_1_60',
  'A global organization operates a multi-account AWS environment and needs to enforce a consistent security baseline across all accounts within a specific Organizational Unit (OU). This baseline includes preventing the use of specific deprecated AWS services and ensuring that no IAM user can elevate their privileges beyond a predefined maximum. The central security team wants to achieve this with the highest level of control and minimal operational overhead for individual account teams, ensuring that these policies cannot be circumvented by local IAM policies. Which combination of AWS Organizations and IAM features should be used?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Config conformance packs for auditing and IAM permission boundaries for privilege control.","AWS Organizations Service Control Policies (SCPs) for global deny and IAM permission boundaries for privilege control.","AWS IAM Access Analyzer for detection and AWS Security Hub for reporting.","AWS Audit Manager for evidence collection and AWS Secrets Manager for credential rotation."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (AWS Organizations Service Control Policies (SCPs) for global deny and IAM permission boundaries for privilege control) is the most effective combination. SCPs provide preventive controls at the organizational level, allowing the central security team to deny access to specific AWS services or actions across all accounts in an OU, overriding local IAM policies. IAM permission boundaries, when applied to IAM users/roles, set the maximum permissions they can ever be granted, ensuring that even if an identity-based policy attempts to grant more, the boundary limits it. Together, they enforce a strong, consistent security baseline. Option A (AWS Config conformance packs for auditing and IAM permission boundaries for privilege control) is incorrect because Config conformance packs are for detective auditing, not preventive control at the organizational level. Option C (AWS IAM Access Analyzer for detection and AWS Security Hub for reporting) are detective controls for identifying external access and aggregating findings, not for enforcing preventive policies. Option D (AWS Audit Manager for evidence collection and AWS Secrets Manager for credential rotation) are for audit evidence and secret management, unrelated to the core requirement of enforcing service and privilege control.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain41_8',
  NOW(),
  82,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_41_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_41_m_41_1_61',
  'A DevOps team uses AWS Secrets Manager to store and automatically rotate database credentials for their applications. The security team needs to implement continuous monitoring for any unusual or unauthorized access patterns to these secrets, such as attempts to retrieve secrets from an unknown IP address or by an unauthorized IAM role. They require a service that can detect these anomalies and generate security findings that can be investigated. Which AWS service should be integrated with AWS Secrets Manager to provide this continuous threat detection for secret access?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Config","Amazon CloudWatch Logs","Amazon GuardDuty","AWS IAM Access Analyzer"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Amazon GuardDuty) is the correct service. Amazon GuardDuty is a threat detection service that continuously monitors for malicious activity and unauthorized behavior, including unusual access patterns to AWS Secrets Manager. It analyzes AWS CloudTrail events (which record Secrets Manager API calls) and other data sources to detect anomalies like attempts to retrieve secrets from unusual locations or by unauthorized principals, generating security findings for investigation. Option A (AWS Config) assesses resource configurations for compliance, not for active threat detection on secret access. Option B (Amazon CloudWatch Logs) can store logs from Secrets Manager, but it requires custom alarms and analysis to detect anomalies, whereas GuardDuty provides built-in, intelligent threat detection. Option D (AWS IAM Access Analyzer) identifies resources shared with external entities by analyzing resource policies, not for detecting active, anomalous access attempts.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain41_8',
  NOW(),
  83,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_41_1',
  'hard'
);

