INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_39_m_39_1_107',
  'A company operates critical applications in a multi-account AWS environment and requires a centralized security operations team to monitor application logs from all accounts in a single Amazon OpenSearch Service domain for compliance and threat detection. The solution must efficiently stream logs from various source accounts to a central logging account. Order the steps to configure cross-account log aggregation.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create an Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose delivery stream in the central logging account, configured to deliver to the Amazon OpenSearch Service domain.","Configure resource policies on the central logging account''s Kinesis Data Firehose delivery stream to allow log group access from source accounts.","In each source account, create subscription filters on relevant Amazon CloudWatch Logs log groups to send logs to the central logging account''s Kinesis Data Firehose.","Set up an Amazon OpenSearch Service domain in the central logging account with appropriate security and access configurations."]'::json,
  '{3,0,1,2}',
  'First, the Amazon OpenSearch Service domain should be set up in the central logging account (Option D) to be the ultimate destination for aggregated logs. Next, an Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose delivery stream is created in the central logging account (Option A) to act as the ingestion point for logs. Then, resource policies on this Firehose stream must be configured (Option B) to grant permission for log groups in the source accounts to send data. Finally, in each source account, subscription filters are created (Option C) on the CloudWatch Logs log groups to stream logs to the central Kinesis Data Firehose, completing the cross-account flow.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain39_8',
  NOW(),
  10,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_39_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_39_m_39_1_108',
  'A development team is migrating a complex microservices application to AWS Lambda and Amazon ECS. They need a comprehensive solution to visualize service dependencies, identify performance bottlenecks, and troubleshoot latency issues across the distributed architecture. The solution should provide end-to-end request tracing. Order the steps to implement AWS X-Ray for distributed tracing.',
  'ordering',
  '["Deploy the X-Ray daemon or integrate the X-Ray SDK into application code for Amazon ECS tasks and AWS Lambda functions.","Configure IAM permissions for application components to send trace data to AWS X-Ray.","Enable active tracing for relevant AWS services, such as API Gateway or Application Load Balancer, to initiate traces.","View service maps, traces, and segment details in the AWS X-Ray console to analyze performance."]'::json,
  '{2,1,0,3}',
  'To implement AWS X-Ray, first, active tracing should be enabled for services like API Gateway or Application Load Balancer (Option C) to start the tracing process for incoming requests. Next, IAM permissions must be configured (Option B) to allow application components to send trace data to X-Ray. Then, the X-Ray daemon or SDK needs to be deployed/integrated (Option A) within the application code or containers (ECS tasks, Lambda functions) to capture detailed segment data. Finally, after data is collected, the X-Ray console can be used (Option D) to view and analyze the service maps and traces.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain39_8',
  NOW(),
  11,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_39_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_39_m_39_1_109',
  'An operations team manages a critical application running on Amazon EC2 instances, processing high volumes of data. They need to monitor specific application-level metrics, such as transaction processing time and queue depth, which are not available as standard CloudWatch metrics. They also require a consolidated view of these custom metrics alongside standard infrastructure metrics. Order the steps to publish custom metrics and create a CloudWatch dashboard.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create a custom CloudWatch dashboard and add widgets to display the newly published custom metrics alongside relevant standard metrics.","Modify the application code on Amazon EC2 instances to use the AWS SDK to publish custom metrics to Amazon CloudWatch.","Ensure the IAM role attached to the Amazon EC2 instances has permissions to publish metrics to CloudWatch.","Verify that the custom metrics are appearing in the CloudWatch console under the specified namespace."]'::json,
  '{1,2,3,0}',
  'The process begins by modifying the application code (Option B) to emit custom metrics using the AWS SDK. Before these metrics can be published, the IAM role associated with the EC2 instances must have the necessary permissions (Option C). Once the application is updated and permissions are set, it''s crucial to verify that the custom metrics are successfully appearing in the CloudWatch console (Option D). Finally, a custom CloudWatch dashboard can be created (Option A) to visualize these metrics alongside other relevant data, providing a consolidated view.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain39_8',
  NOW(),
  12,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_39_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_39_m_39_1_110',
  'A DevOps engineer needs to quickly troubleshoot intermittent errors reported by an application running on AWS Lambda. The application generates verbose logs in Amazon CloudWatch Logs. The engineer needs to identify patterns, filter specific error messages, and analyze trends across a large volume of log data to pinpoint the root cause. Order the steps to effectively use CloudWatch Logs Insights for this troubleshooting scenario.',
  'ordering',
  '["Construct a Logs Insights query to filter for specific error messages, time ranges, and fields of interest.","Navigate to the CloudWatch console and select Logs Insights.","Analyze the query results to identify patterns, frequencies, and correlations of error messages.","Select the relevant log groups associated with the AWS Lambda function."]'::json,
  '{1,3,0,2}',
  'To use CloudWatch Logs Insights, the first step is to navigate to the CloudWatch console and select Logs Insights (Option B). Next, the relevant log groups associated with the Lambda function must be selected (Option D) to specify the data source. After selecting the log groups, a Logs Insights query is constructed (Option A) to filter and analyze the log data for specific error messages or patterns. Finally, the query results are analyzed (Option C) to identify the root cause or trends of the intermittent errors.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain39_8',
  NOW(),
  13,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_39_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_39_m_39_1_111',
  'An e-commerce platform experiences performance degradation during peak hours, and the operations team suspects a few specific customers or product IDs are generating an unusually high load. They need a way to quickly identify the top N contributors (e.g., IP addresses, user IDs, product IDs) causing the most log entries or errors within their Amazon CloudWatch Logs. Order the steps to configure and utilize CloudWatch Contributor Insights.',
  'ordering',
  '["Navigate to the CloudWatch console, select Contributor Insights, and create a new rule.","Specify the log group containing the application logs and define the fields (e.g., ''customer_id'', ''product_id'') to analyze as contributors.","Review the generated Contributor Insights graphs and reports to identify the top N contributors and their impact.","Define a filter pattern for the rule to focus on relevant log events, such as errors or high-volume requests."]'::json,
  '{0,1,3,2}',
  'To use CloudWatch Contributor Insights, first, navigate to the CloudWatch console and initiate the creation of a new rule (Option A). Next, specify the target log group and define the fields that represent the contributors (Option B), such as customer_id or product_id. After that, define a filter pattern (Option D) to narrow down the analysis to specific events like errors or high-volume requests. Finally, review the generated graphs and reports (Option C) to identify the top N contributors causing the performance degradation.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain39_8',
  NOW(),
  14,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_39_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_39_m_39_1_112',
  'A security team requires a highly available and scalable solution for real-time security event monitoring and historical log analysis from various AWS services. They need to perform complex queries, create custom dashboards, and set up alerts based on security findings. Order the steps to set up Amazon OpenSearch Service for this log analytics use case.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create an Amazon OpenSearch Service domain, configuring instance types, storage, and network access (e.g., VPC access).","Configure Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose delivery streams to ingest logs from various sources (e.g., CloudWatch Logs, CloudTrail) and deliver them to the OpenSearch Service domain.","Set up IAM access policies for the OpenSearch Service domain to control who can perform searches and create dashboards.","Use OpenSearch Dashboards to create visualizations, dashboards, and alerts for security event monitoring and analysis."]'::json,
  '{0,2,1,3}',
  'To establish an Amazon OpenSearch Service for log analytics, the first step is to create the OpenSearch Service domain (Option A), specifying its core infrastructure and network configuration. Next, IAM access policies must be set up (Option B) to define who can interact with the domain. After the domain is ready, Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose delivery streams are configured (Option C) to ingest logs from various AWS services and deliver them to the OpenSearch domain. Finally, OpenSearch Dashboards are used (Option D) to create visualizations, dashboards, and alerts for security event monitoring.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain39_8',
  NOW(),
  15,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_39_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_39_m_39_1_113',
  'An application logs contain specific error codes that indicate critical failures. The operations team wants to create an Amazon CloudWatch metric based on the occurrence of these error codes in the logs, and then set an alarm if the count exceeds a threshold. Order the steps to create a CloudWatch metric from log data and configure an alarm.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create a metric filter on the relevant Amazon CloudWatch Logs log group, specifying the pattern for the error code.","Configure an Amazon CloudWatch alarm based on the newly created custom metric, setting a threshold and notification action.","Verify that the metric filter is correctly extracting data by observing the custom metric in the CloudWatch console.","Define the metric namespace, metric name, and value to be extracted from the log events using the metric filter."]'::json,
  '{0,3,2,1}',
  'The process begins by creating a metric filter on the relevant CloudWatch Logs log group (Option A), which is the mechanism to scan log data. Within this filter, the metric namespace, name, and value extraction must be defined (Option D) to specify how the metric is generated from log events. After configuration, it''s important to verify that the metric filter is working correctly by checking the custom metric in the CloudWatch console (Option C). Finally, an Amazon CloudWatch alarm is configured (Option B) to trigger based on this custom metric''s threshold.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain39_8',
  NOW(),
  16,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_39_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_39_m_39_1_114',
  'A central operations team needs to monitor the health and performance of applications deployed across multiple AWS accounts within their organization. They want to consolidate metrics, logs, and traces into a single monitoring account for unified visibility without requiring engineers to switch accounts. Order the steps to configure cross-account observability in CloudWatch.',
  'ordering',
  '["In the monitoring account, create a monitoring account sink and attach a resource policy that allows source accounts to link.","In each source account, create a monitoring account source and link it to the monitoring account sink.","Configure CloudWatch dashboards, Logs Insights queries, and X-Ray service maps in the monitoring account to visualize aggregated data.","Ensure appropriate IAM permissions are set in both monitoring and source accounts for cross-account data access."]'::json,
  '{0,1,3,2}',
  'To set up cross-account observability, first, a monitoring account sink is created in the central monitoring account (Option A), along with a resource policy to allow source accounts to link. Next, in each source account, a monitoring account source is created and linked to the sink in the monitoring account (Option B). It is crucial to ensure that appropriate IAM permissions are configured in both monitoring and source accounts (Option D) to facilitate secure cross-account data access. Finally, after data is flowing, CloudWatch dashboards, Logs Insights queries, and X-Ray service maps can be configured in the monitoring account (Option C) to visualize the aggregated data.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain39_8',
  NOW(),
  17,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_39_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_39_m_39_1_115',
  'A DevOps engineer needs to collect application logs and system metrics from an Amazon EC2 instance using the CloudWatch agent. Order the following steps to install and configure the CloudWatch agent.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create an IAM role with `CloudWatchAgentServerPolicy` and attach it to the EC2 instance.","Install the CloudWatch agent package on the EC2 instance.","Create a CloudWatch agent configuration file (`config.json`) specifying logs and metrics to collect.","Start the CloudWatch agent service on the EC2 instance."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'To install and configure the CloudWatch agent, Option A (Create an IAM role with `CloudWatchAgentServerPolicy` and attach it to the EC2 instance) is the crucial first step to grant the necessary permissions. Next, Option B (Install the CloudWatch agent package on the EC2 instance) places the agent software on the instance. After installation, Option C (Create a CloudWatch agent configuration file (`config.json`) specifying logs and metrics to collect) defines what data the agent should gather. Finally, Option D (Start the CloudWatch agent service on the EC2 instance) activates the agent to begin collecting and sending data. Attempting to install or start the agent without proper permissions or configuration would result in failure.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain39_8',
  NOW(),
  18,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_39_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_39_m_39_1_116',
  'A company wants to send application logs from Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose directly to Amazon OpenSearch Service for real-time analytics. Order the following steps to configure this delivery stream.',
  'ordering',
  '["Configure the Kinesis Data Firehose delivery stream to use Amazon OpenSearch Service as the destination.","Create an Amazon OpenSearch Service domain with appropriate scaling and access policies.","Specify indexing options, buffer hints, and error logging for the Firehose stream.","Grant Kinesis Data Firehose permissions to write to the OpenSearch Service domain."]'::json,
  '{1,3,0,2}',
  'To configure Kinesis Data Firehose to deliver logs to Amazon OpenSearch Service, Option B (Create an Amazon OpenSearch Service domain with appropriate scaling and access policies) is the prerequisite destination. Next, Option D (Grant Kinesis Data Firehose permissions to write to the OpenSearch Service domain) ensures Firehose has authorization. After that, Option A (Configure the Kinesis Data Firehose delivery stream to use Amazon OpenSearch Service as the destination) sets up the stream''s target. Finally, Option C (Specify indexing options, buffer hints, and error logging for the Firehose stream) fine-tunes the delivery behavior. Configuring Firehose before the OpenSearch domain exists or without permissions would lead to an invalid setup.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain39_8',
  NOW(),
  19,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_39_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_39_m_39_1_117',
  'A DevOps team needs to stream logs from a CloudWatch Log Group to an AWS Lambda function for custom processing. Order the following steps to create a subscription filter for this purpose.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create a CloudWatch Logs subscription filter, specifying the Lambda function as the destination.","Grant the CloudWatch Logs service principal permission to invoke the Lambda function.","Create an AWS Lambda function to process the incoming log events.","Select the target CloudWatch Log Group for the subscription filter."]'::json,
  '{2,1,3,0}',
  'To create a CloudWatch Log Group subscription filter, Option C (Create an AWS Lambda function to process the incoming log events) must exist first as the target. Next, Option B (Grant the CloudWatch Logs service principal permission to invoke the Lambda function) is essential for CloudWatch to be able to send events to Lambda. After that, Option D (Select the target CloudWatch Log Group for the subscription filter) specifies which logs to stream. Finally, Option A (Create a CloudWatch Logs subscription filter, specifying the Lambda function as the destination) establishes the connection. Creating the filter before the Lambda function or permissions are ready would cause the setup to fail.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain39_8',
  NOW(),
  20,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_39_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_39_m_39_1_118',
  'A DevOps team needs to create a custom metric in Amazon CloudWatch based on specific patterns found in application logs. Order the following steps to achieve this using a CloudWatch Logs metric filter.',
  'ordering',
  '["Define the metric filter pattern to match the desired log events.","Specify the metric namespace, metric name, and value to be extracted.","Navigate to the CloudWatch console and select Log Groups.","Create the metric filter for the target log group."]'::json,
  '{2,3,0,1}',
  'To create a custom metric from CloudWatch Logs, Option C (Navigate to the CloudWatch console and select Log Groups) is the initial navigation step. Next, Option D (Create the metric filter for the target log group) initiates the filter creation process. After that, Option A (Define the metric filter pattern to match the desired log events) specifies which log entries will generate the metric. Finally, Option B (Specify the metric namespace, metric name, and value to be extracted) defines the properties of the custom metric itself. Defining the pattern or metric properties before selecting the log group or creating the filter would be out of sequence.',
