INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_41_m_41_1_62',
  'Which AWS Organizations features are used to establish maximum permissions for IAM entities and enforce guardrails across member accounts? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Security Hub","Amazon GuardDuty","Service Control Policies (SCPs)","Permission Boundaries","AWS Secrets Manager"]'::json,
  '{2,3}',
  'Option C (Service Control Policies or SCPs) are a type of organization policy that you can use to manage permissions in your organization, offering central control over the maximum available permissions for all accounts. Option D (Permission Boundaries) are an advanced feature for using a managed policy to set the maximum permissions that an identity-based policy can grant to an IAM entity. AWS Security Hub (Option A) centralizes security findings. Amazon GuardDuty (Option B) is for threat detection. AWS Secrets Manager (Option E) is for secret management.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain41_8',
  NOW(),
  57,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_41_1',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_41_m_41_1_63',
  'To enforce least privilege for IAM users and roles within an AWS account and ensure that no entity can exceed a predefined set of permissions, which two AWS IAM features are most effective? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Permission Boundaries","IAM Policies","AWS Organizations SCPs","MFA","AWS IAM Access Analyzer"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Permission Boundaries) are advanced features for delegating administration and ensuring that the permissions granted to an IAM entity (user or role) do not exceed the boundary''s permissions. This acts as a ''ceiling'' for permissions. Option B (IAM Policies) are fundamental for granting specific permissions to users, groups, or roles. When combined with permission boundaries, they define the actual permissions within the boundary''s limits, enforcing least privilege. Option C (AWS Organizations SCPs) apply at the organizational level to restrict maximum permissions across accounts, but within a single account for specific IAM entities, permission boundaries and IAM policies are used. Option D (MFA) enhances authentication security but doesn''t define permissions. Option E (AWS IAM Access Analyzer) identifies external access or unused access, not for defining or enforcing least privilege.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain41_8',
  NOW(),
  58,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_41_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_41_m_41_1_64',
  'A company wants to identify any IAM roles or users that have not accessed AWS for a significant period, indicating potential unused access that should be reviewed or revoked. They also want to ensure that any external access granted to their Amazon S3 buckets is promptly identified. Which two AWS services can assist with these tasks? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS IAM Access Analyzer","AWS Security Hub","AWS CloudTrail","AWS Config","AWS Organizations"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (AWS IAM Access Analyzer) helps identify resources shared with an external entity and also identifies unused access for IAM roles and users, highlighting credentials that haven''t been used recently. Option B (AWS Security Hub) can ingest findings from IAM Access Analyzer, providing a centralized view of these security findings, including unused access and external access to S3 buckets, for easier management and remediation. Option C (AWS CloudTrail) logs API activity, which can be analyzed to determine usage, but Access Analyzer automates the identification of unused access. Option D (AWS Config) monitors resource configurations, not IAM access patterns or unused identities. Option E (AWS Organizations) is for managing multiple AWS accounts, not for analyzing IAM access patterns or unused access within accounts.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain41_8',
  NOW(),
  59,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_41_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_41_m_41_1_65',
  'To enhance the security of human access to AWS accounts, an organization needs to implement an additional layer of authentication beyond just a username and password. They also want to ensure that temporary, limited-privilege credentials can be granted to users and applications. Which two AWS IAM features or services address these requirements? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA)","AWS Security Token Service (STS)","IAM Access Keys","AWS Organizations SCPs","AWS Secrets Manager"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Multi-Factor Authentication - MFA) adds an extra layer of security to your AWS account by requiring more than just a password to log in, fulfilling the requirement for enhanced authentication. Option B (AWS Security Token Service - STS) enables you to create and provide trusted users with temporary, limited-privilege credentials to access your AWS resources, which is crucial for granting temporary access to users and applications. Option C (IAM Access Keys) are long-term credentials for programmatic access, not an additional layer of authentication for human login or temporary credentials. Option D (AWS Organizations SCPs) are for managing permissions across accounts, not for individual user authentication or temporary credentials. Option E (AWS Secrets Manager) is for managing and rotating secrets, not for user authentication or temporary access tokens.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain41_8',
  NOW(),
  60,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_41_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_41_m_41_1_66',
  'A DevOps team needs to ensure that all database credentials and API keys used by their applications are automatically rotated on a regular schedule without manual intervention. They also want to identify any IAM roles that are granting more permissions than necessary. Which two AWS services are most suitable for these tasks? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Secrets Manager","AWS IAM Access Analyzer","AWS Lambda","AWS Config","AWS Organizations SCPs"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (AWS Secrets Manager) is designed for managing, retrieving, and automatically rotating secrets like database credentials and API keys. Option B (AWS IAM Access Analyzer) can identify unused access for IAM roles and users, helping to pinpoint roles that might have excessive permissions that are not being utilized. Option C (AWS Lambda) is often used in conjunction with Secrets Manager to implement custom rotation logic, but Secrets Manager is the primary service for secret rotation itself. Option D (AWS Config) monitors resource configurations for compliance, not specifically for identifying over-privileged IAM roles or managing secrets. Option E (AWS Organizations SCPs) are for setting maximum permissions across accounts, not for analyzing specific IAM role usage within an account.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain41_8',
  NOW(),
  61,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_41_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_41_m_41_1_67',
  'An organization with multiple AWS accounts wants to centrally manage and enforce security guardrails, ensuring that no account can perform actions outside of defined policies. They also need to identify any external access granted to resources within these accounts. Which two AWS services or features are best suited for these requirements? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Organizations Service Control Policies (SCPs)","AWS IAM Access Analyzer","AWS Security Hub","AWS Config conformance packs","Amazon GuardDuty"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (AWS Organizations Service Control Policies - SCPs) are powerful preventive controls that can be applied at the Organization Unit (OU) or account level to restrict the maximum permissions available to IAM users and roles, effectively enforcing security guardrails across multiple accounts. Option B (AWS IAM Access Analyzer) helps identify resources in your organization and accounts that are shared with an external entity, fulfilling the requirement to identify external access. Option C (AWS Security Hub) aggregates findings from various services but doesn''t enforce preventive controls or perform the primary access analysis. Option D (AWS Config conformance packs) are for detecting non-compliance, not preventing actions at the organizational level. Option E (Amazon GuardDuty) is a threat detection service, not for enforcing preventive controls or identifying external access policies.',
  NOW(),
