INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_40_m_40_2_112',
  'An operations team uses Amazon CloudWatch Alarms to detect issues. They want to automatically create an operational item (OpsItem) in a central dashboard whenever an alarm enters an ALARM state. Order the steps to streamline the creation of actionable operational issues from monitoring alerts.',
  'ordering',
  '["Configure an Amazon CloudWatch Alarm to monitor a specific metric.","Create an Amazon EventBridge rule that captures CloudWatch Alarm state change events.","Set AWS Systems Manager OpsCenter as the target for the EventBridge rule.","Ensure OpsCenter is enabled and has permissions to receive events."]'::json,
  '{0,1,3,2}',
  'To automatically create OpsItems from CloudWatch Alarms, first, configure an Amazon CloudWatch Alarm (Option A) to monitor a specific metric and trigger on an ALARM state. Next, create an Amazon EventBridge rule (Option B) that specifically captures state change events from CloudWatch Alarms. Before routing, ensure that OpsCenter is enabled and has the necessary permissions (Option D) to receive events and create OpsItems. Finally, set AWS Systems Manager OpsCenter as the target (Option C) for the EventBridge rule, so that every time the alarm changes state to ALARM, an OpsItem is automatically created.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain40_8',
  NOW(),
  15,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_40_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_40_m_40_2_113',
  'After a major incident is resolved, the DevOps team needs to conduct a post-incident review to identify root causes and implement preventative measures. Order the steps to facilitate structured post-incident analysis and tracking of corrective actions using AWS Systems Manager Incident Manager.',
  'ordering',
  '["Record key incident details, timeline, and impact within the Incident Manager incident record.","Initiate a post-incident analysis within the Incident Manager console.","Document root causes, lessons learned, and corrective actions in the Incident Manager post-incident report.","Assign and track corrective action items to relevant teams using Incident Manager''s features."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'To facilitate post-incident analysis with Incident Manager, first, record key incident details, timeline, and impact (Option A) within the incident record itself during or immediately after the incident. Next, initiate a formal post-incident analysis (Option B) directly within the Incident Manager console. Then, document the identified root causes, lessons learned, and proposed corrective actions (Option C) in the dedicated post-incident report section. Finally, assign and track these corrective action items (Option D) to relevant teams, leveraging Incident Manager''s capabilities to ensure follow-through and prevent recurrence.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain40_8',
  NOW(),
  16,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_40_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_40_m_40_2_114',
  'An administrator wants to periodically check if all Amazon EC2 instances in their account are tagged with a ''Project'' tag. Order the steps to continuously monitor resource compliance against a defined rule using AWS Config.',
  'ordering',
  '["Enable AWS Config recording for EC2 instances.","Create an AWS Config managed rule for required tags (e.g., ''required-tags'').","Specify the ''Project'' tag as a parameter for the Config rule.","Deploy the AWS Config rule to start evaluating compliance."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'To continuously monitor EC2 instance tagging compliance with AWS Config, first, enable AWS Config recording (Option A) for EC2 instances to track their configuration. Next, create an AWS Config managed rule (Option B), such as ''required-tags'', which is designed to check for the presence of specific tags. Then, specify the ''Project'' tag (Option C) as a parameter for this Config rule, defining the exact tag key to be checked. Finally, deploy the AWS Config rule (Option D) to activate its continuous evaluation of EC2 instance compliance against the ''Project'' tag requirement.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain40_8',
  NOW(),
  17,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_40_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_40_m_40_2_115',
  'A custom compliance check requires complex logic to determine non-compliance and a multi-step remediation process that involves calling multiple AWS APIs. Order the steps to implement a highly flexible and programmatic remediation for custom compliance findings using AWS Config and AWS Lambda.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create an AWS Config custom rule, linking it to the Lambda evaluator function.","Develop an AWS Lambda function to evaluate resource compliance and return compliance status.","Develop a separate AWS Lambda function to perform the multi-step remediation actions.","Configure the Config rule to trigger the remediation Lambda function upon non-compliance."]'::json,
  '{1,2,0,3}',
  'To implement a flexible programmatic remediation, first, develop an AWS Lambda function (Option B) that serves as the custom evaluator, determining resource compliance and returning the status to AWS Config. Next, develop a separate AWS Lambda function (Option C) that encapsulates the multi-step remediation logic, calling various AWS APIs as needed. Then, create an AWS Config custom rule (Option A), linking it to the Lambda evaluator function. Finally, configure the Config rule (Option D) to automatically trigger the remediation Lambda function whenever a resource is found to be non-compliant.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain40_8',
  NOW(),
  18,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_40_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_40_m_40_2_116',
  'A company has different types of incidents (e.g., database outage, application error, security breach), each requiring a specific set of contacts and escalation paths. Order the steps to define distinct response plans for different incident types to ensure the right people are engaged immediately using AWS Systems Manager Incident Manager engagement plans.',
  'ordering',
  '["Define contacts and contact channels (e.g., email, SMS) in Incident Manager.","Create distinct engagement plans for each incident type, specifying contacts and escalation paths.","Create incident templates that reference the appropriate engagement plan for each incident type.","Configure Amazon EventBridge rules to automatically create incidents with the correct template based on alert categories."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'To define distinct response plans in Incident Manager, first, define contacts and their preferred contact channels (Option A) within Incident Manager. Next, create distinct engagement plans (Option B) for each incident type, specifying which contacts to engage and their escalation paths. Then, create incident templates (Option C) that reference the appropriate engagement plan for each specific incident type. Finally, configure Amazon EventBridge rules (Option D) to automatically create incidents in Incident Manager, selecting the correct template based on the category or severity of incoming alerts.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain40_8',
  NOW(),
  19,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_40_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_40_m_40_2_117',
  'A system administrator identifies an issue during a manual check and wants to log it in the central operational dashboard for tracking. Order the steps to manually create an operational item (OpsItem) for tracking and resolution using AWS Systems Manager OpsCenter.',
  'ordering',
  '["Navigate to AWS Systems Manager in the AWS Management Console.","Select ''OpsCenter'' from the Systems Manager navigation pane.","Choose ''Create OpsItem'' and fill in the required details (e.g., title, description, severity).","Submit the OpsItem for tracking and resolution."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'To manually create an OpsItem in AWS Systems Manager OpsCenter, first, navigate to AWS Systems Manager (Option A) in the AWS Management Console. Next, select ''OpsCenter'' (Option B) from the Systems Manager navigation pane. Then, choose ''Create OpsItem'' (Option C) and fill in all the necessary details such as title, description, and severity. Finally, submit the OpsItem (Option D) to add it to the central dashboard for tracking and resolution by the operations team.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain40_8',
  NOW(),
  20,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_40_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_40_m_40_2_118',
  'A compliance officer needs to deploy a collection of AWS Config rules and remediation actions to ensure adherence to a specific industry standard across multiple accounts. Order the steps to deploy and manage a standardized set of compliance rules and remediation actions efficiently using AWS Config Conformance Packs.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create a YAML template defining the AWS Config rules and remediation actions for the industry standard.","Deploy the Conformance Pack to target AWS accounts.","Review the compliance status of resources against the Conformance Pack.","Upload the YAML template to AWS Config to create a Conformance Pack."]'::json,
  '{0,3,1,2}',
  'To deploy a standardized set of compliance rules using AWS Config Conformance Packs, first, create a YAML template (Option A) that defines all the necessary AWS Config rules and their associated remediation actions for the specific industry standard. Next, upload this YAML template to AWS Config (Option D) to create a Conformance Pack. Then, deploy the Conformance Pack (Option B) to the target AWS accounts where compliance needs to be enforced. Finally, review the compliance status (Option C) of resources against the Conformance Pack in the AWS Config console to monitor adherence to the standard.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain40_8',
  NOW(),
  21,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_40_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_40_m_40_2_119',
  'A central operations team manages incidents across several AWS accounts. They need to initiate incident response actions, including runbook execution, in affected spoke accounts from their central account. Order the steps to orchestrate incident response actions across multiple AWS accounts from a single pane of glass using AWS Systems Manager Incident Manager.',
  'ordering',
  '["Configure cross-account access for Incident Manager to interact with resources in spoke accounts.","Define incident templates in the central account that include runbooks targeting spoke account resources.","Establish an incident in the central account, selecting a template that executes actions in a spoke account.","Ensure AWS Systems Manager Agent is running on instances in spoke accounts where runbooks will execute."]'::json,
  '{0,3,1,2}',
  'To orchestrate cross-account incident response with Incident Manager, first, configure cross-account access (Option A) for Incident Manager from the central account to securely interact with resources in the spoke accounts. Next, ensure that the AWS Systems Manager Agent (Option D) is running on instances in the spoke accounts, as this is required for runbook execution. Then, define incident templates (Option B) in the central account that include runbooks specifically designed to target and execute actions on resources within the spoke accounts. Finally, establish an incident (Option C) in the central account, selecting a template that will trigger the execution of these cross-account runbooks.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain40_8',
  NOW(),
  22,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_40_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_40_m_40_2_120',
  'A large enterprise needs to automate the remediation of non-compliant Amazon S3 bucket policies detected by AWS Config, ensuring that public access is immediately revoked and the incident is tracked for review. The solution must be highly automated and minimize manual intervention, adhering to strict security compliance. Order the steps to implement this automated remediation workflow.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create an AWS Systems Manager Automation document that remediates the S3 bucket policy to restrict public access.","Integrate AWS Config with AWS Systems Manager OpsCenter to create an OpsItem for each non-compliance for tracking.","Configure an AWS Config rule to detect non-compliant S3 bucket policies that allow public access.","Associate the AWS Config rule with the Systems Manager Automation document for auto-remediation."]'::json,
  '{2,0,3,1}',
  'The correct order to implement automated remediation for AWS Config non-compliance involves setting up the detection first, then defining the remediation action, associating them, and finally integrating with an operational tracking system. Option C (Configure an AWS Config rule...) is the initial step to identify non-compliant resources. Option A (Create an AWS Systems Manager Automation document...) defines the specific actions to fix the non-compliance. Option D (Associate the AWS Config rule with the Systems Manager Automation document...) links the detection to the remediation, enabling automatic fixes. Option B (Integrate AWS Config with AWS Systems Manager OpsCenter...) is typically the final step to ensure operational visibility and tracking of the remediation actions, providing a centralized place for incident management.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain40_8',
  NOW(),
  23,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_40_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_40_m_40_2_121',
  'A development team needs to implement a custom remediation for AWS Config non-compliance findings that cannot be addressed by standard AWS Systems Manager Automation documents. The remediation involves custom API calls to an external system. The solution must be serverless and notify relevant stakeholders upon completion. Order the steps to set up this Lambda-based custom auto-remediation.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create an AWS Config rule to detect the specific non-compliance requiring custom remediation.","Configure an Amazon EventBridge rule to capture the non-compliance events from AWS Config.","Develop an AWS Lambda function containing the custom remediation logic and external API calls.","Set up an Amazon SNS topic for notifications and configure the Lambda function to publish to it."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'Implementing custom auto-remediation starts with detection, then event capture, custom logic, and finally notification. Option A (Create an AWS Config rule...) is the initial step to identify resources that are out of compliance. Option B (Configure an Amazon EventBridge rule...) is used to capture the `Config Compliance Change` event generated by AWS Config when a resource becomes non-compliant. Option C (Develop an AWS Lambda function...) contains the custom code to perform the specific remediation actions. Option D (Set up an Amazon SNS topic...) ensures that stakeholders are informed about the remediation status or any issues encountered.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain40_8',
  NOW(),
  24,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_40_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_40_m_40_2_122',
  'A large organization with multiple AWS accounts wants to centralize operational issue tracking using AWS Systems Manager OpsCenter. This involves collecting OpsItems from various source accounts into a single, dedicated security account for unified management and response. Order the steps to establish this cross-account OpsCenter integration.',
  'ordering',
  '["Configure an Amazon EventBridge resource policy on the central event bus to allow source accounts to send events.","Create an Amazon EventBridge event bus in the central security account to receive events.","In each source account, create an EventBridge rule to send OpsCenter-related events to the central event bus.","In the central account, create EventBridge rules to process incoming events and create OpsItems in the central OpsCenter."]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}',
  'Centralizing OpsCenter tracking across accounts requires setting up a central event bus, granting permissions, and then configuring event forwarding and processing. Option B (Create an Amazon EventBridge event bus...) establishes the central point for event collection. Option A (Configure an Amazon EventBridge resource policy...) grants necessary permissions for other accounts to publish events to this central bus. Option C (In each source account, create an EventBridge rule...) ensures that relevant events (e.g., from CloudWatch alarms, Config rules) are forwarded from the source accounts. Finally, Option D (In the central account, create EventBridge rules...) processes these forwarded events to create or update OpsItems in the centralized OpsCenter.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain40_8',
  NOW(),
  25,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_40_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_40_m_40_2_123',
  'A compliance team needs to create a custom AWS Config rule to monitor the presence of a specific tag (`Environment:Production`) on all Amazon EC2 instances within their production accounts. This rule requires custom logic beyond what managed rules offer. Order the steps to deploy this custom AWS Config rule.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create an AWS Lambda function that evaluates the compliance of EC2 instances based on the tag.","Deploy the custom AWS Config rule to the target AWS accounts.","Define the custom AWS Config rule, specifying the Lambda function as the source of evaluation.","Configure the necessary IAM permissions for AWS Config to invoke the Lambda function."]'::json,
  '{0,3,2,1}',
  'Deploying a custom AWS Config rule involves creating the evaluation logic, setting up permissions, defining the rule, and then deploying it. Option A (Create an AWS Lambda function...) is the first step, as it contains the core logic for evaluating resource compliance. Option D (Configure the necessary IAM permissions...) ensures that AWS Config has the authority to execute this Lambda function. Option C (Define the custom AWS Config rule...) formally registers the rule with AWS Config, linking it to the Lambda function. Finally, Option B (Deploy the custom AWS Config rule...) makes the rule active in the desired accounts, starting the compliance evaluations.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain40_8',
  NOW(),
  26,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_40_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_40_m_40_2_124',
  'A DevOps team has an existing AWS Systems Manager Automation document that performs an automated remediation action for a specific AWS Config non-compliance. A new requirement necessitates an update to this Automation document to improve its efficiency and add a new parameter. The team needs to update it safely without impacting ongoing operations. Order the steps to safely update the Automation document.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create a new version of the existing AWS Systems Manager Automation document with the updated logic.","Update the AWS Config rule to reference the new version of the Automation document for auto-remediation.","Test the new version of the Automation document in a staging or non-production environment.","Monitor the production environment for successful remediation and any unexpected behavior."]'::json,
  '{0,2,1,3}',
  'Safely updating an Automation document involves creating a new version, thorough testing, updating references, and then monitoring. Option A (Create a new version...) allows for changes without immediately affecting the currently active version. Option C (Test the new version...) is crucial to validate the changes and ensure they work as expected in a controlled environment. Option B (Update the AWS Config rule...) then points the auto-remediation to the validated new version. Finally, Option D (Monitor the production environment...) is essential to confirm the successful deployment and ongoing correct operation of the updated remediation.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain40_8',
  NOW(),
  27,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_40_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_40_m_40_2_125',
  'A company wants to enforce a policy that only specific Amazon EC2 instance types are allowed in their development environment to manage costs. If a non-compliant instance type is launched, it should be automatically stopped and an alert sent to the development team. Order the steps to implement this auto-remediation and notification system.',
  'ordering',
  '["Define an AWS Config rule to detect non-compliant Amazon EC2 instance types.","Create an AWS Systems Manager Automation document to stop non-compliant EC2 instances.","Configure an Amazon SNS topic for notifications to the development team.","Associate the AWS Config rule with the Automation document and configure SNS notifications for remediation actions."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'Implementing auto-remediation for EC2 instance types starts with detection, followed by defining the remediation action, setting up notifications, and then linking them. Option A (Define an AWS Config rule...) is the first step to identify EC2 instances that do not adhere to the allowed types. Option B (Create an AWS Systems Manager Automation document...) defines the specific action to take (stopping the instance) when non-compliance is detected. Option C (Configure an Amazon SNS topic...) sets up the communication channel for alerts. Option D (Associate the AWS Config rule with the Automation document and configure SNS notifications...) links the detection, remediation, and notification components together, ensuring the entire workflow is automated.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain40_8',
  NOW(),
  28,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_40_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_40_m_40_2_126',
  'A security team needs to ensure a consistent set of AWS Config rules is applied across all accounts in their AWS Organization to enforce baseline security and compliance standards. They also want to enable auto-remediation for some of these rules. Order the steps to achieve this centralized Config rule deployment and management.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create an AWS Config conformance pack definition that includes the desired Config rules and remediation actions.","Deploy the conformance pack across the AWS Organization using the delegated administrator account.","Monitor the compliance dashboard in the central account to track compliance status across all accounts.","Configure specific AWS Config rules within the conformance pack to use AWS Systems Manager Automation for auto-remediation."]'::json,
  '{0,3,1,2}',
  'Centralized Config rule deployment with auto-remediation involves defining the rules and actions, deploying them organization-wide, and then monitoring. Option A (Create an AWS Config conformance pack definition...) bundles multiple AWS Config rules and their associated remediation actions into a single deployable unit. Option D (Configure specific AWS Config rules within the conformance pack to use AWS Systems Manager Automation...) integrates the auto-remediation directly into the conformance pack. Option B (Deploy the conformance pack across the AWS Organization...) ensures that these rules and remediations are consistently applied to all target accounts. Option C (Monitor the compliance dashboard...) provides a centralized view of compliance status and remediation effectiveness.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain40_8',
  NOW(),
  29,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_40_2',
  'hard'
);

