  'm_40_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_40_m_40_1_133',
  'A compliance team requires that all events published to a critical Amazon EventBridge event bus are archived for auditing purposes for 90 days. Additionally, the team wants the ability to replay these archived events into a test environment for debugging and development without affecting production. Order the steps to configure EventBridge archiving and replay.',
  'ordering',
  '["Configure an Amazon EventBridge archive for the critical event bus, specifying a 90-day retention period.","Create a new Amazon EventBridge event bus in the test environment to receive replayed events.","Initiate an Amazon EventBridge replay, selecting the desired archived events and targeting the test environment event bus.","Verify that the replayed events are correctly received and processed in the test environment."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'Configuring EventBridge archiving and replay involves setting up the archive, preparing a target for replay, initiating the replay, and then verifying. Option A (Configure an Amazon EventBridge archive...) is the first step to ensure events are retained for the specified duration. Option B (Create a new Amazon EventBridge event bus in the test environment...) provides a safe, isolated destination for replayed events. Option C (Initiate an Amazon EventBridge replay...) triggers the process of sending archived events to the test environment. Option D (Verify that the replayed events are correctly received...) is essential to confirm the replay process worked as intended for debugging or testing purposes.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain40_8',
  NOW(),
  61,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_40_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_40_m_40_1_134',
  'A security operations center (SOC) frequently receives benign or expected Amazon GuardDuty findings that generate unnecessary alerts, leading to alert fatigue. The SOC wants to implement a mechanism to automatically suppress these specific findings while still logging their occurrence for audit purposes. Order the steps to implement GuardDuty finding suppression using EventBridge and Lambda.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create an Amazon EventBridge rule to capture the specific benign Amazon GuardDuty finding patterns.","Develop an AWS Lambda function to process the captured finding and mark it as ''archived'' in GuardDuty.","Configure the EventBridge rule to target the Lambda function.","Set up logging for the Lambda function to Amazon CloudWatch Logs, and optionally archive to Amazon S3 for audit."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'Implementing GuardDuty finding suppression involves capturing the specific findings, processing them with custom logic, routing, and logging. Option A (Create an Amazon EventBridge rule...) is used to precisely identify and capture the benign GuardDuty findings that need to be suppressed. Option B (Develop an AWS Lambda function...) contains the logic to interact with GuardDuty''s API to archive or suppress the finding. Option C (Configure the EventBridge rule to target the Lambda function...) connects the detection of the finding to the suppression action. Option D (Set up logging for the Lambda function...) ensures that even suppressed findings are recorded for audit and future analysis, preventing loss of visibility.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain40_8',
  NOW(),
  62,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_40_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_40_m_40_1_135',
  'A development team is building a new application that publishes a variety of events to a custom Amazon EventBridge event bus. To ensure robust consumer development and data validation, they want to automatically discover the schema of these events and generate code bindings. Order the steps to leverage EventBridge schema discovery and Schema Registry.',
  'ordering',
  '["Enable schema discovery for the custom Amazon EventBridge event bus.","Publish events from the application to the EventBridge event bus.","Use the Amazon EventBridge Schema Registry to view discovered schemas and generate code bindings for various languages.","Integrate the generated code bindings into consumer applications for strong typing and validation."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'Leveraging EventBridge schema discovery involves enabling discovery, publishing events, generating code bindings, and then integrating them into consumers. Option A (Enable schema discovery...) is the initial configuration step that allows EventBridge to automatically infer event schemas. Option B (Publish events from the application...) provides the actual event data from which schemas can be discovered. Option C (Use the Amazon EventBridge Schema Registry...) allows developers to inspect the discovered schemas and generate language-specific code for consumers. Option D (Integrate the generated code bindings...) enhances consumer development by providing type safety and simplifying event parsing and validation.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain40_8',
  NOW(),
  63,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_40_1',
  'hard'
);

