INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_41_m_41_2_104',
  'A large enterprise is establishing a new multi-account AWS environment and needs to implement preventive security controls at scale. The security team wants to ensure that all new accounts and resources adhere to a strict baseline from inception. Order the steps required to establish a robust preventive control framework using AWS Organizations and Security Hub.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create Organizational Units (OUs) to group accounts based on function or compliance needs.","Enable AWS Security Hub in the management account and configure it to aggregate findings across all member accounts.","Enable all features in AWS Organizations to allow for full management of member accounts and policies.","Develop and apply Service Control Policies (SCPs) to OUs to enforce guardrails on permissions for member accounts."]'::json,
  '{2,0,3,1}',
  'To establish a robust preventive control framework, the foundational step is to enable all features in AWS Organizations (Option C) to unlock full management capabilities. Next, create Organizational Units (OUs) (Option A) to logically group accounts, which allows for granular policy application. Then, develop and apply Service Control Policies (SCPs) (Option D) to these OUs to enforce preventive guardrails, restricting what actions accounts within those OUs can perform. Finally, enable AWS Security Hub (Option B) in the management account to centralize security findings and monitor compliance against standards, providing visibility into the effectiveness of the preventive controls.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain41_8',
  NOW(),
  9,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_41_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_41_m_41_2_105',
  'A DevOps team is tasked with automating the rotation of database credentials for a critical application hosted on Amazon EC2 instances. The goal is to enhance security by eliminating hardcoded credentials and ensuring regular, automated rotation without application downtime. Order the steps to implement this solution using AWS Secrets Manager and AWS Lambda.',
  'ordering',
  '["Configure the application to retrieve the database credentials from AWS Secrets Manager at runtime.","Create a new secret in AWS Secrets Manager to store the database credentials.","Develop and deploy an AWS Lambda function that contains the logic to rotate the database credentials.","Configure automatic rotation for the secret in AWS Secrets Manager, linking it to the Lambda rotation function."]'::json,
  '{1,2,3,0}',
  'The first step is to create a new secret in AWS Secrets Manager (Option B) to securely store the database credentials. Next, develop and deploy an AWS Lambda function (Option C) that contains the specific logic required to rotate the credentials in the database itself. After the Lambda function is ready, configure automatic rotation for the secret in AWS Secrets Manager (Option D), associating it with the newly created Lambda rotation function. Finally, update the application (Option A) to retrieve the database credentials from AWS Secrets Manager at runtime, ensuring it always uses the current, rotated credentials.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain41_8',
  NOW(),
  10,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_41_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_41_m_41_2_106',
  'A security engineer needs to implement continuous vulnerability scanning for a fleet of Amazon EC2 instances and container images deployed in Amazon ECR. The objective is to identify software vulnerabilities and exposures (CVEs) across the compute environment automatically. Order the steps to set up Amazon Inspector v2 for this purpose.',
  'ordering',
  '["Review and prioritize the findings generated by Amazon Inspector, integrating them into existing vulnerability management workflows.","Ensure the AWS Systems Manager (SSM) Agent is installed and running on all target Amazon EC2 instances.","Enable Amazon Inspector v2 across the AWS environment, including all desired accounts and regions.","Define the scanning scope and resource tags to ensure all relevant EC2 instances and ECR repositories are covered."]'::json,
  '{2,1,3,0}',
  'To set up Amazon Inspector v2, the initial step is to enable the service (Option C) across the AWS environment, which activates its scanning capabilities. For EC2 scanning, it''s crucial to ensure the AWS Systems Manager (SSM) Agent is installed and running (Option B) on all target instances, as Inspector leverages SSM for data collection. Next, define the scanning scope and resource tags (Option D) to specify which EC2 instances and ECR repositories Inspector should monitor. Finally, review and prioritize the findings (Option A) generated by Inspector, integrating them into the organization''s vulnerability management process for remediation.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain41_8',
  NOW(),
  11,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_41_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_41_m_41_2_107',
  'A company is expanding its AWS footprint to multiple accounts and requires a centralized view of its security posture and compliance status. The security team wants to aggregate findings from various AWS security services into a single dashboard. Order the steps to effectively centralize security findings using AWS Security Hub.',
  'ordering',
  '["Enable AWS Security Hub in the designated management account.","Invite member accounts to associate with the Security Hub management account.","Enable desired security standards (e.g., AWS Foundational Security Best Practices, CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark) within Security Hub.","Review and analyze aggregated security findings and compliance status from the Security Hub console."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'To centralize security findings, the first step is to enable AWS Security Hub in the designated management account (Option A), which acts as the central aggregator. Next, invite member accounts (Option B) to associate with this management account, allowing their findings to be sent to the central hub. Once accounts are linked, enable desired security standards (Option C) within Security Hub to evaluate resources against specific compliance benchmarks. Finally, review and analyze the aggregated security findings and compliance status (Option D) from the Security Hub console to gain a comprehensive overview of the security posture.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain41_8',
  NOW(),
  12,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_41_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_41_m_41_2_108',
  'A security audit has revealed potential external access to several Amazon S3 buckets, posing a data leakage risk. The security team needs to identify and remediate any unintended public or cross-account access to these critical resources. Order the steps to effectively use AWS IAM Access Analyzer for this task.',
  'ordering',
  '["Review the findings generated by IAM Access Analyzer to identify resources with external access.","Enable AWS IAM Access Analyzer for the AWS account or organization.","Define the ''zone of trust'' within Access Analyzer to specify trusted accounts and organizations.","Remediate identified external access by modifying resource policies or access control lists."]'::json,
  '{1,2,0,3}',
  'To identify and remediate external access, the first step is to enable AWS IAM Access Analyzer (Option B) for the relevant AWS account or organization. Next, define the ''zone of trust'' (Option C) within Access Analyzer, which helps differentiate intended cross-account access from unintended external access. After configuration, review the findings (Option A) generated by Access Analyzer, which highlights resources exposed to entities outside the zone of trust. Finally, remediate identified external access (Option D) by adjusting resource policies (e.g., S3 bucket policies) or access control lists to restrict unintended access.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain41_8',
  NOW(),
  13,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_41_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_41_m_41_2_109',
  'A company requires a strict preventive control to ensure that no Amazon S3 buckets in any of its development accounts can ever be made publicly accessible. This control must be enforced at the organizational level. Order the steps to implement this using an AWS Organizations Service Control Policy (SCP).',
  'ordering',
  '["Attach the created SCP to the Organizational Unit (OU) containing the development accounts.","Create a new Service Control Policy (SCP) that explicitly denies ''s3:PutBucketPublicAccessBlock'' and ''s3:PutBucketPolicy'' actions that allow public access.","Verify the SCP''s effectiveness by attempting to make an S3 bucket public in a development account.","Monitor AWS Config and Security Hub for any non-compliant S3 buckets, ensuring the SCP is working as intended."]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}',
  'To enforce a preventive control for S3 public access, begin by creating a new Service Control Policy (SCP) (Option B) that explicitly denies actions allowing public access. Next, attach this SCP to the Organizational Unit (OU) (Option A) that contains the development accounts, ensuring the policy applies to all accounts within that OU. After attachment, verify the SCP''s effectiveness (Option C) by attempting to perform the restricted action (e.g., making an S3 bucket public) in a development account, which should be denied. Finally, monitor AWS Config and Security Hub (Option D) to continuously ensure compliance and confirm the SCP is functioning as expected.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain41_8',
  NOW(),
  14,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_41_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_41_m_41_2_110',
  'A development team needs to create new IAM roles for their applications, but the security team wants to ensure that these roles never exceed a predefined set of maximum permissions, even if an administrator mistakenly attaches a more permissive policy. Order the steps to implement this using an IAM Permission Boundary.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create a new IAM policy that defines the maximum permissions allowed for any role.","Attach the permission boundary policy to the IAM role during its creation or modification.","Define the actual granular permissions for the IAM role using inline or managed policies.","Test the effective permissions of the IAM role to ensure it adheres to both the permission boundary and its identity-based policies."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'To implement an IAM Permission Boundary, first create a new IAM policy (Option A) that explicitly defines the maximum permissions that any role can ever possess. Next, attach this permission boundary policy to the IAM role (Option B) when it is created or modified. Then, define the actual granular permissions (Option C) for the IAM role using standard identity-based policies (inline or managed policies). Finally, test the effective permissions (Option D) of the IAM role to confirm that the combined effect of the permission boundary and the identity-based policies correctly limits access as intended.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain41_8',
  NOW(),
  15,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_41_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_41_m_41_2_111',
  'A DevOps team is deploying a new microservice that requires access to sensitive API keys and database credentials. To adhere to security best practices, these secrets must not be hardcoded and should be managed centrally with automated rotation. Order the steps for integrating AWS Secrets Manager into the deployment pipeline for this new application.',
  'ordering',
  '["Configure the application''s deployment process to retrieve the secrets from AWS Secrets Manager at runtime.","Store the sensitive API keys and database credentials as secrets in AWS Secrets Manager.","Implement an AWS Lambda function for automated rotation of the database credentials.","Set up the rotation schedule for the secrets in AWS Secrets Manager, linking to the Lambda function for database credentials."]'::json,
  '{1,2,3,0}',
  'To integrate Secrets Manager, the first step is to store the sensitive API keys and database credentials as secrets in AWS Secrets Manager (Option B). For database credentials, implement an AWS Lambda function (Option C) specifically designed for automated rotation. Next, set up the rotation schedule for the secrets in AWS Secrets Manager (Option D), ensuring the Lambda function is correctly linked for database credential rotation. Finally, configure the application''s deployment process (Option A) to retrieve these secrets from Secrets Manager at runtime, ensuring secure and dynamic access to credentials.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain41_8',
  NOW(),
  16,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_41_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_41_m_41_2_112',
  'A security team wants to establish a baseline for compliance standards across their AWS environment and continuously monitor adherence to these standards. They need to use AWS Security Hub to achieve this. Order the steps to effectively set up and utilize Security Hub for compliance monitoring.',
  'ordering',
  '["Enable AWS Security Hub in the primary security account.","Select and enable the desired compliance standards, such as CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark or AWS Foundational Security Best Practices.","Review the initial findings and prioritize remediation efforts based on severity and compliance impact.","Configure automated responses or integrations with other services like AWS Chatbot for critical findings."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'To set up Security Hub for compliance monitoring, first enable AWS Security Hub (Option A) in the designated primary security account. Next, select and enable the desired compliance standards (Option B), which will trigger Security Hub to begin evaluating resources against these benchmarks. After the initial evaluation, review the findings (Option C) and prioritize remediation based on their severity and impact on compliance. Finally, configure automated responses or integrations (Option D) for critical findings to streamline the remediation process and enhance operational efficiency.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain41_8',
  NOW(),
  17,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_41_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_41_m_41_2_113',
  'A large organization with numerous AWS accounts aims to identify and reduce the attack surface created by overly permissive or unused IAM access. The security team wants to automate the process of finding and remediating these access issues. Order the steps to achieve this using AWS IAM Access Analyzer.',
  'ordering',
  '["Enable IAM Access Analyzer for the organization or individual accounts.","Identify unused access findings and review the last accessed information for services and actions.","Create a remediation plan to revoke unused permissions and refine overly permissive policies to least privilege.","Implement the remediation plan, then re-evaluate findings to confirm the reduction in access."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'To identify and remediate access issues with IAM Access Analyzer, begin by enabling the service (Option A) for the organization or specific accounts. Next, identify unused access findings (Option B) and review the last accessed information provided by Access Analyzer to understand which permissions are not being utilized. Based on these insights, create a comprehensive remediation plan (Option C) to revoke unused permissions and refine overly permissive policies to adhere to the principle of least privilege. Finally, implement the remediation plan (Option D) and then re-evaluate the Access Analyzer findings to confirm that the desired reduction in access has been achieved.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain41_8',
  NOW(),
  18,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_41_2',
  'hard'
);

